. #R$CC5A, #R$CC82, #R$CD8B, #R$D6C1 and #R$DD35.
W $A01F,$02

g $A021 Match Flag
D $A021 Match flag cleared by #R$C654 and set by #R$C6B8.
B $A021,$01

g $A022 Token Value Buffer
@ $A022 label=TokenValueBuffer
D $A022 Token Value Buffer read by #R$A887.
W $A022,$02

g $A024 Command Mode
D $A024 Command mode copied onto a new display record by #R$B8FC.
. Read by #R$AAC4, #R$AAD1, #R$AB52, #R$AB91, #R$ACFB, #R$AD57, #R$AFB3, #R$B135, #R$B151,
. #R$B8AA and #R$D6C1.
B $A024,$01

g $A025 Print Position Flag
@ $A025 label=PrintPositionFlag
D $A025 Print Position Flag read by #R$A9B7 and #R$AA14.
B $A025,$01

g $A026 Token Format Flag
@ $A026 label=TokenFormatFlag
D $A026 Token Format Flag read by #R$A45E, #R$A815, #R$A887, #R$AA14 and #R$D6C1.
B $A026,$01

g $A027 Print Mode Flag
@ $A027 label=PrintModeFlag
D $A027 Print Mode Flag read by #R$9A80, #R$A2E4, #R$A7EB, #R$A9B7, #R$C1E1, #R$CB67, #R$CBA7,
. #R$D6C1 and #R$D898.
B $A027,$01

g $A028 Object Processing Source Flag
@ $A028 label=ObjectProcessingSourceFlag
D $A028 Flag indicating the source of object processing: #N$00 for current character/text token, #N$01 for room display/graphics parameters.
. Read by #R$A37D, #R$A65D, #R$A702, #R$A724, #R$A751, #R$D1C6, #R$D405 and #R$D526.
B $A028,$01

g $A029 Saved Text Pointer
@ $A029 label=SavedTextPointer
D $A029 Saved copy of the text pointer.
. Used by #R$AB00, #R$AB4C, #R$AB6F, #R$AB83, #R$ACD6, #R$AE7F, #R$B092, #R$B8A3,
. #R$D6F5 and #R$D9CD.
W $A029,$02

g $A02B Active Text Pointer
@ $A02B label=ActiveTextPointer
D $A02B Text pointer being walked. #R$B894 and #R$B8AA also store the word from #R$B868 here.
. Used by #R$AADB, #R$AB03, #R$AB0E, #R$AB4C, #R$AB6F, #R$AB83, #R$ACDC, #R$AD2E, #R$AE7F,
. #R$AEA4, #R$B06D, #R$B894, #R$B8AA, #R$B8C7, #R$D788 and #R$D797.
W $A02B,$02

g $A02D Saved Source Byte
@ $A02D label=SavedSourceByte
D $A02D Source byte saved beside the text pointer.
. Used by #R$AB37, #R$AE86 and #R$B015.
B $A02D,$01

g $A02E Location Match Flag
@ $A02E label=LocationMatchFlag
D $A02E Set when a location field matches, and cleared before the display scan. It is written but never read back by address.
. Used by #R$A4E9 and #R$B3D9.
B $A02E,$01

g $A02F Display Pointer
@ $A02F label=DisplayPointer
D $A02F Stores the display pointer for special display mode.
. Read by #R$A37D, #R$B4A3, #R$C628, #R$DD35, #R$E3A9 and #R$E510.
W $A02F,$02

g $A033 Flags: Saved Character
D $A033 Saved character id written by the display handlers.
. Read by #R$A2E4, #R$B191, #R$B936, #R$C4D1, #R$C929, #R$CA76, #R$CDBF, #R$CE18, #R$CE57,
. #R$CE6A, #R$D6C1, #R$D85B, #R$DBF7, #R$DCA7, #R$E2EE and #R$E3F5.
B $A033,$01

g $A034 Room Display Parameter
@ $A034 label=RoomDisplayParameter
D $A034 Room Display Parameter read by #R$9AA3, #R$9B50, #R$9C43, #R$9CF2, #R$9DFF, #R$A2E4,
. #R$A724, #R$A751, #R$A887, #R$B191, #R$B4A3, #R$B4FD, #R$B517, #R$B8AA, #R$B936, #R$C4D1,
. #R$C591, #R$C628, #R$C8EF, #R$C929, #R$C9BF, #R$CAAF, #R$CB7B, #R$CDA1, #R$CDBF, #R$CE18,
. #R$CE3B, #R$CE4F, #R$CE6A, #R$CF80, #R$D26C, #R$D6C1, #R$DBF7, #R$DCA7, #R$DD35, #R$DEE4,
. #R$DF5B, #R$E03A, #R$E151, #R$E207, #R$E2EE, #R$E3A9, #R$E3F5, #R$E4FB, #R$E510, #R$E58C,
. #R$E691, #R$EAF8, #R$F19D and #R$F1D6.
B $A034,$01

g $A035 Room Graphics Parameter
@ $A035 label=RoomGraphicsParameter
D $A035 Room Graphics Parameter read by #R$9B25, #R$A724, #R$B191, #R$B2DF, #R$B54A, #R$B5A4,
. #R$B936, #R$C4D1, #R$C542, #R$C560, #R$C591, #R$C5FC, #R$C613, #R$C6BE, #R$C8EF, #R$C9BF,
. #R$CE18, #R$CE3B, #R$CE4F, #R$CE6A, #R$DEC1, #R$DEE4, #R$E17B, #R$E2EE, #R$E34F, #R$E510,
. #R$E58C and #R$E691.
B $A035,$01

g $A036 Currently Processed Character ID
@ $A036 label=CurrentCharacter_ID
D $A036 Currently Processed Character ID read by #R$9A80, #R$9B25, #R$9B50, #R$9C6A, #R$9ED7,
. #R$A360, #R$A37D, #R$A724, #R$A751, #R$A887, #R$B91F, #R$BA20, #R$C7A6, #R$C821, #R$CA7F,
. #R$CAAF, #R$CB43, #R$CB58, #R$CBC2, #R$CC29, #R$CC37, #R$CC82, #R$CEDE, #R$CF80, #R$CFB6,
. #R$D113, #R$D187, #R$D275, #R$D4EF, #R$DC6C, #R$DCA7, #R$DD0B, #R$DD0E, #R$DE60, #R$DEE4,
. #R$DFCC, #R$E03A, #R$E207, #R$E2EE, #R$E34F, #R$E3A9, #R$E3F5, #R$E510, #R$E560, #R$E58C,
. #R$E691 and #R$EFB3.
B $A036,$01

g $A037 Flags: Random Seed
D $A037 Copy of the memory refresh register, stored at the game entry point.
. Read by #R$A040 and #R$D2AD.
B $A037,$01

g $A038 Object Attribute Flag
@ $A038 label=ObjectAttributeFlag
D $A038 Stores bit 0 of the object attribute value (from #R$A01D).
. Read by #R$B4C1, #R$B568, #R$BDC2, #R$D36A and #R$D39B.
B $A038,$01

c $A040 Game Entry Point
@ $A040 label=GameEntryPoint
D $A040 Starts a game: clears the parser, asks whether pictures are wanted, then runs each
. character from the timetable.
  $A040,$08 Clear the parser workspace and the initialised flag.
N $A048 Draw decorative line.
  $A048,$03 Call #R$C14A to draw the decorative line.
  $A04B,$03 Point the stack at #R$5D80.
  $A04E,$03 Call #R$C415 to wait for keypress.
  $A051,$04 Jump to #R$A05B if the player pressed "n".
  $A055,$02 See whether the key is N.
N $A057 The player didn't press N, so pictures stay enabled.
  $A057,$04 Go to #R$A05C with #N$01 (pictures on) unless the key was N.
N $A05B The player pressed either "N" or "n".
@ $A05B label=GameEntryPoint_ClearFlags
  $A05B,$01 Clear the pictures-enabled flag. The player pressed n.
@ $A05C label=GameEntryPoint_Next
  $A05C,$03 Record the pictures-enabled flag.
  $A05F,$05 Write the contents of the Memory Refresh Register to *#R$A037.

c $A064 Run The Game Loop
@ $A064 label=Game_Initialisation
D $A064 Clears the startup flag, updates the date bar, and walks each character until play
. begins.
  $A064,$04 Clear the startup flag.
@ $A068 label=Game_Loop
  $A068,$06 Jump to #R$A0E7 if the startup flag is set.
  $A06E,$06 Call #R$BFD9 to show the game clock, then call #R$BF79 to show the date bar.
N $A074 Handler for Sherlock (as he's the first character).
  $A074,$04 Use the timetable at #R$9860.
  $A078,$03 Fetch Sherlocks location.
  $A07B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A093 if the schedule entry is not #N$32.
  $A07F,$07 Call #R$C41F to read the keyboard, and go to #R$A093 if no key was pressed.
  $A086,$04 Jump to #R$A093 if the player did not press "ENTER".
  $A08A,$09 Write *#R$9FDD to *#REGiy+#N$06/ *#REGiy+#N$07.
N $A093 Cycle through the character timetable and run through each characters
. script according to their schedule and current game time.
@ $A093 label=Handler_ProcessCharacters
  $A093,$04 Use the timetable at #R$9860.
@ $A097 label=CharacterProcessing_Loop
  $A097,$06 Jump to #R$A068 if the character id is #N$FF.
N $A09D This is a valid character so begin processing them.
  $A09D,$06 Call #R$A0DB to check character schedule.
  $A0A3,$02 Go to #R$A0D1 if the current time is below this character's schedule time.
N $A0A5 The currently processed character has an active schedule so process it.
  $A0A5,$03 Fetch the character ID and store it in #REGa.
  $A0A8,$06 Call #R$CC37 to run this character's action.
N $A0AE Repeatedly execute the characters script until it completes or
. encounters a wait condition.
@ $A0AE label=CharacterScript_Loop
  $A0AE,$02 Restore the character ID from the stack, keeping a copy there.
N $A0B0 Script interpreter.
  $A0B0,$03 Call #R$CC78 to run this character's script.
N $A0B3 Keep looping until the script for this character is finished.
  $A0B3,$07 Jump back to #R$A0AE if #R$A01F is zero.
  $A0BA,$03 Restore the saved character ID and the schedule entry from the stack.
N $A0BD Set up the next schedule time for this character.
  $A0BD,$07 Add the characters schedule time to the current time.
  $A0C4,$06 And write it back to the characters schedule.
  $A0CA,$07 Call #R$9C77 to execute character action.
N $A0D1 Move to the next character in the timetable.
@ $A0D1 label=Character_Next
  $A0D1,$07 Jump back to #R$A097, the character processing loop.

b $A0D8 Word: Location Graphics
D $A0D8 Pointer to #R$F60C. #R$A0DA is cleared at the start of the game.
. Read by #R$A25D.
W $A0D8,$02
B $A0DA,$01 Read by #R$A064, #R$B0AC and #R$B0C6.

c $A0DB Check Character Schedule
@ $A0DB label=CheckCharacterSchedule
D $A0DB Compares #REGhl with the schedule time at #REGiy+#N$06-#N$07.
R $A0DB HL Current game time
R $A0DB O:F Carry flag is set if the character should be skipped
  $A0DB,$06 Read this character's place in the schedule.
  $A0E1,$01 Read the high byte of the pointer.
  $A0E2,$01 See whether the two values match.
  $A0E3,$01 Return if they differ.
  $A0E4,$01 Read the low byte of the pointer.
  $A0E5,$01 See whether the two values match.
  $A0E6,$01 Return.

c $A0E7 Load Or Save The Game
@ $A0E7 label=LoadOrSaveGame
D $A0E7 Loads the game unless the parameter is #N$02, which saves it instead.
R $A0E7 A #N$02 saves the game. Any other value loads it
  $A0E7,$04 Go to #R$A0F1 if it is #N$02.

c $A0EB Load The Game
D $A0EB Loads the saved game and starts it from #R$A040.
  $A0EB,$06 Jump back to #R$A040 to start the game.

c $A0F1 Save The Game
@ $A0F1 label=LoadOrSaveGame_Save
D $A0F1 Saves the game and continues at #R$A064.
  $A0F1,$06 Jump back to #R$A064, the game initialisation.

c $A0F7 Fetch Object Pointer From Stack
@ $A0F7 label=FetchObjectPointerFromStack
D $A0F7 Takes the record linked just before this one, or reports that the link is empty.
R $A0F7 IX Object pointer (on stack)
R $A0F7 O:IX Object pointer
R $A0F7 O:HL Zero if object pointer was zero, otherwise preserved
N $A0F7 This will be restored back into #REGhl by the end of the routine.
  $A0F7,$07 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $A0FE,$02 Is #REGhl zero?.
N $A100 Pretty clever way of using the stack here.
  $A100,$01 Swap the *#REGsp with the object pointer, restoring #REGhl to the original
. value.
  $A101,$02 Restore the saved character record from the stack.
  $A103,$01 Return.

c $A104 Increment Pointer And Compare
@ $A104 label=IncrementPointerAndCompare
D $A104 Step pointer and compare.
  $A104,$04 Step on two places.
@ $A108 label=IncrementPointerAndCompare_Loop
  $A108,$08 Read the previous pointer, clear the carry and save #REGhl on the stack.
  $A110,$06 Jump ahead to #R$A118 if the two pointers meet.
  $A116,$02 Jump back to #R$A108 to read the next schedule entry.

N $A118 Return.
@ $A118 label=IncrementPointerAndCompare_Next
  $A118,$01 Restore the matching pointer from the stack.
  $A119,$01 Return.

g $A11A Workspace
D $A11A Workspace between the pointer routines.
. Read by #R$A11C.
W $A11A,$02

c $A11C Allocate Memory
@ $A11C label=AllocateMemory
D $A11C Grows the workspace at #R$9FE3 by the size asked for, plus a three-byte header, and returns the
. new block.
  $A11C,$08 Add #N$03 to the total.
  $A124,$05 Start the free-space search and read the list at #R$9FE3.
  $A129,$04 Point #REGiy at the free-space list (#R$9FE3).
@ $A12D label=AllocateMemory_Loop
  $A12D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A160 if the pointer is #N($0000,$04,$04).
  $A131,$08 Jump ahead to #R$A13D when the high byte of this free block is set.
  $A139,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A157 if the first value is below the second.
@ $A13D label=AllocateMemory_Next
  $A13D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A14E if bit 0 is clear.
  $A141,$07 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $A148,$03 Compare #REGhl with #REGde.
  $A14B,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $A14C,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A157 if #REGhl was below #REGde.
@ $A14E label=AllocateMemory_KeepAllocationRemember
  $A14E,$07 Copy the allocation into #REGix and remember the caller's index.
  $A155,$02 Set the mark on this character.
@ $A157 label=AllocateMemory_NextBlock
  $A157,$09 Follow the next free block and repeat from #R$A12D.

N $A160 See whether bit 0 of the count is set, then read the link stored ahead of this
. record, then clear the high byte of the count.
@ $A160 label=AllocateMemory_TestBitCountLink
  $A160,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A1AC if bit 0 is clear.
  $A164,$08 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record and count #N$00.
  $A16C,$01 Clear the carry before subtracting the block length from the free space.
  $A16D,$02 Measure the pointer against the count.
  $A16F,$01 Read the high byte of the pointer.
  $A170,$01 Clear the carry so a zero high byte means only a few bytes of free space remain.
  $A171,$02 Jump to #R$A18A when the free space still has a high byte.
  $A173,$05 Go to #R$A18A if at least four bytes of free space remain.
  $A178,$07 Bring the saved index back from #R$A11A and read the character id.
  $A17F,$03 Store the character id.
  $A182,$08 Jump ahead to #R$A193 to step to the next character record.

c $A18A Store That Byte In The Link Ahead
@ $A18A label=StoreThatByteLinkAhead
D $A18A Store that byte in the link ahead of this record, then exchange the source with the
. pointer.
  $A18A,$07 Store the pointer in the previous memory entry.
  $A191,$02 Step to the next memory entry.
@ $A193 label=StoreThatByteLinkAhead_TestBytePreviousMemory
  $A193,$08 Store this byte in the previous memory entry and take its address.
  $A19B,$02 Point #REGhl at the two bytes before this record.
  $A19D,$01 Copy the count into #REGb.
  $A19E,$01 Drop one from that count.
  $A19F,$01 Clear #REGa so the following loop writes zeros.
@ $A1A0 label=StoreThatByteLinkAhead_CountEntryRepeat
  $A1A0,$04 Count the next entry and repeat from #R$A1A0.
  $A1A4,$02 Reset the zero flag.
  $A1A6,$05 Restore the saved registers from the stack.
  $A1AB,$01 Return.

N $A1AC Join the next step.
@ $A1AC label=StoreThatByteLinkAhead_JoinStep
  $A1AC,$03 Jump to #R$C1D8, the out of memory.

c $A1AF Follow Record Pointer
@ $A1AF label=FollowRecordPointer
D $A1AF Copies the character-record pointer and jumps to the routine that pointer holds.
R $A1AF IX Pointer to jump to
R $A1AF HL (preserved on stack)
  $A1AF,$06 Keep the pointer, copy the record into it, and go to #R$A1C1.

N $A1B5 Walks the linked character records from the link stored just ahead of the
. current record, running each one until the link is empty.
@ $A1B5 label=FollowRecordPointer_ReadLink
  $A1B5,$09 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record, then call #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.
  $A1BE,$03 Use the saved pointer as the character record.

N $A1C1 Loop while the link just read is not zero.
  $A1C1,$04 Jump back to #R$A1B5 while the pointer is not zero.
  $A1C5,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $A1C6,$01 Return.

c $A1C7 Process Memory Entry
@ $A1C7 label=ProcessMemoryEntry
D $A1C7 Follows one linked record, stores its link, and clears the marker after that link.
  $A1C7,$04 Save the pointer, the source and the character record.
  $A1CB,$08 Step back over the block header and point #REGix at the free-space list (#R$9FE3).
@ $A1D3 label=ProcessMemoryEntry_Loop
  $A1D3,$06 Read the record's flag byte and read the moved marker.
  $A1D9,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A1EB if the pointer is zero.
  $A1DD,$04 Compare #REGhl with #REGde, keeping a copy of #REGhl.
  $A1E1,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $A1E2,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A1EB if #REGhl was below #REGde.
  $A1E4,$07 Step the source on two bytes, copy it into the record, and go to #R$A1D3.

N $A1EB Stores the link in the record flag, clears the marker after that link, and stores the source address.
@ $A1EB label=ProcessMemoryEntry_Next
  $A1EB,$07 Store the record's flag byte and store the moved marker.
  $A1F2,$02 Clear the marker after the link.
  $A1F4,$07 Call #R$A20C to calculate pointer offset.
  $A1FB,$02 Go to #R$A206 if the offset meets this pointer.
  $A1FD,$06 Read the link word.
  $A203,$03 Copy the link word from #REGhl into #REGix.
  $A206,$05 Call #R$A20C to calculate pointer offset.
@ $A206 label=ProcessMemoryEntry_Call
  $A20B,$01 Return.

c $A20C Calculate Pointer Offset
@ $A20C label=CalculatePointerOffset
D $A20C Adds the link word to its own address so the pointer reaches the linked record.
  $A20C,$08 Copy the record into the pointer, step back two bytes, and read the link.
  $A214,$07 Add the link to its own address to reach the linked record, and read the record's flag byte.
  $A21B,$03 Read the moved marker.
  $A21E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A225 if the pointer is not zero.
  $A222,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $A224,$01 Return.

N $A225 Return unless the two pointers are the same distance apart.
@ $A225 label=CalculatePointerOffset_Next
  $A225,$04 Return if the adjusted pointer does not match the link.
  $A229,$08 Read the link in this record and the field it points at.
  $A231,$03 Read the high byte of the link into #REGd.
  $A234,$03 Read the following word into #REGbc.
  $A237,$03 Read the low byte saved ahead of this record.
  $A23A,$07 Add #REGde to the saved pointer and store the low byte.
  $A241,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $A244,$07 Store the record's flag byte and store the moved marker.
  $A24B,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A24C,$01 Return.

g $A24D Token Word Table
D $A24D Eight token words. #R$A7EB indexes them from the token parameter, and uses the half at #R$A255 when the print mode or the object display flag is set.
. Used by #R$A7EB.
W $A24D,$02 "#R($5DBF+(#PEEK(#PC+$01)*$0100+#PEEK(#PC)))(#TOKENPTR)".
L $A24D,$02,$08

c $A25D Check Text Token Match
@ $A25D label=CheckTextTokenMatch
D $A25D Sets the zero flag when pictures are off, the token pointer is zero, or #R$D34E returns #N$FF.
R $A25D A Token ID to check
R $A25D O:F Zero flag set if token matches or end reached
  $A25D,$08 Jump to #R$A275 if the pictures-enabled flag is zero.
  $A265,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A275 if the pointer is zero.
  $A26C,$07 Copy the pointer into the record and call #R$D34E with the count in #REGb to search
. the three-byte table.
  $A273,$02 See whether the entry is the end marker.
  $A275,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $A276,$01 Return.

c $A277 Process Text Token
@ $A277 label=ProcessTextToken
R $A277 A Token ID
D $A277 If this token is not already on screen, clears the picture lines and draws the room again.
  $A277,$08 Call #R$A25D to check text token match.
  $A27F,$02 Go to #R$A290 if there is no text token.
  $A281,$06 Call #R$A296 and then #R$C129 to initialise the screen display.
  $A287,$09 Call #R$F3C2 to draw.
@ $A290 label=ProcessTextToken_Next
  $A290,$05 Restore the saved result, the saved count, the saved pointer and the saved character record from the stack.
  $A295,$01 Return.

c $A296 Clear Display Lines
@ $A296 label=ClearDisplayLines
D $A296 Blanks the lines used for the room picture.
  $A296,$02 Count #N$12.
@ $A298 label=ClearDisplayLines_Loop
N $A298 Print newline.
  $A298,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $A29B,$06 Clear the parser flag at #R$A019 and repeat from #R$A298.
  $A2A1,$01 Return.

c $A2A2 Print Newline
@ $A2A2 label=PrintNewline
E $A2A2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8583 }
. LIST#
D $A2A2 Prints a carriage return and puts the flags back afterwards.
  $A2A2,$06 Keep #REGa, print a carriage return through #R$A9B7.
  $A2A8,$01 Restore #REGa from the stack.
  $A2A9,$01 Return.

g $A2AA Object Display Flag
@ $A2AA label=ObjectDisplayFlag
D $A2AA Object Display Flag read by #R$A2E4, #R$A7EB, #R$D187 and #R$D590.
B $A2AA,$01

c $A2AB Clear Buffer
@ $A2AB label=ClearBuffer
E $A2AB View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$70E2 }
. LIST#
D $A2AB Blanks the buffer before the next step uses it.
  $A2AB,$01 Start from zero.
  $A2AC,$04 Count down and repeat from #R$A2AC until the count runs out.
  $A2B0,$01 Return.

c $A2B1 Calculate Object Table Offset
@ $A2B1 label=CalculateObjectTableOffset
E $A2B1 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$70E8 }
. LIST#
D $A2B1 Turns an object index into the address of its eight-byte runtime record at #R$8259.
R $A2B1 A Object index (0-based)
R $A2B1 O:HL Pointer to object entry in table
  $A2B1,$04 Count down one, store that in the low byte and clear the high byte.
  $A2B5,$03 Multiply #REGhl by #N$08.
  $A2B8,$03 Use the object runtime table as the source.
  $A2BB,$01 Advance the pointer by the source length.
  $A2BC,$01 Return.

c $A2BD Extract Object Attributes
@ $A2BD label=ExtractObjectAttributes
E $A2BD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$70F3 }
. LIST#
D $A2BD Packs two attribute nibbles from the object record into #R$A01E and #R$A01D.
R $A2BD IX Pointer to object data
N $A2BD Extract the first attribute value from bit-packed object data.
  $A2BD,$07 Read the object attribute.
  $A2C4,$02,b$01 Keep the high nibble of the object attribute (now in bits 0-3) as the low half of the display flag.
  $A2C6,$04 Read the character record.
  $A2CA,$02,b$01 Take the high nibble of byte #N$07 of the object data as the high half of the display flag.
  $A2CC,$01 Join the two halves into one byte.
  $A2CD,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$A01E.
N $A2D0 Extract the second attribute value from bit-packed object data.
  $A2D0,$07 Read the moved marker.
  $A2D7,$02,b$01 Keep the high nibble of the moved marker (now in bits 0-3) as the low half of the object attribute value.
  $A2D9,$04 Read the action flags.
  $A2DD,$02,b$01 Take the high nibble of the action flags (byte #N$03) as the high half of the object attribute value.
  $A2DF,$01 Join the two halves into one byte.
  $A2E0,$03 Record the result in the object attribute value at #R$A01D.
  $A2E3,$01 Return.

c $A2E4 Process Object Display
@ $A2E4 label=ProcessObjectDisplay
D $A2E4 Sets the object display flag and prints the object when the player can see it.
  $A2E4,$08 Write #N$01 to the object display flag.
  $A2EC,$04 Save the character record, the pointer and the source.
  $A2F0,$06 Write *#R$A033 to *#R$A35D.
  $A2F6,$06 Write *#R$A034 to *#R$A35E.
N $A2FC Are the parser flags clear?
  $A2FC,$09 Jump to #R$A306 if the parser flags are clear.
  $A305,$01 Start the object from zero.
  $A306,$06 Store it in the print mode, then call #R$D078 to test object visibility.
  $A30C,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A31A if #R$D078 finds the object is not visible.
  $A30E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A31A if the count is not zero.
  $A312,$06 Take the cannot see message, then call #R$A59F to print the cannot see
. message.
  $A318,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A34E to finish this object.

N $A31A Read the saved object fields at #R$A35D, then use the saved pointer as the character
. record, then read the object attribute value at #R$A01D.
@ $A31A label=ProcessObjectDisplay_Next
  $A31A,$06 Call #R$A2B1 to calculate object table offset.
  $A320,$06 Call #R$A2BD to extract object attributes.
  $A326,$03 Read the object attribute value.
  $A329,$04,b$02,$02 Go to #R$A34E to finish this object if bit 4 of its attribute value is set.
  $A32D,$03 Go to #R$A336 if #REGb is zero.
  $A330,$06 Print a newline at #R$A2A2 if the character is #N$00, #N$01 or Sherlock at #R$DC6C.
@ $A336 label=ProcessObjectDisplay_CharacterBeingProcessed
  $A336,$03 Call #R$A724 to read which character is being processed.
  $A339,$07 Go to #R$A348 if #REGb is not zero.
  $A340,$08 Print the token at #R$A887, call #R$A360, then go to #R$A34B.

N $A348 Prints a new line, then clears the object-display flag and the print mode.
@ $A348 label=ProcessObjectDisplay_PrintsNewLine
  $A348,$03 Call #R$A37D to process the object display.
  $A34B,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
@ $A34E label=ProcessObjectDisplay_WriteList
  $A34E,$07 Write #N$00 to #LIST { *#R$A2AA } { *#R$A027 } LIST#.
  $A355,$07 Restore the saved source, the saved pointer, the saved character record, the saved count and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $A35C,$01 Return.

g $A35D Saved Object Fields
D $A35D Scratch copy of an object id and two room bytes. #R$A2E4 stores the id from #R$A033 and the room word from #R$A034. #R$CDBF stores three record bytes here and copies them back.
. Used by #R$A2E4, #R$A37D and #R$CDBF.
B $A35D,$01 Object id.
B $A35E,$01 Room byte, or the low byte of the room word from #R$A034.
B $A35F,$01 Second room byte, or the high byte of that word.

c $A360 Check And Reset Character State
@ $A360 refs=$A5C3
@ $A360 label=CheckAndResetCharacterState
D $A360 Clears #R$A35E if it holds the current character, then runs #R$A37D with the current character set to zero, and restores the character.
.
. Control code #N$18 in the table at #R$A55D.
  $A360,$07 Read the character being processed and take the saved object field at #R$A35E.
  $A367,$05 Clear #REGa and jump ahead to #R$A36D unless #R$A35E holds this character.
  $A36C,$01 Store the cleared character state back in the record.
@ $A36D label=CheckAndResetCharacterState_Next
  $A36D,$03 Set the current character to zero.
  $A370,$05 Keep the character record, call #R$A37D to print this object's tokens.
  $A375,$03 Restore the saved character from the stack.
  $A378,$03 Store the character id at #R$A036.
  $A37B,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A37C,$01 Return.

c $A37D Process Object Display
@ $A37D label=ProcessObject_Display
D $A37D Clears the object-processing flag, reads this object's attributes, and prints the tokens the display bits select.
  $A37D,$04 Clear the object processing source flag.
  $A381,$06 Call #R$A2B1 to calculate object table offset.
  $A387,$07 Call #R$A2BD to extract object attributes.
  $A38E,$03 Call #R$C493 to test bits 3 and 7 of the object attribute.
  $A391,$03 Call #R$C4B2 to test bits 2 and 6 of the object attribute.
  $A394,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $A395,$08 Read the byte at #R$A01D into #REGc, save #REGhl and take an offset of #N($0006,$04,$04).
  $A39D,$05 Call #R$A880 to print the token.
  $A3A2,$07 Call #R$A880, then jump ahead to #R$A3E2 if bit 3 of #REGc is clear.
  $A3A9,$05 If bit 5 of the token is set, call #R$A880 to extract the token.
  $A3AE,$08 If bit 7 of the saved token is set, call #R$A880 to extract the token.
  $A3B6,$07 Jump to #R$A3C7 if the saved object field at #R$A35E is not #N$FE.
  $A3BD,$08 Point #REGhl at #R$A02F and call #R$A419, keeping the saved pointer.
  $A3C5,$02 The picture marker has been handled and the saved pointer is already back in #REGhl, so carry on at #R$A3E2.

N $A3C7 Compare this object with the character being processed, and take the token at #N$055B when they match.
@ $A3C7 label=ProcessObject_Display_Next
  $A3C7,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A3D6 if this object is not the character being processed.
  $A3CE,$06 Take #N$055B for the object, then call #R$A887 to print token #N$055B.
  $A3D4,$02 The token for the character being processed has been printed, so skip the location data and carry on at #R$A3E1.

N $A3D6 When the display mode is the picture marker, run the location data in the saved object fields.
@ $A3D6 label=ProcessObject_Display_DisplayModePicture
  $A3D6,$03 Read the display mode.
  $A3D9,$08 See whether the display mode is #N$05 and read the saved object field at #R$A35E, then call #R$A745 to
. run location data if valid.
@ $A3E1 label=ProcessObject_Display_WorkspaceBringingSaved
  $A3E1,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $A3E2,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A414 if the saved object field at #R$A35F is #N$FF.
  $A3E9,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A414 if bit 2 is clear.
  $A3ED,$05 If bit 5 of the token is clear, call #R$A880 to extract the token.
  $A3F2,$08 If bit 7 of the display flag at #R$A01E is clear, call #R$A880 to extract the token.
  $A3FA,$07 Jump to #R$A409 if the saved object field at #R$A35F is not #N$FE.
  $A401,$06 Read the display pointer, then call #R$A419 to handle the picture for this
. message.
  $A407,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A414 to take #N$2E and jump to #R$A9B7.

N $A409 When the other display mode is the picture marker, run the location data in the saved object fields.
@ $A409 label=ProcessObject_Display_OtherDisplayMode
  $A409,$03 Read the display mode.
  $A40C,$08 See whether the display mode is #N$05 and read the saved object field at #R$A35F, then call #R$A745 to
. run location data if valid.

c $A414 Print A Full Stop
@ $A414 label=PrintFullStop
D $A414 Adds a full stop to the message and goes back to the token walk.
  $A414,$05 Go to #R$A9B7 to print a full stop.

c $A419 Shift The Text Pointer And Print A Space
@ $A419 label=ShiftTextPointer
D $A419 Clear the top bit of the pointer, then scale the pointer down to the next bit
  $A419,$07 Save #REGbc and rescale the text pointer.
  $A420,$02 Rotate the bit from #REGh into #REGl, finishing the shift of #REGhl.
  $A422,$05 Print a space.
  $A427,$06 Take #N($00F0,$04,$04) as the unit #R$A476 subtracts from #REGhl.
  $A42D,$03 Jump ahead to #R$A442 if #REGhl was below #N($00F0,$04,$04).
  $A430,$06 Keep the count in #REGb and print character #N$60 through #R$A9B7.
  $A436,$07 Call #R$A490 to print the count, then jump ahead to #R$A467 if the remainder in #REGhl is zero.
  $A43D,$05 Print a space.
@ $A442 label=ShiftTextPointer_PrintObjects
  $A442,$06 Take #N($000C,$04,$04) as the unit for #R$A476.
  $A448,$03 Jump ahead to #R$A469 if #R$A476 counted no groups of #N$0C.
  $A44B,$04 Use the count, then call #R$A490 to print it.
  $A44F,$05 Print '/'.
  $A454,$05 Jump ahead to #R$A45E if the remainder in #REGl is zero.
  $A459,$05 Call #R$A490 to print the units and return through #R$A467.

c $A45E Print A Hyphen
@ $A45E label=PrintHyphen
D $A45E Prints a hyphen and clears the marker that forces a capital letter.
  $A45E,$02 Take a hyphen.
  $A460,$03 Call #R$A9B7 to print the character.
  $A463,$04 Clear the token format flag.
@ $A467 label=PrintHyphen_Return
  $A467,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $A468,$01 Return.

c $A469 Print The Letter D
@ $A469 label=PrintLetterD
D $A469 Prints the units, then the letter D and a full stop.
  $A469,$04 Put the units in the count and call #R$A490 to print them.
  $A46D,$05 Print the letter D.
  $A472,$04 Print a full stop and continue at #R$A460.

c $A476 Divide By Repeated Subtraction
@ $A476 label=DivideBySubtraction
D $A476 Counts how many times #REGde can be subtracted from #REGhl. Returns the count in #REGa and the remainder in #REGhl.
  $A476,$02 Start the count at #N$FF so the first pass makes it zero.
  $A478,$06 Count in #REGa how many times #REGde can be subtracted from #REGhl, and repeat until #REGhl goes below #REGde.
  $A47E,$01 Add #REGde back to leave the remainder in #REGhl.
  $A47F,$01 Return.

c $A480 Print One Decimal Digit
@ $A480 label=PrintOneDecimalDigit
D $A480 Prints one decimal digit, suppressing leading zeros.
  $A480,$03 Call #R$A476 to divide #REGhl by #REGde (quotient in #REGa, remainder in #REGhl).
  $A483,$02 Turn the number into a character.
  $A485,$04 Print the digit at once if an earlier digit was printed.
  $A489,$03 Return when the character is the digit zero.
  $A48C,$01 Note that a digit has been printed.
  $A48D,$03 Jump to #R$A9B7 to print the digit.

c $A490 Print Hundreds, Tens And Units
@ $A490 label=PrintHundredsTensAndUnits
D $A490 Prints #REGb as up to three decimal digits.
  $A490,$06 Clear the high byte.
  $A496,$03 Take #N($0064,$04,$04), one hundred, as the next unit.
  $A499,$03 Call #R$A480 to print this digit.
  $A49C,$06 Take #N($000A,$04,$04), ten, as the unit for #R$A480.
  $A4A2,$08 Add #N$30 to the units left in #REGl, call #R$A9B7 to print the digit, and restore the registers.
  $A4AA,$01 Return.

c $A4AB Point To Location Data Plus One
@ $A4AB label=PointToLocationDataPlusOne
E $A4AB View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$71D5 }
. LIST#
D $A4AB Points one byte past the room record for the id just looked up.
R $A4AB A Room ID
R $A4AB O:HL Pointer to location data + 1
  $A4AB,$06 Keep the character record, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $A4B1,$07 Copy the record into the pointer and add #N($0001,$04,$04).
  $A4B8,$03 Restore the saved character record and the saved source from the stack.
  $A4BB,$01 Return.

c $A4BC Point To Object Attribute Byte
@ $A4BC label=PointToObjectAttributeByte
E $A4BC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$71E2 }
. LIST#
D $A4BC Points at the flag byte seven bytes into the object's record.
R $A4BC A Object ID
R $A4BC O:HL Pointer to the object attribute byte
  $A4BC,$06 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $A4C2,$07 Copy the record into the pointer and add #N($0007,$04,$04).
  $A4C9,$03 Restore the saved character record and the saved source from the stack.
  $A4CC,$01 Return.

c $A4CD Compare Location Data
@ $A4CD label=CompareLocationData
D $A4CD Compares the two room records from the exit field onward and reports whether they match.
  $A4CD,$08 Save #REGde, #REGhl and #REGiy, then point #REGhl six bytes further on
. (#N($0006,$04,$04)).
  $A4D5,$05 Call #R$A51B to read the script position.
  $A4DA,$06 Jump to #R$A516 when the script position does not match, and otherwise restore the records this test used.

c $A4E0 Leave Success For The Script
@ $A4E0 label=CompareFollowingFields
E $A4E0 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$71F3 }
. LIST#
D $A4E0 Compares the following fields and records a match when they agree.
  $A4E0,$07 Keep the schedule entry, then call #R$A51B to compare location data.
  $A4E7,$02 Go to #R$A516 if the script position does not match.
  $A4E9,$08 Take #N$01, store it at #R$A02E and call #R$A51B to compare location data.
  $A4F1,$02 Go to #R$A4FA if the script position does not match.
  $A4F3,$07 Go to #R$A516 if this field matches.
@ $A4FA label=CompareFollowingFields_Next
  $A4FA,$06 Restore the schedule entry and the pointer from the stack, keeping a copy of each there.
  $A500,$03 Take an offset of #N($0004,$04,$04).
  $A503,$07 Step to the next schedule entry and step on two places, then call #R$A51B to compare
. location data.
  $A50A,$02 Go to #R$A516 if the script position does not match.
  $A50C,$08 Step the schedule entry back by #N$04 and call #R$A51B to compare the location data.
  $A514,$02 Take #N$01.
  $A516,$04 Restore the saved schedule entry, the saved pointer and the saved source from the stack.
  $A51A,$01 Return.

c $A51B Read The Script Position
@ $A51B label=ReadScriptPosition
E $A51B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$722E }
. LIST#
D $A51B Reads a pair of bytes and reports whether the low digit matches the script.
  $A51B,$06 Go to #R$A52E if both bytes of this word are zero.
N $A521 Does the low nibble of #REGiy+#N$01 differ from the script byte at #REGhl?
  $A521,$08 Jump to #R$A52E when the low nibble of #REGiy+#N$01 differs from the script byte at #REGhl.
  $A529,$05 Step back one script byte and compare it with this schedule field.
@ $A52E label=ReadScriptPosition_MoveOneByte
  $A52E,$02 Move one byte onward and restore the pointer saved for this step.
  $A530,$01 Advance through the script.
  $A531,$02 Step to the next field of the schedule entry.
  $A533,$02 Step to the next field of the schedule entry.
  $A535,$01 Return.

c $A536 Wait For A Key With Timeout
@ $A536 label=WaitForKeyWithTimeout
D $A536 Waits for a key until the timer at #R$A039+#N$02 runs out. #R$C41F sets bit 7 when a key is pressed. When the timer expires, #R$D9B7 runs. Either way the timer is reset to #N$0BB8.
  $A536,$04 Save the pointer and read the timeout at #R$A039+#N$02.
@ $A53A label=WaitForKeyWithTimeout_Loop
  $A53A,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A553 if #R$C41F reports a key.
  $A541,$02 Start the delay at zero.
  $A543,$05 Spin until the delay runs out.
  $A548,$05 Count the timeout down.
  $A54D,$01 Restore the pointer from the stack.
  $A54E,$04 Call #R$D9B7, keeping the pointer.
  $A552,$01 Clear the key code.
@ $A553 label=WaitForKeyWithTimeout_KeyPressed
  $A553,$08 Clear bit 7 and reset the timeout to #N$0BB8.
  $A55B,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $A55C,$01 Return.

g $A55D Control Character Jump Table
@ $A55D label=JumpTable_ControlCharacters
D $A55D Control Character Jump Table read by #R$A5F2.
W $A55D,$02 ID: #N((#PC-$A55D)/$02).
L $A55D,$02,$20

g $A59D Text Pointer
D $A59D Word #R$A66C reads the next text token through, stepping it on two bytes. #R$A59F also keeps the caller's stack pointer here while a message is printed.
. Used by #R$A59F and #R$A66C.
W $A59D,$02

c $A59F Print Text And Handle Control Characters
@ $A59F label=PrintTextAndHandleControlCharacters
E $A59F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$72DD }
. LIST#
D $A59F Prints the message at the pointer, carrying out the control characters inside
. it and then returns to the instruction after the call.
R $A59F HL Pointer to text data
  $A59F,$06 Save the count, the source, the character record, the result and the pointer.
  $A5A5,$03 Point twelve bytes up the stack, just past the saved return address.
  $A5A8,$04 Add the stack pointer, store that address in the text pointer at #R$A59D.
  $A5AC,$01 Restore the message pointer from the stack.
  $A5AD,$06 Jump to #R$A5B6 if the parser is set.
  $A5B3,$03 Record the parser.
@ $A5B6 label=PrintTextAndHandleControlCharacters_Next
  $A5B6,$08 Call #R$A5C4 to run text stream.
  $A5BE,$01 Restore the return address from the stack.
  $A5BF,$04 And switch to the stack saved in the text pointer at #R$A59D.

c $A5C3 Dispatch Looked-Up Handler
@ $A5C3 label=DispatchTextControlHandler
D $A5C3 Jumps to the handler the caller looked up.
  $A5C3,$01 Jump to the handler address in #REGhl.

c $A5C4 Process Text Stream
@ $A5C4 label=ProcessTextStream
R $A5C4 HL Pointer to text data
D $A5C4 Prints every byte of the message, and stops when the printer says the text has ended.
  $A5C4,$03 Use the saved pointer as the character record.
N $A5C7 Keep on looping through until all of the text has been printed.
@ $A5C7 label=TextProcessing_Loop
  $A5C7,$03 Call #R$A5CD to carry out this text command.
  $A5CA,$02 Jump back to #R$A5C7 until all the text has been processed.
  $A5CC,$01 Return.

c $A5CD Process Text Command
@ $A5CD label=ProcessTextCommand
R $A5CD IX Pointer to text data
D $A5CD Reads the next text byte. A command keeps its parameter, and any other byte is printed
. as text.
  $A5CD,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A5F2 if bit 7 of the record's flag byte is clear.
  $A5D4,$02,b$01 Strip the command marker (bit 7) to leave the token code.
  $A5D6,$06 Keep the token code in #REGd and its parameter in #REGe, and step #REGix onto
. that parameter.
  $A5DC,$02,b$01 Keep the token type (the high nibble) for the checks below.
  $A5DE,$04 Go to #R$A647 when the token type is #N$30.
  $A5E2,$04 Go to #R$A647 when the token type is #N$20.
  $A5E6,$04 Go to #R$A647 when the token type is #N$60.

c $A5EA Mark Success For The Script
@ $A5EA label=MarkSuccessScript
D $A5EA Prints the dictionary word, steps past it, and leaves a non-zero result so the caller continues.
N $A5EA Print text token.
  $A5EA,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the dictionary word.
@ $A5ED label=MarkSuccessScript_MarkSuccess
  $A5ED,$02 Step past the dictionary word.
  $A5EF,$02 Force a non-zero result so the caller continues.
  $A5F1,$01 Return.

c $A5F2 Test For A Space
@ $A5F2 label=TestSpace
D $A5F2 Sorts a text byte into a blank, a vocabulary code, or a packed letter.
  $A5F2,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A621 if this text byte is below #N$20.
  $A5F6,$05 Jump ahead to #R$A83A if it is at least #N$60.
  $A5FB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A61C if it is below #N$40.
  $A5FF,$02,b$01 Take the letter count of this packed word from bits 0-4.
  $A601,$08 Set #REGb to the length plus one, point #REGhl at the byte after #REGix,
. and clear #REGc.
  $A609,$06 Call #R$A990 to rotate and extract character code, then call #R$A9B7 to
. print character.
  $A60F,$02 Count the next character of the message and repeat from #R$A609.
  $A611,$07 Copy the pointer into the record, and go to #R$A5ED if the count in #REGc is not
. zero.
  $A618,$04 Step the pointer back one byte and go back to #R$A5ED.

N $A61C Call #R$A9B7 to print this character.
@ $A61C label=TestSpace_PrintSpace
  $A61C,$05 Print this character and go back to #R$A5ED for the next letter.

N $A621 Store the result in the low byte of the source, then clear the high byte of the
. source, then use the control character jump table at #R$A55D as the pointer.
@ $A621 label=TestSpace_IndexByCode
  $A621,$08 Use the control character jump table at #R$A55D.
  $A629,$06 Move the pointer on by the length, step to the next character of the message
. and swap the two pointers.
  $A62F,$04 Jump back to #R$A5C3 if the token is at least #N$1D.
  $A633,$05 Go to #R$A5ED if the looked-up handler returns zero.
  $A638,$02 Carry on from #R$A5EA once the handler has returned.

c $A63A Print This Character
@ $A63A label=PrintThisCharacter
E $A63A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$72C3 }
. LIST#
D $A63A Sends the character in #REGa to #R$A9B7 and returns zero. The same three
. instructions as #R$A736.
.
. Control code #N$0D in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
  $A63A,$03 Call #R$A9B7 to print this character.
  $A63D,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A63E,$01 Return.

c $A63F Choose The Line Break
@ $A63F label=ChooseTheLineBreak
E $A63F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7340 }
. LIST#
D $A63F Puts #N$60 in #REGd and jumps to #R$A64A, which skips the full stop but still
. prints a new line. #R$A643 puts #N$30 in #REGd, which makes #R$A64A print a full stop
. and a new line.
.
. Control code #N$1D in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
  $A63F,$04 Put #N$60 in #REGd and jump to #R$A64A.

N $A643 Puts #N$30 in #REGd so #R$A64A prints a full stop and a new line.
@ $A643 label=ChooseTheLineBreak_PrintStop
  $A643,$04 Put #N$30 in #REGd and jump to #R$A64A.

c $A647 Print Character With Time Period
@ $A647 label=PrintCharacterWithTimePeriod
E $A647 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7348 }
. LIST#
D $A647 Prints the dictionary word for the character.
R $A647 D Time period value (#N$30 or #N$60)
R $A647 O:A Character code
N $A647 Print text token.
  $A647,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the dictionary word.

c $A64A Leave A Full Stop As The Result
@ $A64A label=LeaveFullStop
D $A64A Prints a break when bit 6 or bit 4 of the character asks for one.
  $A64A,$06 Jump ahead to #R$A657 if bit 6 is present.
  $A650,$05 Call #R$A9B7 to print the full stop if bit 4 of #REGd is set.
  $A655,$02 See whether bit 4 of the character is set.
  $A657,$03 Call #R$A2A2 if bit 4 of this byte is set.
  $A65A,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A65B,$01 Return.

N $A65C Returns to the caller without changing the result. Commands use it when there is
. nothing to do.
.
. Control code #N$12 in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
@ $A65C label=LeaveFullStop_Return
  $A65C,$01 Return.

c $A65D Process Text Token And Call Handler
@ $A65D label=ProcessTextTokenAndCallHandler
E $A65D View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7367 }
. LIST#
D $A65D Fetches the next token and calls #R$A7BA when that pointer is not zero.
.
. Control code #N$00 in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
  $A65D,$07 Call #R$A66C to fetch next text token.
  $A664,$06 Call #R$A7BA if the pointer is not zero.
  $A66A,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A66B,$01 Return.

c $A66C Fetch Next Text Token
@ $A66C refs=$A5C3
@ $A66C label=FetchNextTextToken
E $A66C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7376 }
. LIST#
D $A66C Reads the next word from the pointer at #R$A59D into #REGde and steps that pointer on two bytes.
.
. Control code #N$01 in the table at #R$A55D.
R $A66C O:DE Token value (word)
R $A66C O:F Zero flag always reset
  $A66C,$07 Read the next word from the pointer at #R$A59D into #REGde and step it on two bytes.
  $A673,$03 Store the pointer back at #R$A59D.
  $A676,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag, with the next text token in #REGde.
  $A678,$01 Return.

c $A679 Step The Record By Its Length
@ $A679 label=StepRecordByItsLength
E $A679 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$737E }
. LIST#
D $A679 Adds the word at offset #N$01, minus one, to the record pointer and returns zero.
.
. Control code #N$02 in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
R $A679 IX Record pointer
R $A679 O:IX Record pointer plus the length word, minus one
  $A679,$07 Read the length word and subtract one.
  $A680,$02 Add that length to the record pointer.
N $A682 Returns zero. Control code #N$1C enters here.
@ $A682 label=StepRecordByItsLength_ReturnZero
  $A682,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A683,$01 Return.

c $A684 Continue At The Next Token
@ $A684 label=ContinueAtTheNextToken
D $A684 Reads the next word from the text pointer at #R$A59D and uses that address as the record
. pointer, then returns zero. The time-of-day entries from #R$A68C return when the
. period matches, and otherwise skip one token from #R$A6B2.
.
. Control code #N$1A in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
R $A684 O:IX Address of the next word
  $A684,$03 Call #R$A66C to fetch the next word.
  $A687,$03 Use that word as the record pointer.
  $A68A,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A68B,$01 Return.

N $A68C Returns when the time period is 6 AM. Any other period skips a token from #R$A6B2.
@ $A68C label=ContinueAtTheNextToken_SixInTheMorning
  $A68C,$06 Return when the time period is 6 AM (#N$05).
  $A692,$02 Jump to #R$A6B2 for any other period.

N $A694 Returns when the time period is morning or midday. Any other period skips a token.
@ $A694 label=ContinueAtTheNextToken_Morning
  $A694,$06 Return when the time period is morning or midday (#N$04).
  $A69A,$02 Jump to #R$A6B2 for any other period.

N $A69C Returns when the time period is afternoon. Any other period skips a token.
@ $A69C label=ContinueAtTheNextToken_Afternoon
  $A69C,$06 Return when the time period is afternoon (#N$03).
  $A6A2,$02 Jump to #R$A6B2 for any other period.

N $A6A4 Returns when the time period is 7 PM. Any other period skips a token.
@ $A6A4 label=ContinueAtTheNextToken_SevenInTheEvening
  $A6A4,$06 Return when the time period is 7 PM (#N$02).
  $A6AA,$02 Jump to #R$A6B2 for any other period.

N $A6AC Returns when the time period is late at night. Any other period falls through into
. #R$A6B2.
@ $A6AC label=ContinueAtTheNextToken_LateAtNight
  $A6AC,$06 Return when the time period is late at night (#N$01).

N $A6B2 Skips one text token and returns zero.
  $A6B2,$05 Step on one byte and call #R$A6BB to skip the token.
  $A6B7,$02 Step back one byte.
  $A6B9,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A6BA,$01 Return.

c $A6BB Skip Text Token Based On Type
@ $A6BB label=SkipTextTokenBasedOnType
D $A6BB Skips a text token whose record flag says this token type is not printed.
R $A6BB IX Pointer to text token
R $A6BB O:IX Updated pointer (skips token data)
  $A6BB,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A6CC if bit 7 of the record's flag byte is present.
  $A6C2,$04 Go to #R$A6CA if it is #N$02.
  $A6C6,$04 Go to #R$A6CE if it is not #N$0B.
@ $A6CA label=SkipTextTokenBasedOnType_Next
  $A6CA,$02 Step to the next byte of this text token.
@ $A6CC label=SkipTextTokenBasedOnType_SkipPair
  $A6CC,$02 Step to the next byte of this text token.
  $A6CE,$02 Step to the next byte of this text token.
  $A6D0,$01 Return.

g $A6D1 Alternatives Left
D $A6D1 Count of alternatives still to print or skip.
. Used by #R$A6D3.
B $A6D1,$01

g $A6D2 Chosen Alternative
D $A6D2 The alternative #R$A6D3 picked at random, counted from #N$01.
. Used by #R$A6D3.
B $A6D2,$01

c $A6D3 Print A Random Alternative
@ $A6D3 label=PrintRandomAlternative
D $A6D3 Reads how many alternatives follow, picks one at random with #R$D2A4, prints that one through #R$A5CD and skips the rest.
.
. Control code #N$0A in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
R $A6D3 IX Pointer to text token data
  $A6D3,$08 Read how many alternatives follow and keep that count.
  $A6DB,$04 Pick one of them at random with #R$D2A4.
  $A6DF,$04 Put that choice back in the range and remember it.
  $A6E3,$02 Step on to the first alternative.
  $A6E5,$06 Read the choice and point #REGhl at the count still left.
  $A6EB,$03 Jump ahead to #R$A6F5 if the choice differs from the alternatives left.
  $A6EE,$07 Call #R$A5CD to print this alternative.
@ $A6F5 label=PrintRandomAlternative_Next
N $A6F5 Skip this alternative.
  $A6F5,$03 Call #R$A6BB to skip this alternative.
  $A6F8,$04 Count one fewer alternative.
  $A6FC,$02 Jump back to #R$A6E5 until none remain.
  $A6FE,$02 Step the record back one byte.
  $A700,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A701,$01 Return.

