  $A7DF,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $A7E0,$02,b$01 Take the dictionary page (the low nibble of the token's high byte) to compare with the #N$09 of token #N$09EE.
  $A7E2,$01 See whether the two values match.
  $A7E3,$01 Return if they match.
@ $A7E4 label=CheckTokenMatchAndSetFlag_Next
  $A7E4,$05 Write #N$01 to the token match flag at #R$A7EA.
  $A7E9,$01 Return.

g $A7EA Token Match Flag
D $A7EA Cleared by #R$A7D0, then set when the token is not the special match. #R$D3E8 clears it again unless bit 6 of the record is set, and #R$A815 folds it into the token format flag.
. Used by #R$A7D0, #R$A815 and #R$D3E8.
B $A7EA,$01

c $A7EB Print Token Based On Parameters
@ $A7EB label=PrintTokenBasedOnParameters
D $A7EB Picks the token word table from the print mode and shifts the token parameter into place.
R $A7EB D Token parameter
  $A7EB,$07 Point #REGhl at the token table and read the print mode into #REGe.
  $A7F2,$03 Read the object display flag.
  $A7F5,$01 Merge in the print mode, so both can be tested for zero at once.
  $A7F6,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A7FB if the object display flag and the print mode are both zero.
  $A7F8,$03 Point #REGhl at the other half of the token table.
  $A7FB,$04 Shift the token parameter in #REGd down by three bits.
  $A7FF,$02,b$01 Turn the shifted parameter into an even offset into the token word table.
  $A801,$08 Save #REGde, index the token table by the shifted parameter and read that word into #REGde.
  $A809,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the token.
  $A80C,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $A80D,$01 Return.

c $A80E Process Text Token With Attributes
@ $A80E label=ProcessTextTokenWithAttributes
E $A80E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7478 }
. LIST#
R $A80E DE Token value
D $A80E Checks whether this token matches, then keeps the dictionary page of the following word.
  $A80E,$05 Call #R$A7D0 to compare the token and set the match flag.
  $A813,$02 Use #N$50 for the attribute.

c $A815 Read The High Byte Of The Token
D $A815 Keeps the low digit of the token and folds it into the dictionary page.
  $A815,$02 Keep the token and take its high byte.
  $A817,$02,b$01 Keep the dictionary page (the low nibble of the token's high byte).
  $A819,$01 Keep the low digit of the token in #REGd.
  $A81A,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $A81B,$01 Set the bits from #REGd.
  $A81C,$01 Fold the saved token bits back into #REGd.
  $A81D,$02,b$01 Keep the dictionary page of the folded token, ready to test the whole token.
  $A81F,$01 Fold in the low byte of the token, so the zero flag shows an empty token.
  $A820,$01 Return if the folded token is zero.
N $A821 Print text token.
  $A821,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the dictionary word.
  $A824,$07 Read the token match flag at #R$A7EA and point #REGhl at the token format flag at #R$A026.
  $A82B,$01 Set the bits from *#REGhl.
  $A82C,$01 Store the result at the pointer.
  $A82D,$01 Restore the text pointer from the stack.
  $A82E,$01 Return.

c $A82F Process Object Or Print Token
@ $A82F label=ProcessObjectOrPrintToken
E $A82F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7488 }
. LIST#
R $A82F A Object ID (FF = print token)
D $A82F See whether the text byte is the end marker, then print the dictionary word.
  $A82F,$05 Jump back to #R$A751 while the object is not the end marker.
  $A834,$06 Take token #N$080E and print it from #R$A887.

c $A83A Get Common Word And Process
@ $A83A label=GetCommonWordAndProcess
D $A83A Looks the common word up and prints it.
  $A83A,$06 Look the word up at #R$A840 and continue from #R$A5EA.

c $A840 Get Common Word
@ $A840 label=GetCommonWord
E $A840 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7493 }
. LIST#
D $A840 Turns a vocabulary code into the dictionary address of that word.
R $A840 A Letter reference
R $A840 DE Common word address
  $A840,$08 Use the common words at #R$67B7.
  $A848,$02 Point at word #REGde of the common-word table at #R$67B7.
  $A84A,$01 Fetch the LSB of the dictionary address and store it in #REGe.
  $A84B,$05 Fetch the MSB of the dictionary address, add #N$50 and store it in #REGd.
  $A850,$01 Return.

g $A851 Lowercase Handling Flag
@ $A851 label=LowercaseHandlingFlag
D $A851 Lowercase Handling Flag read by #R$A852 and #R$AA14.
B $A851,$01

c $A852 Toggle Lowercase Handling
@ $A852 label=ToggleLowercaseHandling
D $A852 Toggles whether the next word is printed in capitals.
.
. Control code #N$1B in the table at #R$A55D.
.
. Used by the routine at #R$A5C3.
  $A852,$08 Read the lowercase handling flag and store it in the lowercase handling
. flag.
  $A85A,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $A85B,$01 Return.

g $A85C Table: Format Data
@ $A85C label=Table_FormatData
D $A85C Format data entries indexed by format type (#N$00-#N$08).
. Each entry is #N$04 bytes that are copied to the format buffer.
. Read by #R$A92F.
N $A85C Format type #N((#PC-$A85C)/$04):
B $A85C,$04,$01 Format data bytes:
. #TABLE(default,centre,centre,centre,centre)
. { =h Position | =h Command or Data | =h Command Type | =h Parameter/ Data }
. { =h Byte | =h Bit 7 | =h Bits 4-6 | =h Bits 0-3 }
. #FOR$00,$03(x,{ #N((#PC+x-$A85C)%$04) | #IF(#PEEK(#PC+x)&$80)(Command,Data) | #N((#PEEK(#PC+x)&$70)>>$04) | #N(#PEEK(#PC+x)&$0F) })
. TABLE#
L $A85C,$04,$09

c $A880 Extract Token From Text
@ $A880 label=ExtractTokenFromText
E $A880 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$74BA }
. LIST#
D $A880 Reads the next dictionary token from the message and keeps its low digit as the page.
R $A880 HL Pointer to text data
R $A880 O:DE Token value (word)
R $A880 O:HL Updated pointer
N $A880 Read the token value from the text data.
  $A880,$04 Read this message byte and the one after it.
  $A884,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 0-3 (extract the low nibble from the second byte).
  $A886,$01 Keep the low digit as the dictionary page.

c $A887 Print A Dictionary Word
@ $A887 label=PrintTextToken
D $A887 Prints a space and the dictionary word when the dictionary flag is set, and returns at once
. when that flag is clear.
  $A887,$05 Return if *#SKOOLKID$5C3C(TV_FLAG) is not set.
  $A88C,$04 Write #REGde (the token value) to *#R$A022.
  $A890,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $A891,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 0-3 (ensure we have just the low nibble).
  $A893,$01 Set the bits from #REGe (merge with the first byte).
  $A894,$01 Return if both token digits are zero.
  $A895,$05 Save the character record, the pointer, the count and the source.
N $A89A Process the token format and prepare for dictionary lookup.
  $A89A,$02 Use #N$20 for the character code.
  $A89C,$03 Print the character in #REGa through #R$A9B7.
  $A89F,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $A8A0,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 4-7 (extract the high nibble).
  $A8A2,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the count.
  $A8A3,$02 See whether this byte is a dictionary word.
N $A8A5 Prepare to set a flag if the token format is #N$70.
  $A8A5,$04 Load #N$01 and jump ahead to #R$A8AC unless the high nibble is #N$70.
  $A8A9,$03 Record the token format flag.
@ $A8AC label=PrintTextToken_ProcessToken
  $A8AC,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $A8AD,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 0-3 (extract the low nibble).
  $A8AF,$01 Store the result in the high byte of the source.
  $A8B0,$01 Move the token into #REGhl so its high byte can be merged with the format bits.
  $A8B1,$02 Read the high byte of the pointer, saving pointer.
  $A8B3,$01 Set the bits from #REGc (merge with the format bits).
  $A8B4,$01 Keep the high byte of this token.
  $A8B5,$03 Write #REGhl (the modified token) to *#R$A022.
  $A8B8,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $A8B9,$07 Use the dictionary index at #R$5D80, saving count and.
N $A8BA Search the dictionary index table for the token value.
@ $A8C0 label=PrintTextToken_SearchLoop
  $A8C0,$07 Count this dictionary entry and keep searching when it is still below the token.
  $A8C7,$02 Jump to #R$A8D9 when this entry is past the token.
  $A8C9,$06 Compare the low byte and jump to #R$A8D9 when the entry has reached the token.
@ $A8CF label=PrintTextToken_ContinueSearch
  $A8CF,$09 Step to the next dictionary entry and keep searching until #N$7B entries have been tried.
  $A8D8,$01 Count another dictionary entry.
@ $A8D9 label=PrintTextToken_FoundEntry
  $A8D9,$02 Go to #R$A8E0 if the count wrapped to zero.
@ $A8DB label=PrintTextToken_BackupEntry
  $A8DB,$05 Step back one dictionary entry.
@ $A8E0 label=PrintTextToken_GetWordAddress
  $A8E0,$06 Read the word address at the start of the record.
  $A8E6,$04 Jump back to #R$A8DB when the word address is still zero.
N $A8EA Print the next letter of this dictionary word.
  $A8EA,$01 Take the count.
  $A8EB,$03 Print the character in #REGa through #R$A9B7.
  $A8EE,$07 Take the dictionary index and swap the two pointers.
@ $A8F5 label=PrintTextToken_PrintWordContinue
  $A8F5,$06 Compare #REGhl with #REGde, keeping #REGhl, and save the result flags.
@ $A8F6 label=PrintTextToken_PrintWordLoop
  $A8FB,$03 Call #R$A95B to read the next format byte.
  $A8FE,$01 Restore the comparison flags from the stack.
  $A8FF,$04 . Jump back to #R$A8F5 while the two pointers differ, and drop the saved pointers when they match.
N $A903 Handle special token format cases.
  $A903,$05 Jump to #R$A91C when the format byte is #N$50.
  $A908,$04 Jump to #R$A919 when the format byte is #N$40.
  $A90C,$07 Load the room display parameter and jump ahead to #R$A916 if the format byte is #N$10.
  $A913,$03 Load #REGa with *#R$A036.
@ $A916 label=PrintTextToken_Format_TestCharacterBeingProcessed
  $A916,$03 Jump ahead to #R$A91C if the character being processed is zero.
@ $A919 label=PrintTextToken_ProcessFormat
  $A919,$03 Call #R$A92F to handle the token format.
@ $A91C label=PrintTextToken_PrintRemainingText
  $A91C,$03 #REGhl=#SKOOLKID$5C42(NEWPPC).
@ $A91F label=PrintTextToken_PrintLoop
  $A91F,$01 Load a byte from *#SKOOLKID$5C42(NEWPPC).
  $A920,$03 Jump ahead to #R$A929 if this byte is zero.
  $A923,$03 Print the character in #REGa through #R$A9B7.
  $A926,$03 Step to the next letter and go back to #R$A91F.

N $A929 Return.
@ $A929 label=PrintTextToken_End
N $A929 All text printed - restore registers and return.
  $A929,$05 Restore the saved source, the saved count, the saved pointer and the saved character record from the stack.
  $A92E,$01 Return.

c $A92F Process Text Token Format
@ $A92F label=ProcessTextTokenFormat
R $A92F IX Buffer pointer (where to write format data)
R $A92F HL Pointer to token format byte
D $A92F Reads the format byte and copies its four data bytes into the buffer, stopping when the type is #N$0F.
N $A92F Extract the format type and copy format data to the buffer.
  $A92F,$01 Read the format byte.
  $A930,$02,b$01
M $A930,$02 Extract the format type.
  $A932,$03 Return when the format type is #N$0F.
N $A935 Calculate the table offset for this format type (multiply by 4).
  $A935,$02 Multiply by #N$04 to get the table offset.
  $A937,$03 Set #REGhl to the offset value (#REGl=#REGa, #REGh=#N$00).
  $A93A,$06 Use the format data at #R$A85C.
@ $A940 label=ProcessTextTokenFormat_CopyLoop
  $A940,$07 Store the record's flag byte.
  $A947,$02 Count the next character of the message and repeat from #R$A940.
  $A949,$03 Write #REGb (#N$04, the terminator) to *#REGix+#N$00.
  $A94C,$01 Return.

c $A94D Handle Nested Format Processing Recursive Call
D $A94D Handles one nested format byte, then continues with the format that enclosed it.
N $A94D Handle nested format processing (recursive call).
  $A94D,$03 Save the character record and the pointer.
  $A950,$03 Call #R$A95B to read the next format byte.
  $A953,$01 Swap in the saved pointer.
  $A954,$03 Call #R$A92F to handle the token format.
  $A957,$03 Restore the saved pointer and the saved character record from the stack.
  $A95A,$01 Return.

c $A95B Process Format Data Byte
@ $A95B label=ProcessFormatDataByte
R $A95B HL Pointer to format data byte
R $A95B O:A Result value
R $A95B O:F Zero flag set if end of format reached
D $A95B Reads the next format byte and skips the extra bytes when its top bit is set.
  $A95B,$05 #REGix=#SKOOLKID$5C42(NEWPPC), saving the count.
N $A960 Read and process the format data byte.
  $A960,$07 Step to the format byte and go to #R$A969 if bit 7 is clear.
  $A967,$02 Step on two places.
@ $A969 label=ProcessFormatDataByte_ProcessBits
  $A969,$01 Keep the format byte.
  $A96A,$02,b$01 Keep bits 4-6, which name the format command.
  $A96C,$02 Jump ahead to #R$A974 when the format command is empty.
@ $A96E label=ProcessFormatDataByte_AdjustBuffer
N $A96E Adjust the buffer pointer based on the command type.
  $A96E,$06 Jump back to #R$A96E while at least #N$10 is still left.
@ $A974 label=ProcessFormatDataByte_ProcessLowNibble
  $A974,$02 Reset the count in #REGc.
  $A976,$01 Restore the format byte from the stack.
  $A977,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 0-3 (extract the low nibble).
  $A979,$02 Go to #R$A986 if the low four bits are clear.
@ $A97B label=ProcessFormatDataByte_ProcessLoop
  $A97B,$01 Use the low digit as the repeat count.
@ $A97C label=ProcessFormatDataByte_ProcessCommand
  $A97C,$03 Call #R$A990 to unpack the next letter.
  $A97F,$03 Write #REGa (the processed value) to *#REGix+#N$00.
  $A982,$02 Step the text pointer on one byte.
  $A984,$02 Count the next entry and repeat from #R$A97C.
@ $A986 label=ProcessFormatDataByte_End
  $A986,$04 Write #N$00 (terminator) to *#REGix+#N$00.
  $A98A,$01 Take the bit offset from #REGc.
  $A98B,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $A98C,$02 Return if the bit offset is zero.
  $A98E,$01 Step to the next byte of the format data.
  $A98F,$01 Return.

c $A990 Rotate And Extract Character Code
@ $A990 label=RotateAndExtractCharacterCode
D $A990 Unpacks the next character code from the bit-packed dictionary word.
R $A990 HL Pointer to bit-packed character data
R $A990 C Rotation count (bit offset within the data)
R $A990 O:A Character code (#N$60-#N$7F)
R $A990 O:C Updated rotation count
  $A990,$09 Read the word at #REGhl into #REGde with the bytes swapped, and jump to #R$A9A0 if #REGc is zero.
N $A991 Read two bytes from the bit-packed data.
N $A999 Rotate the two bytes left by the rotation count.
@ $A999 label=RotateAndExtractCharacterCode_RotateLoop
  $A999,$07 Move the packed character one bit and repeat from #R$A999 until the bit
. offset is used up.
@ $A9A0 label=RotateAndExtractCharacterCode_UpdateRotation
N $A9A0 Update the rotation count for the next character extraction.
  $A9A0,$07 Jump ahead to #R$A9AA if the rotation count plus #N$05 is still below #N$08.
  $A9A7,$03 Drop #N$08 from the count and step to the next character.
@ $A9AA label=RotateAndExtractCharacterCode_Extract
  $A9AA,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the count.
  $A9AB,$01 Read the high byte of the token.
  $A9AC,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $A9AD,$01 Move the bottom bit of the result up to the top.
N $A9AD Extract the 5-bit character code from the rotated byte.
  $A9AE,$01 Move the bottom bit of the result up to the top.
  $A9AF,$01 Move the bottom bit of the result up to the top.
  $A9B0,$02,b$01 Keep only bits 0-4 (extract the 5-bit character code).
  $A9B2,$02 Advance the result by #N$60.
  $A9B4,$01 Return.

g $A9B5 Word Buffer Pointer
@ $A9B5 label=BufferPointer
D $A9B5 Pointer into the word buffer at #R$5C0B. Read and written by #R$A9B7; reset to
. #R$5C0B by #R$A9F6 after the buffered word is printed.
W $A9B5,$02

c $A9B7 Print Character
@ $A9B7 label=Print_Character
D $A9B7 In word-wrap mode, stores each letter at *#R$A9B5 in the word buffer at #R$5C0B
. until a space, newline or quote, then prints the buffered word. When the print mode
. flag at #R$A027 is set, uses the alternate printer instead.
R $A9B7 A Character code to print
  $A9B7,$07 Jump ahead to #R$AA10 if the print mode flag at #R$A027 is not zero.
N $A9BE Handle word-wrap mode - write the character into the word buffer.
  $A9BE,$06 Read the word buffer pointer from *#R$A9B5.
  $A9C4,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A9DF if the character is a space.
  $A9C8,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A9DF if the character is a new line.
  $A9CC,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A9DF if the character is a quotation mark.
  $A9D0,$04 Jump ahead to #R$A9D7 if the character is not a backspace.
N $A9D4 The character IS a backspace, so handle this.
  $A9D4,$03 Step the word buffer pointer back and join #R$A9D9 to store it.

N $A9D7 Write the character into the word buffer and move the pointer on.
@ $A9D7 label=Print_Character_WriteToBuffer
N $A9D7 Write the character to the word buffer.
  $A9D7,$01 Write the character to the word buffer.
  $A9D8,$01 Increment the word buffer pointer by one.
@ $A9D9 label=Print_Character_UpdateBufferPointer
  $A9D9,$03 Write the word buffer pointer back to *#R$A9B5.
  $A9DC,$02 Restore the saved pointer and the saved result from the stack.
  $A9DE,$01 Return.

N $A9DF The character ends a word. If the word buffer holds letters, print them.
@ $A9DF label=Print_Character_ScreenPrintMode
N $A9DF Flush the word buffer when it is not empty.
  $A9DF,$09 Jump to #R$AA0B if the word buffer is empty.
  $A9E8,$03 Read the screen width.
  $A9EB,$01 Compare *#R$C1F5 with #REGl (the buffered word length).
  $A9EC,$03 Go to #R$A9F4 if the word fits on the line.
  $A9EF,$02 Take a new line.
  $A9F1,$03 Call #R$AA14 to print one character and remember the line.
@ $A9F4 label=Print_Character_ScrollScreen
N $A9F4 Print the buffered letters from #R$5C0B and reset the word buffer pointer.
  $A9F4,$02 Keep the print column and use the buffered length as the glyph counter.
  $A9F6,$03 #REGhl=#N$5C0B (the start of the word buffer, which overwrites #SKOOLKID$5C0B(DEFADD) onwards).
  $A9F9,$03 Write #REGhl (the start of the word buffer) to *#R$A9B5.
@ $A9FC label=Print_Character_ScrollLoop
  $A9FC,$01 Read the next character of the buffered word.
  $A9FD,$03 Call #R$AA14 to print one character and remember the line.
  $AA00,$01 Move to the next character.
  $AA01,$02 Count the next character and repeat from #R$A9FC.
  $AA03,$02 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $AA05,$02 And the saved flags, and go to #R$AA0B when the zero flag is clear.
N $AA07 Clear the print position flag after the buffered word is printed.
  $AA07,$04 Reset *#R$A025.
@ $AA0B label=Print_Character_RestoreCharacter
  $AA0B,$03 Restore the character from the stack.
  $AA0E,$02 And jump to #R$AA14 to print it.

N $AA10 Print the character on the screen.
@ $AA10 label=Print_Character_AlternateMode
N $AA10 Use alternate print method (direct screen printing).
  $AA10,$04 Jump to #R$C1E1 to check the television flag.

c $AA14 Print One Character And Remember The Line
@ $AA14 label=Print_Character_ProcessCharacter
D $AA14 Turns an upper-case letter into lower case, remembers that a character has been
. printed on this line, and starts a new line when the character is a carriage return.
N $AA14 Convert uppercase letters to lowercase if needed.
  $AA14,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA1E if the character is below A.
  $AA18,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA1E if the character is past Z.
  $AA1C,$02 Set bit 5 to make the letter lower case.
@ $AA1E label=Print_Character_CheckSpace
  $AA1E,$08 Keep the character and jump to #R$AA2A when this line has already started.
  $AA26,$03 Return if the character is a space.
  $AA29,$06 Keep the character and set the print position flag at #R$A025.
@ $AA2A label=Print_Character_MarkPrintStarted
N $AA2A Mark that we've started printing on this line.
  $AA2F,$06 Jump ahead to #R$AA3B if the character is not a new line.
N $AA35 Reset the print position flag for a carriage return.
  $AA35,$04 Clear *#R$A025.
  $AA39,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AA6D to print the character.

N $AA3B A quotation mark is printed as a blank, and the picture flag is consulted.
@ $AA3B label=Print_Character_CheckQuote
  $AA3B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA46 if the character is not a quotation mark.
  $AA3F,$02 Take a space.
  $AA41,$03 Call #R$C1E1 to honour the television flag.
  $AA44,$02 As after a full stop, go to #R$AA4A to set the flag at #R$A026 so the next letter is a capital.

N $AA46 A sentence stop marks the token so the next letter can be printed in capitals.
@ $AA46 label=Print_Character_CheckPeriod
  $AA46,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA51 if the character is not a full stop.
@ $AA4A label=Print_Character_SetTokenFormatFlag
  $AA4A,$05 Set *#R$A026.
  $AA4F,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AA6D to print the character.

N $AA51 See whether the character byte is a lower-case letter, then see whether the
. character byte is past the lower-case letters, then read the lowercase Handling Flag.
@ $AA51 label=Print_Character_CheckLowercase
N $AA51 Handle lowercase letters (dictionary word characters).
  $AA51,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA6D if the character is below the letter a.
  $AA55,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AA6D if the character is at least #N$7B.
  $AA59,$06 Jump to #R$AA65 if the lowercase handling flag is set.
  $AA5F,$06 Jump to #R$AA6D if the token format flag is zero.
@ $AA65 label=Print_Character_ResetTokenFormatFlag
N $AA65 Reset the token format flag before converting to uppercase.
  $AA65,$04 Reset *#R$A026.
  $AA69,$01 Restore the character from the stack.
N $AA6A Clear bit 5 from the character code (convert to uppercase).
  $AA6A,$02,b$01 Clear bit 5 so a lowercase letter becomes uppercase.
  $AA6C,$01 Keep the character while it is printed.
@ $AA6D label=Print_Character_Print
  $AA6D,$04 Jump to #R$C1E1 to check the television flag.

g $AA71 Lookup Table: Object Display Data
@ $AA71 label=Table_ObjectDisplayData
D $AA71 Words indexed by the object attribute minus #N$01.
. Each word points at a display record. A record is two bytes, plus two when bit 6
. is set and five when bit 4 is set.
. Read by #R$C966.
B $AA71,$02
L $AA71,$02,$18

b $AAA1 Display Bytes
@ $AAA1 label=DisplayBytes
D $AAA1 Bytes read while a picture is drawn.
. Read by #R$ACCC and #R$ACFB.
B $AAA1,$02 Two zero bytes read while a picture is drawn.
B $AAA3,$01 Display byte read with the one at #R$AAA6, read by #R$ACCC and #R$ACFB.
B $AAA4,$02 Word read as the display record, read by #R$ACCC and #R$ACFB.
B $AAA6,$01 Display byte read with the one at #R$AAA3, read by #R$ACCC and #R$ACFB.
B $AAA7,$01 Cleared state byte, read by #R$AAD1 and #R$AC24.

g $AAA8 Buffer: Co-ordinate Header
D $AAA8 Four bytes at the start of the co-ordinate buffer cleared by #R$AC24.
. Read by #R$AC24, #R$AC8F and #R$ACA6.
  $AAA8,$04

g $AAAC Buffer: Co-ordinate Data
@ $AAAC label=Buffer_CoordinateData
D $AAAC 6-byte buffer used for storing co-ordinate pairs and related display data.
. Read by #R$AAD1, #R$AC24 and #R$B6FD.
B $AAAC,$06

c $AAB2 Prepare Character For Display
@ $AAB2 label=PrepareCharacterForDisplay
D $AAB2 Clears the parser and gets the character ready for the picture.
  $AAB2,$07 Clear the parser and clear the pictures-enabled byte at #R$A012.
  $AAB9,$02 Mark this display column as unused.
  $AABB,$02 Point the display row at the top of the screen buffer.

c $AABD Force The Display Marker On
D $AABD Forces the display marker on before the character record is built.
  $AABD,$01 Read the low byte of the source.
  $AABE,$02 Mark bits 0, 1, 2, 6, 7 on the result.
  $AAC0,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the source.
  $AAC1,$03 Call #R$AE6A to build the character record.

c $AAC4 Choose The Display Path From The Initialised Flag
D $AAC4 Reads the initialised flag. When the game has been initialised it selects the
. alternate display path and clears the extra display marker.
  $AAC4,$06 Jump to #R$AAD1 if the initialised flag is zero.
  $AACA,$07 Write #N$01 to the command mode at #R$A024 and clear bit 3 of the source.

c $AAD1 Start Picture Mode When The Screen Is Idle
@ $AAD1 label=StartPictureModeScreenIdle
D $AAD1 When nothing is on screen, clears the picture buffer and selects picture mode.
  $AAD1,$07 Save #REGde and jump to #R$AB11 if the command mode at #R$A024 is set.
  $AAD8,$03 Clear the state byte at #R$AAA7.
  $AADB,$07 Read the word at #R$A02B, save it and call #R$AC24 to clear the co-ordinate buffer.
  $AAE2,$02 Go to #R$AB08 when #R$AC24 returns non-zero.
  $AAE4,$06 Jump to #R$AB08 if the state byte at #R$AAA7 is set.
  $AAEA,$05 Write #N$02 to the command mode at #R$A024.
  $AAEF,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $AAF0,$03 Copy the character record from #REGiy into #REGhl.
  $AAF3,$07 Point #REGhl at the co-ordinates #N($0018,$04,$04) bytes into the character record, and #REGde at the buffer at #R$AAAC.
  $AAFA,$06 Swap the pointers, take #N$06 bytes and copy them.
  $AB00,$03 Read the word at #R$A029.
  $AB03,$05 Store it in the word at #R$A02B and jump ahead to #R$AB11 to check display mode.

@ $AB08 label=StartPictureModeScreenIdle_ClearBorrow
  $AB08,$05 Write #N$01 to the command mode at #R$A024.
  $AB0D,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $AB0E,$03 Store the pointer in the word at #R$A02B.
@ $AB11 label=StartPictureModeScreenIdle_CheckDisplayMode
  $AB11,$01 Restore the source from the stack.
  $AB12,$07 Clear the state byte at #R$AAA7 and call #R$AB91 to check display mode.
  $AB19,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AB8E if #R$AB91 finds the marker bit already clear.
  $AB1B,$02 Go to #R$AB47 if bit 1 of the source is clear.
  $AB1D,$05 Jump ahead to #R$AFB3 if it is #N$90.
  $AB22,$05 Jump ahead to #R$ACCC if it is #N$A0.
  $AB27,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AB4C if bit 3 is clear.
  $AB2B,$06 Jump to #R$AB8E if the pictures-enabled byte at #R$A012 is set.
  $AB31,$06 Jump to #R$AB4C if the initialised flag is set.
  $AB37,$07 Jump to #R$AB8E if the byte at #R$A02D is not #N$C0.
  $AB3E,$05 Jump ahead to #R$AB8E if #REGd is not #N$B0.
  $AB43,$02 Leave #N$02 as the result.
  $AB45,$01 Clear the borrow so the script reads the result as success.
  $AB46,$01 Return.

N $AB47 See whether bit 3 of the low byte of the source is set, then read the pointer at
. the object Processing Source Flag, then record the pointer in the object Processing Source Flag.
@ $AB47 label=StartPictureModeScreenIdle_TestBitLowByte
  $AB47,$05 Go to #R$ACFB if bit 3 of this byte is clear.

c $AB4C Read Object Processing Flag
@ $AB4C label=ReadObjectProcessingFlag
D $AB4C Copies the word at #R$A029 into the word at #R$A02B while the character is prepared for display.
  $AB4C,$06 Copy the word at #R$A029 into the word at #R$A02B.

c $AB52 Copy Display If Needed
@ $AB52 label=CopyDisplayIfNeeded
D $AB52 Copies display If Needed.
N $AB52 The display-marker scan has finished.
  $AB52,$05 Jump to #R$AB8E if the display-marker scan has finished.
  $AB57,$02 Skip the copy when bit 5 of the source is set.
  $AB59,$05 Go to #R$AB8E if neither bit 6 nor bit 7 of #REGe is set.
  $AB5E,$02 The copy did not finish, so try the next object from #R$AB4C.

N $AB60 A sentence-stop marker rebuilds the picture once the game has started.
@ $AB60 label=CopyDisplayIfNeeded_Next
  $AB60,$05 Go to #R$AD57 if the token code is #N$B0.
  $AB65,$05 Go to #R$ACCC if the token code is #N$A0.
  $AB6A,$05 Go to #R$AFB3 if it is #N$90.
  $AB6F,$06 Copy the word at #R$A029 into the word at #R$A02B.
  $AB75,$07 Jump to #R$AB8E if the command mode at #R$A024 is not #N$02.
N $AB7C The character-pair search returned carry.
  $AB7C,$05 Jump to #R$AB8E if the character-pair search returns carry.
  $AB81,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AB8E if #R$ABF3 returns non-zero.
  $AB83,$08 Copy the word at #R$A029 into the word at #R$A02B and take
. #N$A0.
  $AB8B,$03 Take token #N$A0 and continue at #R$ACCC.

@ $AB8E label=CopyDisplayIfNeeded_ReportDone
  $AB8E,$03 Jump to #R$AF67 to return non-zero.

c $AB91 Check Display Mode
@ $AB91 label=CheckDisplayMode
D $AB91 Jumps to #R$ABF3 when the command mode at #R$A024 is #N$02. Otherwise walks the clause in #REGe and stores the words it accepts.
  $AB91,$07 Jump to #R$ABF3 if the command mode at #R$A024 is #N$02.
  $AB98,$01 Read the low byte of the source.
  $AB99,$02 Mark bits 0-2 on the result.
  $AB9B,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the source.
@ $AB9C label=CheckDisplayMode_Loop
  $AB9C,$07 Go to #R$AB9C if it is #N$E0.
  $ABA3,$04 Go to #R$ABB7 if it is not #N$30.
@ $ABA7 label=CheckDisplayMode_TestBitClear
  $ABA7,$04 Jump ahead to #R$ABB5 if bit 1 is clear.
@ $ABAB label=CheckDisplayMode_CharacterLowByte
  $ABAB,$08 Clear bit 1 of #REGe and store #REGbc at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01.
  $ABB3,$02 Go back and test the next display marker.

N $ABB5 The condition holds, so the caller continues.
@ $ABB5 label=CheckDisplayMode_ConditionHoldsCaller
  $ABB5,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $ABB6,$01 Return.

N $ABB7 A zero code clears the third flag bit when that bit is set.
@ $ABB7 label=CheckDisplayMode_Next
  $ABB7,$04 Go to #R$ABC9 if it is not #N$00.
@ $ABBB label=CheckDisplayMode_TestBitClearAgain
  $ABBB,$04 Jump back to #R$ABB5 if bit 2 is clear.
  $ABBF,$08 Clear bit 2 of #REGe and store #REGbc at #REGiy+#N$02-#N$03.
  $ABC7,$02 Go back and test the next display marker.

N $ABC9 A code of #N$40 clears the first flag bit.
@ $ABC9 label=CheckDisplayMode_TestCodeClearsFirst
  $ABC9,$04 Go to #R$ABD1 unless the code is #N$40.
  $ABCD,$04 Clear the mark on this character.

N $ABD1 A blank keeps the second flag bit, or the first when the second is clear.
@ $ABD1 label=CheckDisplayMode_TestBlankKeepsSecond
  $ABD1,$04 Go to #R$ABEF if it is not #N$20.
  $ABD5,$04 Jump back to #R$ABA7 while bit 1 is present.
  $ABD9,$04 Jump back to #R$ABBB while bit 0 is present.
  $ABDD,$07 Clear the mark on this character, then set the mark on this character.
  $ABE4,$03 Store #REGa at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $ABE7,$08 Copy the high byte beside the low byte and repeat the store from #R$ABAB.

N $ABEF See whether the display mode is zero, then see whether bit 1 of the low byte of the
. source is set.
@ $ABEF label=CheckDisplayMode_TestDisplayModeZero
  $ABEF,$01 See whether the display mode is zero.
  $ABF0,$02 See whether bit 1 of the low byte of the source is set.
  $ABF2,$01 Return.

c $ABF3 Match Character Pair
@ $ABF3 label=MatchCharacterPair
D $ABF3 Scans the character-id pairs at #R$994D for the two characters being compared, and stops
. at the zero word that ends the table.
  $ABF3,$08 Call #R$AE7F, and call it again if it returns #N$80.
  $ABFB,$07 Use the character pairs (#R$994D).
@ $AC02 label=MatchCharacterPair_Loop
  $AC02,$05 Go to #R$AC14 if this word is zero.
  $AC07,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AC11 if this pair's first id differs.
  $AC0B,$05 Jump ahead to #R$AC17 if this pair's second id matches.
  $AC10,$01 Step to the next character.
  $AC11,$03 Step to the next character pair and go back to #R$AC02.

@ $AC14 label=MatchCharacterPair_Next
  $AC14,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $AC16,$01 Return.

N $AC17 Store this character's id and script position, then clear the link bit.
@ $AC17 label=MatchCharacterPair_CharacterScriptPosition
  $AC17,$08 Store #REGbc at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01 and clear bit 1 of #REGe.
  $AC1F,$03 Call #R$AE7F to save the text pointer.
  $AC22,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $AC23,$01 Return.

c $AC24 Clear Co-ordinate Buffer
@ $AC24 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer
D $AC24 Clears the co-ordinate buffer and marks bits 2, 4 and 5 of #REGe.
  $AC24,$08 Call #R$A2AB to clear buffer.
  $AC2C,$01 Read the low byte of the source.
  $AC2D,$02 Mark bits 2, 4, 5 on the result.
  $AC2F,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the source.

N $AC30 Saves the text pointer, and a code of 128 clears the fourth flag bit.
@ $AC30 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_Loop
  $AC30,$07 Go to #R$AC41 if it is not #N$80.
  $AC37,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AC3F if bit 4 is clear.
  $AC3B,$04 Clear the mark on this character.

N $AC3F The condition holds, so the caller continues.
@ $AC3F label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_Next
  $AC3F,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $AC40,$01 Return.

N $AC41 Accepts token types #N$10 and #N$70 when their flag bits are set.
@ $AC41 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_TestLowAndHighToken
  $AC41,$04 Jump to #R$AC49 when the token type is #N$10.
  $AC45,$04 Jump to #R$AC65 when the token type is not #N$70.
@ $AC49 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_AcceptFlaggedToken
  $AC49,$04 Jump back to #R$AC3F if bit 4 is clear.
  $AC4D,$04 Jump back to #R$AC3F if bit 2 is clear.
  $AC51,$07 Jump to #R$AC3F if the state byte at #R$AAA7 is at least #N$03.
  $AC58,$07 Count one more at #R$AAA7, then call #R$AC8F.
  $AC5F,$02 Jump back to #R$AC3F to return if #R$AC8F does not return #N$00.
  $AC61,$04 Jump back to #R$AC30 to check token #N$10 or #N$70.

N $AC65 A vocabulary code clears the upper flag bits when the second flag bit is set.
@ $AC65 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_TestVocabularyCode
  $AC65,$04 Jump to #R$AC78 when the token type is not #N$60.
  $AC69,$04 Jump back to #R$AC3F if bit 2 is clear.
  $AC6D,$02 Clear the marker for check token #N$10 or #N$70.
  $AC6F,$05 Clear bit 5 and call #R$AC94 to read the next co-ordinate pair.
  $AC74,$02 Jump back to #R$AC3F to return if #R$AC94 does not return #N$00.
  $AC76,$02 Jump back to #R$AC30 to check token #N$10 or #N$70.

N $AC78 A code of #N$F0 or #N$50 keeps the co-ordinate bits that this token uses.
@ $AC78 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_TestCodeKeepsOrdinate
  $AC78,$04 Jump to #R$AC80 when the token type is #N$F0.
  $AC7C,$04 Jump to #R$AC8B when the token type is not #N$50.
@ $AC80 label=ClearCoordinateBuffer_RecordBufferOrdinate
  $AC80,$08 Record the buffer: co-ordinate data.
  $AC88,$03 Call #R$AE7F to remember the text pointer.
  $AC8B,$01 Clear the carry before testing bit 5 of the source.
  $AC8C,$02 See whether bit 5 of the low byte of the source is set.
  $AC8E,$01 Return.

c $AC8F Point At The Picture Origin
@ $AC8F label=PointPictureOrigin
D $AC8F Points at the four-byte picture origin and continues with the token check.
  $AC8F,$05 Jump ahead to #R$AC97 to take buffer: Co-ordinate Header (#R$AAA8) to check token 10
. or 70.

c $AC94 Read The Next Coordinate Pair
@ $AC94 label=ReadBytePointer
E $AC94 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7809 }
. LIST#
D $AC94 Points at the co-ordinate data and skips the token check when the next pair is empty.
  $AC94,$03 Use the buffer: co-ordinate data (#R$AAAC+#N$02).
@ $AC97 label=ReadBytePointer_CheckToken
  $AC97,$05 Go to #R$ACA1 if this word is zero.
  $AC9C,$01 Advance through the text.
  $AC9D,$01 Read the co-ordinate stored at the pointer.
  $AC9E,$01 Advance through this record.
  $AC9F,$01 Merge the next co-ordinate into the pair.
  $ACA0,$01 Return when the co-ordinate pair is not empty.
  $ACA1,$01 Store #REGb at the second byte of this word.
  $ACA2,$01 Step back to the first byte.
  $ACA3,$01 Store #REGc there.
  $ACA4,$01 Clear #REGa, as the pair was empty and has now been filled from #REGbc.
  $ACA5,$01 Return.

c $ACA6 Copy Display Data To Display Buffer
@ $ACA6 label=CopyDisplayDataToDisplayBuffer
E $ACA6 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$782B }
. LIST#
D $ACA6 Copies display data from the character data structure to the display buffer at #R$AAA8. If bit 6 of #REGe is set, copies from offset #N$04; if bit 7 of #REGe is set, copies from offset #N$0E. Always sets bit 0 of the return value.
R $ACA6 E Flags (bits 6 and 7 indicate which display data set to copy)
R $ACA6 O:A Bit 0 is set if data was copied
  $ACA6,$04 Jump ahead to #R$ACB2 if bit 6 is clear.
  $ACAA,$02 Clear the marker.
  $ACAC,$06 Keep #REGde, take offset #N$04, and join the copy at #R$ACBB.

N $ACB2 Bit 7 selects the second display block.
@ $ACB2 label=CopyDisplayDataToDisplayBuffer_Next
  $ACB2,$02 See whether bit 7 of the low byte of the source is set.
  $ACB4,$01 Return if that marker is clear.
  $ACB5,$06 Clear bit 7 and take offset #N($000E,$04,$04), keeping #REGde.
@ $ACBB label=CopyDisplayDataToDisplayBuffer_AddOffset
  $ACBB,$08 Use the buffer: co-ordinate header (#R$AAA8).
  $ACC3,$06 Take a length of #N$0A entries and copy these bytes across.
  $ACC9,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $ACCB,$01 Return.

c $ACCC Handle A Class #N$A0 Word
@ $ACCC label=HandleClassAWord
E $ACCC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$770B }
. LIST#
D $ACCC Skips class #N$A0 tokens, saves the word pointer and this clause record, and carries on at the class #N$B0 matcher (#R$AD57). When the parser count at #R$A019+#N$01 is #N$02, it sets bit 6 of the clause record first.
R $ACCC E Flags (bit 3 is reset)
  $ACCC,$03 Run text token and save state.
@ $ACCF label=HandleClassAWord_Loop
  $ACCF,$07 Jump back to #R$ACCF if the character code is #N$A0.
  $ACD6,$06 Copy the word at #R$A029 into #R$AAA1.
  $ACDC,$03 Store #REGhl in the word at #R$A02B.
  $ACDF,$05 Store the low byte in the display byte at #R$AAA3.
  $ACE4,$04 Store the record pointer at #R$AAA4.
  $ACE8,$06 Remember whether pictures are enabled, in the display byte at #R$AAA6.
  $ACEE,$07 Jump to #R$AD57 if the parser is not #N$02.
  $ACF5,$06 Set bit 6 of the clause record and jump to #R$AD57.

c $ACFB Apply The Saved Clause
@ $ACFB label=ApplySavedClause
D $ACFB When the command mode at #R$A024 is #N$02 and bit 6 of #REGe is clear, copies six bytes from offset #N$08 of the clause record to offset #N$18 and returns to the scan at #R$AB4C. Once the game has started, any other case sets bit 3 of #REGe and returns to that scan. Before the game has started it restores the saved word as class #N$B0, unlinks the clause record, and falls into #R$AD57.
  $ACFB,$07 Jump to #R$AD23 if the command mode at #R$A024 is not #N$02.
  $AD02,$05 Jump back to #R$AB4C while bit 6 is present.
  $AD07,$08 Set the mark on this character
  $AD0F,$08 Point #REGhl at byte #N$08 of the record (#REGiy+#N$08), save it and take
. #N($0010,$04,$04).
  $AD17,$08 Add #N($0010,$04,$04), so #REGhl points at byte #N$18, and copy #N$06 bytes from byte
. #N$08.
  $AD1F,$04 Switch back to the saved registers and resume the object scan at #R$AB4C.

N $AD23 Once the game has started, sets bit 3 of #REGe and returns to #R$AB4C.
@ $AD23 label=ApplySavedClause_GameStarted
  $AD23,$06 Jump to #R$AD2E if the initialised flag is zero.
  $AD29,$05 Set bit 3 of the flag in #REGe and resume the object scan at #R$AB4C.

N $AD2E Restores the saved word as class #N$B0, unlinks the clause record, and falls into #R$AD57.
@ $AD2E label=ApplySavedClause_RestoreWord
  $AD2E,$08 Copy the saved word to #R$A02B and take class #N$B0.
  $AD36,$07 Read the saved flag bytes at #R$AAA3 and #R$AAA6.
  $AD3D,$03 Put the saved flag back at #R$A012.
  $AD40,$07 Point #REGiy at the saved clause record and read the low byte of its link.
  $AD47,$03 Read the high byte of the link.
  $AD4A,$06 Call #R$A1AF to follow the record pointer.
  $AD50,$07 Clear the clause record's link.

c $AD57 Match A Class #N$B0 Word
@ $AD57 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause
E $AD57 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$75FA }
. LIST#
D $AD57 Grammar handler for word class #N$B0. It matches the word in #REGd against the earlier clause records, which are kept in a linked list.
  $AD57,$07 Read the parser count, count down one and point #REGhl at #R$A012.
  $AD5E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AD66 unless the parser count was #N$02.
  $AD62,$04 Jump to #R$AD67 when the token is #N$A0.
@ $AD66 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_AdvanceCount
  $AD66,$01 Count one more at #R$A012.
  $AD67,$05 Jump back to #R$AABD if the token is #N$A0.
  $AD6C,$06 Jump to #R$ADD4 if the initialised flag is zero.
  $AD72,$04 Point #REGiy at the clause list at #R$9FF0.
  $AD76,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the clause link.
  $AD79,$03 Read the clause marker.
  $AD7C,$07 Go to #R$ADB8 if this clause word is not zero.
  $AD83,$06 Read the initialised flag into #REGde.
  $AD89,$03 Point #REGhl at the clause list at #R$9FF0.
  $AD8C,$08 Keep bit 7 of the clause marker and merge it into the second byte of the record at the pointer in #R$9FF0.
  $AD94,$07 Step #REGhl back, add the offset, and move #REGiy on eight bytes.
  $AD9B,$06 Call #R$AE59 to copy this pair when it is still clear.
  $ADA1,$05 Call #R$AE59 again and go to #R$ADAF when the source low byte is zero.
  $ADA6,$08 Call #R$AE59 to copy the pair two bytes back.
  $ADAE,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $ADAF,$03 Call #R$AE59.
  $ADB2,$06 Point #REGiy at the clause list at #R$9FF0 and jump to #R$ADBC.

N $ADB8 Starts this walk at the saved clause list.
@ $ADB8 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_LaterList
  $ADB8,$04 Point #REGiy at #R$9FF0+#N$02.
@ $ADBC label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_Next
  $ADBC,$06 Read this clause record's link.
  $ADC2,$03 Copy the clause link from #REGhl into #REGix.
  $ADC5,$06 Read the link of the previous record.
  $ADCB,$03 Store the low byte of that link.
  $ADCE,$06 Store the high byte and call #R$A1C7.
@ $ADD4 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_CountClauses
  $ADD4,$08 Point #REGiy at #R$9FF0+#N$02 and take the count at #R$A012.
  $ADDC,$04 Call #R$AEB6 to step to the next clause record.
@ $ADE0 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_Loop
  $ADE0,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AE1E if the count is zero.
N $ADE4 Steps to the next clause record.
  $ADE4,$03 Call #R$AEB6 to step to the next clause record.
  $ADE7,$07 Jump to #R$AE11 if the command mode at #R$A024 is not #N$02.
  $ADEE,$08 Point #REGhl at byte #N$18 of the record (#REGiy+#N$18) and save it.
  $ADF6,$03 Take a length of #N$06 and clear the merge.
@ $ADF9 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_TestCountFinished
  $ADF9,$05 Jump back to #R$ADF9 while the count is not finished.
  $ADFE,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $ADFF,$03 Go to #R$AE11 when any of the six bytes just merged is set.
  $AE02,$08 Swap the pointers and point #REGhl at #REGix, with #N($0018,$04,$04) ready to add.
  $AE0A,$07 Point #REGhl at byte #N$18 of the record (#REGix+#N$18) and copy #N$06 bytes.
@ $AE11 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_ClauseMarker
  $AE11,$03 Read the byte at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $AE14,$08 Call #R$AEF1 if the link word is zero.
  $AE1C,$02 Jump back to #R$ADE0 for the next clause record.

N $AE1E Matches the word against the saved clause list, and returns zero when none match.
@ $AE1E label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_MatchClause
  $AE1E,$04 Call #R$AEEA to initialise character search.
  $AE22,$03 Call #R$AEC5 to step through the saved clause list.
  $AE25,$05 Jump ahead to #R$AE57 if the count is zero.
N $AE2A Steps to the next saved clause record.
  $AE2A,$03 Call #R$AEC5 to step through the saved clause list.
  $AE2D,$08 Go back to #R$AE25 if the next link is set.
  $AE35,$08 Jump back to #R$AE25 while this id does not match the record's flag byte.
  $AE3D,$08 Jump back to #R$AE25 while this id does not match the moved marker.
  $AE45,$08 Go back to #R$AE25 if the attribute word is zero.
  $AE4D,$07 Call #R$AF27 to read the schedule field.
  $AE54,$03 Jump back to #R$AE25 for the next clause record.

@ $AE57 label=MatchWordAgainstEarlierClause_SignalStepFailed
  $AE57,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $AE58,$01 Return.

c $AE59 Copy Display Data If Zero
@ $AE59 label=CopyDisplayDataIfZero
D $AE59 Copies a display pair only when both of its bytes are still clear.
R $AE59 HL Destination pointer (2-byte display data)
R $AE59 DE Source pointer (2-byte display data)
  $AE59,$06 Leave this pair unchanged when either byte is already set.
  $AE5F,$02 Copy the first byte of this display pair into the picture.
@ $AE61 label=CopyDisplayDataIfZero_Next
  $AE61,$04 Jump to #R$AE67 if either byte of this display pair is already set.
  $AE65,$02 Copy the second byte of this display pair into the picture.
@ $AE67 label=CopyDisplayDataIfZero_AdvancePointers
  $AE67,$01 Advance through the display entry.
  $AE68,$01 Step to the next source byte.
  $AE69,$01 Return.

c $AE6A Initialise Character Data Structure
@ $AE6A label=InitialiseCharacterDataStructure
D $AE6A Starts a character search, asks for #N$1E bytes, and stores the new record as the
. previous character on the list.
R $AE6A E Flags
R $AE6A O:IY Pointer to the initialised character data structure
  $AE6A,$08 Call #R$AEEA to start the character search, then ask for #N$1E bytes and call #R$A11C to allocate them.
  $AE72,$06 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous character record.
  $AE78,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous character record.
  $AE7B,$03 Point #REGiy at the new character record.
  $AE7E,$01 Return.

c $AE7F Save Text Pointer
@ $AE7F label=SaveTextPointer
E $AE7F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7873 }
. LIST#
D $AE7F Remembers the text pointer so the message can be continued afterwards.
  $AE7F,$07 Copy the word at #R$A02B into the word at #R$A029 and keep #REGd.
  $AE86,$03 Store #REGa at #R$A02D.
  $AE89,$05 Take the saved text pointer and start its length at zero.
  $AE8E,$05 Move past this byte and go to #R$AE9F if it is #N$E0.
  $AE93,$04 Go to #R$AE9F if the token code is #N$F0.
  $AE97,$04 Go to #R$AE9F if it is #N$90.
  $AE9B,$04 Go to #R$AEA4 if the token code is at least #N$80.
@ $AE9F label=SaveTextPointer_Next
  $AE9F,$05 Read the next text pair and step past it.
@ $AEA4 label=SaveTextPointer_RecordObjectProcessing
  $AEA4,$03 Store #REGhl in the word at #R$A02B.
  $AEA7,$01 Read the high byte of the source.
  $AEA8,$01 Return.

c $AEA9 Follow Character Link
@ $AEA9 label=FollowCharacterLink
D $AEA9 Reads the link stored ahead of the current character and hands that record back to the
. caller. It is empty when the list ends.
  $AEA9,$07 Read the link stored ahead of this character.
  $AEB0,$02 See whether the pointer is empty.
  $AEB2,$03 Point #REGiy at the link that was in #REGhl.
  $AEB5,$01 Return.

c $AEB6 Step To The Next Clause Record
@ $AEB6 label=StepToNextClauseRecord
D $AEB6 Counts down #REGb and follows the clause list, skipping records whose bit 6 is set. It returns the first record without that bit, leaving the previous record in #REGix.
  $AEB6,$01 Count down one.
  $AEB7,$04 Keep the previous clause record in #REGix.
  $AEBB,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the clause link.
  $AEBE,$06 Jump back to #R$AEB7 if bit 6 of the clause marker is set.
  $AEC4,$01 Return.

c $AEC5 Step Through The Saved Clause List
@ $AEC5 label=StepThroughSavedClauseList
D $AEC5 Counts down #REGb and continues at #R$AEC6, which walks the saved clause list until the pointer meets #R$9FF0+#N$02 or bit 6 is clear.
  $AEC5,$01 Count down one.

c $AEC6 Walk The Saved Clause List
@ $AEC6 label=WalkSavedClauseList
D $AEC6 Follows the clause list from #R$9FF0 until the pointer meets #R$9FF0+#N$02 or bit 6
. of the clause marker is clear.
  $AEC6,$04 Keep the previous clause record in #REGix.
  $AECA,$0C Call #R$A104 to step pointer and compare, saving pointer and the source.
@ $AECC label=WalkSavedClauseList_Loop
  $AED6,$07 Point #REGhl at this clause record and #REGde at #R$9FF0+#N$02, and clear the carry.
  $AEDD,$02 See whether this pointer matches #R$9FF0+#N$02.
  $AEDF,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AEE7 if the two pointers meet.
  $AEE1,$06 Jump to #R$AECC if bit 6 of #REGiy+#N$01 is set.
@ $AEE7 label=WalkSavedClauseList_Next
  $AEE7,$02 Restore the saved source and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $AEE9,$01 Return.

c $AEEA Initialise Character Search
@ $AEEA label=InitialiseCharacterSearch
D $AEEA Starts a character search with no current record, then joins the list walk at #R$AEC6.
  $AEEA,$04 Start with no current character.
  $AEEE,$03 Jump to the character search routine.

c $AEF1 Merge Character Flags
@ $AEF1 label=MergeCharacterFlags
E $AEF1 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$78B7 }
. LIST#
D $AEF1 Copies the id, script marker and attribute bytes from the character record onto the
. schedule entry so the two stay in step.
  $AEF1,$04 Read the moved marker.
  $AEF5,$08 Merge #REGa into the byte at #REGiy+#N$01, keeping that byte's top bit.
  $AEFD,$07 Store the character id.
  $AF04,$03 Read the attribute type.
  $AF07,$03 Merge the object attribute into the result.
  $AF0A,$01 Return when this character has no attribute.
  $AF0B,$06 Return if copying this display entry did not match.
  $AF11,$08 Switch register sets and point #REGhl at byte #N$12 of the record at #REGiy.
  $AF19,$06 Take the graphics: baker street and move the pointer on by the length.
  $AF1F,$03 Take a length of #N$06 bytes.
  $AF22,$05 Copy the bytes and take an offset of #N$04.

c $AF27 Read The Schedule Field
D $AF27 Sets the copy length from the script position, using two bytes when that position is empty.
  $AF27,$03 Call #R$AF52 to set the copy length to #N$0A.
  $AF2A,$06 Test whether the word at #REGiy+#N$02-#N$03 is zero.
  $AF30,$03 Take a length of #N$02 bytes.
N $AF33 Set copy length 2.
  $AF33,$03 Call #R$AF56 to set the copy length to #N$02 if the link word is zero.
  $AF36,$01 Switch back to the values saved for the caller.
  $AF37,$01 Return.

c $AF38 Copy Schedule Entry
@ $AF38 label=CopyScheduleEntry
D $AF38 Copies the ten-byte schedule entry into the character record the caller is building.
  $AF38,$03 Save the source, the count and the pointer.
  $AF3B,$02 Take a count of #N$0A.
  $AF3D,$04 Point #REGhl at the schedule entry and keep #REGa as the low byte of the offset.
  $AF41,$02 Clear the high byte of the source.
  $AF43,$01 Advance the pointer by the source length.
  $AF44,$01 #HTML(Clear #REGa, ready to <code>OR</code> the #N$0A bytes together.)
@ $AF45 label=CopyScheduleEntry_Loop
  $AF45,$04 Or each entry into #REGa and repeat from #R$AF45 until #REGb runs out.
  $AF49,$03 Restore the pointer, the count and the source from the stack.
  $AF4C,$01 Clear the borrow so an empty entry returns as a failed match.
  $AF4D,$01 Return.

N $AF4E Sets copy length #N$06.
@ $AF4E label=CopyScheduleEntry_SixBytes
  $AF4E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$AF58 to set copy length #N$06.

c $AF52 Set Copy Length #N$0A
@ $AF52 label=SetCopyLengthTen
E $AF52 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$78FF }
. LIST#
D $AF52 Chooses a length of #N$0A bytes and copies them from the character record into the
. schedule entry.
  $AF52,$04 Copy the bytes for the chosen length.

c $AF56 Set Copy Length #N$02
@ $AF56 label=SetCopyLengthTwo
E $AF56 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7903 }
. LIST#
D $AF56 Chooses a length of #N$02 bytes and copies them from the character record into the
. schedule entry.
  $AF56,$02 Count #N$02.

c $AF58 Copy Bytes For The Given Length
@ $AF58 label=CopyBytes
D $AF58 Advances the destination by the length already chosen, then copies that many bytes from
. the source and returns.
  $AF58,$05 Move the pointer on by the length.
  $AF5D,$04 Copy the destination into the pointer and add the length.
  $AF61,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $AF62,$02 Clear the high byte of the count.
  $AF64,$02 Copy the counted bytes.
  $AF66,$01 Return.

N $AF67 Tells the script the command succeeded, leaving #N$01 with the borrow clear. The
. command search and the display steps share this exit.
@ $AF67 label=CopyBytes_ReportSuccess
  $AF67,$03 Set a non-zero result.
  $AF6A,$01 Return.

g $AF6B Table: Command Handler Lookup (Standard)
@ $AF6B label=Table_CommandHandlerLookup_Standard
D $AF6B Lookup table for command handlers when *#R$A024 is not equal to #N$02. Each entry consists of a 1-byte first value, a 1-byte second value, and a 2-byte handler pointer.
. Read by #R$AFB3.
B $AF6B,$01 First value
B $AF6C,$01 Second value
W $AF6D,$02 Handler pointer
L $AF6B,$04,$0C

g $AF9B Table: Command Handler Lookup (Alternate)
@ $AF9B label=Table_CommandHandlerLookup_Alternate
D $AF9B Lookup table for command handlers when *#R$A024 is equal to #N$02. Each entry consists of a 1-byte first value, a 1-byte second value, and a 2-byte handler pointer.
. Read by #R$AFB3.
B $AF9B,$01 First value
B $AF9C,$01 Second value
W $AF9D,$02 Handler pointer
L $AF9B,$04,$06

c $AFB3 Search Command Handler Table
@ $AFB3 label=SearchCommandHandlerTable
E $AFB3 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8251 }
. LIST#
D $AFB3 Searches through a command handler lookup table for a matching value pair and jumps to the associated handler routine if found.
R $AFB3 B Second value to match
R $AFB3 C First value to match
  $AFB3,$03 Set the table pointer to #R$AF6B.
  $AFB6,$05 Check which lookup table to use based on the current mode.
  $AFBB,$02 Jump ahead to #R$AFC0 if the mode is not #N$02.
  $AFBD,$03 Point #REGhl at the alternate table at #R$AF9B.
@ $AFC0 label=SearchCommandHandlerTable_StartSearch
  $AFC0,$07 Count #N$0C entries, or #N$06 when the mode is #N$02, keeping the source.
@ $AFC7 label=SearchCommandHandlerTable_Loop
  $AFC7,$03 Check if the first value in the current table entry matches the search
. value.
  $AFCA,$02 Skip to the next entry if it doesn't match.
  $AFCC,$02 Check if the second value in the current table entry matches the search
. value.
  $AFCE,$02 Jump to the handler if both values match.
@ $AFD0 label=SearchCommandHandlerTable_NextEntry
  $AFD0,$03 Move to the next table entry.
  $AFD3,$03 Decrease the entry counter and continue searching if there are more entries.
@ $AFD6 label=SearchCommandHandlerTable_NotFound
  $AFD6,$04 Jump back to #R$AF67 to return non-zero.

N $AFDA Reads the handler address that follows and jumps to it.
@ $AFDA label=SearchCommandHandlerTable_Found
N $AFDA A handler was found, use it!
  $AFDA,$06 Load the handler pointer from the table entry.
  $AFE0,$01 Jump to the handler.

c $AFE1 Check Bit And Jump
@ $AFE1 label=CheckBitAndJump
D $AFE1 Returns when bit 1 of #REGe is set. Otherwise drops the return address and jumps to #R$AF67.
  $AFE1,$02 See whether bit 1 of the low byte of the source is set.
  $AFE3,$01 Return if that marker is set.
  $AFE4,$04 Jump back to #R$AF67 to return non-zero.

c $AFE8 Test Bit 1 Of The Source
@ $AFE8 label=TestSourceBitOne
D $AFE8 Returns to the caller when bit 1 of #REGe is clear, and also when the game has started and pictures are disabled. Otherwise it drops the return address and leaves through #R$AF67.
  $AFE8,$02 See whether bit 1 of the source is set.
  $AFEA,$01 Return if that marker is clear.
  $AFEB,$06 Jump to #R$AFF6 if the pictures-enabled byte at #R$A012 is set.
  $AFF1,$05 Return if the initialised flag is set.
@ $AFF6 label=TestSourceBitOne_DropReturn
  $AFF6,$04 Jump back to #R$AF67 to return non-zero.

c $AFFA Remember The Display Page
@ $AFFA label=RememberDisplayPage
D $AFFA Remembers display page #N$A0 and joins the routine that saves the display state.
. Reached through the alternate command table at #R$AF9B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $AFFA,$05 Take token #N$A0 and continue at #R$ACCC.

c $AFFF Save Display State
@ $AFFF label=SaveDisplayState
D $AFFF Tests bit 6 of port #N$FB, and stores #N$01 in the input flag when that bit is clear.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $AFFF,$08 Go to #R$B015 if bit 6 of port #N$FB is set.
  $B007,$04 Take #N$01 and continue at #R$B012.

c $B00B Clear The Display Lines
@ $B00B label=ClearTheDisplayLines
D $B00B Clears the display lines when the flag test passes, then returns zero.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B00B,$06 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit, then call #R$A296 to clear the display lines.
  $B011,$01 Start from zero.

c $B012 Record The Result In The Input Flag
@ $B012 label=RecordInputFlag
D $B012 Stores the result in the input flag and returns to the game state at #R$B015.
  $B012,$03 Store it in the input.

N $B015 Handles game State.
@ $B015 label=RecordInputFlag_UseGameState
  $B015,$07 Jump back to #R$AAC4 with the source byte at #R$A02D in #REGd.

c $B01C Step Display State
@ $B01C label=StepDisplayState
D $B01C Advances the room-display state when the parser flag at #R$A019+#N$01 is #N$01, builds the
. character record, and copies the display.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B01C,$08 Jump to #R$AF67 if the parser is not #N$01.
  $B024,$08 Store #N$02 at #R$A019+#N$01 and call #R$AE6A to initialise the character.
  $B02C,$07 Jump back to #R$AB52 to copy display if needed.

c $B033 Step Display State When Parser Clear
@ $B033 label=StepDisplayStateWhenParserClear
D $B033 Takes the next display-state step when the parser flag is clear, sets that flag, and
. copies the display.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B033,$03 Call #R$AFE8 to test the low flag bit.
  $B036,$07 Jump to #R$AF67 if the parser is set.
  $B03D,$05 Write #N$01 to the parser.
  $B042,$04 Set bit 7 of #REGiy+#N$01.
  $B046,$03 Jump back to #R$AB52 to copy display if needed.

c $B049 Link Character When Flagged
@ $B049 label=LinkCharacterWhenFlagged
D $B049 When the action flag is set, stores this character on the display list and allocates a
. record for the next one.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B049,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B058 if bit 1 is clear.
  $B04D,$06 Point #REGhl at #N($088B,$04) and store its low byte at #REGiy+#N$00.
  $B053,$03 Store #REGh at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $B056,$02 Clear the link bit on this character.
  $B058,$06 Keep the character record.
  $B05E,$04 Use the character script entry list (#R$9FEB+#N$01).
  $B062,$06 Call #R$A104 to step pointer and compare.
  $B068,$05 Call #R$A11C to allocate #N$02 bytes.
  $B06D,$06 Store the low byte of the word at #R$A02B as the record's flag byte.
  $B073,$03 Store the moved marker.
  $B076,$06 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $B07C,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $B07F,$06 Restore the saved character record, the saved schedule entry, the saved source and the saved pointer from the stack.
@ $B085 label=LinkCharacterWhenFlagged_Loop
  $B085,$03 Call #R$AE7F to save the text pointer.
  $B088,$02 See whether the text byte is the link marker.
  $B08A,$02 Go back to #R$B085 until the text byte is the link marker.
  $B08C,$06 Jump back to #R$B085 while the low nibble of #REGb or #REGc is not zero.
  $B092,$08 Write #N$C0 through the pointer at #R$A029 and jump back to #R$AB52 to copy display if needed.

c $B09A Wait For A Key
@ $B09A label=WaitForAKey
D $B09A Waits until a key is pressed, then returns to the command state.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B09A,$06 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit, then call #R$C415 to wait for a key.
  $B0A0,$03 Jump back to #R$B015 to handle game state.

c $B0A3 Reset Clock
@ $B0A3 label=ResetClock
D $B0A3 Puts the clock back to the time a picture starts from, then returns to the game.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B0A3,$06 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit, then call #R$B0AC to initialise the character timer.
  $B0A9,$03 Jump back to #R$B015 to handle game state.

c $B0AC Initialise Character Timer
@ $B0AC label=InitialiseCharacterTimer
D $B0AC Initialises character timing data by resetting the time ticker, setting the character's timer in the character table to zero, and initialising the character script cycles counter.
  $B0AC,$06 Reset #R$9FDD to zero.
  $B0B2,$04 Point to #R$9860.
  $B0B6,$06 Set the character's timer to zero.
  $B0BC,$04 Write #N($0001,$04,$04) to *#R$A01F.
  $B0C0,$04 Record the startup flag.
  $B0C4,$01 Clear the carry so an idle character timer returns as not yet running.
  $B0C5,$01 Return.

c $B0C6 Start A Fresh Script Cycle
@ $B0C6 label=StartFreshScriptCycle
D $B0C6 Starts a fresh script cycle and returns to the command state.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B0C6,$03 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit.
  $B0C9,$06 Write #N($0001,$04,$04) to *#R$A01F.
  $B0CF,$08 Write #N$02 to the startup flag at #R$A0DA and jump back to #R$B015.

c $B0D7 Load Game State
@ $B0D7 label=LoadGameState
D $B0D7 Reads a stored game back into the working state.
R $B0D7 O:IY Restored game state pointer
  $B0D7,$06 Call #R$B0EE to calculate the location offset, then call #R$C0DF to load the game.
  $B0DD,$02 Jump back to #R$B0D7 if there was a tape loading error to try
. again.
  $B0DF,$04 Read the saved character pointer.
  $B0E3,$01 Return.

c $B0E4 Save Game State
@ $B0E4 label=SaveGameState
D $B0E4 Writes the working state out so the game can be restored.
R $B0E4 IY Game state pointer to save
  $B0E4,$07 Call #R$B0EE to calculate location offset.
  $B0EB,$03 Jump to #R$C040 to save game.

c $B0EE Calculate Location Offset
@ $B0EE label=CalculateLocationOffset
D $B0EE Subtracts the location table at #R$840B from the room pointer and returns that distance.
R $B0EE O:DE Offset from base location address
  $B0EE,$07 Use the locations at #R$840B and the character script cycle at #R$A01F.
  $B0F5,$03 Use the locations at #R$840B.
  $B0F8,$01 Clear the carry before subtracting the two room pointers.
  $B0F9,$02 Measure the gap between the two pointers.
  $B0FB,$01 Hand the offset back in #REGde.
  $B0FC,$01 Return.

c $B0FD Process Character Data
@ $B0FD label=ProcessCharacterData
D $B0FD Follows the character link, and calls #R$AEC6 when bit 3 of the flag is clear.
. Reached through the alternate command table at #R$AF9B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B0FD,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B107 if bit 3 is present.
  $B104,$03 Call #R$AEC6 to walk the saved clause list.
@ $B107 label=ProcessCharacterData_Next
  $B107,$06 Run character data.
  $B10D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B118 if bit 3 is present.
  $B111,$02 Mark this character as already linked.
  $B113,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link.
  $B116,$02 Jump ahead to #R$B11F to step on #N$18 bytes.

N $B118 Mark another picture enabled and initialise the character.
@ $B118 label=ProcessCharacterData_PicturesEnabledPointer
  $B118,$07 Call #R$AE6A to initialise character data structure.
@ $B11F label=ProcessCharacterData_LengthCharacters
  $B11F,$08 Point #REGhl #N$18 bytes past #REGiy and restore #REGde.
  $B127,$06 Swap the pointers, copy #N$06 bytes.
  $B12D,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.

N $B12E Read the low byte of the source, then mark bits 0, 1, 2, 6, 7 on the result,
. then store the result in the low byte of the source.
  $B12E,$07 Jump back to #R$AAD1 to prepare character for display.

c $B135 Set Display Mode #N$02
@ $B135 label=SetDisplayMode_SelectsPictureMode
D $B135 Selects picture mode and marks the primary picture bit.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B135,$03 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit.
  $B138,$07 Set the command mode at #R$A024 to #N$02 and continue at #R$B14B.

c $B13F Follow Link When Flagged
@ $B13F label=FollowLinkWhenFlagged
D $B13F Follows the link when bit 3 of the display flags is present.
. Reached through the alternate command table at #R$AF9B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B13F,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B14B if bit 3 is clear.
  $B143,$04 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link, saving source.
  $B147,$04 Call #R$AE6A to initialise character data structure.

N $B14B Set the mark on this character.
@ $B14B label=FollowLinkWhenFlagged_MarkTopBit
  $B14B,$06 Set bit 7 of #REGiy+#N$19 and jump back to #R$B12E.

c $B151 Select Picture Mode Alternate
@ $B151 label=SelectPictureModeAlternate
D $B151 Selects picture mode and marks the secondary picture bit.
. Reached through the standard command table at #R$AF6B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B151,$03 Call #R$AFE1 to test the flag bit.
  $B154,$07 Set the command mode at #R$A024 to #N$02 and continue at #R$B167.

c $B15B Initialise When Flagged
@ $B15B label=InitialiseWhenFlagged
D $B15B Initialises the character when bit 3 of the display flags is present.
. Reached through the alternate command table at #R$AF9B, dispatched by #R$AFE0.
  $B15B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B167 if bit 3 is clear.
  $B15F,$04 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link, saving source.
  $B163,$04 Call #R$AE6A to initialise character data structure.

N $B167 Set the mark on this character.
@ $B167 label=InitialiseWhenFlagged_MarkBitSix
  $B167,$06 Set bit 6 of #REGiy+#N$19 and jump back to #R$B12E.

g $B16D Display Flag
@ $B16D label=DisplayFlag
D $B16D Flag byte for display processing (bit 1 indicates objects have been processed).
. Read by #R$B191, #R$B405, #R$B4FD, #R$B5B6 and #R$B633.
B $B16D,$01
B $B16E,$02 Display flag word.
B $B170,$01 Count of passes through the primary display flag.
B $B171,$01 Count of passes through the secondary display flag.

g $B172 Display Data Pointer
@ $B172 label=DisplayDataPointer
D $B172 Pointer to display data used for processing room displays.
. Read by #R$B191, #R$B405, #R$B4A3, #R$B4FD, #R$B5B6, #R$B633, #R$BD7E and #R$BE3C.
B $B172,$01

g $B173 Display Mode Flag
@ $B173 label=DisplayModeFlag
D $B173 Flag controlling display mode (bit 7 indicates special display mode).
. Read by #R$B4A3, #R$B4C1 and #R$B633.
B $B173,$01
W $B174,$02 Saved display pointer, read by #R$B191.
W $B176,$02 Saved display pointer, read by #R$B191.
B $B178,$01 Low byte of the display mode word, read by #R$B405, #R$B54A, #R$B5A4, #R$B5B6, #R$B633, #R$BD7E and #R$BE4B.
B $B179,$01 High byte of the display mode flag, read by #R$B54A, #R$B568 and #R$B633.
B $B17A,$04 Bytes beside the display mode word.

g $B17E Object Table Pointer
@ $B17E label=ObjectTablePointer
D $B17E Pointer to the current position in the object table being processed.
. Read by #R$B308, #R$B34F, #R$B4C1, #R$B936 and #R$D6C1.
W $B17E,$02
B $B184,$01 Saved room display parameter. Written by #R$B22E and #R$B2A1, and read by #R$B244 and #R$B2B7.
B $B188,$02 Saved display pointer, read by #R$B405.
B $B18A,$02 Saved display pointer, read by #R$B405.

c $B191 Initialise Display Buffer
@ $B191 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer
D $B191 Clears the room parameters and the display buffer, then looks for a room record.
  $B191,$08 Write #N$FF to the room graphics parameter and write #N$FF to the room
. display parameter.
  $B199,$08 Call #R$A2AB to clear buffer.
  $B1A1,$05 Go to #R$B1AB if a room record matches.
  $B1A6,$03 Leave the object-table handler at #R$B84C.
  $B1A9,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B1AA,$01 Return.

@ $B1AB label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_Next
  $B1AB,$08 Use the object runtime table (#R$8259).
  $B1B3,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B1BF if the two pointers meet.
  $B1B7,$03 Take a length of #N$08 entries.
@ $B1BA label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_Loop
  $B1BA,$05 Count one more record and go back to #R$B1BA until the pointer reaches the base.
@ $B1BF label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_RecordSavedCharacter
  $B1BF,$07 Store this record count as the saved character and keep the object pointer at #R$B18C+#N$02.
  $B1C6,$04 Go to #R$B1DD if it is not #N$2E.
  $B1CA,$08 Test bit 0 of the display flag at #R$B16D and take #R$9FEE+#N$14.
  $B1D2,$08 Point #REGde at the display data pointer at #R$B172 and take #N$06 bytes. Skip the swap when bit 0 of the display flag is clear.
  $B1DA,$01 Swap the two pointers.
@ $B1DB label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_CopyTheseBytes
  $B1DB,$02 Copy these bytes across.
@ $B1DD label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_CheckDisplayFlag
  $B1DD,$06 Call #R$C493 to test bits 3 and 7 of the object attribute, then call #R$C4B2 to test bits 2 and 6.
  $B1E3,$06 Jump to #R$B1F2 if the display flag at #R$A01C is set.
  $B1E9,$09 Copy the display flag at #R$B39C into #R$A01C and call #R$B34F to report that this display step found nothing.
@ $B1F2 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_RecordObjectTable
  $B1F2,$08 Keep bit 4 of the display flag at #R$A01E in the saved flag at #R$B17E+#N$12.
  $B1FA,$02 Go to #R$B200 if bit 4 of the saved flag is clear.
  $B1FC,$04 Clear the display flag at #R$A01C.
@ $B200 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_ClearParser
  $B200,$04 Clear the parser flag at #R$A00B.
N $B204 A bit from #N$0C is set in the object attribute value?
  $B204,$07 Go to #R$B213 if bit 2 or bit 3 of the object attribute is set.
@ $B20B label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_BytesDiffer
  $B20B,$04 Return if drawing the object did not match.
  $B20F,$03 Leave the object-display handler at #R$B863.
  $B212,$01 Return.

N $B213 Reads the object attribute, and refreshes the room when the display flag is clear.
@ $B213 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_ReadsObjectAttribute
  $B213,$05 Call #R$B387 to read the object attribute, and go to #R$B280 if it returns non-zero.
  $B218,$06 Jump to #R$B224 if the display flag at #R$A01C is set.
  $B21E,$06 Jump ahead to #R$B259 if the pass count at #R$B170 is zero.
@ $B224 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_TestFlagDisplay
  $B224,$05 Go to #R$B244 if #R$B4C1 returns zero.
  $B229,$05 Jump back to #R$B224 while the object has nothing to display.
  $B22E,$06 Save the room display parameter at #R$B184.
  $B234,$03 Point #REGhl at the pass count at #R$B170.
  $B237,$07 Count one more pass at #R$B170, and jump to #R$B244 if the saved flag is set.
  $B23E,$06 Jump to #R$B224 if the display flag at #R$A01C is zero.
@ $B244 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_RecordRoomDisplay
  $B244,$06 Record the room display parameter.
  $B24A,$06 Jump to #R$B259 if the pass count at #R$B170 is zero.
  $B250,$04 Go to #R$B20B if it is #N$01.
  $B254,$03 Leave the display handler at #R$B5E2.
  $B257,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B258,$01 Return.

N $B259 Carry on only when the saved character is a full stop and the display flags are clear.
@ $B259 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_SavedCharacterCheck
  $B259,$07 Jump to #R$B27B if the saved character is not #N$2E.
  $B260,$06 Jump to #R$B27B if the display is set.
  $B266,$07 Go to #R$B27B if bit 0 of this byte is set.
  $B26D,$06 Clear the display data pointer at #R$B172.
  $B273,$03 Clear the saved display pointer at #R$B173+#N$01.
  $B276,$05 Clear the saved display pointer at #R$B176 and jump back to #R$B213 for the next object attribute.

N $B27B Leave the display handler at #R$B65F and return zero.
@ $B27B label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_LeaveDisplayHandler
  $B27B,$03 Leave the display handler at #R$B65F.
  $B27E,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B27F,$01 Return.

N $B280 Reads the display flag, and follows the secondary flag when the first one is clear.
@ $B280 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_ReadsDisplayFlag
  $B280,$06 Jump to #R$B292 if the display is set.
  $B286,$06 Jump back to #R$B259 if the display is zero.
  $B28C,$06 Go to #R$B303 if #R$B32D returns zero.
@ $B292 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_TestFlagDisplayAgain
  $B292,$05 Go to #R$B2B7 if #R$B4C1 returns zero.
  $B297,$03 Call #R$B2DF to read the room graphics parameter.
  $B29A,$07 Jump to #R$B292 if the pass count at #R$B171 is not #N$01.
  $B2A1,$06 Save the room display parameter at #R$B184.
  $B2A7,$03 Point #REGhl at the pass count at #R$B170.
  $B2AA,$07 Count one more pass at #R$B170, and jump to #R$B2B7 if the saved flag is set.
  $B2B1,$06 Jump to #R$B292 if the display is zero.
@ $B2B7 label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_RecordRoomDisplayAgain
  $B2B7,$06 Record the room display parameter.
  $B2BD,$06 Jump to #R$B259 if the pass count at #R$B170 is zero.
  $B2C3,$04 Go to #R$B2DA if it is not #N$01.
  $B2C7,$03 Call #R$B2DF to read the room graphics parameter.
  $B2CA,$06 Jump to #R$B303 if the pass count at #R$B171 is zero.
  $B2D0,$05 Go to #R$B20B if it is #N$01.
  $B2D5,$03 Leave the display handler at #R$B5D3.
  $B2D8,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B2D9,$01 Return.

N $B2DA Leave the display handler at #R$B5E2 and return zero.
@ $B2DA label=InitialiseDisplayBuffer_LeaveDisplayHandlerAgain
  $B2DA,$03 Leave the display handler at #R$B5E2.
  $B2DD,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B2DE,$01 Return.

c $B2DF Read The Room Graphics Parameter
@ $B2DF label=ReadRoomGraphicsParameter
D $B2DF Asks the picture handler whether this object has anything to draw.
  $B2DF,$03 Call #R$B36B to report that this display step found nothing.
  $B2E2,$05 Go to #R$B2FC if bit 7 of the display mode flag at #R$B179 is set or the handler returns #N$FF.
  $B2E7,$05 Jump back to #R$B2E2 while the object has nothing to display.
  $B2EC,$06 Save the room graphics parameter at #R$B185.
  $B2F2,$03 Point #REGhl at the pass count at #R$B171.
  $B2F5,$07 Count one more pass at #R$B171 and jump back to #R$B2E2 if the saved flag at #R$B17E+#N$12 is zero.
@ $B2FC label=ReadRoomGraphicsParameter_RecordRoomGraphics
  $B2FC,$06 Record the room graphics parameter.
  $B302,$01 Return.

N $B303 Leave the room-graphics handler at #R$B6AE and return zero.
@ $B303 label=ReadRoomGraphicsParameter_LeaveRoomGraphics
  $B303,$03 Leave the room-graphics handler at #R$B6AE.
  $B306,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B307,$01 Return.

c $B308 Read The Display Setup Flag
@ $B308 label=ReadDisplaySetupFlag
D $B308 Reads the object table pointer, calls #R$B34F, and tests bit 7 of the display flag.
  $B308,$08 Read the object table pointer and keep it with the flag at #R$B4A2.
  $B310,$03 Call #R$B34F to report that this display step found nothing.
  $B313,$05 Go to #R$B31E if #R$B4C1 returns zero.
  $B318,$06 Count one more in #R$B170 and try the next entry from #R$B313.

N $B31E Store the result in the display setup flag before returning.
@ $B31E label=ReadDisplaySetupFlag_ResultDisplaySetup
  $B31E,$07 Record the display setup flag.
  $B325,$07 Clear the display-setup flag and keep the object-table pointer.
  $B32C,$01 Return.

c $B32D Read The Secondary Display Flag
@ $B32D label=ReadSecondaryDisplayFlag
D $B32D Reads the secondary display pointer, calls #R$B36B, and tests bit 7 of the display mode flag at #R$B179.
  $B32D,$07 Call #R$B36B to report that this display step found nothing.
  $B334,$04 Read the display.
@ $B338 label=ReadSecondaryDisplayFlag_TestFlagDisplay
  $B338,$05 Go to #R$B343 if bit 7 of the display mode flag at #R$B179 is set or the handler returns #N$FF.
  $B33D,$06 Count one more in #R$B171 and try the next entry from #R$B338.

N $B343 Store the result in the display flag, then read the working value before returning.
@ $B343 label=ReadSecondaryDisplayFlag_ResultDisplayFlag
  $B343,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $B344,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$B549.
  $B347,$03 Read the pass count at #R$B171.
  $B34A,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $B34B,$03 Store it at #R$B180.
  $B34E,$01 Return.

c $B34F Report An Empty Display Step
@ $B34F label=ReportEmptyDisplayStep
D $B34F Tells the caller this display step found nothing.
  $B34F,$06 Clear the pass count at #R$B170 and take #N$FF.
  $B355,$03 Record the display setup flag.
N $B358 Adjust object table for sum.
  $B358,$03 Call #R$D04F to point the object list at this room.
  $B35B,$07 Jump to #R$B366 if the display mode is at least #N$07.
  $B362,$04 Move through the record.
@ $B366 label=ReportEmptyDisplayStep_RecordCharacter
  $B366,$04 Record the character record in the object table pointer at #R$B17E.
  $B36A,$01 Return.

c $B36B Report The Next Empty Display Step
@ $B36B label=ReportNextEmptyDisplayStep
D $B36B Tells the caller this display step found nothing.
  $B36B,$06 Clear the display flag.
  $B371,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$B549.
N $B374 Adjust object table for sum.
  $B374,$03 Call #R$D04F to point the object list at this room.
  $B377,$07 Jump to #R$B382 if the display mode is at least #N$07.
  $B37E,$04 Move through the record.
@ $B382 label=ReportNextEmptyDisplayStep_RecordCharacter
  $B382,$04 Store the character record at #R$B180.
  $B386,$01 Return.

c $B387 Read The Object Attribute
@ $B387 label=ReadObjectAttribute
E $B387 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7AD8 }
. LIST#
D $B387 Returns the object attribute when its bit 2 is clear, or when bit 0 of #R$B16E is set. Returns zero when bit 1 of the attribute is set.
  $B387,$03 Read the object attribute.
  $B38A,$02 See whether bit 2 of it is set.
  $B38C,$01 Return if that marker is clear.
  $B38D,$03 Point at the display flag.
  $B390,$02 See whether the display flag is set.
  $B392,$01 Return if that marker is set.
  $B393,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B39A if bit 1 of the attribute is set.
  $B397,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B399,$01 Return.

@ $B39A label=ReadObjectAttribute_SignalStepFailed
  $B39A,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B39B,$01 Return.

g $B39C Flags: Display
D $B39C Display flag beside #R$B39D.
. Read by #R$B191 and #R$B39D.
B $B39C,$01

c $B39D Process Display Pointer
@ $B39D label=ProcessDisplayPointer
E $B39D View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7B9E }
. LIST#
D $B39D Stores the result in the display, then clears the mark on this character.
  $B39D,$08 Read the word at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01 into #REGhl, keeping #REGiy.
  $B3A5,$01 Read the high byte of the pointer.
  $B3A6,$02 Keep bit 7.
  $B3A8,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$B39C.
  $B3AB,$02 Clear the top bit of the pointer.
  $B3AD,$06 Store the pointer at #R$B185+#N$01 and read the low byte at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $B3B3,$03 Read the high byte at #REGiy+#N$03.
  $B3B6,$07 Store that word at #R$B18C and push the pointer at #R$B188.
  $B3BD,$02 Take #N$04 bytes for #R$A2AB to clear.
  $B3BF,$04 Call #R$A2AB to clear buffer.
  $B3C3,$07 Take #N$02 bytes and call #R$B483 to copy the character into the display.
  $B3CA,$05 Take #N$0E and call #R$B483 to copy the character into the display.
  $B3CF,$05 Take #N$06 and call #R$B483 to copy the character into the display.
  $B3D4,$05 Take #N$10 and call #R$B483 to copy the character into the display.
  $B3D9,$07 Clear the byte at #R$A02E and point #REGhl at #R$B17E+#N$08.
  $B3E0,$03 Take a length of #N$08 entries.
  $B3E3,$04 Use the object runtime table (#R$8259).
@ $B3E7 label=ProcessDisplayPointer_Loop
  $B3E7,$04 Use the character record as the schedule entry.
  $B3EB,$03 Call #R$A4CD to compare location data.
  $B3EE,$02 Go to #R$B3FD if the room fields match.
  $B3F0,$02 Step to the next display entry.
  $B3F2,$08 Go back to #R$B3E7 if this object word is not zero.
  $B3FA,$02 Restore the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $B3FC,$01 Return.

N $B3FD Restores the character, swaps the display pointers, and leaves a non-zero result.
@ $B3FD label=ProcessDisplayPointer_Next
  $B3FD,$02 Restore the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $B3FF,$03 Call #R$B405.
  $B402,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B404,$01 Return.

c $B405 Swap The Object Display Pointers
@ $B405 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers
E $B405 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7C23 }
. LIST#
D $B405 When the count is still set, swaps the two display pointers and reads the object flags.
  $B405,$03 Jump ahead to #R$B416 if the count is zero.
  $B408,$07 Read the two pointers at #R$B188 and #R$B17E+#N$0C.
  $B40F,$04 Store the pointer from #REGde at #R$B188.
  $B413,$03 Store the pointer from #REGhl at #R$B18A.
@ $B416 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_ExtractObjectAttributes
N $B416 Extract object attributes.
  $B416,$03 Call #R$A2BD to read the object's flags.
  $B419,$08 Jump ahead to #R$B426 when the saved display pointer at #R$B188 is set.
  $B421,$05 Take the display flag and continue at #R$B445.

N $B426 Step back to the object id and see whether it matches the schedule.
@ $B426 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_TestStepObjectMatches
  $B426,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B435 if this id does not match the schedule entry.
  $B42D,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B440 if this id matches the schedule entry.
  $B434,$01 Step back one entry.
  $B435,$06 Go to #R$B440 if this display byte does not match the schedule.
  $B43B,$05 Read the next display byte and compare it with #REGiy+#N$11.
@ $B440 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_ObjectAttributeContinue
  $B440,$05 Take the object attribute and continue at #R$B447 when the display byte does not match #REGiy+#N$11.
@ $B445 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_RunDisplayPointer
  $B445,$02 Flip bit 5 of the value.
@ $B447 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_TestBitClear
  $B447,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B451 if bit 5 is clear.
  $B44E,$03 Take #N$0812 for the display.
@ $B451 label=SwapObjectDisplayPointers_ObjectTablePointer
  $B451,$07 Use the object table pointer (#R$B17E+#N$04).
  $B458,$03 Take the display data pointer.
  $B45B,$06 Point #REGhl at the display flag at #R$B16D and call #R$B468 to copy six bytes.
  $B461,$07 Take the attribute count and point at the second display flag.

c $B468 Copy Six Display Bytes
@ $B468 label=CopySixDisplayBytes
D $B468 Copies six display bytes and reports failure when every copied byte is zero.
  $B468,$08 Save the count, take #N$00 as its high byte, and point #REGhl at the record.
  $B470,$07 Add the offset, copy #N$06 bytes and clear #REGa.
  $B477,$02 Count the #N$06 copied bytes.
@ $B479 label=CopySixDisplayBytes_TestCopied
  $B479,$04 Step back one byte, or that byte into #REGa, and repeat from #R$B479 for all
. six.
  $B47D,$02 Restore the pointer and the count from the stack.
  $B47F,$01 Return if the six copied bytes are all zero.
  $B480,$02 Mark this display flag as set.
  $B482,$01 Return.

c $B483 Copy The Character Into The Display
@ $B483 label=CopyCharacterDisplay
E $B483 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7CAC }
. LIST#
D $B483 Returns at once when the count is zero. Otherwise copies this character's id and script
. position into the display.
  $B483,$03 Return at once if the count in #REGb is zero.
  $B486,$06 Step to the next schedule entry.
  $B48C,$04 Copy this character's id to the byte at #REGhl.
  $B490,$05 Step to the next entry, read the script position and store it at the display
. flag.
  $B495,$01 Step back one byte.
  $B496,$03 Merge this character's id into the result.
  $B499,$02 Restore the schedule entry from the stack.
  $B49B,$01 Return when the character id and the script position are both clear.
  $B49C,$03 Count down one and step past this entry.
  $B49F,$01 Return.

c $B4A0 Dispatch Looked-Up Handler
E $B4A0 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7CC9 }
. LIST#
D $B4A0 Jumps to the handler the caller looked up.
  $B4A0,$02 Jump to the handler address in #REGiy.

g $B4A2 Display Setup Flag
@ $B4A2 label=DisplaySetupFlag
D $B4A2 Flag indicating whether the display has been set up (FF = not set up, 00 = set up).
. Read by #R$B308, #R$B34F and #R$B4A3.
B $B4A2,$01

c $B4A3 Check Flag And Set Display
@ $B4A3 label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay
D $B4A3 Sets up which room to display based on display flags and processes objects for that room.
  $B4A3,$06 Jump to #R$B4CF if the display setup flag is not #N$FF.
  $B4A9,$06 Record the display setup flag.
  $B4AF,$02,b$01 Check bit 7.
M $B4AF,$04 Go to #R$B4CF if bit 7 of the display flag is clear.
N $B4B3 Set up special display mode.
  $B4B3,$05 Write #N$FE to the room display parameter.
  $B4B8,$06 Write *#R$B172 to *#R$A02F.
  $B4BE,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B4C0,$01 Return.

c $B4C1 Find The First Noun
@ $B4C1 label=FindFirstNoun
E $B4C1 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7CCB }
. LIST#
D $B4C1 Finds the first noun. A value of #N$05 at #R$A00F takes the noun already prepared in the flag bytes. Otherwise, when bit 7 of #R$B173 is clear, it scans objects from #R$B17E with the handler at #R$D39B or #R$D36A.
  $B4C1,$07 Jump to #R$B4A3 if the display mode is #N$05.
  $B4C8,$03 Read the display mode flag.
  $B4CB,$02,b$01 Check bit 7.
M $B4CB,$04 Go to #R$B4D4 if bit 7 of the display mode flag is clear.
@ $B4CF label=FindFirstNoun_NoAction
  $B4CF,$04 Set the zero flag.
  $B4D3,$01 Return.
@ $B4D4 label=FindFirstNoun_ProcessObjects
  $B4D4,$06 Keep the schedule entry and read the object table pointer.
  $B4DA,$03 Read the object attribute value.
  $B4DD,$02,b$01 Keep only bit 0.
  $B4DF,$03 Store it in the object attribute flag.
  $B4E2,$05 Compare *#R$A00F with #N$05.
  $B4E7,$06 Go to #R$B4F1, keeping the picture record at #R$D39B, if the display mode is below
. #N$05.
  $B4ED,$04 Point at the routine at #R$D36A.
@ $B4F1 label=FindFirstNoun_ProcessLoop
  $B4F1,$03 Call #R$B4FD to follow the display pointer.
  $B4F4,$02 See whether the entry is the end marker.
  $B4F6,$04 Store the object pointer at #R$B17E.
  $B4FA,$02 Restore the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $B4FC,$01 Return.

c $B4FD Process Display Pointer
@ $B4FD label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay_ProcessPointer
E $B4FD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7CFC }
. LIST#
D $B4FD Runs the looked-up handler for each object that belongs in this picture.
  $B4FD,$03 Take the display data pointer.
  $B500,$03 Call #R$B4A0 to run the handler that was looked up.
  $B503,$03 Return if the entry is the end marker.
  $B506,$03 Record the room display parameter.
  $B509,$03 Call #R$B517 to check the objects.
  $B50C,$02 Jump back to #R$B4FD while an object matches this picture.
  $B50E,$06 Read the room display parameter and take the display flag.
  $B514,$02 Set bit 1 of the display flag.
  $B516,$01 Return.

c $B517 Check And Process Objects
@ $B517 label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay_CheckObjects
E $B517 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7A73 }
. LIST#
D $B517 Returns when the display flag at #R$A01C is zero. Otherwise walks the character's objects and returns non-zero when one matches the room display parameter.
  $B517,$05 Return if the display flag (#R$A01C) is zero.
  $B51C,$05 Keep the character record.
@ $B521 label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay_ObjectLoop
  $B521,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link.
  $B524,$04 See whether bit 6 of the schedule entry is clear.
  $B528,$02 Jump ahead to #R$B543 if bit 6 of the schedule entry is clear.
  $B52A,$03 Call #R$D04F to adjust the object table for this room.
@ $B52D label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay_ProcessObject
  $B52D,$07 Point #REGhl at byte #N$08 of the record (#REGiy+#N$08).
  $B534,$03 Call #R$D36A to check the next object.
  $B537,$04 Jump back to #R$B521 if the entry is the end marker.
  $B53B,$06 Jump back to #R$B52D while the object is not in the room on screen.
  $B541,$02 Clear the zero flag to report that an object in the room on screen was found.
@ $B543 label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplay_RestoreAndReturn
  $B543,$05 Restore the saved character record, the saved source and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $B548,$01 Return.

g $B549 Flags: Display
D $B549 Display flag beside #R$B54A.
. Read by #R$B32D, #R$B36B and #R$B54A.
B $B549,$01

c $B54A Check Flag And Set Display Parameter
@ $B54A label=CheckFlagAndSetDisplayParameter
D $B54A Sets the room graphics parameter to #N$FE and returns non-zero when the count at #R$B549 has reached #N$FF and bit 7 of #R$B179 is set. Otherwise returns with the zero flag set.
  $B54A,$06 Jump to #R$B576 if the byte at #R$B549 is not #N$FF.
  $B550,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$B549.
  $B553,$07 Go to #R$B576 if bit 7 of the display mode flag at #R$B179 is clear.
  $B55A,$08 Write #N$FE to the room graphics parameter and read the display mode word.
  $B562,$03 Record the display pointer.
  $B565,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B567,$01 Return.

c $B568 Match The Second Noun
@ $B568 label=MatchSecondNoun
E $B568 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7D17 }
. LIST#
D $B568 Matches the second noun, using the second set of variables: #R$A010, #R$B179, #R$B549, #R$B180, #R$A035 and #R$B178. The tests are the same shape as #R$B4C1.
  $B568,$07 Jump to #R$B54A if the display mode is #N$05.
  $B56F,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B57B if bit 7 of display mode flag (#R$B179) is clear.
@ $B576 label=MatchSecondNoun_Next
  $B576,$02 Mark the entry as finished.
  $B578,$02 See whether this byte is the end marker.
  $B57A,$01 Return.

N $B57B Read the pointer at #R$B180, then read the object attribute at #R$A01D.
@ $B57B label=MatchSecondNoun_SaveFrame
  $B57B,$06 Keep the schedule entry and read the pointer at #R$B180.
  $B581,$08 Read the object attribute value, keep the selected bits of #N$FF and store
. it in the object attribute flag.
  $B589,$05 Read the display mode.
  $B58E,$04 Point at the routine at #R$D39B.
  $B592,$02 Jump ahead to #R$B598 if display mode (#R$A010) is below #N$05.
  $B594,$04 Point at the routine at #R$D36A.
@ $B598 label=MatchSecondNoun_CheckEnd
  $B598,$03 Call #R$B5A4 to test for the end marker.
  $B59B,$08 See whether the entry is the end marker.
  $B5A3,$01 Return.

c $B5A4 Test For The End Marker
@ $B5A4 label=TestEndMarker
E $B5A4 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7D54 }
. LIST#
D $B5A4 Returns when the display byte is the end marker.
  $B5A4,$06 Call #R$B4A0 to run the handler that was looked up.
  $B5AA,$03 Return when the handler reports the end marker.
  $B5AD,$08 Record the room graphics parameter and set bit 1 of the display flag at #R$B16E.
  $B5B5,$01 Return.

c $B5B6 Unlink The Next Display Record
@ $B5B6 label=UnlinkNextDisplayRecord
D $B5B6 Unlinks the next display record and clears the pointer left in the previous entry.
  $B5B6,$07 Store it in the initialised flag and read the next display link.
  $B5BD,$04 Store that pointer in the first display link.
  $B5C1,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $B5C7,$03 Store the pointer in the next display link.
  $B5CA,$08 Clear the pointer in the previous display entry.
  $B5D2,$01 Return.
  $B5D3,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B610 if bit 0 of display flag (#R$B16E) is clear.
  $B5DA,$08 Jump ahead to #R$B5EF to check flag and set display parameter.
  $B5E2,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B5FC if bit 0 of display flag at #R$B16D is clear.
  $B5E9,$06 Read the byte at #R$B180+#N$02 and take the display data pointer.
@ $B5EF label=UnlinkNextDisplayRecord_ReadNext
  $B5EF,$06 Call #R$B83C to restore the saved character record, then call #R$B5B6 to read the next display link.
  $B5F5,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B5FB,$01 Return.

N $B5FC Calls the display-parameter check, using the pointer saved at #R$B17E+#N$08.
@ $B5FC label=UnlinkNextDisplayRecord_CallsDisplayParameter
  $B5FC,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B633 to plant the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode and test the character record.)
  $B5FF,$0A Call #R$B5B6 to read the next character record.
  $B609,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B60F,$01 Return.

N $B610 #HTML(Plant the <code>JR Z</code> test, push a zero pointer and #R$B172, test the character record through #R$B633, read the next record through #R$B5B6 and print the message at #R$687C.)
@ $B610 label=UnlinkNextDisplayRecord_ClearPointer
  $B610,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B62F to plant the <code>JR Z</code> opcode.)
  $B613,$08 Clear the pointer and take the display data pointer.
  $B61B,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B633 to plant the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode and test the character record.)
  $B61E,$0A Call #R$B5B6 to read the next character record.
  $B628,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B62E,$01 Return.

c $B62F Select Jump If Zero
@ $B62F label=SelectJumpIfZero
E $B62F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7E78 }
. LIST#
D $B62F #HTML(Plants the <code>JR Z</code> opcode for the two tests at #R$B645 and #R$B656.)
  $B62F,$04 #HTML(Use the <code>JR Z</code> opcode (#N$28) and go to #R$B635.)

c $B633 Select Jump If Not Zero
@ $B633 label=SelectJumpIfNotZero
E $B633 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7E7C }
. LIST#
D $B633 #HTML(Plants the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode for the two tests at #R$B645 and #R$B656, then reads the character record.)
  $B633,$02 #HTML(Use the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode (#N$20).)
@ $B635 label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_WriteOpcode
  $B635,$06 Write the opcode into the jumps at #R$B649 and #R$B658.
  $B63B,$04 Read the pointer at #R$B18C+#N$02.
  $B63F,$06 Read the handler address from bytes #N$04-#N$05 of the record.
  $B645,$06 Jump to #R$B64E if a marker on the character record is set. The opcode at
. #R$B649 is modified by #R$B635.
  $B64B,$03 Clear the pointer.
@ $B64E label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_LocationActionFlags
  $B64E,$01 Swap #REGhl with the return address.
  $B64F,$07 Save that address, and read the location and the action flags.
  $B656,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B65D if bit 5 is present. The opcode at #R$B658 is
. modified by #R$B635.
  $B65A,$03 Clear the pointer.
@ $B65D label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_ReturnWithPointers
  $B65D,$01 Leave the location pointer (or #N($0000,$04,$04) when bit 5 is clear) on the stack in place of the address saved there.
  $B65E,$01 Return to the caller (its address is now in #REGhl), leaving the location pointer and the handler address on the stack.
  $B65F,$07 Jump to #R$B671 if the display mode is #N$05.
  $B666,$04 Jump to #R$B671 when the display mode is #N$06.
  $B66A,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B678 if bit 7 of display mode flag at #R$B173 is clear.
@ $B671 label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_MessagePrintMessage
  $B671,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B677,$01 Return.

N $B678 Test the display flag, and use the display data pointer when that flag is set.
@ $B678 label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_TestDisplayFlag
  $B678,$08 Test bit 0 of the display flag and point #REGhl at the display data pointer.
N $B680 Build this display entry when the flag is clear.
  $B680,$03 Call #R$B6DA if bit 0 of this byte is set.
  $B683,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B633 to plant the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode and test the character record.)
  $B686,$06 Read the display flag and take the display data pointer.
@ $B68C label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_BitDisplayFlag
  $B68C,$09 Jump ahead to #R$B69D if bit 0 of display flag at #R$B16D is clear.
  $B695,$08 Call #R$B83C to restore the saved character record, then jump to #R$B6A6 with the message at #N$05B3.

N $B69D Clear the stacked pointers, then print the message at #N$05DB.
@ $B69D label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_ClearStackedPointers
  $B69D,$06 Push three zero words for the message.
  $B6A3,$03 Take the message at #N$05DB.
@ $B6A6 label=SelectJumpIfNotZero_MessagePrintMessageAgain
  $B6A6,$07 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B6AD,$01 Return.
  $B6AE,$07 Jump to #R$B671 if the display mode is #N$05.
  $B6B5,$04 Jump to #R$B671 when the display mode is #N$06.
  $B6B9,$07 Jump back to #R$B671 while bit 7 of display mode flag (#R$B179) is present.
  $B6C0,$08 Take the display flag and take the display mode flag.
N $B6C8 Build the alternate display entry.
  $B6C8,$03 Call #R$B6F0 if bit 0 of this byte is set.
  $B6CB,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B62F to plant the <code>JR Z</code> opcode.)
  $B6CE,$08 Jump back to #R$B68C to check flag and set display parameter.

g $B6D6 Word: Display Pointers
D $B6D6 Two display pointers. #R$B768 reads the character record from the first, and #R$B760
. reads the saved display pointer from the second.
. Read by #R$B6FD and #R$B77F.
W $B6D6,$02
W $B6D8,$02 Read by #R$B6FD.

c $B6DA Process Display Entry
@ $B6DA label=ProcessDisplayEntry
D $B6DA Builds the display entry, and returns at once when that entry is empty.
  $B6DA,$04 Return if there is no location display text.
  $B6DE,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $B6DF,$03 Call #R$B83C.
  $B6E2,$03 #HTML(Call #R$B633 to plant the <code>JR NZ</code> opcode and test the character record.)

c $B6E5 Print Object Message
D $B6E5 Prints the message at #R$689C+#N$06, then returns into the handler whose address is held at #R$B185+#N$01.
  $B6E5,$0A Push the handler address at #R$B185+#N$01, print the message at #R$689C+#N$06 and return into that handler.
  $B6EF,$01 Return.

c $B6F0 Process Display Entry Alternate
@ $B6F0 label=ProcessDisplayEntryAlternate
D $B6F0 Builds the display entry, and prints the object line when the entry is present.
  $B6F0,$04 Return if there is no location display text.
  $B6F4,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $B6F5,$03 Call #R$B83C.
  $B6F8,$05 Call #R$B62F and jump back to #R$B6E5 to run the display entry.

c $B6FD Process Location Display
@ $B6FD label=ProcessLocationDisplay
R $B6FD HL Location pointer
D $B6FD Clears the display mark, saves the location pointer, and reads the character's room.
  $B6FD,$04 Clear the display.
  $B701,$06 Call #R$D33C to fetch current character location.
  $B707,$06 Read the word at offset #N$07 of this character record.
  $B70D,$02 See whether the pointer is empty.
  $B70F,$01 Return when this character has no script pointer.
  $B710,$03 Point #REGix at the script.
  $B713,$06 Write #N($0000,$04,$04) to *#R$B6D6.
  $B719,$04 Use the buffer: co-ordinate data at #R$AAAC.
@ $B71D label=ProcessLocationDisplay_Loop
  $B71D,$08 Call #R$A2AB to clear buffer.
@ $B725 label=ProcessLocationDisplay_RunTextToken
  $B725,$04 Return if there is no further text token.
  $B729,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B75A if it is #N$50.
  $B730,$04 Jump back to #R$B725 while it is not #N$60.
  $B734,$09 Store the word pointer at offset #N$02 and call #R$B77F to read the text.
  $B73D,$01 Return if there is no further text token.
  $B73E,$07 Jump ahead to #R$B75A if it is #N$50.
  $B745,$04 Jump back to #R$B71D while it is not #N$60.
  $B749,$09 Store the word pointer at offset #N$04 and call #R$B77F to read the text.
  $B752,$01 Return if there is no further text token.
  $B753,$07 Jump back to #R$B71D while it is not #N$50.
@ $B75A label=ProcessLocationDisplay_Next
  $B75A,$06 Store #REGde at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01.
  $B760,$03 Read the display pointers.
  $B763,$05 Jump back to #R$B71D while the room fields do not match.
  $B768,$07 Call #R$A1AF to follow the record pointer.
  $B76F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B771,$01 Return.

c $B772 Read Dictionary High Nibble
@ $B772 label=ReadDictionaryHighNibble
D $B772 Reads the high nibble of the dictionary byte at the token's index.
R $B772 D Character code
R $B772 O:A Attribute value (bits 4-7)
  $B772,$04 Take the dictionary index.
  $B776,$02,b$01 Keep the dictionary page (the low nibble of the token's high byte) as the high byte of the index.
  $B778,$01 Store the result in the high byte of the source.
  $B779,$01 Add the token offset in #REGde to the dictionary base.
  $B77A,$01 Read the dictionary byte this index points at.
  $B77B,$02,b$01 Keep the high nibble of the dictionary byte, which is the attribute value.
  $B77D,$01 Return.

g $B77E Flags: Display
D $B77E Display flag beside the character-attribute routine.
. Read by #R$B6FD and #R$B77F.
B $B77E,$01

c $B77F Process Text Token Stream
@ $B77F label=ProcessTextTokenStream
R $B77F O:F Zero flag set if end of stream reached
D $B77F Walks the text tokens while the display flag is not the end marker.
  $B77F,$07 Jump to #R$B7AA if the display is not #N$FF.
@ $B786 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_Loop
  $B786,$06 Return if the pointer at #R$B6D6 is zero.
  $B78C,$07 Clear the display.
  $B793,$03 Read the low byte saved ahead of this record.
  $B796,$06 Record the display pointers.
  $B79C,$03 Read the record's flag byte.
  $B79F,$06 Call #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.
  $B7A5,$05 Step on two bytes and point #REGix at that address.
@ $B7AA label=ProcessTextTokenStream_BitRecordFlag
  $B7AA,$09 Jump ahead to #R$B7CD if bit 7 of the record's flag byte is clear, and step
. through the text token.
  $B7B3,$06 Keep this token and read the next byte as its parameter.
  $B7B9,$02,b$01 Keep the token type (bits 4-6) for the checks below.
  $B7BB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B7C6 if the record flag is #N$60.
  $B7BF,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B7C6 if the record flag is #N$30.
  $B7C3,$03 Return if the record flag is not #N$20.
@ $B7C6 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_Next
  $B7C6,$05 Write #N$FF to the display.
  $B7CB,$01 Clear the carry so a blank character ends this token.
  $B7CC,$01 Return.

N $B7CD Treats a blank as a break and a vocabulary code as a common word.
@ $B7CD label=ProcessTextTokenStream_TestCharacterClass
  $B7CD,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B7F7 if the character code is below #N$20.
  $B7D1,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B7DD if the character code is below #N$60.
N $B7D5 Get common word.
  $B7D5,$03 Call #R$A840 to look up this common word.
  $B7D8,$02 Set the found marker.
  $B7DA,$02 Leave #REGa clear. The flags still show that the word was found.
  $B7DC,$01 Return.

N $B7DD Keep the token's low five bits and add one for the letter count.
@ $B7DD label=ProcessTextTokenStream_TestKeepBitsCount
  $B7DD,$02,b$01 Take the letter count of this packed word from bits 0-4.
  $B7DF,$08 Set #REGb to the token length plus one, point #REGhl at the byte after #REGix, and clear #REGc.
N $B7E7 Rotate and extract character code.
  $B7E7,$03 Call #R$A990 to unpack the next letter.
  $B7EA,$02 Count the next character of the message and repeat from #R$B7E7.
  $B7EC,$07 Jump back to #R$B7AA while the count is not zero.
  $B7F3,$04 Step back one byte and continue at #R$B7AA.

N $B7F7 Command 2 adds the following offset to the text pointer.
@ $B7F7 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_CommandTwo
  $B7F7,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B807 if the command is not #N$02.
  $B7FB,$08 Read the flag word and add it to the record pointer.
@ $B803 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_TestRecordFlagStep
  $B803,$04 Step back one byte and read the next token from #R$B7AA.

N $B807 Command 11 saves the text pointer and adds the following offset.
@ $B807 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_CommandEleven
  $B807,$04 Go to #R$B834 when the text command is not #N$0B.
  $B80B,$08 Read the record's flag byte and read the moved marker.
  $B813,$06 Call #R$A11C to allocate #N$02 bytes.
  $B819,$07 Store the link word at the start of the record and keep it.
  $B820,$03 Read the display pointers.
  $B823,$07 Remember this record at #R$B6D6 and store the previous pointer's low byte just ahead of it.
  $B82A,$03 Store the high byte of the previous pointer just ahead of this record.
  $B82D,$01 Restore the pointer from the stack.
  $B82E,$06 Add the offset, copy it into the record, and go to #R$B803.

N $B834 A control below 29 goes back to the main text walk.
@ $B834 label=ProcessTextTokenStream_TestControlBelowGoes
  $B834,$05 Jump back to #R$B7AA if the character is below #N$1D.
  $B839,$03 Jump back to #R$B786 to read the display pointers and take the low byte of
. the display pointers.

c $B83C Restore The Saved Character Record
@ $B83C label=RestoreSavedCharacterRecord
D $B83C Restores the saved character record for the caller.
  $B83C,$02 Restore #REGix from the stack.
  $B83E,$02 Count #N$03 words.
@ $B840 label=RestoreSavedCharacterRecord_ReadWords
  $B840,$08 Read each counted word onto the stack, then take the last one as the pointer.
  $B848,$02 Exchange #REGhl with the return address and push #REGhl.
  $B84A,$02 Jump to the handler address in #REGix.
  $B84C,$08 Push the pointers at #R$B18A and #R$B188.
  $B854,$08 Push the pointers at #R$B185+#N$01 and #R$B18C.
  $B85C,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $B862,$01 Return.
  $B863,$03 Jump ahead to #R$C929 to draw this object.

g $B866 Word: Saved Character Record
D $B866 Character record pointer kept while its object attribute is read.
. Written by #R$B911 and read by #R$B96B, #R$B9BA and #R$B9CA.
W $B866,$02

g $B868 Word: Saved Record Flag
D $B868 Flag word copied from the character record while its link is followed.
. Written by #R$B87A and read by #R$B894 and #R$B8AA.
W $B868,$02

c $B86A Follow The Link Pointer
@ $B86A label=FollowLinkPointer
D $B86A Reads the link pointer beside the character lists and follows it.
  $B86A,$08 Point at the link pointer, then call #R$A0F7 to fetch object pointer from
. stack.
  $B872,$02 Go to #R$B8A8 if the object pointer is zero.
  $B874,$06 Read the record's flag byte and read the moved marker.
  $B87A,$04 Store that flag word at #R$B868.
  $B87E,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $B884,$03 Record the character script entry list.
  $B887,$04 Call #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.
  $B88B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B894 if the saved result is zero.
  $B88F,$05 Go to #R$B8A8 if the display list was rebuilt.
@ $B894 label=FollowLinkPointer_CopyLink
  $B894,$08 Read the word at #R$B868 and store it in the word at #R$A02B.
@ $B89C label=FollowLinkPointer_Call
  $B89C,$07 Jump back to #R$B89C while it is not #N$C0.
  $B8A3,$05 Write #N$90 through the pointer at #R$A029.
@ $B8A8 label=FollowLinkPointer_HandResultCaller
  $B8A8,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $B8A9,$01 Return.

c $B8AA Read The Pointer At The First Display
@ $B8AA label=ReadPointerFirstDisplay
D $B8AA Reads the word at #R$B868 and stores it in the word at #R$A02B.
  $B8AA,$07 Read the word at #R$B868, store it in the word at #R$A02B and clear #REGa.
  $B8B1,$03 Clear the command mode at #R$A024.
  $B8B4,$07 Read the first display link and clear the pointer.
  $B8BB,$03 Store the pointer in the first display link.
@ $B8BE label=ReadPointerFirstDisplay_PrepareCharacterDisplay
  $B8BE,$05 Go to #R$B8C7 if #R$AAB2 returns zero.
  $B8C3,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B904 if it is not #N$02.
@ $B8C7 label=ReadPointerFirstDisplay_ObjectProcessingSource
  $B8C7,$03 Read the word at #R$A02B.
  $B8CA,$07 Jump back to #R$B8BE while the high nibble is not #N$C0.
  $B8D1,$07 Use #N$08 for the character code, then call #R$A11C to allocate memory.
  $B8D8,$07 Read the list head and make this record the new head.
  $B8DF,$03 Store the low byte of the old list head in the link word ahead of this record.
  $B8E2,$06 Store the high byte of the old list head in the link word ahead of this record, and read the word at #R$9FEE+#N$02.
  $B8E8,$03 Store the low byte of the first display link at offset #N$05.
  $B8EB,$07 Store the high byte of that link at offset #N$06 and keep the stacked pointer as the first display link.
  $B8F2,$07 Clear the moved marker and read the room display parameter.
  $B8F9,$03 Store the record's flag byte.
  $B8FC,$07 Copy #R$A024 to offset 7.
  $B903,$01 Return.

N $B904 Reads the first display link, then stores the saved pointer back into that link.
@ $B904 label=ReadPointerFirstDisplay_ReadsFirstDisplay
  $B904,$06 Call #R$D87D to read the first display link.
  $B90A,$03 Store the pointer in the first display link.
  $B90D,$01 Return.

c $B90E Read The Object Attribute
D $B90E Finds the character being processed and remembers that record while it reads the object attribute.
  $B90E,$03 Call #R$B91F to check the character being processed.
  $B911,$07 Save this character record at #R$B866 and read offset #N$05.
  $B918,$03 Read offset #N$06 of that record.
  $B91B,$03 Point #REGiy at that word.
  $B91E,$01 Return.

c $B91F Check The Character Being Processed
@ $B91F label=CheckCharacterBeingProcessed
R $B91F O:F Zero flag set if object found, bit 0 set if not found
D $B91F Compares the character being processed with each display link. The zero flag is set when one matches.
  $B91F,$04 Point at the first display link.
@ $B923 label=CheckCharacterBeingProcessed_FetchObjectPointer
  $B923,$05 Go to #R$B931 if the object pointer is zero.
  $B928,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $B92B,$03 See whether it matches the record flag.
  $B92E,$01 Return if they match.
  $B92F,$02 Jump back to #R$B923 to check object in a036.

@ $B931 label=CheckCharacterBeingProcessed_Succeed
  $B931,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B933,$01 Return.

g $B934 Flags: Display
D $B934 Display flag beside the script display.
. Read by #R$B936, #R$CBA7 and #R$CC82.
B $B934,$01
B $B935,$01 Read by #R$B936.

c $B936 Process Script Display
@ $B936 label=ProcessScriptDisplay
D $B936 Saves the character record and reads the object attribute for this script display.
  $B936,$05 Save the character record, the schedule entry and the pointer.
  $B93B,$03 Record the result in the display.
@ $B93E label=ProcessScriptDisplay_Loop
  $B93E,$03 Call #R$B90E to read the object attribute.
  $B941,$08 Jump to #R$BA20 if byte #N$07 of the character record is #N$02.
  $B949,$07 Jump to #R$B98C if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $B950,$03 Read the display.
  $B953,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$B934.
  $B956,$03 Read the character's room.
  $B959,$03 Record the result in the display flag at #R$A01C.
  $B95C,$03 Read the action flags.
  $B95F,$0A Call #R$B191 to initialise display buffer.
  $B969,$02 Go to #R$B9B4 if no room record matches.
  $B96B,$08 Read the saved character record and the object-table pointer.
  $B973,$06 Store it in the action flags and store it in the attribute type.
  $B979,$0A Store the display flag (#R$A01C) and call #R$B9CA if that flag is zero.
@ $B983 label=ProcessScriptDisplay_Store
  $B983,$02 Take #N$01.
@ $B985 label=ProcessScriptDisplay_BringSavedPointer
  $B985,$05 Restore the saved pointer, the saved schedule entry and the saved character record from the stack.
  $B98A,$01 Clear the borrow so an empty entry returns as a failed match.
  $B98B,$01 Return.

N $B98C Read the character's room, then record the result in the saved character, then
. read the action flags.
@ $B98C label=ProcessScriptDisplay_Next
  $B98C,$06 Read the character's room, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $B992,$03 Record the saved character.
  $B995,$06 Read the action flags, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $B99B,$03 Record the room display parameter.
  $B99E,$06 Read the next parameter and call #R$CEDE to resolve it.
  $B9A4,$06 Store the room graphics parameter at #R$A035 and call #R$B9FA.
  $B9AA,$02 Jump back to #R$B983 to take #N$01.

N $B9AC Follows the link until it runs out, then clears the result.
@ $B9AC label=ProcessScriptDisplay_FollowsLinkRuns
  $B9AC,$05 Call #R$B9CA and jump back to #R$B9AC while it returns non-zero.
@ $B9B1 label=ProcessScriptDisplay_Zero
  $B9B1,$03 Jump back to #R$B985 to see whether it is zero.

N $B9B4 Read the display flag, then take the saved character record.
@ $B9B4 label=ProcessScriptDisplay_DisplayFlagSaved
  $B9B4,$06 Jump to #R$B9AC if the display is zero.
  $B9BA,$08 Read the saved character record and clear its location.
  $B9C2,$05 Go to #R$B9B1 if the link pointer is zero.
  $B9C7,$03 Go back to #R$B93E for the next character.

c $B9CA Copy The Link Ahead Of This Record
@ $B9CA label=CopyLinkAhead
D $B9CA Reads the saved character record and copies the link ahead of it into the object attribute.
  $B9CA,$04 Read the saved character record.
@ $B9CE label=CopyLinkAhead_StoreLink
  $B9CE,$06 Read the link word stored before the record at #REGiy.
  $B9D4,$03 Store the low byte of the link at offset #N$05.
  $B9D7,$05 Store the high byte of the link at offset #N$06 and keep #REGix.
  $B9DC,$04 Copy the memory-link record from #REGiy into #REGix.
  $B9E0,$05 Call #R$A1C7 to follow this memory link.
  $B9E5,$04 Jump ahead to #R$B9F5 if the pointer is #N($0000,$04,$04).
  $B9E9,$09 Point #REGiy at #REGhl and jump to #R$B9CE if bit 6 of #REGiy+#N$01 is set.
  $B9F2,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $B9F4,$01 Return.

N $B9F5 Copies the pointer and clears the result.
@ $B9F5 label=CopyLinkAhead_ClearAndReturn
  $B9F5,$03 Call #R$B9FA to copy pointer to stack.
  $B9F8,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $B9F9,$01 Return.

c $B9FA Copy Pointer To Stack
@ $B9FA label=CopyPointerToStack
D $B9FA Copies pointer To Stack.
  $B9FA,$0A Call #R$A104 to step pointer and compare.
  $BA04,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $BA0A,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $BA0D,$06 Jump back to #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.

g $BA13 Word: Display Pointer
D $BA13 Pointer used while copying a character display.
. Read by #R$BDC2 and #R$BE3C.
  $BA13,$0D

c $BA20 Process Character Display
@ $BA20 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay
D $BA20 Reads the character being processed and clears its script list for ids #N$02 and #N$03.
  $BA20,$06 Read the character being processed and take the workspace byte at #R$BA67.
  $BA26,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BA31 if the character being processed is #N$03.
  $BA2A,$07 Jump ahead to #R$BA34 if the character being processed is not #N$02.
@ $BA31 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_Next
  $BA31,$03 Call #R$BA3E to clear the character-script list.
@ $BA34 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_Store
  $BA34,$02 Take #N$01.
  $BA36,$05 Restore #REGhl, #REGiy and #REGix from the stack.
  $BA3B,$01 Clear the carry before marking this character display as finished.
  $BA3C,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $BA3D,$01 Return.

c $BA3E Record The Result In The Character Script
@ $BA3E label=RecordResultCharacterScript
D $BA3E Clears the character-script list, then reads the script address stored on this character.
  $BA3E,$06 Clear the character script entry list.
  $BA44,$03 Read the object attribute.
  $BA47,$0A Call #R$D1DB to jump to handler.
  $BA51,$07 Call #R$A1AF to follow the record pointer.
  $BA58,$07 Call #R$B9FA to copy pointer to stack.
  $BA5F,$01 Return.
B $BA60,$01 State byte for this character display. Written by #R$BA67 and #R$BB4D.
. Read by #R$BAAB and #R$BB16.
B $BA61,$01 Second state byte, written beside #R$BA60 by #R$BA67 and #R$BB46.
. Read by #R$BAB2.
B $BA62,$01 First byte of the object id kept while the display is built.
. Read by #R$BAC1 and #R$BAFA.
B $BA63,$01 Second byte of that object id. With #R$BA64 it is also read as a word.
. Read by #R$BAC9.
B $BA64,$01 Third byte of that object id. #R$BAD1 compares it with the record.
B $BA65,$01 Low byte of the saved character record. Written by #R$BA7F and read by
. #R$BB5C.
B $BA66,$01 High byte of the saved character record, stored with #R$BA65.
  $BA67,$06 Clear both state bytes at #R$BA60 and #R$BA61.
@ $BA6D label=RecordResultCharacterScript_FollowingDisplay
N $BA6D A following display value is not #N$FF.
  $BA6D,$06 Call #R$BD7E and jump to #R$BBBE if a following display value is not #N$FF.
  $BA73,$02 Go to #R$BAA6 if the room's object list is empty.
  $BA75,$05 Go to #R$BA85 if the object table ends before the moved marker.
  $BA7A,$02 Take #N$01.
  $BA7C,$03 Call #R$BB46 to save the object state.
  $BA7F,$06 Save the character record at #R$BA65 and jump ahead to #R$BAAB.

N $BA85 Leave #N$02 as the result, then see whether bit 5 of the action flags is set,
. then check for character id 50.
@ $BA85 label=RecordResultCharacterScript_LeaveResultBit
  $BA85,$05 Go to #R$BA9D if the object table ends before the moved marker.
  $BA8A,$08 Take #N$02 and jump to #R$BA7C if bit 5 of the action flags is set.
N $BA92 Get character attribute.
  $BA92,$03 Call #R$D29D to read the character's attribute.
N $BA95 Is the attribute below #N$32?
  $BA95,$06 Go to #R$BA7C with #REGa=#N$03 if the value from #R$D29D is below #N$32.
  $BA9B,$02 The attribute is #N$32 or more, so skip the next test and follow the character link at #R$BAA6.

N $BA9D Reads the character's attribute. A value below #N$46 selects picture #N$04; otherwise the character
. link is followed.
@ $BA9D label=RecordResultCharacterScript_ReadAttribute
N $BA9D Get character attribute.
  $BA9D,$03 Call #R$D29D to read the character's attribute.
N $BAA0 Is the attribute below #N$46?
  $BAA0,$06 Go to #R$BA7C with #REGa=#N$04 if the value from #R$D29D is below #N$46.
@ $BAA6 label=RecordResultCharacterScript_FollowCharacterLink
  $BAA6,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link.
  $BAA9,$02 Go back to #R$BA6D when the character link is still set.
@ $BAAB label=RecordResultCharacterScript_StateByteZero
  $BAAB,$07 Jump to #R$BB3F if the state byte at #R$BA60 is zero.
  $BAB2,$06 Jump to #R$BB16 if the state byte at #R$BA61 is zero.
  $BAB8,$04 Point at the object display link.
@ $BABC label=RecordResultCharacterScript_Loop
  $BABC,$05 Go to #R$BAF5 if the object pointer is zero.
  $BAC1,$08 Jump back to #R$BABC while this id does not match the record's flag byte.
  $BAC9,$08 Jump back to #R$BABC while this id does not match the moved marker.
  $BAD1,$08 Jump back to #R$BABC while this id does not match the location.
  $BAD9,$04 Save the schedule entry and the character record.
  $BADD,$05 Use the saved record as the source and point #REGiy at #R$9FF0+#N$04.
N $BAE2 Step pointer and compare.
  $BAE2,$03 Call #R$A104 to compare the pointer with the next record.
  $BAE5,$06 Copy the pointer into the previous character record.
  $BAEB,$08 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous character record.
  $BAF3,$02 Jump ahead to #R$BAFA to read the saved display pointer.

N $BAF5 Ask for five bytes, then read the saved display pointer.
@ $BAF5 label=RecordResultCharacterScript_AskFiveBytes
  $BAF5,$05 Take #N$05, then call #R$A11C to allocate memory.
@ $BAFA label=RecordResultCharacterScript_SavedDisplayDisplay
  $BAFA,$06 Read the object id saved at #R$BA62.
  $BB00,$03 Store the record's flag byte.
  $BB03,$06 Store the moved marker and store the location.
  $BB09,$03 Read the saved display link.
  $BB0C,$07 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous character record.
  $BB13,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous character record.
@ $BB16 label=RecordResultCharacterScript_StateByte
  $BB16,$07 Jump to #R$BB5C if the state byte at #R$BA60 is #N$01.
  $BB1D,$07 Call #R$D29D to read the character attribute and jump to #R$BB3F when it is below #N$19.
  $BB24,$07 Point at the workspace byte at #R$BA62, then call #R$BD06 to run object
. pointer.
  $BB2B,$02 Go to #R$BB3F if no differing object pointer remains.
  $BB2D,$06 Take the count and call #R$A4BC to point to object attribute byte.
  $BB33,$04 Call #R$A4BC to point to object attribute byte, saving pointer.
  $BB37,$07 Take the police message, then call #R$A59F to print the police message.
  $BB3E,$01 Return.

N $BB3F Use the police as the pointer.
@ $BB3F label=RecordResultCharacterScript_PolicePointer
  $BB3F,$06 Take the police message, then call #R$A59F to print the police message.
  $BB45,$01 Return.

c $BB46 Save The Object State
@ $BB46 label=SaveObjectState
D $BB46 Records the result in #R$BA60, sets the byte at #R$BA61, and copies three object bytes from #R$BA13+#N$06 to #R$BA62.
  $BB46,$07 Set the byte at #R$BA61, keeping #REGa.
  $BB4D,$03 Store it in the workspace byte at #R$BA60.
  $BB50,$06 Point #REGhl at #R$BA13+#N$06 and #REGde at #R$BA62.
  $BB56,$03 Take a length of #N$03 bytes.
  $BB59,$02 Copy the counted bytes.
  $BB5B,$01 Return.

c $BB5C Read The Action Flags
@ $BB5C label=ReadActionFlags
D $BB5C Reads the saved character record and dispatches through its action handler.
  $BB5C,$0A Read the saved character record and the handler stored in it.
  $BB66,$01 Jump to the handler address in #REGhl.
  $BB67,$06 Store #REGa in the flag at #R$BA13+#N$0A and the count at #R$BA13+#N$09.
@ $BB6D label=ReadActionFlags_CalculateObjectPointer
N $BB6D A following display value is not #N$FF.
  $BB6D,$06 Call #R$BD7E and jump to #R$BBBE if a following display value is not #N$FF.
  $BB73,$05 Go to #R$BB7C if the object is not a moving marker.
  $BB78,$04 Count one more at #R$BA13+#N$09.
@ $BB7C label=ReadActionFlags_FollowCharacterLink
  $BB7C,$03 Call #R$AEA9 to follow the character link.
  $BB7F,$02 Go back to #R$BB6D when the character link is still set.
  $BB81,$06 Jump to #R$BB8E if the count at #R$BA13+#N$09 is set.
  $BB87,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $BB8D,$01 Return.

N $BB8E Tests the flag at #R$BA13+#N$0A.
@ $BB8E label=ReadActionFlags_SavedFlag
  $BB8E,$06 Jump to #R$BBC9 if the flag at #R$BA13+#N$0A is zero.
  $BB94,$09 Point #REGix at the record at #R$BA13+#N$0B and call #R$BE5A.
@ $BB9D label=ReadActionFlags_ActionFlags
  $BB9D,$09 Jump to #R$BBB1 if the top two bits of the action flags are #N$80.
  $BBA6,$06 Jump to #R$BBB1 if bit 3 of the action flags is set.
  $BBAC,$05 Call #R$BE72 to count down this repeating entry.

N $BBB1 Print the message before returning.
@ $BBB1 label=ReadActionFlags_PrintMessageBefore
  $BBB1,$04 Restore the record from the stack and go to #R$BBC4 if bit 3 of the action flags is set.
  $BBB5,$03 Point #REGhl at the message at #R$722D+#N$68.
@ $BBB8 label=ReadActionFlags_KeepCharacterRecord
  $BBB8,$05 Keep the character record, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $BBBD,$01 Return.

N $BBBE Use the message as the pointer.
@ $BBBE label=ReadActionFlags_MessagePointer
  $BBBE,$06 Jump back to #R$A59F to print this message.

N $BBC4 Use the message as the pointer.
@ $BBC4 label=ReadActionFlags_Next
  $BBC4,$05 Point at this message and print it from #R$BBB8.

N $BBC9 Read the count, then clear the outstanding
. borrow.
@ $BBC9 label=ReadActionFlags_CountClearOutstanding
  $BBC9,$06 Call #R$BC46 to run game status entries, then call #R$BC9C to run game
. status entries alternate.
  $BBCF,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BBE5 if the count is not zero.
  $BBD3,$06 Jump ahead to #R$BBDE if the count is zero.
  $BBD9,$05 Point at this message and print it from #R$BBB8.

N $BBDE Use the message as the pointer.
@ $BBDE label=ReadActionFlags_MessagePointerAgain
  $BBDE,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $BBE4,$01 Return.

c $BBE5 Print Text Based On Flag
@ $BBE5 label=PrintTextBasedOnFlag
D $BBE5 Prints the common-word lead-in, and a second message when the flag is not #N$01.
  $BBE5,$08 Take the common words and call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $BBED,$08 Call #R$A59F if the character is not #N$01.
  $BBF5,$06 Jump to #R$BC1A if the action state flag is clear.
  $BBFB,$06 Clear the action-taken flag.
  $BC01,$03 Point #REGhl at the message at #R$722D+#N$B4.
N $BC04 Print text and handle control characters.
  $BC04,$03 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $BC07,$06 Read the message address from bytes #N$04-#N$05 of the record.
  $BC0D,$05 Jump to #R$A59F to print this text when #REGhl is not zero.
  $BC12,$08 Print a quotation mark and a full stop.

N $BC1A Read the handler address and jump to it.
@ $BC1A label=PrintTextBasedOnFlag_Next
  $BC1A,$06 Read the handler address into #REGhl.
  $BC20,$01 Jump to the handler address in #REGhl.

c $BC21 Check Object Attribute
@ $BC21 label=CheckObjectAttribute
D $BC21 Returns zero unless #R$BD51 passes and the top two bits of the action flags are #N$80. A match is stored at #R$BA1B+#N$03 and passed to #R$BCC9.
  $BC21,$05 Go to #R$BC2F if the object table ends before the moved marker.
  $BC26,$09 Jump to #R$BC31 if the top two bits of the action flags are #N$80.
@ $BC2F label=CheckObjectAttribute_Next
  $BC2F,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $BC30,$01 Return.

N $BC31 Stores this record and tests it.
@ $BC31 label=CheckObjectAttribute_StoreRecord
  $BC31,$07 Store this record at #R$BA13+#N$0B and call #R$BCC9.
  $BC38,$02 Go to #R$BC43 if the attribute needs no extra bit handling.
  $BC3A,$05 Write #N$01 to the flag at #R$BA13+#N$0A.
  $BC3F,$04 Set the action-pending flag.
@ $BC43 label=CheckObjectAttribute_TestActionSucceeded
  $BC43,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $BC45,$01 Return.

c $BC46 Process Game Status Entries
@ $BC46 label=ProcessGameStatusEntries
D $BC46 See whether this byte is the end marker, then test the mark on this byte
  $BC46,$08 Use the character pairs (#R$994D+#N$18).
@ $BC4E label=ProcessGameStatusEntries_Loop
  $BC4E,$07 Jump to #R$BC68 if the record's flag byte is #N$FF.
  $BC55,$06 Jump to #R$BC63 if the action-pending flag is clear.
  $BC5B,$06 Jump to #R$BC63 if a marker on the action flags is set.
  $BC61,$05 Call #R$BE5A, keeping character record.
  $BC66,$02 Jump back to #R$BC4E to read the record flag, see whether it is the end marker and
. jump to #R$BC68.

N $BC68 Keep the match flags before returning.
@ $BC68 label=ProcessGameStatusEntries_KeepMatchFlags
  $BC68,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $BC69,$02 See whether the pointer is empty.
  $BC6B,$01 Return when the status pointer is empty.
  $BC6C,$06 Call #R$BCC9 to check bit and process.
  $BC72,$02 Go back to #R$BC68 for the next status entry.

c $BC74 Process Game Status Entries With Counter
@ $BC74 label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesWithCounter
D $BC74 Walks the character pairs at #R$994D+#N$18 and counts the entries that still have an action pending.
  $BC74,$06 Use the character pairs (#R$994D+#N$18).
@ $BC7A label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesWithCounter_Loop
  $BC7A,$07 Jump to #R$BC96 if the record's flag byte is #N$FF.
  $BC81,$06 Jump to #R$BC91 if the action-pending flag is clear.
  $BC87,$05 Go to #R$BC91 if the attribute needs no extra bit handling.
  $BC8C,$05 Remember this record at #R$BA13+#N$0B and count one more.
@ $BC91 label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesWithCounter_Next
  $BC91,$05 Jump back to #R$BC7A to read the record flag, see whether it is the end marker and
. jump to #R$BC96.

N $BC96 Returns the count and the record saved for this pass.
@ $BC96 label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesWithCounter_ReturnCount
  $BC96,$05 Take the count in #REGa and the record at #R$BA13+#N$0B.
  $BC9B,$01 Return.

c $BC9C Process Game Status Entries Alternate
@ $BC9C label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesAlternate
D $BC9C Walks those character pairs and skips an entry whose action is still pending.
  $BC9C,$06 Use the character pairs (#R$994D+#N$18).
@ $BCA2 label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesAlternate_Loop
  $BCA2,$07 Jump to #R$BCC4 if the record's flag byte is #N$FF.
  $BCA9,$06 Jump to #R$BCBD if the action-pending flag is clear.
  $BCAF,$05 Go to #R$BCBD if bit 2 is set and the entry check succeeds.
  $BCB4,$05 Jump ahead to #R$BCBD if the count is not zero.
  $BCB9,$04 Store this record at #R$BA13+#N$0B.
@ $BCBD label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesAlternate_Next
  $BCBD,$05 Call #R$BE72 to run entry with counter.
  $BCC2,$02 Jump back to #R$BCA2 for the next entry.

N $BCC4 Read the working value before returning.
@ $BCC4 label=ProcessGameStatusEntriesAlternate_SavedRecord
  $BCC4,$04 Point #REGix at the record saved at #R$BA13+#N$0B.
  $BCC8,$01 Return.

c $BCC9 Check Bit And Process
@ $BCC9 label=CheckBitAndProcess
D $BCC9 Tests the action flags and runs #R$BF08 when bit 2 is set. Returns non-zero when that entry is accepted, and zero when it is not.
  $BCC9,$06 Jump to #R$BCDA if a marker on the action flags is clear.
  $BCCF,$07 Call #R$BF08 to test bit and process entry.
  $BCD6,$04 Go to #R$BD03 if the entry check returns zero.
@ $BCDA label=CheckBitAndProcess_Next
  $BCDA,$06 Jump to #R$BCE6 if a marker on the action flags is clear.
  $BCE0,$06 Set the action-pending flag and jump ahead to #R$BD01.

N $BCE6 Mark the action taken and clear the bit that would repeat it.
@ $BCE6 label=CheckBitAndProcess_MarkActionTaken
  $BCE6,$08 Mark the action taken so the script will not repeat it.
  $BCEE,$06 Jump to #R$BD01 if the action state flag is clear.
  $BCF4,$08 Read the handler address from bytes #N$04-#N$05 of the record and keep the record.
  $BCFC,$05 Call #R$D1DB to jump to handler.
@ $BD01 label=CheckBitAndProcess_ClearResult
  $BD01,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $BD02,$01 Return.

@ $BD03 label=CheckBitAndProcess_TestStepSucceedsScript
  $BD03,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $BD05,$01 Return.

c $BD06 Process Object Pointer
@ $BD06 label=ProcessObjectPointer
D $BD06 Follows the object link and compares it with the character on screen.
  $BD06,$02 Save the schedule entry.
  $BD08,$04 Copy the character record from #REGix into #REGiy.
  $BD0C,$04 Point at the object display link.
@ $BD10 label=ProcessObjectPointer_Loop
  $BD10,$05 Go to #R$BD46 if the object pointer is zero.
  $BD15,$06 Take the byte at #REGix+#N$02 into #REGb and the byte at #REGiy+#N$02 into #REGc.
  $BD1B,$08 Jump ahead to #R$BD3E if this id matches the character id.
  $BD23,$08 Store #REGiy+#N$00 in #REGc and jump ahead to #R$BD3E if the id in #REGa matches #REGiy+#N$02.
  $BD2B,$03 Read the record's flag byte.
  $BD2E,$08 Jump ahead to #R$BD3E if the byte at #REGix+#N$02 matches the byte at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $BD36,$08 Jump back to #R$BD10 while this id does not match the character id.
@ $BD3E label=ProcessObjectPointer_Next
  $BD3E,$06 Go to #R$BD10 if the object id matches the character.
  $BD44,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $BD45,$01 Return.

@ $BD46 label=ProcessObjectPointer_TestStepSucceedsScript
  $BD46,$02 Reset the zero flag.
  $BD48,$02 Restore #REGiy from the stack.
  $BD4A,$01 Return.

c $BD4B Set Object Table Pointer
@ $BD4B label=SetObjectTablePointer
D $BD4B Starts the object scan at the action separator.
  $BD4B,$06 Start the object scan at the action separator.

c $BD51 Search Object Table
@ $BD51 label=SearchObjectTable
D $BD51 Starts the object scan at the character table.
  $BD51,$04 Use the character pairs (#R$994D+#N$18).

c $BD55 Read The Record Flag
@ $BD55 label=ScanObjectsUntilEndMarker
D $BD55 Scans the objects until the flag byte is #N$FF, checking each one against the moved marker.
  $BD55,$07 Jump to #R$BD66 if the record's flag byte is #N$FF.
  $BD5C,$05 Go to #R$BD68 if the object matches the moved marker.
  $BD61,$05 Read the next object and try again from #R$BD55.

@ $BD66 label=ScanObjectsUntilEndMarker_Next
  $BD66,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $BD68,$01 Return.

c $BD69 See Whether It Matches The Moved Marker
@ $BD69 label=SeeWhetherItMatchesMovedMarker
D $BD69 Compares the saved object's moved marker, flag byte and room with this record.
  $BD69,$03 Read the saved object's moved marker.
  $BD6C,$03 See whether this object is the one that was moved.
  $BD6F,$01 Return if they differ.
  $BD70,$03 Read the saved object's flag byte.
  $BD73,$03 See whether it matches the record flag.
  $BD76,$01 Return if they differ.
  $BD77,$03 Read the saved object's room.
  $BD7A,$03 See whether it matches the character's room.
  $BD7D,$01 Return.

c $BD7E Calculate Object Pointer Offset
@ $BD7E label=CalculateObjectPointerOffset
D $BD7E Points #REGhl at byte #N$18 of the record, or at the object display link when bit 7 is set.
  $BD7E,$07 Point #REGhl at byte #N$18 of the record (#REGiy+#N$18).
  $BD85,$06 Jump to #R$BD8E if a marker on the schedule entry is clear.
  $BD8B,$03 Take the object display link.
@ $BD8E label=CalculateObjectPointerOffset_Next
  $BD8E,$06 Jump to #R$BD97 if a marker on the schedule entry is clear.
  $BD94,$03 Take the alternate display link.
@ $BD97 label=CalculateObjectPointerOffset_DisplayDataPointer
  $BD97,$08 Copy #N$06 bytes from the display data at #R$B172.
  $BD9F,$07 Point #REGhl at byte #N$08 of the record (#REGiy+#N$08).
  $BDA6,$03 Take the display mode flag.
  $BDA9,$08 Copy #N$06 bytes and read #REGiy+#N$00 into #REGl.
  $BDB1,$06 Keep the low nibble of #REGiy+#N$01 in #REGh.
  $BDB7,$03 Record the display pointer.
  $BDBA,$07 Call #R$BDC2 to store the character record.
  $BDC1,$01 Return.

c $BDC2 Point The Object List At This Room
@ $BDC2 label=PointObjectListRoom
D $BDC2 Points the object list at this room and remembers the character record in the display pointer.
N $BDC2 Adjust object table for sum.
  $BDC2,$03 Call #R$D04F to point the object list at this room.
  $BDC5,$08 Store this object pointer at #R$BA13 and at #R$BA13+#N$02.
  $BDCD,$07 Read the display pointer and point #REGhl at the character pairs at #R$994D.
  $BDD4,$02 Count #N$01.
@ $BDD6 label=PointObjectListRoom_Loop
  $BDD6,$01 Read the next object-list byte.
  $BDD7,$01 Advance through the object list.
  $BDD8,$01 Merge the following object-list byte.
  $BDD9,$01 Step back one byte.
  $BDDA,$01 Return when this object-list pair is empty.
  $BDDB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BDE5 if the low byte differs from this object pair.
  $BDDF,$06 Jump ahead to #R$BDEA if the high byte matches this object pair.
@ $BDE5 label=PointObjectListRoom_Next
  $BDE5,$05 Step to the next object pair and go back to #R$BDD6.

N $BDEA Remember how many objects were listed and read the next one.
@ $BDEA label=PointObjectListRoom_CountRecordResult
  $BDEA,$06 Record the display pointer.
  $BDF0,$03 Store it in the object attribute flag.
  $BDF3,$06 Call #R$BE3C to read the next display object and return when the list has ended.
  $BDF9,$06 Call #R$BE3C to read the character record from the display link.
  $BDFF,$04 Jump to #R$BE3A when another display object follows.
  $BE03,$06 Call #R$BE22 to copy the object's picture link.
  $BE09,$06 Call #R$BE4B to read the next saved object and return when that list has ended.
  $BE0F,$06 Call #R$BE4B to read the character record from the saved value.
  $BE15,$04 Jump to #R$BE3A when another saved object follows.
  $BE19,$06 Call #R$BE22 to copy the object's picture link.
  $BE1F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $BE21,$01 Return.

c $BE22 Copy The Object Picture Link
@ $BE22 label=UseObjectDisplayLink
D $BE22 Looks the object up and copies either its picture link or the alternate link.
  $BE22,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $BE25,$07 See whether bit 6 of it is set.
  $BE2C,$03 Take the object display link.
  $BE2F,$02 Jump ahead to #R$BE34 if the special-description flag is present.
  $BE31,$03 Take the alternate display link.
@ $BE34 label=UseObjectDisplayLink_Length
  $BE34,$03 Take a length of #N$06.
  $BE37,$02 Copy the counted bytes.
  $BE39,$01 Return.

N $BE3A The condition holds, so the caller continues.
@ $BE3A label=UseObjectDisplayLink_ConditionHoldsCaller
  $BE3A,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $BE3B,$01 Return.

c $BE3C Read The Next Object On The Display List
@ $BE3C label=ReadCharacterRecordWord
D $BE3C Reads the character remembered in the display pointer and stores the next one back.
  $BE3C,$0A Read the display pointer and take the display data pointer, then call #R$D36A to run
. object entry.
  $BE46,$04 Remember this character record in the display pointer.
  $BE4A,$01 Return.

c $BE4B Read The Next Object On The Saved List
@ $BE4B label=ReadCharacterRecordValue
D $BE4B Reads the next object on the saved list through the display mode flag.
  $BE4B,$0A Read the display pointer and take the display mode flag, then call #R$D36A to run
. object entry.
  $BE55,$04 Store the record pointer at #R$BA13+#N$02.
  $BE59,$01 Return.

c $BE5A Advance Pointer And Check Bit
@ $BE5A label=AdvancePointerAndCheckBit
D $BE5A Steps to the next record and tests its flag bits.
  $BE5A,$08 Step to the next record
  $BE62,$06 Go to #R$BE6E if bit 5 of this byte is set.
  $BE68,$06 Go to #R$BE70 if bit 4 of this byte is clear.
@ $BE6E label=AdvancePointerAndCheckBit_Next
  $BE6E,$02 Step to the next record.
@ $BE70 label=AdvancePointerAndCheckBit_ReturnSaved
  $BE70,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $BE71,$01 Return.

c $BE72 Process Entry With Counter
@ $BE72 label=ProcessEntryWithCounter
D $BE72 Counts the entry down when its repeat mark is set, after measuring the field.
  $BE72,$03 Save the flags and start the count at zero.
@ $BE75 label=ProcessEntryWithCounter_Loop
  $BE75,$06 Call #R$BE5A to advance pointer and check bit.
  $BE7B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BE84 if bit 3 is clear.
  $BE7F,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BE84 if the count is zero.
  $BE83,$01 Count down one.
  $BE84,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BE8B if bit 2 of #REGc is clear.
  $BE88,$03 Count one more and test the next bit from #R$BE75.

N $BE8B Read the count.
@ $BE8B label=ProcessEntryWithCounter_Next
  $BE8B,$04 Go back to #R$BE75 if the count is not zero.
  $BE8F,$01 Restore the saved flags from the stack.
  $BE90,$01 Return.

c $BE91 Test The Action Taken Flag
@ $BE91 label=InspectActionTakenFlag
D $BE91 Tests the action-taken flag and the top bits of the action flags before looking the object up.
  $BE91,$06 Jump to #R$BE72 if the action-taken flag is set.
  $BE97,$09 Jump to #R$BECD if either bit 6 or bit 7 of the action flags is set.
  $BEA0,$08 Call #R$D237 to look up the current object.
  $BEA8,$08 Jump to #R$BE72 if bit 7 of the looked-up object's byte at offset #N$06 is clear.
  $BEB0,$08 Take the object id at offset #N$02 and call #R$D237 to look it up.
  $BEB8,$08 Jump to #R$BE72 if bit 7 of the looked-up object's byte at offset #N$06 is clear.
@ $BEC0 label=InspectActionTakenFlag_SetTaken
  $BEC0,$08 Mark the action taken so the script will not repeat it.
  $BEC8,$05 Set #REGa non-zero and jump back to #R$BE72.

N $BECD The top bit counts the entry down. The bit under it reads the record itself.
@ $BECD label=InspectActionTakenFlag_TestHighBit
  $BECD,$04 Jump back to #R$BE72 if it is #N$80.
  $BED1,$04 Jump ahead to #R$BEF6 if it is not #N$40.
  $BED5,$08 Call #R$D237 to look up the current object.
  $BEDD,$08 Jump to #R$BE72 if bit 6 of the looked-up object's byte at offset #N$06 is clear.
  $BEE5,$08 Call #R$D237 to look up the current object.
  $BEED,$09 Jump to #R$BE72 if bit 6 of the looked-up object's byte at offset #N$06 is clear.
@ $BEF6 label=InspectActionTakenFlag_TestMarker
  $BEF6,$06 Jump to #R$BEC0 if a marker on the action flags is clear.
  $BEFC,$07 Call #R$BF08 to test bit and process entry.
  $BF03,$02 Jump back to #R$BEC0 when #R$BF08 returns non-zero.
  $BF05,$03 The check at #R$BF08 passed, so count the entry again from #R$BE72.

c $BF08 Check Bit And Process Entry
@ $BF08 label=CheckBitAndProcessEntry
D $BF08 Advances the entry with #R$BE5A and returns zero when #R$BE91 rejects it. Keeps going while bit 3 of the action flags is clear.
N $BF08 Advance pointer and check bit.
  $BF08,$03 Call #R$BE5A to advance the pointer and test the bit.
@ $BF0B label=CheckBitAndProcessEntry_Loop
  $BF0B,$08 See whether bit 3 of it is set, then call #R$BE91 to run entry with counter.
  $BF13,$03 Jump ahead to #R$BF18 when the entry counter is still running.
  $BF16,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $BF17,$01 Return.

N $BF18 Return.
@ $BF18 label=CheckBitAndProcessEntry_Next
  $BF18,$01 Restore the bit test from the stack.
  $BF19,$02 Go back to #R$BF0B if bit 3 is clear.
  $BF1B,$01 Return.

c $BF1C Process Text Token Entry
@ $BF1C label=ProcessTextTokenEntry
D $BF1C Checks the object's flag byte, expands its text, and prints the dictionary word when the check
. passes.
  $BF1C,$06 Read the record flag, then call #R$A4BC to point to object attribute byte.
  $BF22,$06 Call #R$D3E8 to check object has special description.
  $BF28,$05 Go to #R$BF3D if this token has no text.
N $BF2D Print text token.
  $BF2D,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the dictionary word.
@ $BF30 label=ProcessTextTokenEntry_Loop
  $BF30,$06 Read the character's room, then call #R$A4BC to point to object attribute byte.
  $BF36,$06 Call #R$D3E8 to check object has special description.
  $BF3C,$01 Return.

N $BF3D Expand token #N($005D,$04,$04).
@ $BF3D label=ProcessTextTokenEntry_Next
  $BF3D,$06 Take token #N($005D,$04,$04) and call #R$A887 to expand it.
  $BF43,$02 Jump back to #R$BF30 for the next character.

c $BF45 Process Text Token Data
@ $BF45 label=ProcessTextTokenData
D $BF45 Returns with the carry clear when this token has no more data.
  $BF45,$05 Return if the moved marker is zero.
  $BF4A,$08 Use the character pairs (#R$994D).
  $BF52,$06 Read the word at #REGhl into #REGde.
  $BF58,$01 Return.

t $BF59 Table: Days Of The Week Strings
@ $BF59 label=Table_DaysOfWeekStrings
D $BF59 Days Of The Week Strings read by #R$BF79.
  $BF59,$15,$03

g $BF6E Messaging: Current Date
@ $BF6E label=Messaging_CurrentDate
D $BF6E Messaging: Current Date read by #R$BF79.
T $BF6E,$0B "#STR$BF6E,$08,$0B".

c $BF79 Handler: Date Bar
@ $BF79 label=Handler_DateBar
D $BF79 Carries out this step while the game is running.
N $BF79 #PUSHS #SIM(start=$BF79,stop=$C1BF)
. #UDGTABLE
. { #SCR$02{$00,$0120,$0200,$10}(date-bar) }
. UDGTABLE# #POPS
  $BF79,$07 Point #REGix at the current date at #R$BF6E and read the day of the week at #R$9FE1.
  $BF80,$03 Use the days of the week strings at #R$BF59.
N $BF83 Populate day of the week.
  $BF83,$03 Call #R$BFC6 to write the day of the week.
  $BF86,$08 Call #R$BFB0 to convert number to string.
  $BF8E,$08 Call #R$BFB0 to convert number to string.
  $BF96,$06 Write *#R$9FE2 to *#REGix+#N$00.
N $BF9C Now the date bar has been updated, set up printing it to the screen.
  $BF9C,$03 Load #REGhl with a pointer to #R$BF6E.
  $BF9F,$02 Set the length of the date bar in #REGb (#N$0B bytes).
  $BFA1,$03 Jump ahead to #R$C1A8 to print date bar.

c $BFA4 Convert Number To ASCII
@ $BFA4 label=ConvertNumberToASCII
D $BFA4 Turns the number into digits the print routine can use.
  $BFA4,$03 Call #R$BFAA to store the record flag.
  $BFA7,$03 Turn the number into a character.

c $BFAA Store It In The Record Flag
@ $BFAA label=StoreRecordFlag
D $BFAA Store it in the record flag, then step to the next field of the character record.
  $BFAA,$05 Store the record's flag byte.
  $BFAF,$01 Return.

c $BFB0 Convert Number To String
@ $BFB0 label=ConvertNumberToString
D $BFB0 Turns the number into a string of digits.
R $BFB0 A Number to convert (0-99)
R $BFB0 IX Pointer to output buffer
  $BFB0,$09 Call #R$BFBB to start the count.
  $BFB9,$02 Jump back to #R$BFA4 to convert number to ascii.

c $BFBB Make One Decimal Digit
@ $BFBB label=MakeOneDecimalDigit
D $BFBB Subtracts one power of ten until the number goes below zero, then adds
. that power back and returns the ASCII digit.
  $BFBB,$02 Start at one below the ASCII code for 0.
@ $BFBD label=MakeOneDecimalDigit_Loop
  $BFBD,$06 Keep subtracting this power of ten, counting the digit up in #REGc, until #REGhl
. goes below zero.
  $BFC3,$01 Add back the last power of ten to leave the remainder.
  $BFC4,$01 Put the digit in #REGa.
  $BFC5,$01 Return.

c $BFC6 Populate Day Of The Week
@ $BFC6 label=PopulateDayOfWeek
D $BFC6 Writes the three-letter day name for the day number in hand into the date line.
R $BFC6 A Number representing the current day of the week
R $BFC6 HL Pointer to the table containing the days of the week strings
R $BFC6 IX Pointer to the current date messaging
  $BFC6,$06 Multiply the day of the week by #N$03 and store the result in
. #REGbc.
  $BFCC,$01 Add this to the base address of the table containing the days of
. the week strings.
N $BFCD Each day string is three characters long (e.g. "Mon" for Monday).
  $BFCD,$02 Set a counter in #REGb for three characters.
@ $BFCF label=PopulateDayOfWeek_Loop
  $BFCF,$01 Fetch a character from the table containing the days of the week
. strings.
  $BFD0,$03 Write the character to the current date messaging.
  $BFD3,$02 Move to the next character in the current date messaging.
  $BFD5,$01 Move to the next character in the days of the week string table.
  $BFD6,$02 Count the next entry and repeat from #R$BFCF.
  $BFD8,$01 Return.

c $BFD9 Handler: Game Clock
@ $BFD9 label=Handler_GameClock
D $BFD9 Carries out this step while the game is running.
N $BFD9 Handle the game ticker.
  $BFD9,$07 Increment *#R$9FDD by one.
N $BFE0 Handle the clock minutes.
  $BFE0,$04 Increment *#R$9FDF by one.
  $BFE4,$04 Return unless the minute count has reached #N$3C.
N $BFE8 The minutes is at 60, which is invalid - so handle rolling the clock
. hour digits.
  $BFE8,$02 Reset the minutes back to #N$00 at *#REGhl.
N $BFEA Handle the clock hours.
  $BFEA,$04 Increment *#R$9FE0 by one.
  $BFEE,$05 Jump to #R$BFF6 unless the hour has reached #N$0D.
N $BFF3 The hours is at 13. which is invalid - so handle rolling the clock.
  $BFF3,$02 Reset the hours back to #N$01 at *#REGhl.
  $BFF5,$01 Return.

N $BFF6 Noon or midnight flips the AM/PM marker.
@ $BFF6 label=Handler_GameClock_AM_PM
  $BFF6,$03 Return unless the hour is #N$0C.
N $BFF9 It's either midday or midnight, so flip between AM and PM.
  $BFF9,$03 Read the AM/PM marker.
N $BFFC Smart way of altering the letter from "a" to "p" and back again.
. #TABLE(default,centre,centre)
. { =h Letter | =h Bits }
. { "a" | #EVAL($61,$02,$08) }
. { "p" | #EVAL($70,$02,$08) }
. TABLE#
  $BFFC,$02,b$01 Flip bits 0, 4.
  $BFFE,$03 Write this back to *#R$9FE2.
N $C001 If the clock went from PM to AM, handle rolling the day of the week.
  $C001,$03 Return unless the AM/PM marker is the letter a.
  $C004,$07 Increment *#R$9FE1 by one.
  $C00B,$01 Return.

c $C00C Get Time Period
@ $C00C label=GetTimePeriod
D $C00C Handles calculating a time-period code for scheduling events.
. #TABLE(default,centre)
. { =h Time Period Code | =h Meaning }
. { #N$01 | Late night/ evening (8 PM - 5 AM) }
. { #N$02 | 7 PM }
. { #N$03 | Afternoon (1 PM - 6 PM) }
. { #N$04 | Morning/ Midday (7 AM - 12 PM) }
. { #N$05 | 6 AM }
. TABLE#
R $C00C O:A Time period code
  $C00C,$03 Fetch *#R$9FE2.
  $C00F,$02 Check if its "A" for AM.
  $C011,$03 Read the hour.
  $C014,$02 Go to #R$C02B if the period letter is #N$61 (morning).
N $C016 It's the afternoon (as in, "PM").
  $C016,$04 Go to #R$C035 if the hour is twelve (noon).
  $C01A,$04 Go to #R$C021 if the hour is before eight.
N $C01E It's late PM/ evening; 8 PM - 11 PM.
@ $C01E label=TimePeriod_LateEvening
  $C01E,$02 Leave #N$01 as the result.
  $C020,$01 Return.

N $C021 Seven in the evening leaves time-of-day code 2.
@ $C021 label=TimePeriod_BeforeEightPM
N $C021 It's before 8 PM, but break this down further.
  $C021,$04 Go to #R$C028 unless the hour is seven.
N $C025 It's 7 PM.
  $C025,$02 Leave #N$02 as the result.
  $C027,$01 Return.

N $C028 The afternoon, from one until six, leaves time-of-day code 3.
@ $C028 label=TimePeriod_Afternoon
N $C028 It's afternoon/ early PM; 1 PM - 6 PM.
  $C028,$02 Leave #N$03 as the result.
  $C02A,$01 Return.

N $C02B The morning checks leave time-of-day code 4, or continue into early morning.
@ $C02B label=TimePeriod_AM
N $C02B It's morning, but break this down further.
  $C02B,$04 Go to #R$C01E if the hour is twelve.
  $C02F,$04 Go to #R$C01E if the hour is before six.
  $C033,$02 Go to #R$C038 if the hour is six.
N $C035 It's morning; 7 AM - noon (12 PM).
@ $C035 label=TimePeriod_Morning
  $C035,$02 Leave #N$04 as the result.
  $C037,$01 Return.

N $C038 Six in the morning leaves time-of-day code 5.
@ $C038 label=TimePeriod_EarlyMorning
N $C038 It's early morning; 6 AM.
  $C038,$02 Leave #N$05 as the result.
  $C03A,$01 Return.

c $C03B Check Input Port
@ $C03B label=CheckInputPort
D $C03B Reads port #N$FB, keeps bit 6 and returns.
B $C03B,$04 Read port #N$FB, keep bit 6.
B $C03F,$01 Return.

c $C040 Save Game
@ $C040 label=SaveGame
D $C040 Stores the current game so it can be restored later.
  $C040,$06 Print the message at #N$09A2 and wait for a key.
  $C046,$08 Record the tape header: saved game.
  $C04E,$07 Use the tape header: saved game (#R$C0FE).
  $C055,$01 Set #REGa to #N$00 to indicate this is a header block.
  $C056,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
  $C059,$02 Count #N$32.
@ $C05B label=SaveGame_PauseLoop
  $C05B,$03 Wait for #N$32 frames (one second) between the header and the data block.
  $C05E,$05 Read the tape header: saved game.
  $C063,$04 Read the tape header: saved game.
  $C067,$02 Set #REGa to #N$FF to indicate this is a data block.
  $C069,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES).
  $C06C,$01 Turn interrupts off again, as #SKOOLKID$04C2(SA_BYTES) leaves them on.
  $C06D,$06 Take the messaging: rewind tape, then call #R$C1CB to print string.
  $C073,$06 Print the message at #N$09AC and wait for a key.
  $C079,$07 Use the tape header: saved game (#R$C0FE).
  $C080,$02 Set #REGa to #N$00 to indicate this is a header block.
  $C082,$01 Clear the carry so the ROM verifies this block.
  $C083,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES).
  $C086,$02 Jump to #R$C098 if the header did not load.
  $C088,$01 Clear the carry before the next tape block.

c $C089 Load The Saved Game Block
@ $C089 label=LoadGameData
D $C089 Loads the saved game from the tape header and returns when the load fails.
  $C089,$08 Read the tape header: saved game.
  $C091,$02 Set #REGa to #N$FF to indicate this is a data block.
  $C093,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES).
  $C096,$01 Turn interrupts off again, as #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES) leaves them on.
  $C097,$01 Return when the saved game loaded.
N $C098 There were errors ... so alert the player.
@ $C098 label=LoadGameData_Error
  $C098,$01 Make sure interrupts are off before reporting the tape error.
  $C099,$06 Take the messaging: tape error, then call #R$C1CB to print string.
  $C09F,$06 Jump ahead to #R$C1C0 to print string and wait for keypress.

t $C0A5 Messaging: Rewind Tape
@ $C0A5 label=Messaging_RewindTape
D $C0A5 Messaging: Rewind Tape read by #R$C040.
  $C0A5,$0C "#STR$C0A5".

t $C0B1 Messaging: Tape Error
@ $C0B1 label=Messaging_TapeError
D $C0B1 Messaging: Tape Error read by #R$C089.
  $C0B1,$0B "#STR$C0B1".

t $C0BC Messaging: Play Saved Game Tape
@ $C0BC label=Messaging_PlaySavedGameTape
D $C0BC Messaging: Play Saved Game Tape read by #R$C0DF.
  $C0BC,$23 "#STR$C0BC".

c $C0DF Load Game
@ $C0DF label=LoadGame
D $C0DF Reads a stored game back into play.
  $C0DF,$08 Record the tape header: saved game.
N $C0E7 "#STR$C0BC".
  $C0E7,$06 Take the messaging: play saved game tape, then call #R$C1CB to print string.
@ $C0ED label=LoadGame_TryTape
  $C0ED,$07 Use the tape header: saved game (#R$C0FE).
  $C0F4,$02 Set #REGa to #N$00 to indicate this is a header block.
  $C0F6,$01 Clear the carry flag so #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES) verifies the header against #R$C0FE instead of loading it.
  $C0F7,$03 Call #SKOOLKID$0556(LD_BYTES).
  $C0FA,$02 Jump back to #R$C0ED if there was a tape loading error to try again.
  $C0FC,$02 Jump back to #R$C089 once the game block has loaded.

b $C0FE Tape Header: Saved Game
D $C0FE Header for the save and load blocks. The name begins SHD V2.
. Read by #R$C040 and #R$C0DF.
B $C0FE,$01 Type.
T $C0FF,$0A Name.
W $C109,$02 Saved-game length, read by #R$C040, #R$C089 and #R$C0DF.
W $C10B,$02 Address the saved game loads to, read by #R$C040, #R$C089 and #R$C0DF.
W $C10D,$02 Parameter.

c $C10F Clear Screen
@ $C10F label=ClearScreen
E $C10F View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$6FD3 }
. LIST#
D $C10F Blanks the screen before a new picture is drawn.
  $C10F,$04 Set border colour to #INK$07.
  $C113,$06 Point at the start of the screen and the byte after it, ready to fill the pixels with zero.
  $C119,$03 Take the length of the screen bitmap.
  $C11C,$02 Plant a zero at the start of the screen, ready for the fill.
  $C11E,$02 Copy the #N$00 across the rest of the screen buffer bytes.
  $C120,$03 Take the length of the attributes still to paint.
  $C123,$02 Paint this square black ink on white paper, ready to fill the rest of the screen.
  $C125,$02 Copy these bytes across.
  $C127,$01 Return.

g $C128 Flags: Screen
D $C128 Screen mode. #R$C142 clears it and the surrounding code stores #N$01 or #N$02.
. Read by #R$C129, #R$C142, #R$C2BA and #R$C316.
B $C128,$01

c $C129 Initialise Screen Display
@ $C129 label=InitialiseScreenDisplay
D $C129 Clears the parser flag, marks the screen in use, and sets the width and the print position.
  $C129,$08 Clear the parser flag (#R$A019) and set the screen flag (#R$C128) to #N$01.
  $C131,$02 Use #N$12 for the display.

N $C133 Record the result in the screen width at #R$C1F5, then use the screen as the
. pointer, then record the pointer in the storage Decorative Line Screen Position.
  $C133,$03 Store it in the screen width.
  $C136,$06 Write #N$5020 (screen buffer location) to *#R$C1F8.
  $C13C,$05 Write #N$02 to the decorative line.
  $C141,$01 Return.

c $C142 Reset Screen Display
@ $C142 label=ResetScreenDisplay
D $C142 Clears the screen flags and redraws the picture frame.
  $C142,$04 Clear the screen flags.
  $C146,$04 Take a width of #N$24 and continue at #R$C133.

c $C14A Draw Decorative Line
@ $C14A label=DrawDecorativeLine
D $C14A Draws a decorative horizontal pattern on screen as a separator.
.
. #PUSHS #SIM(start=$C14A,stop=$C18F)
. #UDGTABLE
. { #SCR$02{$00,$0120,$0200,$10}(decorative-line) }
. UDGTABLE# #POPS
N $C14A Clear screen.
  $C14A,$03 Call #R$C10F to clear the screen.
  $C14D,$05 Store the width value (#N$24) at *#R$C1F4.
  $C152,$06 Initialise the screen buffer starting point (#N$50E0) at *#R$C1F6.
  $C158,$02 Use #N$02 for the picture.
@ $C15A label=DrawDecorativeLine_Custom
  $C15A,$03 Store the height value in *#R$C1FB.
N $C15D Reset screen display.
  $C15D,$03 Call #R$C142 to reset the screen.
  $C160,$06 Point #REGhl at the screen address #N$5140 and #REGde at the decorative line data at #R$C190.
  $C166,$02 Set a counter in #REGb for #N$06 rows.
@ $C168 label=DrawDecorativeLine_RowLoop
  $C168,$02 Set a counter in #REGc for #N$08 repetitions.
  $C16A,$05 Count #N$04, saving count and the pointer.
@ $C16C label=DrawDecorativeLine_SegmentLoop
@ $C16F label=DrawDecorativeLine_CopyPatternLoop
  $C16F,$02 Copy the pattern byte to the current screen position.
  $C171,$01 Increment the pattern pointer by one.
  $C172,$01 Increment the screen position column by one.
  $C173,$02 Count the next attribute and repeat from #R$C16F.
  $C175,$01 Restore the caller's registers from the stack.
  $C176,$01 Decrease the segment counter by one.
  $C177,$02 Jump back to #R$C16C until all segments have been drawn.
N $C179 Skip to the next pattern.
  $C179,$04 Increment the pattern pointer by four.
  $C17D,$02 Restore the screen address from the stack.
  $C17F,$01 The count, and step to the next row.
  $C180,$02 Count the next attribute and repeat from #R$C168.
N $C182 Now colour the line BRIGHT WHITE.
  $C182,$0D Take the attributes, take a length of #N$1F attributes and copy these bytes
. across.
  $C18F,$01 Return.

b $C190 Data: Decorative Line
@ $C190 label=Data_DecorativeLine
D $C190 Data: Decorative Line read by #R$C14A.
  $C190,$18

c $C1A8 Print Date Bar
@ $C1A8 label=PrintDateBar
D $C1A8 Prints the date string at screen position #N$5041, with a space in front of it.
R $C1A8 HL Date messaging string
R $C1A8 B Length of messaging string
  $C1A8,$03 Start the date text at screen position #N$5041.
  $C1AB,$03 Swap the two pointers and count #N$00.
N $C1AE Print a "SPACE" before the date string.
  $C1AE,$05 Take a space and print it through #R$C388.
N $C1B3 Loop through the date messaging string and output it to the screen.
@ $C1B3 label=PrintDateBar_Loop
  $C1B3,$01 Fetch a character from *#REGde and store it in #REGa.
N $C1B4 Print character.
  $C1B4,$03 Call #R$C388 to print this character.
  $C1B7,$01 Move to the next character of the message.
  $C1B8,$02 Count the next character of the message and repeat from #R$C1B3.
N $C1BA Print a "SPACE" after the date string.
  $C1BA,$02 Leave #N$20 as the result.
  $C1BC,$03 Call #R$C388 to print this character.
  $C1BF,$01 Return.

c $C1C0 Print String And Wait For Keypress
@ $C1C0 label=PrintStringAndWaitForKeypress
D $C1C0 Prints a string and waits for a key.
  $C1C0,$06 Print the string at #REGhl and wait for a key at #R$C415.
  $C1C6,$05 Feed a carriage return into the character input at #R$C1FD.

c $C1CB Print String
@ $C1CB label=PrintString
D $C1CB Prints each character of a bit-7 terminated string.
R $C1CB HL Pointer to the string data
  $C1CB,$01 Read the next character of the string.
N $C1CC Strip off the termination bit to leave only the ASCII value.
  $C1CC,$02,b$01 Strip the end-of-string marker (bit 7).
  $C1CE,$03 Call #R$C1FD to print this character.
N $C1D1 The string is terminated by bit 7 being set, so check if this is the
. final character or not.
  $C1D1,$02 See whether this character ends the string.
  $C1D3,$01 Return if that marker is set.
  $C1D4,$01 Move to the next byte in the string.
  $C1D5,$02 Jump back to #R$C1CB to print the next character of the string.

u $C1D7 Unused
D $C1D7 Single #N$C9 byte between the string printer and the out-of-memory handler.
  $C1D7,$01 Byte #N$C9.

c $C1D8 Error: Out Of Memory
@ $C1D8 label=Error_OutOfMemory
D $C1D8 Out of memory.
  $C1D8,$06 Print the out-of-memory message at #N$13C5 and wait for a key.
  $C1DE,$03 Jump back to #R$A0EB to handle game state based on parameter.

c $C1E1 Check TV Flag And Process
@ $C1E1 label=CheckTVFlagAndProcess
D $C1E1 Checks the television flag and prints the character when the print mode is on.
  $C1E1,$01 Keep it.
  $C1E2,$06 Jump to #R$C1EA if *#SKOOLKID$5C3C(TV_FLAG) is not zero.
  $C1E8,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $C1E9,$01 Return.

N $C1EA Read the print mode.
  $C1EA,$06 Go to #R$C1FC if print mode is on.
  $C1F0,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $C1F1,$03 Jump ahead to #R$C2BA to run screen character.

g $C1F4 Storage Decorative Line Width
@ $C1F4 label=DecorativeLine_Width
D $C1F4 Storage Decorative Line Width read by #R$C14A, #R$C1FD, #R$C22D and #R$C249.
B $C1F4,$01

g $C1F5 Screen Width
@ $C1F5 label=ScreenWidth
D $C1F5 Screen Width read by #R$A9B7, #R$C129 and #R$C2BA.
B $C1F5,$01

g $C1F6 Storage Decorative Line Screen Position
@ $C1F6 label=DecorativeLine_ScreenPosition
D $C1F6 Storage Decorative Line Screen Position read by #R$C14A, #R$C1FD and #R$C22D.
W $C1F6,$02

g $C1FA Flags: Decorative Line
D $C1FA Initialised with the decorative-line position.
. Read by #R$C129 and #R$C2BA.
B $C1FA,$01

g $C1FB Print Column
D $C1FB Column read by #R$C200 while printing.
. Read by #R$C14A, #R$C1FD and #R$C22D.
B $C1FB,$01

c $C1FC Process Character Input
@ $C1FC label=ProcessCharacterInput
R $C1FC A Character code
D $C1FC Restores the character code the caller had saved.
  $C1FC,$01 Restore saved character id for the caller.

c $C1FD Read The Pointer At The Storage Decorative
@ $C1FD label=PrintScreenCharacter
D $C1FD Prints one character at the current column, and starts a new line on ENTER.
  $C1FD,$03 Keep the character.
  $C200,$04 Read the print column.
  $C204,$09 Read the screen position at #R$C1F6 and jump to #R$C214 if the character is not a new line.
  $C20D,$07 Print a space and carry on from #R$C224.

N $C214 A code of 8 starts the decorative line. Any other code counts the width down.
@ $C214 label=PrintScreenCharacter_Next
  $C214,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C249 if it is #N$08.
  $C218,$06 Call #R$C240 to check the key, then print the character through #R$C388.
  $C21E,$06 Jump ahead to #R$C22D if the count is not finished.
@ $C224 label=PrintScreenCharacter_SetDecorative
  $C224,$07 Call #R$C271 to scroll screen up.
  $C22B,$02 Take a width of #N$24 for the decorative line.

c $C22D Record The Result In The Storage Decorative
@ $C22D label=RecordResultStorageDecorative
D $C22D Stores the decorative-line width and updates the print column.
  $C22D,$07 Store it in the storage decorative line width, take the count and store it in the
. print column.
  $C234,$03 Record the storage decorative line screen position.
  $C237,$02 Load #REGa with ASCII code #N$2B ("#CHR$2B").
  $C239,$03 Print the character through #R$C388.
  $C23C,$03 Restore the saved character, the saved count and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $C23F,$01 Return.

c $C240 Validate Keypress
@ $C240 label=ValidateKeypress
D $C240 Checks the keypress and rejects a key the game does not use.
  $C240,$03 Return if it is below #N$61.
  $C243,$03 Return if it is at least #N$7B.
  $C246,$02,b$01 Convert the lower-case letter to upper case.
  $C248,$01 Return.

c $C249 Handle Backspace Key
@ $C249 label=HandleBackspaceKey
D $C249 Deletes the character before the cursor, and scrolls the line when the width is #N$24.
  $C249,$08 Print a space at #R$C388, then call #R$C267.
  $C251,$03 Call #R$C267 to subtract seven.
  $C254,$08 Go to #R$C22D unless the line width is #N$24.
  $C25C,$07 Call #R$C29C to scroll screen down.
  $C263,$04 Jump back to #R$C22D to run character input.

c $C267 Subtract Seven
@ $C267 label=SubtractSeven
E $C267 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8761 }
. LIST#
D $C267 Steps the print column back by one cell and wraps when the column underflows.
  $C267,$01 Read the low byte of the count.
  $C268,$02 Step back one character cell, wrapping to the previous row when the column underflows.
  $C26A,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the count.
  $C26B,$01 Return when the column still fits on this row.
  $C26C,$01 Count down the low byte of the pointer.
  $C26D,$02 Advance the result by #N$08.
  $C26F,$01 Store the column.
  $C270,$01 Return.

c $C271 Scroll Screen Up
@ $C271 label=ScrollScreenUp
D $C271 Scrolls the screen up one character row.
  $C271,$06 Keep the caller's registers and point #REGhl at the screen address #N$5080.
  $C277,$03 Take the screen.
  $C27A,$02 Use #N$08.
@ $C27C label=ScrollScreenUp_Loop
  $C27C,$05 Take a length of #N$80 entries.
  $C281,$02 Scroll #N$80 bytes of this screen third down one character row.
  $C283,$07 Jump back to #R$C27C while the count is not finished.
  $C28A,$03 Point #REGhl at the screen address #N$50E0.

c $C28D Print A Row Of Spaces
@ $C28D label=PrintARowOfSpaces
D $C28D Fills the scrolled line with #N$24 spaces.
  $C28D,$04 Count #N$24 and count #N$01 for scroll screen up.
  $C291,$02 Take a space.
  $C293,$03 Call #R$C388 to print a space.
  $C296,$02 Count the next entry and repeat from #R$C293.
  $C298,$03 Restore the saved count, the saved source and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $C29B,$01 Return.

c $C29C Scroll Screen Down
@ $C29C label=ScrollScreenDown
D $C29C Scrolls the screen down one character row.
  $C29C,$06 Keep the caller's registers and point #REGhl at the screen address #N$50DF.
  $C2A2,$03 Take the screen.
  $C2A5,$02 Use #N$08.
@ $C2A7 label=ScrollScreenDown_Loop
  $C2A7,$05 Take a length of #N$80 entries.
  $C2AC,$02 Scroll #N$80 bytes of this screen third up one character row.
  $C2AE,$07 Jump back to #R$C2A7 while the count is not finished.
  $C2B5,$05 Jump back to #R$C28D to scroll screen up.

c $C2BA Process Screen Character
@ $C2BA label=ProcessScreenCharacter
R $C2BA A Character code
D $C2BA On a new line, resets the parser row. Any other character is printed.
  $C2BA,$06 Read the storage decorative line screen position.
  $C2C0,$04 Read the decorative line into #REGc.
  $C2C4,$01 Restore the character from the stack.
  $C2C5,$01 Keep a copy of the character on the stack.
  $C2C6,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C2EE if this character is not a new line (#N$0D).
@ $C2CA label=ProcessScreenCharacter_Loop
  $C2CA,$07 Jump to #R$C2D6 if the parser is not #N$0F.
N $C2D1 Scroll screen and reset display.
  $C2D1,$03 Call #R$C306 to scroll the screen and reset the display.
  $C2D4,$02 Start the count at #N$FF so the next step makes it zero.
@ $C2D6 label=ProcessScreenCharacter_Next
  $C2D6,$07 Add one to the count at #R$A019 and point #REGhl at #N$5020.
  $C2DD,$02 Use a length of #N$02.
N $C2DF Update screen display.
  $C2DF,$03 Call #R$C316 to update the screen.
N $C2E2 Screen flag selects the width.
  $C2E2,$08 Take #N$24 and go to #R$C2F8 if the byte at #R$C128 is zero.
  $C2EA,$04 Take a width of #N$12 and store it from #R$C2F8.

N $C2EE Print this character and leave the row once the width runs out.
@ $C2EE label=ProcessScreenCharacter_ScreenWidthCount
N $C2EE Print character.
  $C2EE,$03 Call #R$C388 to print this character.
  $C2F1,$07 Jump back to #R$C2CA if the count is finished.
@ $C2F8 label=ProcessScreenCharacter_RecordScreenWidth
  $C2F8,$07 Record the screen width and record the storage decorative line screen position.
  $C2FF,$03 Record the decorative line.
  $C302,$03 Restore the saved result, the saved count and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $C305,$01 Return.

c $C306 Scroll Screen And Reset Display
@ $C306 label=ScrollScreenAndResetDisplay
D $C306 Waits for a key, then resets the screen display.
  $C306,$09 Call #R$C415 to wait for keypress.
  $C30F,$05 Clear bit 7, call #R$C142 to reset the screen.
  $C314,$01 Restore #REGhl from the stack.
  $C315,$01 Return.

c $C316 Update Screen Display
@ $C316 label=UpdateScreenDisplay
D $C316 Copies the picture up one character row when the screen is being updated.
R $C316 HL Screen position
R $C316 C Column count
  $C316,$07 Call #R$C3E4 if the screen flags is zero.
  $C31D,$06 Save the registers and point #REGhl at the second character row of the screen
. (#N$4020).
  $C323,$03 Point the row copy at the top of the screen.
  $C326,$03 Take a length of #N$11 entries.
  $C329,$08 Go to #R$C333 with #REGa=#N$20 if the flag at #R$C128 is zero (otherwise use #N$10).
  $C331,$02 Use #N$10 for the display.
@ $C333 label=UpdateScreenDisplay_Loop
  $C333,$05 Save #REGhl, #REGde and #REGbc, and take a count of #N$08.
@ $C338 label=UpdateScreenDisplay_KeepRowDestination
  $C338,$04 Keep the row, the destination and the count while this pixel line is copied.
  $C33C,$02 Copy one row of the character being drawn onto the screen.
  $C33E,$08 Jump back to #R$C338 while the count is not finished.
  $C346,$03 Restore the screen registers from the stack.
  $C349,$04 And call #R$C37D to advance the screen position.
  $C34D,$04 Call #R$C37D to advance screen position.
  $C351,$03 Jump back to #R$C333 while the count is not finished.
  $C354,$04 Go to #R$C363 if it is #N$10.
  $C358,$06 Point at the second and first attribute rows, ready to scroll the attributes up one
. row.
  $C35E,$03 Take #N$0220 bytes, seventeen attribute rows.
  $C361,$02 Scroll the attributes up one row.
@ $C363 label=UpdateScreenDisplay_Next
  $C363,$08 Set #REGb to #N$24 and jump to #R$C36D if the screen flag at #R$C128 is zero.
  $C36B,$02 Count #N$12.
@ $C36D label=UpdateScreenDisplay_FillSpaces
  $C36D,$07 Point #REGhl at the screen address #N$5020, set #REGc to #N$02 and take a space.
  $C374,$03 Call #R$C388 to print a space.
  $C377,$02 Count the next entry and repeat from #R$C374.
  $C379,$03 Restore the saved source, the saved pointer and the saved count from the stack.
  $C37C,$01 Return.

c $C37D Advance Screen Position
@ $C37D label=AdvanceScreenPosition
D $C37D Moves the screen address on by one character row (#N$20, and #N$08 in the high byte).
R $C37D HL Screen position
R $C37D O:HL Updated screen position
  $C37D,$01 Swap in the other screen pointer, so calling this twice moves both #REGhl and #REGde on by one character row.
  $C37E,$01 Read the low byte of the pointer.
  $C37F,$02 Advance the result by #N$20.
  $C381,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the pointer.
  $C382,$01 Return when the row still fits on the screen.
  $C383,$01 Read the high byte of the pointer.
  $C384,$02 Advance the result by #N$08.
  $C386,$01 Store the result in the high byte of the pointer.
  $C387,$01 Return.

c $C388 Print Character
@ $C388 label=PrintCharacter
E $C388 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$87C9 }
. LIST#
D $C388 Looks the character up in the font and draws it at the screen address.
R $C388 A Character to print
R $C388 HL Screen buffer address
  $C388,$07 Store that in the low byte and clear the high byte.
  $C38F,$03 Multiply by #N$08 (each font character is #N$08 bytes of data).
  $C392,$05 Point #REGde at #SKOOLKID$3D00(CHARSET) minus #N$0100, plus this character's offset.
  $C397,$01 Restore the screen address from the stack.
  $C398,$01 Keep a copy of it.
  $C399,$02 Set a counter in #REGb for #N$08 bytes of the font data.
  $C39B,$03 Read the next byte of this character's bitmap and keep the counters.
N $C39E Does this character need shifting?
  $C39E,$02 See whether this character needs shifting.
  $C3A0,$04 Go to #R$C3AB if the count reaches zero.
@ $C3A4 label=PrintCharacter_Loop
  $C3A4,$07 Jump back to #R$C3A4 while the count is not finished.
@ $C3AB label=PrintCharacter_Next
  $C3AB,$02 Take the count.
  $C3AD,$01 Invert the shifted mask so it covers the screen pixels to keep.
  $C3AE,$01 Keep the screen pixels outside the character.
  $C3AF,$01 Add the pixels of the character.
  $C3B0,$03 Store it at the pointer.
N $C3B3 Is the character byte-aligned?
  $C3B3,$02 See whether the shift count is zero without changing it, so the character is
. byte-aligned.
  $C3B5,$02 Go to #R$C3D0 when the shift count is zero, so the character is byte-aligned.
  $C3B7,$07 Work out how many bit positions are left in this row.
  $C3BE,$02 Start a mask of all ones, shifted with the character data.
@ $C3C0 label=PrintCharacter_CountFinished
  $C3C0,$07 Jump back to #R$C3C0 while the count is not finished.
  $C3C7,$02 Take the count.
  $C3C9,$01 Invert the shifted mask so it covers the screen pixels to keep in the next cell.
  $C3CA,$01 Step to the next character.
  $C3CB,$01 Keep the screen pixels in that cell outside the character.
  $C3CC,$01 Add the part of the character that spills into that cell.
  $C3CD,$03 Store it at the pointer and step back one character.
@ $C3D0 label=PrintCharacter_NextBitmapByte
  $C3D0,$02 Step to the next bitmap byte and the next pixel row.
  $C3D2,$02 Draw the next pixel row and repeat from #R$C39B until the character is finished.
  $C3D4,$06 Add #N$07 to the total.
  $C3DA,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C3E1 if the total is below #N$08.
  $C3DE,$03 Drop #N$08 from the count.
  $C3E1,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the count.
  $C3E2,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $C3E3,$01 Return.

c $C3E4 Redraw The Screen When Input Is Waiting
D $C3E4 Returns at once when the input flag is clear. Otherwise it redraws the screen
. before the next key is taken.
  $C3E4,$05 Return if the input is zero.
  $C3E9,$08 Keep the text position and point at the top row, with eight pixel lines to redraw.
  $C3F1,$08 Keep the count and the row, call #R$C403, then step #REGh to the next pixel row.
  $C3F9,$02 Count the next pixel row and repeat from #R$C3F1.
  $C3FB,$02 Take #N$04.
  $C3FD,$02 Stop the ZX Printer motor (port #N$FB).
  $C3FF,$03 Restore the saved count, the saved source and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $C402,$01 Return.

c $C403 Set The Printer Motor Speed Via Port #N$FB
@ $C403 label=SetPrinterMotorSpeed
D $C403 The game's copy of the ROM #SKOOLKID$0EF4(COPY_LINE) code: writes #N$02 to port
. #N$FB when the count in #REGb is below three (slowing the printer motor for the last two
. pixel lines), and #N$00 otherwise, then waits until a read from that port comes back negative.
  $C403,$04 Compare #REGb with #N$03 and leave #N$FF in #REGa when the count is below.
  $C407,$02,b$01 Keep just bit 1, giving #N$02 (slow the printer motor) when the count is below three and #N$00 otherwise.
  $C409,$02 Send that strobe to the ZX Printer on port #N$FB.
  $C40B,$01 Keep the motor value just sent in #REGd.
  $C40C,$04 Read port #N$FB and return if the ZX Printer is busy.
  $C410,$02 Go back to #R$C40C while the ZX Printer is not ready.
  $C412,$03 Jump to #SKOOLKID$0EF4(COPY_LINE) at #N$0F12.

c $C415 Wait For Keypress
@ $C415 label=WaitForKeypress_Loop
D $C415 Waits until a key is held, then returns that key without the press bit.
R $C415 O:A The ASCII code keypress value
N $C415 Bit 7 is only set after a key has been pressed.
  $C415,$03 Call #R$C41F to read the keyboard.
  $C418,$04 Jump back to #R$C415 while bit 7 of #REGa is clear.
N $C41C Strip off the set bit for the response.
  $C41C,$02,b$01 Strip the key-pressed marker (bit 7) to leave the ASCII code.
  $C41E,$01 Return.

c $C41F Get Keypress
@ $C41F label=GetKeypress
D $C41F Waits for the next frame and reads the key the Spectrum last reported.
R $C41F O:A Keypress value, or #N$00 for no keypress
  $C41F,$07 Save the schedule entry, the character record, the pointer, the source and
. the count.
  $C426,$04 #REGiy=#SKOOLKID$5C3A(ERR_NR).
  $C42A,$03 Let one frame interrupt through so the ROM can scan the keyboard, then turn interrupts off again.
  $C42D,$07 Jump to #R$C437 if bit 5 of *#SKOOLKID$5C3B(FLAGS) is set, indicating that a key has been pressed.
N $C434 No key has been pressed.
  $C434,$01 Load #REGa with #N$00 (no keypress).
  $C435,$02 Carry on and wait for a key once the mode flags are stored.

N $C437 Clear the mark on this character, then store the result in the working byte,
. then read the working value.
@ $C437 label=GetKeypress_Process
N $C437 A key has been pressed, so process it.
  $C437,$05 Store it in the system variables.
  $C43C,$07 Jump to #R$C445 if *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K) (last key pressed) is not ASCII #N$5F ("#CHR$5F").
  $C443,$02 Use #N$18 for the key.
N $C445 Did the player press "DELETE"?
@ $C445 label=GetKeypress_CheckDelete
  $C445,$04 Jump to #R$C44B if *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K) (last key pressed) is not ASCII #N$0C ("DELETE").
  $C449,$02 Load #REGa with #N$08 (delete was pressed).
N $C44B Did the player press "UP"?
@ $C44B label=GetKeypress_CheckUp
  $C44B,$04 Jump to #R$C451 if *#SKOOLKID$5C08(LAST_K) (last key pressed) is not ASCII #N$0B ("UP").
  $C44F,$02 Load #REGa with #N$5B (up was pressed).
@ $C451 label=GetKeypress_Validate
N $C451 Validate keypress.
  $C451,$03 Call #R$C240 to accept the key if it is in range.
  $C454,$02 Set the mark on this character.
@ $C456 label=GetKeypress_Return
  $C456,$07 Restore the saved count, the saved source, the saved pointer, the saved character record and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $C45D,$01 Return.

c $C45E Check Display Parameter
@ $C45E label=CheckDisplayParameter
D $C45E Compares the working value at #R$A010 with #N$05, then compares #REGb with #N$6E or #N$6A.
  $C45E,$05 Read the working value.

c $C463 Read The Display Mode
@ $C463 label=ReadDisplayModeForPicture
D $C463 Reads the display mode and checks the picture marker.
  $C463,$03 Read the display mode.
  $C466,$05 Jump ahead to #R$C46E if the display mode is below #N$05.
  $C46B,$02 See whether the count has reached #N$6E.
  $C46D,$01 Return.

N $C46E Return when the character is the letter j.
@ $C46E label=ReadDisplayModeForPicture_Next
  $C46E,$02 See whether the count has reached #N$6A.
  $C470,$01 Return.
B $C471,$10 Token numbers for a clear graphics flag. A zero word is an empty slot, read by #R$CB7B.
B $C481,$10 Token numbers for a set graphics flag, read by #R$CB7B.
B $C491,$01 Character attribute saved by #R$CA22 and read by #R$CB7B.
B $C492,$01 Object attribute saved by #R$CA2C and read by #R$CB7B.

c $C493 Check Display Flag Bits 3 And 7
@ $C493 label=CheckDisplayBitsThreeAndSeven
D $C493 Tests bits 3 and 7 of the object attribute at #R$A01D. When both allow it, the display flag chooses the mode.
  $C493,$08 Point #REGhl at the object attribute and go to #R$C4AE if bit 3 is clear.
  $C49B,$06 Go to #R$C4AE if bit 7 of the object attribute is set.
  $C4A1,$03 Read the display.
N $C4A4 Bits 2 and 3 of the display flag choose the mode.
  $C4A4,$08 Jump to #R$C4AE with #N$05 if bits 2 and 3 of the display flag are both set.
  $C4AC,$02 Use #N$07 for the display.
  $C4AE,$03 Store it in the display mode.
  $C4B1,$01 Return.

c $C4B2 Check Display Flag Bits 2 And 6
@ $C4B2 label=CheckDisplayBitsTwoAndSix
D $C4B2 Tests bits 2 and 6 of the object attribute at #R$A01D. When both allow it, the display flag chooses the mode.
  $C4B2,$08 Point #REGhl at the object attribute and go to #R$C4CD if bit 2 is clear.
  $C4BA,$06 Go to #R$C4CD if bit 6 of the object attribute is set.
  $C4C0,$03 Read the display.
N $C4C3 Bits 0 and 1 of the display flag choose the mode.
  $C4C3,$08 Jump to #R$C4CD with #N$05 if bits 0 and 1 of the display flag are both set.
  $C4CB,$02 Use #N$07 for the display.
  $C4CD,$03 Store it in the display mode.
  $C4D0,$01 Return.

c $C4D1 Process Display Action
@ $C4D1 label=ProcessDisplayAction
D $C4D1 Jumps ahead when bit 7 of the saved character is set, otherwise finds the object's place in the table and checks the display flag.
  $C4D1,$08 Jump ahead to #R$C583 if bit 7 of flags: Saved Character (#R$A033) is present.
N $C4D9 Calculate object table offset.
  $C4D9,$03 Call #R$A2B1 to find the object's place in the table.
  $C4DC,$06 Call #R$A2BD to extract object attributes.
  $C4E2,$06 Call #R$C493 to test bits 3 and 7 of the object attribute, then call #R$C4B2 to test bits 2 and 6.
  $C4E8,$04 Clear the parser.
N $C4EC None of the bits #N$0C are set in the object attribute value?
  $C4EC,$07 Go to #R$C53A if bit 2 and bit 3 of the object attribute are clear.
  $C4F3,$07 Jump to #R$C502 if the display mode is not #N$05.
  $C4FA,$08 Jump to #R$CB41 if the room display parameter is not #N$FE.
@ $C502 label=ProcessDisplayAction_Next
  $C502,$07 Jump to #R$C511 if the display mode is not #N$05.
  $C509,$08 Jump to #R$CB41 if the room graphics parameter is not #N$FE.
@ $C511 label=ProcessDisplayAction_DisplayModeZero
  $C511,$06 Jump to #R$C531 if the display mode is zero.
  $C517,$07 Jump to #R$C531 if the room display parameter is not #N$FF.
  $C51E,$02 Start the count at #N$FF so the first step is #N$00.
@ $C520 label=ProcessDisplayAction_Loop
  $C520,$05 Count one more and return if #R$C463 finds the last room for this display mode.
  $C525,$03 Record the room display parameter.
  $C528,$05 Call #R$C55A to run display action.
  $C52D,$02 Go to #R$C53A if #R$C55A found a picture.
  $C52F,$02 This picture did not match, so try the next one from #R$C520.

N $C531 Accept the graphics parameter, and return when it is empty.
@ $C531 label=ProcessDisplayAction_AcceptGraphicsParameter
  $C531,$05 Go to #R$C53A if the graphics parameter is not accepted.
  $C536,$04 Return if the graphics count has reached its limit.
@ $C53A label=ProcessDisplayAction_RunObjectDisplay
  $C53A,$08 Set the parser flag to #N$01 and draw the object from #R$C929.

c $C542 Accept The Graphics Parameter
@ $C542 label=AcceptGraphicsParameter
D $C542 Returns if the saved marker is clear. Otherwise accepts the graphics parameter unless
. the object is already marked.
  $C542,$05 Return if the display mode is zero.
  $C547,$07 Jump to #R$C558 if the room graphics parameter is not #N$FF.
N $C54E Bit 1 of the object attribute value is set?
  $C54E,$07 Go to #R$C558 if bit 1 of the object attribute is set.
  $C555,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C557,$01 Return.

@ $C558 label=AcceptGraphicsParameter_SignalStepFailed
  $C558,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $C559,$01 Return.

c $C55A Record The Result In The Room Graphics
@ $C55A label=RecordResultRoomGraphics
D $C55A Goes to #R$C929 when the graphics parameter is not accepted.
  $C55A,$06 Go to #R$C929 if the graphics parameter is not accepted.

c $C560 Record The Result In The Room Graphics
@ $C560 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_CountCountRemaining
D $C560 Counts from #N$FF and stores each step in the room graphics parameter until the count runs out.
  $C560,$02 Start the count at #N$FF so the first step is #N$00.
@ $C562 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_TestCountHasReached
  $C562,$06 Jump to #R$C570 if the count has reached zero.
  $C568,$06 Store the room graphics parameter and call #R$C929.
  $C56E,$02 Jump back to #R$C562 while the object has nothing to display.
@ $C570 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_Return
  $C570,$01 Return.

c $C571 Copy The Record Flag Nibble
@ $C571 label=CopyRecordFlagNibble
D $C571 Copies the low nibble of the record flags to #R$A00F.
  $C571,$08 Copy the low nibble of the record flags to #R$A00F.
  $C579,$01 Return.

c $C57A Copy The Record's Value Nibble
@ $C57A label=CopyRecordValueNibble
D $C57A Copies the low nibble of the record's value byte to #R$A010.
  $C57A,$08 Copy the low nibble of the record's value byte to #R$A010.
  $C582,$01 Return.

c $C583 Look Up The Display Record For The Moved Marker
@ $C583 label=LookUpDisplayRecordForMovedMarker
D $C583 Turns the moved marker into an index and reads the display record address
. from #R$9FAB.
  $C583,$08 Keep bits 0-6 of the marker as the index and point #REGhl at #R$9FAB.
  $C58B,$06 Add twice the index and read the display record address into #REGhl.

c $C591 Process Display Record From Script
@ $C591 label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript
D $C591 #REGhl points at a display record. Copies the low nibbles of its flags and
. value byte to #R$A00F/#R$A010, clears #R$A00B and checks the record against the
. current room before drawing it.
  $C591,$06 Point #REGix at the record and copy the low nibble of its flags to #R$A00F
. via #R$C571.
  $C597,$03 Copy the low nibble of the value byte via #R$C57A.
  $C59A,$07 Clear the parser.
  $C5A1,$03 Take the display mode.
  $C5A4,$03 Merge the flag at #R$A010, and go to #R$C5F4 when both flags are clear.
  $C5A7,$07 Jump to #R$C5B2 if the display mode is #N$05.
  $C5AE,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C5BA if the display mode is not #N$06.
@ $C5B2 label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_CompareRoom
  $C5B2,$08 Jump to #R$CB41 if the room display parameter is not #N$FE.
@ $C5BA label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_DisplayMode
  $C5BA,$07 Jump to #R$C5C5 if the display mode is #N$05.
  $C5C1,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C5CD if the display mode is not #N$06.
@ $C5C5 label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_TestGraphics
  $C5C5,$08 Jump to #R$CB41 if the room graphics parameter is not #N$FE.
@ $C5CD label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_DisplayModeZero
  $C5CD,$06 Jump to #R$C5EB if the display mode is zero.
  $C5D3,$07 Jump to #R$C5EB if the room display parameter is not #N$FF.
  $C5DA,$02 Start the picture count at zero.
@ $C5DC label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_TestDisplay
  $C5DC,$04 Return if the count has reached the graphics limit.
  $C5E0,$06 Store it in the room display parameter, then call #R$C60D to run display
. action.
  $C5E6,$02 Go to #R$C5F4 if #R$C60D found a picture.
  $C5E8,$03 Try the next display value from #R$C5DC.

N $C5EB Accept a set graphics parameter, and return when it is empty.
@ $C5EB label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_AcceptGraphics
  $C5EB,$05 Go to #R$C5F4 if the set graphics parameter is not accepted.
  $C5F0,$04 Return if the graphics count has reached its limit.
@ $C5F4 label=ProcessDisplayRecordFromScript_RunObjectDisplay
  $C5F4,$08 Set the parser flag at #R$A00B to #N$01 and continue at #R$C989.

c $C5FC Accept A Set Graphics Parameter
@ $C5FC label=AcceptSetGraphicsParameter
D $C5FC Returns if the saved marker is clear. Otherwise accepts a graphics parameter that is
. still set.
  $C5FC,$05 Return if the display mode is zero.
  $C601,$07 Jump to #R$C60B if the room graphics parameter is not #N$FF.
  $C608,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C60A,$01 Return.

@ $C60B label=AcceptSetGraphicsParameter_SignalStepFailedAgain
  $C60B,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $C60C,$01 Return.

c $C60D Record The Result In The Room Graphics
@ $C60D label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_ClearHighByte
D $C60D Goes to #R$C989 when the set graphics parameter is not accepted.
  $C60D,$06 Go to #R$C989 if the set graphics parameter is not accepted.

c $C613 Record The Result In The Room Graphics
@ $C613 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_CountGraphicsSteps
D $C613 Counts from zero and stores the room graphics parameter until the graphics limit.
  $C613,$03 Save #REGbc and start the graphics count at zero.
@ $C616 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_TestDisplayParameter
  $C616,$05 Go to #R$C626 if the count has reached the graphics limit.
  $C61B,$06 Store it in the room graphics parameter, then call #R$C989 to run object
. display with pointer.
  $C621,$02 Jump ahead to #R$C626 to return if #R$C989 does not return #N$00.
  $C623,$03 Try the next graphics value from #R$C616.

N $C626 Return.
@ $C626 label=RecordResultRoomGraphics_Save
  $C626,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $C627,$01 Return.

c $C628 Test The Character Field Of The Display Record
@ $C628 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter
D $C628 Tests the low nibble of the record flags against the room and the object.
N $C628 Low nibble of the record flags is clear?
  $C628,$07 Read the low nibble of the record flags and jump ahead to #R$C635 when it is not zero.
@ $C62F label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_ReportMatch
  $C62F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C631,$01 Return.

N $C632 The character field did not match.
@ $C632 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_GiveUp
  $C632,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.

N $C635 For a picture code from 5 up to but not including 7, reads the room chosen for the picture.
@ $C635 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_CodeFromFive
  $C635,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C660 if the low nibble of the record flags is below #N$05.
  $C639,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C682 if the low nibble of the record flags is at least #N$07.
  $C63D,$07 Jump to #R$C654 if the room display parameter is not #N$FE.
  $C644,$03 Read the display pointer.
  $C647,$09 Jump to #R$C65A unless the low nibble of the record flags is #N$05.
  $C650,$04 Jump back to #R$C62F if bit 7 is clear.
@ $C654 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_ClearFlag
  $C654,$06 Clear the byte at #R$A021 and return zero through #R$C632.

N $C65A Bit 7 of the room pointer selects the next picture.
@ $C65A label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_TestTopBit
  $C65A,$04 Jump back to #R$C62F while bit 7 is present.
  $C65E,$02 Bit 7 of the room pointer is clear, so give up from #R$C654.

N $C660 For a code below 3, looks the room up and accepts it when the top flag is set.
@ $C660 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_CodeBelowThree
  $C660,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C670 if the low nibble of the record flags is at least #N$03.
  $C664,$06 Read the room display parameter, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $C66A,$06 Jump to #R$C654 if a marker on the record's flag byte is clear.
@ $C670 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_TestMarker
  $C670,$06 Jump to #R$C62F if bit 0 of the record flags is clear.
  $C676,$06 Read the room display parameter and call #R$D471 to find an object by field two.
  $C67C,$04 Jump back to #R$C654 if the entry is the end marker.
  $C680,$02 The entry is not the end marker, so return non-zero from #R$C62F.

N $C682 Read the room display parameter, record it in the room parameter at #R$CB7A and keep it in #REGc.
@ $C682 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_UseRoom
  $C682,$07 Read the room display parameter, store it in the room parameter at #R$CB7A and keep it in #REGc.
  $C689,$08 Test the record flags through #R$C7A6 and go to #R$C654 if it returns zero.
  $C691,$06 Jump to #R$C62F if bit 4 of the record flags is clear.
  $C697,$06 Read record bytes #N$02 and #N$03.
  $C69D,$03 Read record byte #N$04 as the high byte.
  $C6A0,$06 Jump to #R$C6AF unless bit 6 of the record flags is set.
  $C6A6,$06 With a handler word present, read record bytes #N$04-#N$06 instead.
  $C6AC,$03 Read record byte #N$06 as the high byte.
@ $C6AF label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_ShiftNibble
  $C6AF,$08 Shift the parameter down by four bits and call #R$C74E to test the object
. attribute.
  $C6B7,$01 Return if #R$C74E does not match the attribute.
@ $C6B8 label=TestLowBitsOfCharacter_MarkMatched
  $C6B8,$05 Write #N$01 to the byte at #R$A021.
  $C6BD,$01 Return.

c $C6BE Test The Graphics Field Of The Display Record
@ $C6BE label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics
D $C6BE Tests the low nibble of the record's value byte against the room graphics.
N $C6BE Low nibble of the record's value byte is clear?
  $C6BE,$07 Read the low nibble of the record's value byte and jump ahead to #R$C6CB when it is not zero.
@ $C6C5 label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_ReportMatch
  $C6C5,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C6C7,$01 Return.
  $C6C8,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.

N $C6CB For a graphics code from 5 up to but not including 7, reads the room chosen for the graphics.
@ $C6CB label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_CodeFromFive
  $C6CB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C6F5 if the low nibble of the value byte is below #N$05.
  $C6CF,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C719 if the low nibble of the value byte is at least #N$07.
  $C6D3,$08 Jump to #R$C654 if the room graphics parameter is not #N$FE.
  $C6DB,$03 Read the display pointer.
  $C6DE,$09 Jump to #R$C6EE unless the low nibble of the value byte is #N$05.
  $C6E7,$04 Jump back to #R$C6C5 if bit 7 is clear.
  $C6EB,$03 This picture does not match, so give up from #R$C654.

N $C6EE Bit 7 of the graphics pointer selects the next picture.
@ $C6EE label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_TestTopBit
  $C6EE,$04 Jump back to #R$C6C5 while bit 7 is present.
  $C6F2,$03 Bit 7 of the graphics pointer is clear, so give up from #R$C654.

N $C6F5 For a graphics code below 3, looks the room up and accepts it when the top flag is set.
@ $C6F5 label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_CodeBelowThree
  $C6F5,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C706 if the low nibble of the value byte is at least #N$03.
  $C6F9,$06 Read the room graphics parameter, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $C6FF,$07 Jump to #R$C654 if a marker on the record's flag byte is clear.
@ $C706 label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_TestMarker
  $C706,$06 Jump to #R$C6C5 if bit 0 of the value byte is clear.
  $C70C,$06 Read the room graphics parameter and call #R$D471 to find an object by field two.
  $C712,$05 Jump back to #R$C654 if the entry is the end marker.
  $C717,$02 The entry is not the end marker, so return non-zero from #R$C6C5.

N $C719 Read the room graphics parameter and keep it for the picture.
@ $C719 label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_UseGraphics
  $C719,$07 Read the room graphics parameter, store it in the room parameter at #R$CB7A and keep it in #REGc.
  $C720,$07 Return if #R$C7A6 returns zero.
  $C727,$07 Jump to #R$C62F if bit 4 of the record flags is clear.
  $C72E,$06 Read record bytes #N$02 and #N$05.
  $C734,$03 Read record byte #N$06.
  $C737,$06 Jump to #R$C746 unless bit 6 of the record flags is set.
  $C73D,$06 With a handler word present, read record bytes #N$04 and #N$07 instead.
  $C743,$03 Read record byte #N$08.
@ $C746 label=TestLowBitsOfGraphics_TestAttribute
  $C746,$04 Use that byte as the count and call #R$C74E to test the object attribute.
  $C74A,$01 Return if #R$C74E does not match the attribute.
  $C74B,$03 The attribute matched, so record it from #R$C6B8.

c $C74E Read The Object Attribute
@ $C74E label=ReadObjectAttribute_LooksObjectFolds
D $C74E Looks the object up and folds its attribute into the two-bit count.
  $C74E,$04 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C752,$03 Read the object's attribute into #REGc.
  $C755,$09 Shift #REGb down by two bits, keep the low two, put #REGl in #REGd and call #R$C763.
  $C75E,$01 Return if the source test returns zero.
  $C75F,$04 Keep the bits in #N$03.

c $C763 Read The High Byte Of The Source
@ $C763 label=ReadHighByteSource
D $C763 Compares the high byte of the source with the bits set in the count.
  $C763,$02 Jump to #R$C76D when the folded attribute is not zero.
  $C765,$01 Take the byte in #REGd.
  $C766,$01 Keep only the bits that are set in the count.
  $C767,$01 See whether the two values match.
  $C768,$03 Go to #R$C62F if the masked attribute matches.
  $C76B,$02 The masked attribute does not match, so go to #R$C792 to keep only the bits of #REGd outside the mask.

N $C76D For command 1, keeps the matching bits of the high byte.
@ $C76D label=ReadHighByteSource_CommandOne
  $C76D,$02 See whether the attribute is #N$01.
  $C76F,$02 Jump to #R$C77B when the attribute is not one.
  $C771,$01 See whether any attribute bits are still set.
  $C772,$01 Clear the carry before testing the attribute mask.
  $C773,$02 Jump to #R$C768 when the attribute mask is clear.
  $C775,$03 Go to #R$C795 if the attribute mask matches nothing.
  $C778,$03 Jump back to #R$C62F to return non-zero.

N $C77B For command 2, gives up when none of the selected bits are set.
@ $C77B label=ReadHighByteSource_CommandTwo
  $C77B,$02 See whether the attribute is #N$02.
  $C77D,$02 Jump to #R$C788 when the attribute is not two.
  $C77F,$05 Go to #R$C62F to return zero if the attribute mask matches nothing.
  $C784,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C797 to store it in the token select flag and read the parser.

N $C788 Read the high byte of the source, then keep only the bits that are set in the
. count, then see whether the two values match.
@ $C788 label=ReadHighByteSource_HighByteSource
  $C788,$06 Go to #R$C62F if the mask does not cover the attribute.
  $C78E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C797 to store it in the token select flag and read the parser.

@ $C792 label=ReadHighByteSource_FlipBitsResult
  $C792,$03 Invert the mask and keep the bits that are also set in #REGd.
@ $C795 label=ReadHighByteSource_TestWorkspaceKeepingBits
  $C795,$05 Store it in the token select flag at #R$CB79, keeping bits in #N$7F.
@ $C797 label=ReadHighByteSource_Store
  $C79A,$07 Jump to #R$C62F if the parser is zero.
  $C7A1,$03 Use #R$CB7B as the pointer.
  $C7A4,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $C7A5,$01 Return.

c $C7A6 Test For The Seventh Marker
@ $C7A6 label=TestSeventhMarker
D $C7A6 Keeps bits 0-3 and tests the seventh and eighth markers.
  $C7A6,$07 Jump back to #R$C62F if the low nibble is command #N$07.
  $C7AD,$04 Go to #R$C7BB when the command is not #N$08.
  $C7B1,$04 Take the count, then call #R$C821 to test object location match.
  $C7B5,$03 Go to #R$C632 if the object is not in this location.
  $C7B8,$03 Jump back to #R$C62F to return non-zero.

N $C7BB For command 9, checks the low count and gives up when that check fails.
@ $C7BB label=TestSeventhMarker_CommandNine
  $C7BB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C7E4 if the command is not #N$09.
  $C7BF,$07 Go to #R$C632 if the object is not in this location.
  $C7C6,$07 Go to #R$C632 if the record's first byte is not #N$01.
  $C7CD,$07 Go to #R$C632 if the object has a special description.
  $C7D4,$07 Read the character being processed, then call #R$C8C6 to run object attribute.
  $C7DB,$03 Go to #R$C632 if the attribute comparison borrows.
  $C7DE,$06 Jump back to #R$C768 to run display action.

N $C7E4 For command 10, checks the low count and gives up when that check fails.
@ $C7E4 label=TestSeventhMarker_CommandTen
  $C7E4,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C7F9 if the command is not #N$0A.
  $C7E8,$07 Go to #R$C632 if the object is not in this location.
  $C7EF,$07 Jump to #R$C632 if #R$D275 does not match this character.
  $C7F6,$03 Jump back to #R$C62F to return non-zero.

N $C7F9 For command 11, checks the low count and gives up when that check fails.
@ $C7F9 label=TestSeventhMarker_CommandEleven
  $C7F9,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C80A if the command is not #N$0B.
  $C7FD,$07 Go to #R$C632 if the object is not in this location.
  $C804,$06 Call #R$C8A7 for the object in #REGc and jump to #R$C7B5.

N $C80A For command 12, follows the object that contains this one.
@ $C80A label=TestSeventhMarker_CommandTwelve
  $C80A,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C814 if the command is not #N$0C.
  $C80E,$06 Call #R$C8BE and jump back to #R$C7B5, which gives up when the special description is clear.

N $C814 The object is in the room, so follow whatever contains it.
@ $C814 label=TestSeventhMarker_MatchObjectThenRoom
  $C814,$07 Give up on this object when it is not in the room, otherwise follow the object that
. contains it.
  $C81B,$06 Call #R$C8BE and jump back to #R$C7B5, which gives up when the special description is clear.

c $C821 Check Object Location Match
@ $C821 label=CheckObjectLocationMatch
E $C821 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9E40 }
. LIST#
D $C821 Looks the object up and clears the zero flag when its location matches the current character.
R $C821 A Object ID
R $C821 O:F Zero flag clear when the location matches
  $C821,$06 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C827,$07 Keep #REGa in #REGb and call #R$C863 to read the current character's room.
  $C82E,$03 Go to #R$C85D if the character's room is this object.
  $C831,$06 Jump ahead to #R$C85A if the object-attribute flag is clear.
  $C837,$06 Exchange #REGa with #REGb and call #R$C863 to read that object's room.
  $C83D,$07 Go to #R$C85D if this object's room is the character being processed.
  $C844,$04 Go to #R$C85A if the two locations differ.
  $C848,$03 Report a match at #R$C85D unless both locations are #N$FF.
  $C84B,$03 Read the record's flag byte.
  $C84E,$03 Read the character attribute.
  $C851,$05 Jump ahead to #R$C85D if it matches the character attribute.
  $C856,$02 Step to the next byte of the object.
  $C858,$02 Count the next object and repeat from #R$C851.
@ $C85A label=CheckObjectLocationMatch_Next
  $C85A,$03 Set the zero flag and jump ahead to #R$C85F.

@ $C85D label=CheckObjectLocationMatch_TestStepSucceedsScript
  $C85D,$02 Clear the zero flag to report that the character attribute was found.
@ $C85F label=CheckObjectLocationMatch_BringSavedCount
  $C85F,$03 Restore the saved count and the saved character record from the stack.
  $C862,$01 Return.

c $C863 Get Object Location
@ $C863 label=GetObjectLocation
D $C863 Looks the object up and returns the room it is in.
R $C863 A Object ID
R $C863 O:A Location ID
  $C863,$05 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C868,$05 Call #R$C86E to check object mother.
  $C86D,$01 Return.

c $C86E Check Object Mother
@ $C86E label=CheckObjectMother
E $C86E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9E7A }
. LIST#
D $C86E Follows the moved marker until #N$FF and leaves the id it stopped on in the alternate accumulator.
  $C86E,$05 Switch to the saved result and take the end marker.
@ $C873 label=CheckObjectMother_Loop
  $C873,$08 Jump to #R$C895 if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $C87B,$07 Call #R$D237 to look up the current object.
  $C882,$06 Jump ahead to #R$C894 if the object-attribute flag is clear.
N $C888 An object flag is set?
  $C888,$06 Jump to #R$C873 if an object flag is set.
  $C88E,$04 See whether bit 3 of the object attribute is set.
  $C892,$02 Jump to #R$C873 when this object's attribute bit is set.
@ $C894 label=CheckObjectMother_Next
  $C894,$01 Switch to the saved result.
  $C895,$02 Restore the saved character record from the stack.
  $C897,$01 Return.
  $C898,$06 Run #R$C89E, then run #R$C86E.

c $C89E Exchange The Character Record With The Schedule
@ $C89E label=ExchangeCharacterRecordSchedule
D $C89E Return.
  $C89E,$08 Swap the room record with the object record.
  $C8A6,$01 Return.

c $C8A7 Get Object Attribute
@ $C8A7 label=GetObjectAttribute
D $C8A7 Looks the object up and returns one less than its first flag.
R $C8A7 A Object ID
R $C8A7 O:A One less than the record's first byte
  $C8A7,$03 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C8AA,$03 Read the record flag.
  $C8AD,$01 Count down one.
  $C8AE,$01 Return.

c $C8AF Get Object Mother ID
@ $C8AF label=GetObjectMotherID
D $C8AF Looks the object up and follows the object that contains it until the chain ends.
R $C8AF A Object ID
R $C8AF O:A Mother object ID (or #N$FF if none)
  $C8AF,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
@ $C8B2 label=GetObjectMotherID_Loop
  $C8B2,$06 Return if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $C8B8,$04 Return if the object has a special description.
  $C8BC,$02 The bit test returned zero, so read the next entry from #R$C8B2.

c $C8BE Test Object Has Special Description
@ $C8BE label=TestObjectHasSpecialDescription
D $C8BE Looks up the object and reports whether its attribute byte has the special-description
. marker.
R $C8BE A Object ID
R $C8BE O:F Zero flag clear when bit 6 of the attribute is set
  $C8BE,$03 Call #R$D237 to load the object data pointer.
  $C8C1,$04 See whether the special-description flag is set.
  $C8C5,$01 Return.

c $C8C6 Process Object Attribute
@ $C8C6 label=ProcessObjectAttribute_LookObjectRun
D $C8C6 Looks the object up, then reads its attribute through #R$D2F2.
  $C8C6,$06 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C8CC,$04 Take the count, then call #R$D2F2 to get object attribute value.
  $C8D0,$05 Add the object's third parameter, and go to #R$C8D7 when the sum does not carry.
  $C8D5,$02 Use #N$FF when the sum overflowed.
  $C8D7,$06 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C8DD,$04 Take the count, then call #R$D2F2 to get object attribute value.
  $C8E1,$06 Subtract the object's third parameter, negate #REGa and subtract #REGb.
  $C8E7,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $C8E8,$02 Go to #R$C8ED if that subtraction borrows.
  $C8EA,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C8EC,$01 Return.

@ $C8ED label=ProcessObjectAttribute_SignalStepFailed
  $C8ED,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $C8EE,$01 Return.

c $C8EF Set Object Pointers
@ $C8EF label=SetObjectPointers
D $C8EF Chooses a room record or an object record from the picture parameter.
  $C8EF,$07 Jump to #R$C90C if the room display parameter is at least #N$FE.
  $C8F6,$05 Compare *#R$A00F with #N$05.
  $C8FB,$05 Read the room display parameter and jump ahead to #R$C905 if the display mode is at least #N$05.
  $C900,$05 Call #R$D224 to look up this room.

N $C905 Record the character record in the current object, then read the room graphics
. parameter, then check for the other end marker, and return if it reaches the limit.
@ $C905 label=SetObjectPointers_Next
  $C905,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
@ $C908 label=SetObjectPointers_RememberRoomRecord
  $C908,$04 Remember the room record and read the room graphics parameter.
@ $C90C label=SetObjectPointers_RoomGraphicsParameter
  $C90C,$06 Return if the room graphics parameter is at least #N$FE.
  $C912,$05 Compare *#R$A010 with #N$05.
  $C917,$05 Load the room graphics parameter at #R$A035 and jump to #R$C921 if the display
. mode is at least #N$05.
  $C91C,$05 Call #R$D224 to look up this room.

N $C921 Look the object up before returning.
@ $C921 label=SetObjectPointers_LookObjectBefore
  $C921,$03 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $C924,$04 Remember this character record in the secondary pointer.
  $C928,$01 Return.

c $C929 Process Object Display Logic
@ $C929 label=ProcessObjectDisplayLogic
D $C929 Prepares the room or object named for the picture, and skips it when the record is not marked
. for display.
N $C929 Set object pointers.
  $C929,$03 Call #R$C8EF to point at this object's records.
  $C92C,$07 Jump to #R$C972 if the room display parameter is at least #N$FE.
  $C933,$04 Read the current character record.
  $C937,$06 Jump to #R$C94D if the special-description flag is clear.
N $C93D No matching handler?
  $C93D,$06 Jump ahead to #R$C94D if #R$D1DC finds no handler.
  $C943,$06 Read the saved character and call #R$D349 to search the two-byte table.
  $C949,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C960 if the entry is not the end marker.
@ $C94D label=ProcessObjectDisplayLogic_Next
  $C94D,$07 Jump to #R$C972 if the display mode is below #N$07.
  $C954,$0A Call #R$D1DC to calculate handler offset.
  $C95E,$02 Jump ahead to #R$C972 if #R$D1DC finds no handler.
@ $C960 label=ProcessObjectDisplayLogic_MovedMarker
  $C960,$06 Read the moved marker.
  $C966,$03 Use the lookup table: object display data at #R$AA71.
  $C969,$09 Index the word table at #R$AA71 and draw that record from #R$C996.

N $C972 Read the saved character, then use the display records at #R$9F38 as the character
. record, then check for the end marker.
@ $C972 label=ProcessObjectDisplayLogic_SavedCharacterMove
  $C972,$0A Call #R$D34E to search three-byte length table.
  $C97C,$05 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the entry is the end marker.
  $C981,$08 Read the display-record pointer and draw it from #R$C996.

c $C989 Process Object Display With Pointer
@ $C989 label=ProcessObjectDisplayWithPointer
R $C989 IX Object pointer
D $C989 Prepares the record and draws it, then restores the character that was on screen.
  $C989,$07 Call #R$C8EF to set object pointers.
  $C990,$05 Call #R$C996 to draw the record.
  $C995,$01 Return.

c $C996 Process Object Display Entry
@ $C996 label=ProcessObjectDisplayEntry
D $C996 Draws the record, and tells the script to continue when the draw works. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $C996,$07 Call #R$C9BF to draw this record.
  $C99D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$C9A7 if bit 7 of the display record is set.
  $C9A1,$03 Go to #R$CB43 if drawing the object display matched.
  $C9A4,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C9A6,$01 Return.

N $C9A7 Read the pointer at the currently processed character command position at
. #R$9FD9, then take a count of #N$01, then read the character record from the currently
. processed character timetable pointer at #R$9FDB.
@ $C9A7 label=ProcessObjectDisplayEntry_Next
  $C9A7,$0A Read the currently processed character command position and count #N$01 for run
. object display entry, then call #R$CF80 to allocate memory and store character.
  $C9B1,$06 Read the current character timetable pointer.
  $C9B7,$03 Store the moved marker.
  $C9BA,$04 Store #REGh as the location, set the carry.
  $C9BE,$01 Return.

c $C9BF Process Display Record
@ $C9BF label=ProcessDisplayRecord
D $C9BF #REGhl points at a display record (flags, value, optional handler word,
. optional five extra bytes). Checks the record against the current object,
. character and graphics, runs its handler if bit 6 is set, and then either
. returns the address after the record (bit 7 set) or runs the code that follows
. it via #R$CA64. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $C9BF,$06 Point #REGiy at the display record and call #R$D078 to check whether
. the object is visible.
  $C9C5,$02 Go to #R$C9D3 if #R$D078 finds the object is not visible.
N $C9C7 Bit 5 of the record flags is clear?
  $C9C7,$07 Jump to #R$C9D3 if bit 5 of the record flags is clear.
  $C9CE,$03 Point #REGhl at the failure step at #R$CBBB.
@ $C9D1 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_Fail
  $C9D1,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $C9D2,$01 Return.

N $C9D3 Test the character field, the graphics field and the handler bit.
@ $C9D3 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_TestHandler
  $C9D3,$05 Go to #R$C9D1 if the character test on this record fails.
  $C9D8,$05 Go to #R$C9D1 if the graphics test on this record fails.
  $C9DD,$06 Skip ahead to #R$C9F2 unless bit 6 of the record flags is set.
  $C9E3,$08 Read the handler word at bytes #N$02/#N$03 of the record and save
. #REGiy.
  $C9EB,$03 Run the handler via #R$CB34.
  $C9EE,$02 Restore #REGiy from the stack.
  $C9F0,$02 Go to #R$C9D1 if the handler returns zero.
@ $C9F2 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_TestParser
  $C9F2,$06 Jump to #R$C9FB if the parser flag at #R$A00B is set.
  $C9F8,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $C9FA,$01 Return.

N $C9FB Take the high nibble of the record's value byte (#N$01) as the count and
. store it at #R$A01F.
@ $C9FB label=ProcessDisplayRecord_StoreCount
  $C9FB,$05 Save the record pointer and read the value byte (#N$01).
  $CA00,$04 Shift that byte down by four bits.
  $CA04,$02 Keep the high nibble.
  $CA06,$01 Use that nibble as the low byte of the count.
  $CA07,$02 Clear the high byte.
  $CA09,$03 Store the count at #R$A01F.
@ $CA0C label=ProcessDisplayRecord_ComputeLength
  $CA0C,$01 Restore the record address from the stack.
  $CA0D,$04 Step over the two-byte header.
  $CA11,$06 Skip ahead to #R$CA18 unless bit 6 of the record flags is set.
  $CA17,$01 Step over the handler word.
@ $CA18 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_TestFiveExtraBytes
  $CA18,$09 Skip ahead to #R$CA22 unless bit 4 of the record flags is set.
  $CA21,$01 Step over the five extra bytes.
@ $CA22 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_SaveAttributes
  $CA22,$06 Read the character attribute and store it in the workspace byte at #R$C491.
  $CA28,$04 Point #REGix at the current object.
  $CA2C,$07 Read the object's attribute and store it in the workspace byte at #R$C492,
. then save the address after the record.
  $CA33,$0A Save the record, store #N$01 in the parser flag at #R$A00C, and call
. #R$CA76.
  $CA3D,$03 Restore the pointers from the stack.
  $CA40,$06 And skip ahead to #R$CA48 unless bit 7 of the record flags is set.
  $CA46,$01 Set the carry.
  $CA47,$01 Return with #REGhl pointing past the record, so the script resumes there.

N $CA48 Fill a location byte that is still #N$FF from the room parameters.
@ $CA48 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_FillLocationLow
  $CA48,$06 Go to #R$CA54 unless the low byte of #R$9FD7 is #N$FF.
  $CA4E,$06 Copy the room display parameter into #R$9FD7.
@ $CA54 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_FillLocationHigh
  $CA54,$06 Go to #R$CA60 unless the high byte of #R$9FD7 is #N$FF.
  $CA5A,$06 Copy the room graphics parameter into the high byte of #R$9FD7.
@ $CA60 label=ProcessDisplayRecord_RunFollowingCode
  $CA60,$07 Clear the flag at #R$CAAE and run the code that follows the record
. (at #REGhl) via #R$CB2C.
  $CA67,$05 Call #R$CA7F, saving result and the pointer.
  $CA6C,$07 Call #R$CAAF if the room flag at #R$CAAE is clear.
  $CA73,$01 Restore the pointer from the stack.
  $CA74,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $CA75,$01 Return.

c $CA76 Read The Saved Character
@ $CA76 label=ReadSavedCharacter
D $CA76 Call #R$A2E4 if bit 7 of the saved character is clear.
  $CA76,$08 Call #R$A2E4 if bit 7 of the saved character is clear.
  $CA7E,$01 Return.

c $CA7F Read The Character Timetable
@ $CA7F label=ReadCharacterTimetable
D $CA7F Reads the character timetable and returns when Sherlock's disguise matches the character attribute.
  $CA7F,$06 Call #R$D237 to look up the character at #R$9860.
  $CA85,$03 Read Sherlock's disguise.
  $CA88,$03 See whether it matches the character attribute.
  $CA8B,$01 Return if they match.
  $CA8C,$07 Save the current character and read the character at #R$9860.
  $CA93,$03 Record the current character.
  $CA96,$04 Call #R$CA9E to dispatch on the schedule marker.
  $CA9A,$03 Record the current character.
  $CA9D,$01 Return.

c $CA9E Dispatch On The Schedule Marker
@ $CA9E label=DispatchScheduleMarker
D $CA9E Reads the schedule entry and runs the object display when its marker is set.
  $CA9E,$06 Read the room at #R$DD01 and the record at #R$DCFF.
  $CAA4,$05 Go to #R$D1B2 if bit 6 of this byte is set.
  $CAA9,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D102 to run location token display.

g $CAAE Room Flag
D $CAAE Set by a display record's code to skip the disguise check at #R$CAAF.
. Cleared by #R$CA60, tested by #R$CA6C, and set by #R$DF4B, #R$E108 and #R$E140.
B $CAAE,$01

c $CAAF Process Object Display Entry
@ $CAAF label=CheckCharacterDisguise
D $CAAF Checks this character against Sherlock's disguise and returns when the disguise already matches.
  $CAAF,$03 Call #R$DC6C to check the character id.
  $CAB2,$01 Return if the character is #N$00, #N$01 or Sherlock.
  $CAB3,$03 Read the character id saved for the disguise check.
  $CAB6,$03 Use Sherlock's disguise as the pointer.
  $CAB9,$01 See whether it matches Sherlock's disguise.
  $CABA,$01 Return if they match.
  $CABB,$06 Call #R$D495 to read the object's first location.
  $CAC1,$06 Jump ahead to #R$CAD0 if this is not Sherlock's disguise.
  $CAC7,$09 Read the character being processed and take the exits message, then call #R$CBC2 to
. run character display.
@ $CAD0 label=CheckCharacterDisguise_DisplayModeBelow
  $CAD0,$06 Return if the display mode is below #N$07.
  $CAD6,$06 Call #R$D495 to read the object's first location.
  $CADC,$04 Go to #R$CB12 when this is not the #N$FF end marker.
  $CAE0,$06 Read the record's first byte into #REGb and read Sherlock's disguise.
@ $CAE6 label=CheckCharacterDisguise_TestAttributeMatch
  $CAE6,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CAF0 if this id matches the character attribute.
  $CAEB,$04 Count the next object and repeat from #R$CAE6.
  $CAEF,$01 Return.

N $CAF0 Read the character being processed, then store the result in the count, then
. mark the entry as finished.
@ $CAF0 label=CheckCharacterDisguise_CharacterBeingProcessed
  $CAF0,$06 Read the current character.
  $CAF6,$03 Record the current character.
  $CAF9,$05 Set the television flag to #N$01.
  $CAFE,$06 Read Sherlock's disguise and call #R$D471 to find an object by field two.
  $CB04,$05 Call #R$CA76 if the entry is not the end marker.
  $CB09,$08 Store it in the system variables and store it in the character being processed.
  $CB11,$01 Return.

N $CB12 Read the other character id, compare it with Sherlock's disguise, and return when they match.
@ $CB12 label=CheckCharacterDisguise_OtherCharacterCompare
  $CB12,$03 Read the other character id saved for the disguise check.
  $CB15,$03 Use Sherlock's disguise as the pointer.
  $CB18,$01 See whether it matches Sherlock's disguise.
  $CB19,$01 Return if they match.
  $CB1A,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $CB1D,$03 Use the character being processed as the pointer.
  $CB20,$01 See whether it matches the character being processed.
  $CB21,$01 Return if they match.
  $CB22,$06 Point #REGde at the message at #R$68E9+#N$04 and jump to #R$CBC2.

g $CB28 Saved Index Registers
D $CB28 #R$CB34 saves #REGix here and #REGiy at #R$CB2A. #R$CB2C brings both back before the next display record runs.
. Used by #R$CB2C and #R$CB34.
W $CB28,$02
W $CB2A,$02 Read by #R$CB2C and #R$CB34.

c $CB2C Run Display Record Code
@ $CB2C label=RunDisplayRecordCode
D $CB2C Restores #REGix/#REGiy from #R$CB28/#R$CB2A, runs the code at #REGhl via
. #R$CB40, then saves #REGix/#REGiy again (falls through to #R$CB34).
  $CB2C,$08 Bring #REGix and #REGiy back from #R$CB28 and #R$CB2A.

c $CB34 Run Handler Keeping Pointers
D $CB34 Runs the code at #REGhl, then saves #REGix and #REGiy for the next record.
  $CB34,$03 Run the code at #REGhl via #R$CB40.
  $CB37,$08 Save #REGix and #REGiy for the next record.
  $CB3F,$01 Return.

c $CB40 Dispatch Looked-Up Handler
@ $CB40 label=DispatchLookedUpHandlerThird
D $CB40 Jumps to the handler the caller looked up.
  $CB40,$01 Jump to the handler address in #REGhl.

  $CB41,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $CB42,$01 Return.

c $CB43 Process Display With Location Check
@ $CB43 label=ProcessDisplayWithLocationCheck
D $CB43 Asks whether the character is here, and clears the character being processed when the answer is
. no.
  $CB43,$05 Call #R$DC6C to check character id.
  $CB48,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CB65 to return if the character is not zero, one, or Sherlock.
  $CB4A,$04 Clear the current character.
  $CB4E,$06 Jump to #R$CB5F if the parser is zero.
  $CB54,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CB67 if the pointer is not #N($0000,$04,$04).

c $CB58 Select Sherlock After Clearing The Parser
@ $CB58 label=SelectSherlock
D $CB58 Clears the room stored for the parser, checks the character on screen, and then makes
. Sherlock the character being processed.
  $CB58,$07 Clear the parser flag at #R$A00C and call #R$CA76.
@ $CB5F label=SelectSherlock_Loop
  $CB5F,$06 Record the current character.
@ $CB65 label=SelectSherlock_Next
  $CB65,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $CB66,$01 Return.

c $CB67 Report Success
@ $CB67 label=ReportSuccess
D $CB67 Tells the script this step succeeded.
  $CB67,$05 Take #N$01 and store it in the print mode.
  $CB6C,$07 Call #R$CB40 to save and restore pointers.
  $CB73,$06 Clear the flag at #R$A027 and finish from #R$CB5F.

g $CB79 Token Select Flag
D $CB79 Bit mask #R$CB7B rotates to pick a token word. Bit 7 selects the table at #R$C481. #R$CB7A keeps the room parameter copied back to #R$A034.
. Used by #R$C763 and #R$CB7B.
B $CB79,$01
B $CB7A,$01 Room parameter. Written by #R$C628 and #R$C6BE, and read by #R$CB7B.

c $CB7B Get Display Address Based On Flag
@ $CB7B label=GetDisplayAddressBasedOnFlag
D $CB7B Picks a token number from the graphics flag.
. Pointed at by #R$C7A1.
  $CB7B,$08 Read the token select flag at #R$CB79 and take the token table at #R$C471.
  $CB83,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CB88 if bit 7 of the token select flag is clear.
  $CB85,$03 Take the token table at #R$C481.
@ $CB88 label=GetDisplayAddressBasedOnFlag_Next
  $CB88,$02 Force a non-zero result so the caller continues.
@ $CB8A label=GetDisplayAddressBasedOnFlag_Loop
  $CB8A,$07 Read the next token number and repeat from #R$CB8A while this bit of the flag is
. clear.
  $CB91,$04 Jump back to #R$CB58 if this token word is zero.
  $CB95,$06 Record the room display parameter.
  $CB9B,$03 Use the introduction (#R$680B+#N$0C).
  $CB9E,$06 Call #R$A59F to print this message, then call #R$A887 to extract token from
. text.
  $CBA4,$03 Jump back to #R$A2A2 to print newline.

c $CBA7 Check Flag And Jump To Newline
@ $CBA7 label=CheckFlagAndJumpToNewline
D $CBA7 Returns when bit 6 of #R$B934 is clear. Otherwise clears the print mode, prints the introduction at #R$6813+#N$07 and jumps to #R$DC15.
  $CBA7,$03 Read the display flag at #R$B934.
  $CBAA,$02 See whether bit 6 of it is set.
  $CBAC,$01 Return if that marker is clear.
  $CBAD,$08 Start from zero, store it in the print mode and take the introduction message.
  $CBB5,$06 Print the message, then return zero through #R$DC15.
  $CBBB,$06 Take the cannot see message, then call #R$A59F to print the cannot see message.
  $CBC1,$01 Return.

c $CBC2 Process Character Display
@ $CBC2 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_TestDisplayFlagEnd
D $CBC2 See whether the display flag is the end marker before returning.
  $CBC2,$03 Return if the character is the end marker.
  $CBC5,$02 Return if the character is zero.
  $CBC7,$04 Call #R$D495 to read the object's first location.
  $CBCB,$01 Store the result in the low byte of the count.
  $CBCC,$03 Read Sherlock's disguise.
  $CBCF,$01 See whether the two values match.
  $CBD0,$01 Return if they differ.
  $CBD1,$06 Write #N$01 to the system Variables.
@ $CBD7 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_LookObject
  $CBD7,$04 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $CBDB,$07 Jump to #R$CBE5 if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $CBE2,$03 Jump back to #R$CBD7 to take the count, call #R$D237 to fetch object data pointer
. and read the moved marker.

N $CBE5 Point at the object attribute, remember the character, and switch to the one at #R$9860.
@ $CBE5 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_CountExchangeAddress
  $CBE5,$04 Take the count, then call #R$A4BC to point to object attribute byte.
  $CBE9,$05 Read the current character.
  $CBEE,$09 Call #R$CC29 to set character pointer and id.
  $CBF7,$04 Call #R$C821 to check object location match.
  $CBFB,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CC00 to set character pointer and id if #R$C821 does not return
. #N$00.
  $CBFF,$01 Return.

N $CC00 Put the saved character back and print the message.
@ $CC00 label=ProcessCharacterDisplay_ResultCharacterBeing
  $CC00,$07 Call #R$CC29 to set character pointer and id.
  $CC07,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $CC0A,$01 Return.

g $CC0B Script Command Counter
D $CC0B Count of script commands #R$CEB8 has run. #R$CC37 clears it, and #R$CC82 stops the script when it reaches #N$0A. #R$CC0D records whether the character was found in the display list.
. Used by #R$CC37, #R$CC82 and #R$CEB8.
B $CC0B,$01
B $CC0C,$01
B $CC0D,$01 Set by #R$CC37 and tested by #R$CC82.

c $CC0E Find Character
@ $CC0E label=FindCharacter
D $CC0E Searches the timetable at #R$9860 for the character id in #REGa.
R $CC0E A Character ID
R $CC0E O:A The character ID or #N$FF if it wasn't found
R $CC0E O:IY Pointer to character data
R $CC0E O:F The zero flag will be set if the character wasn't found
N $CC0E Stash some registers so they don't get corrupted.
  $CC0E,$03 Store the character ID in #REGc, saving source and the count.
  $CC11,$07 Use the timetable at #R$9860.
@ $CC18 label=FindCharacter_Loop
  $CC18,$03 Fetch the current characters ID from the timetable pointer.
  $CC1B,$03 Go to #R$CC26 if this timetable entry is the wanted character.
  $CC1E,$04 Go to #R$CC26 if it is #N$FF.
  $CC22,$02 Add #N$08 bytes to the timetable pointer to move to the next set
. of character data.
  $CC24,$02 Go back for the next character pair.

N $CC26 Return.
@ $CC26 label=FindCharacter_Return
N $CC26 Return with some housekeeping.
  $CC26,$02 Restore the saved count and the saved source from the stack.
  $CC28,$01 Return.

c $CC29 Set Character Pointer And ID
@ $CC29 label=SetCharacterPointerAndID
D $CC29 Finds the character being processed, and stores that record together with its room.
  $CC29,$06 Call #R$D495 to read the object's first location.
  $CC2F,$04 Remember the character record at #R$A017.
  $CC33,$03 Record the current character attribute.
  $CC36,$01 Return.

c $CC37 Process Character Action
@ $CC37 label=ProcessCharacterAction
R $CC37 A Character ID
D $CC37 Clears the script command counter at #R$CC0B, then runs #R$CC0E.
  $CC37,$05 Save the character id and clear the script command counter at #R$CC0B.
  $CC3C,$04 Call #R$CC0E to find character.
  $CC40,$02 Was the character ID found?.
  $CC42,$06 Take #N($0001,$04,$04) in #REGhl and jump ahead to #R$CC74 if #R$CC0E did not find the character.
N $CC48 The character is active in the timetable.
  $CC48,$04 Write the characters timetable pointer to *#R$9FDB.
  $CC4C,$03 Write the character ID to *#R$A036.
  $CC4F,$07 Go to #R$CC57 if this character is not in the display list.
  $CC56,$01 Count one more.
  $CC57,$03 Store it in the display-list flag at #R$CC0D.

c $CC5A Read The Character Timetable
@ $CC5A label=NoteDisguiseAndReadTimetable
D $CC5A Clears the previous result, notes whether Sherlock is using a disguise, and then reads the
. timetable.
  $CC5A,$03 Call #R$CC29 to point at this character and store its id.
  $CC5D,$03 Call #R$D045 to see whether Sherlock is wearing the China Man disguise.
  $CC60,$04 Clear *#SKOOLKID$5C3C(TV_FLAG) so that printing goes to the upper screen.
  $CC64,$08 Go to #R$CC71 if the object is not in this location.
  $CC6C,$05 Write #N$01 to *#SKOOLKID$5C3C(TV_FLAG).
@ $CC71 label=NoteDisguiseAndReadTimetable_Next
  $CC71,$03 Clear the pointer.
@ $CC74 label=NoteDisguiseAndReadTimetable_RecordCharacterScript
  $CC74,$03 Record the character script cycle.
  $CC77,$01 Return.

c $CC78 Script Interpreter
@ $CC78 label=ScriptInterpreter
D $CC78 Runs the current character's script from the timetable entry at #R$9FDB, one command at
. a time, until the script ends or yields.
R $CC78 A Character ID
  $CC78,$03 Call #R$CC5A to read this character's timetable.
  $CC7B,$07 Read the currently processed character timetable pointer, then call #R$D008 to get
. script pointer from timetable.

c $CC82 Dispatch The Character Script
@ $CC82 label=DispatchCharacterScript
D $CC82 Gets the character's script position and dispatches the next command.
  $CC82,$06 Get the character's script position.
  $CC88,$04 Clear the display.
  $CC8C,$08 Jump to #R$CD8B if the script command counter at #R$CC0B is #N$0A.
@ $CC94 label=DispatchCharacterScript_NextByte
  $CC94,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CC9B if this byte is not zero.
  $CC98,$03 Jump back to #R$CC94 to find next command.

N $CC9B Use the saved pointer as the character record, then record the pointer in the currently
. processed character command position at #R$9FD9, then keep bits 0-3.
@ $CC9B label=DispatchCharacterScript_UseCommand
N $CC9B Process the current script command.
  $CC9B,$06 Record the current character command position.
  $CCA1,$02,b$01 Keep the script command number (the low nibble).
  $CCA3,$04 Jump to #R$CCF3 when the command is at least #N$0A.
  $CCA7,$07 Go to #R$CCC7 unless the display-list flag at #R$CC0D is #N$01.
  $CCAE,$04 Clear the display-list flag at #R$CC0D.
N $CCB2 The room display asked to skip?
  $CCB2,$07 Read this script byte, call #R$B936, and jump to #R$CD91 if it returns carry.
  $CCB9,$02 Go to #R$CCC7 if #R$B936 returns zero.
N $CCBB Did #R$C4D1 accept this display?
  $CCBB,$04 Return if #R$C4D1 does not accept this display.
  $CCBF,$07 Call #R$CFB6 to find the character script, then write #N$02 into the object attribute.
  $CCC6,$01 Return.

N $CCC7 Reads one command byte from the character script and dispatches it. Command
. #N$07 refreshes the room display.
@ $CCC7 label=DispatchCharacterScript_DispatchOpcode
  $CCC7,$01 Get the command byte.
  $CCC8,$02,b$01 Extract the command type.
  $CCCA,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CE3B when the command is #N$07.
  $CCCF,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CD95 when the command is #N$06.
  $CCD4,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CDA1 when the command is #N$05.
  $CCD9,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CDBF when the command is #N$04.
  $CCDE,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CE6A when the command is #N$01.
  $CCE3,$05 Jump ahead to #R$CE4F when the command is #N$03.
  $CCE8,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CD24 when the command is #N$08.
  $CCEC,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CD47 when the command is #N$09.
  $CCF0,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE18 to handle display room description.

N $CCF3 Handles command Type #N$0E/#N$0C/#N$0B/#N$0A.
@ $CCF3 label=DispatchCharacterScript_TestGoto
R $CCF3 A Command type
  $CCF3,$04 Jump to #R$CD06 when the command is not #N$0E.
  $CCF7,$06 Copy #REGix+#N$01 into #REGiy+#N$01.
  $CCFD,$09 Copy the script position into the character record and parse again from #R$CC82.

N $CD06 Command #N$0C repeats, and command #N$0B copies the script pointer, returning when it is #N$02.
@ $CD06 label=DispatchCharacterScript_TestStop
  $CD06,$02 See whether the command is #N$0C.
  $CD08,$02 Jump back to #R$CD08 if the command is #N$0C.
  $CD0A,$04 Jump to #R$CD17 when the command is not #N$0B.
  $CD0E,$06 Call #R$CFCF and return when it reports #N$02.
  $CD14,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

N $CD17 Command 10 copies it is 2.
@ $CD17 label=DispatchCharacterScript_TestNested
  $CD17,$04 Jump to #R$CD82 when the command is not #N$0A.
  $CD1B,$06 Call #R$CFCF and return when it reports #N$02.
  $CD21,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CEB8 to run script command with counter.

N $CD24 Handles push Stack.
@ $CD24 label=DispatchCharacterScript_Push
  $CD24,$09 Read the moved marker and use #N$02 for the record, then call #R$A11C to allocate
. memory.
  $CD2D,$07 Read the saved pointer.
  $CD34,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $CD37,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $CD3A,$06 Write *#R$A036 to *#REGix+#N$00.
  $CD40,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $CD41,$06 Store #REGa at offset #N$01 and jump to #R$CE8F.

N $CD47 Handles pop Stack.
@ $CD47 label=DispatchCharacterScript_Pop
  $CD47,$07 Use the character list pointer at #R$9FE9.
@ $CD4E label=DispatchCharacterScript_Loop
  $CD4E,$06 Go to #R$CEB8 if the object pointer is zero.
  $CD54,$08 Jump back to #R$CD4E while this id does not match the moved marker.
  $CD5C,$06 Jump back to #R$CD4E while this id does not match the record's flag byte.
  $CD62,$02 Save the schedule entry.
  $CD64,$03 Copy the character record from #REGix into #REGde.
  $CD67,$04 Use the saved pointer (#R$9FE7).
N $CD6B Step pointer and compare.
  $CD6B,$03 Call #R$A104 to compare the pointer with the next record.
  $CD6E,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $CD74,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $CD77,$08 Store the high byte of the link and call #R$A1C7.
  $CD7F,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

N $CD82 Sets character Script Error.
@ $CD82 label=DispatchCharacterScript_MarkError
N $CD82 Set script completion flag.
  $CD82,$06 Write #N($0001,$04,$04) to *#R$A01F.
  $CD88,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CD8B Terminate Character Script
@ $CD8B label=TerminateCharacterScript
D $CD8B Ends this character's script by storing a one-cycle delay at #R$A01F and clearing the
. script state.
N $CD8B Set script completion flag.
  $CD8B,$06 Write #N($0001,$04,$04) to *#R$A01F.

c $CD91 Terminate Character Script
D $CD91 Call #R$D026 to update script pointer, and return.
  $CD91,$03 Call #R$D026 to update script pointer.
  $CD94,$01 Return.

c $CD95 Print The Command Message
@ $CD95 label=PrintCommandMessage
D $CD95 Prints the message the command names and resumes the script at #R$CE8F.
  $CD95,$09 Read the moved marker, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $CD9E,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CDA1 Show The Room Description
@ $CDA1 label=ShowRoomDescription
D $CDA1 Resolves the room the command names, prints its description, and resumes the script.
R $CDA1 IX Script pointer
  $CDA1,$07 Read the location and read the action flags.
  $CDA8,$06 Read the moved marker, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CDAE,$08 Record the room display parameter and write #N$07 to the display mode.
  $CDB6,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $CDBC,$03 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CDBF Record The Result In The Room Display
D $CDBF Writes #N$2E to the saved character and resolves the room display parameters.
  $CDBF,$05 Write #N$2E to the saved character.
  $CDC4,$06 Read the moved marker, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CDCA,$06 Record the room display parameter.
  $CDD0,$03 Store it in the saved object field at #R$A35D.
  $CDD3,$06 Read the action flags, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CDD9,$03 Store it in the saved object field at #R$A35E.
  $CDDC,$06 Read the attribute type, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CDE2,$03 Store it in the saved object field at #R$A35F.
  $CDE5,$05 Take #N$08, then call #R$A11C to allocate memory.
  $CDEA,$06 Store the record's flag byte.
  $CDF0,$04 Mark the object as moved.
  $CDF4,$06 Store the location.
  $CDFA,$03 Read the saved object fields at #R$A35E.
  $CDFD,$06 Store it in the action flags and store it in the attribute type.
  $CE03,$03 Read the link pointer.
  $CE06,$07 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous display entry.
  $CE0D,$03 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous display entry.
  $CE10,$06 Take the prompt message, then call #R$A59F to print the prompt message.
  $CE16,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CE18 Read The Character's Room
D $CE18 Copies this character's room display into the saved parameters.
  $CE18,$06 Read the moved marker, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CE1E,$03 Record the saved character.
  $CE21,$06 Call #R$CEDE to resolve the room-display parameter.
  $CE27,$03 Record the room display parameter.
  $CE2A,$06 Read the action flags, then call #R$CEDE to resolve the room display parameter.
  $CE30,$06 Store it in the room graphics parameter, then call #R$C4D1 to run display
. action.
  $CE36,$01 Return if the display record has bit 7 set, so the script resumes.
  $CE37,$02 Go to #R$CEB8 if the display action matched.
  $CE39,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CE3B Handle Room Display
@ $CE3B label=HandleRoomDisplay
D $CE3B Reads the room display parameter and the room graphics parameter from the character
. record and stores them for the room refresh.
  $CE3B,$03 Get the room display parameter.
N $CE3E Process room display parameter.
  $CE3E,$03 Call #R$CEDE to turn the room-display parameter into a room or a character.
  $CE41,$03 Write the room display parameter to *#R$A034.
  $CE44,$03 Get the room graphics parameter.
N $CE47 Process room display parameter.
  $CE47,$03 Call #R$CEDE to turn the room-graphics parameter into a room or a character.
  $CE4A,$03 Write the room graphics parameter to *#R$A035.
  $CE4D,$02 Jump to #R$CE57, the show room description.

c $CE4F Clear Room Display Parameters
@ $CE4F label=ClearRoomDisplayParameters
D $CE4F Writes #N$FF over the room display parameters.
  $CE4F,$08 Write #N$FF to #LIST { *#R$A034 } { *#R$A035 } LIST#.

c $CE57 Read The Character's Room
@ $CE57 label=ReadCharactersRoom
D $CE57 Marks the saved character as finished and returns when the room description does not fit.
N $CE57 Set the room description marker.
  $CE57,$05 Write #N$FF to the saved character.
  $CE5C,$06 Read the room description address.
  $CE62,$04 Return if the display record has bit 7 set, so the script resumes.
  $CE66,$02 Go to #R$CEB8 if the room description matched.
  $CE68,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CE6A Run A Simple Action
@ $CE6A label=RunSimpleAction
D $CE6A Turns the command's parameter into a character, clears both room parameters, and runs the display action.
  $CE6A,$06 Read the command's parameter and call #R$CEDE to turn a special value into a room or
. a character.
  $CE70,$03 Record the saved character.
  $CE73,$08 Write #N$FF to #LIST { *#R$A034 } { *#R$A035 } LIST#.
N $CE7B The display record has bit 7 set, so the script resumes.
  $CE7B,$04 Return if the display record has bit 7 set, so the script resumes.
  $CE7F,$02 Go to #R$CEB8 if the display action matched.
  $CE81,$02 Jump ahead to #R$CE8F to resume script execution.

c $CE83 Get Current Script State
@ $CE83 label=GetCurrentScriptState
D $CE83 Points at the script step now running and returns its first byte.
  $CE83,$07 Read the current character timetable pointer and the current character command position.
  $CE8A,$03 Copy the command position from #REGhl into #REGix.
  $CE8D,$01 Get the current command byte.
  $CE8E,$01 Return.

c $CE8F Continue Script Execution
@ $CE8F label=ContinueScriptExecution
D $CE8F Resumes the character script after a command returns, and follows the script pointer
. when the record flag says to.
  $CE8F,$06 Call #R$CE83 to get current script state, then call #R$CF03 to advance script
. pointer by command size.
  $CE95,$06 Jump to #R$CEA1 if a marker on the record's flag byte is clear.
  $CE9B,$06 Read the word at #REGhl and join the flag test at #R$CEA8.

N $CEA1 See whether bit 4 of the record flag is set, then see whether bit 5 of the
. record flag is set, then store that byte in the script position.
@ $CEA1 label=ContinueScriptExecution_Next
  $CEA1,$07 If bit 4 of the command byte is set, call #R$CF40 to skip its two-byte address.
@ $CEA8 label=ContinueScriptExecution_TestClearScriptCommand
  $CEA8,$07 Call #R$CF43 to clear script command flags if a marker on the record's flag byte
. is set.
  $CEAF,$09 Store #REGhl at #REGiy+#N$01 and jump back to #R$CD91 to finish the character script.

c $CEB8 Process Script Command With Counter
@ $CEB8 label=ProcessScriptCommandWithCounter
D $CEB8 Processes a character script command, tracking how many commands have been executed. If the counter limit is reached, the script is terminated.
R $CEB8 A Counter value (maximum number of commands to process)
  $CEB8,$04 Increment the script command counter.
  $CEBC,$03 Get the current script state (command byte and position).
  $CEBF,$03 Call #R$CEFE to advance past this script command.
  $CEC2,$06 Jump to #R$CECC if a marker on the record's flag byte is clear.
  $CEC8,$04 Read the command's address into #REGhl.
@ $CECC label=ProcessScriptCommandWithCounter_UpdatePosition
  $CECC,$03 Update the character's script position pointer (#REGiy+#N$01/#N$02).
  $CECF,$03 Store #REGh at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $CED2,$06 Calculate how many bytes have been processed in the script (#REGhl-#REGix).
  $CED8,$03 Go to #R$CD8B if the subtraction comes out at zero.
  $CEDB,$03 Another command remains, so parse it from #R$CC82.

c $CEDE Process Room Display Parameter
@ $CEDE label=ProcessRoomDisplayParameter
D $CEDE Resolves special parameter values used in room display scripts to actual game values (current room, character ID, location value).
R $CEDE A Parameter value (#N$F5-#N$FD are special values)
R $CEDE O:A Resolved parameter value
  $CEDE,$03 Return unchanged if the parameter is not a special value (less than #N$F5).
  $CEE1,$03 Return unchanged when the parameter is at least #N$FE.
  $CEE4,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CEEC if the room is not #N$FA.
  $CEE8,$03 Read the room id at #R$9FD6 into #REGa.
  $CEEB,$01 Return.

N $CEEC A parameter of #N$FB is the character being processed.
@ $CEEC label=ProcessRoomDisplayParameter_CheckCharacterID
  $CEEC,$04 Go to #R$CEF4 when the parameter is not the current character (#N$FB).
  $CEF0,$03 Load the character being processed.
  $CEF3,$01 Return.

N $CEF4 A parameter of #N$FC is the high byte of the location value.
@ $CEF4 label=ProcessRoomDisplayParameter_GetLocationValue
  $CEF4,$05 Compare the parameter with #N$FC and load the high byte of the location value at #R$9FD7.
  $CEF9,$01 Return if it is #N$FC.
  $CEFA,$03 Read the location value at #R$9FD7.
  $CEFD,$01 Return.

c $CEFE Advance Script Pointer By Command Size
@ $CEFE label=AdvanceScriptPointerByCommandSize
D $CEFE Advances the script pointer past a command byte by the appropriate number of bytes based on the command type. Different command types have different data sizes, so the pointer must be advanced accordingly.
R $CEFE A Command byte
R $CEFE O:HL Updated script pointer
N $CEFE Extended commands (bit 7 set) require an extra 2-byte advance before the normal command type advance.
  $CEFE,$05 If bit 7 of the command is set, call #R$CF40 to step past its two-byte address.

c $CF03 Advance By Command Type
@ $CF03 label=AdvanceByCommandType
D $CF03 Advances the script pointer by the size of this command type.
  $CF03,$02,b$01 Extract the command type (bits 0-3).
N $CF05 Route to the appropriate handler based on command type to advance the script pointer by the correct number of bytes.
  $CF05,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF41 if the command is #N$0F.
  $CF09,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF40 if the command is #N$08.
  $CF0D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF40 if the command is #N$09.
  $CF11,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF40 if the command is #N$01.
  $CF15,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3E if the command is #N$02.
  $CF19,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3F if the command is #N$03.
  $CF1D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF40 if the command is #N$0D.
  $CF21,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3F if the command is #N$0E.
  $CF25,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3D if the command is #N$07.
  $CF29,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3D if the command is #N$0C.
  $CF2D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF41 if the command is #N$0B.
  $CF31,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF41 if the command is #N$0A.
  $CF35,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3E if the command is #N$05.
  $CF39,$04 Jump ahead to #R$CF3F if the command is not #N$04.
@ $CF3D label=AdvanceByCommandType_AdvanceFive
  $CF3D,$01 Step to the next entry.
@ $CF3E label=AdvanceByCommandType_AdvanceFour
  $CF3E,$01 Step to the next entry.
@ $CF3F label=AdvanceByCommandType_AdvanceThree
  $CF3F,$01 Step to the next entry.

c $CF40 Step To The Next Byte
@ $CF40 label=StepNextByte
D $CF40 Moves the script pointer on by one byte of the current command.
  $CF40,$01 Step to the next entry.
@ $CF41 label=StepNextByte_AdvanceOne
  $CF41,$01 Step one byte further along the script command.
  $CF42,$01 Return.

c $CF43 Clear Script Command Flags
@ $CF43 label=ClearScriptCommandFlags
D $CF43 Clears flag bytes in a script command based on the command type and flag bits. Different command types require clearing different numbers of flag bytes to mark them as processed.
R $CF43 IX Pointer to script command data
  $CF43,$03 Read the command flag byte.
N $CF46 Clear the first flag byte if certain flag bits are set, indicating those conditions have been processed.
  $CF46,$05 If bit 7 of the command is set, call #R$CF73 to clear one flag byte.
  $CF4B,$05 If bit 4 of the command is set, call #R$CF73 to clear one flag byte.
  $CF50,$02,b$01 Extract the command type (bits 0-3).
N $CF52 Route to the appropriate handler based on command type to clear the required number of flag bytes.
  $CF52,$04 Go to #R$CF73 when the command is #N$01.
  $CF56,$04 Go to #R$CF6B when the command is #N$02.
  $CF5A,$04 Go to #R$CF6B when the command is #N$05.
  $CF5E,$04 Go to #R$CF68 when the command is #N$07.
  $CF62,$04 Go to #R$CF70 when the command is #N$06.
  $CF66,$02 Compare the command with #N$04.
@ $CF68 label=ClearScriptCommandFlags_ClearOne
  $CF68,$03 Call #R$CF79 if the command is #N$04 or #N$07.
@ $CF6B label=ClearScriptCommandFlags_ClearTwo
  $CF6B,$03 Call #R$CF73 if the command is #N$02, #N$04, #N$05 or #N$07.
  $CF6E,$02 See whether the command type is #N$03.
@ $CF70 label=ClearScriptCommandFlags_ClearOneAlt
  $CF70,$03 Call #R$CF79 if the command is #N$03 or #N$06.

c $CF73 Clear The Record Flag
@ $CF73 label=ClearScriptCommandFlags_ClearFirst
D $CF73 Clear the record flag, then step to the next field of the character record
  $CF73,$04 Clear the first flag byte.
  $CF77,$02 Step to the next flag byte of the script command.

c $CF79 Clear The Record Flag
@ $CF79 label=ClearScriptCommandFlags_ClearSecond
D $CF79 Clear the record flag, then step to the next field of the character record.
  $CF79,$04 Clear the second flag byte.
  $CF7D,$02 Step to the next field of the character record.
  $CF7F,$01 Return.

c $CF80 Allocate Memory And Store Character
@ $CF80 label=AllocateMemoryAndStoreCharacter
D $CF80 Creates a new character entry in the character tracking system and adds it to the active character list, storing the character's current state for game logic purposes.
R $CF80 HL Character location co-ordinates
R $CF80 B Character attribute value
  $CF80,$04 Allocate #N$07 bytes of memory for the character entry, keeping character
. record.
N $CF84 Allocate memory.
  $CF84,$03 Call #R$A11C to allocate the character entry.
  $CF87,$06 Store *#R$A036 in the entry.
  $CF8D,$06 Store the character's location co-ordinates in the entry.
  $CF93,$0A Store *#R$A034 in the entry.
@ $CF9D label=AllocateMemoryAndStoreCharacter_StoreAttribute
  $CF9D,$03 Store it in the object attribute.
  $CFA0,$06 Store *#R$9FD6 in the entry.
  $CFA6,$03 Get the current head of *#R$9FE9.
  $CFA9,$04 Add this character entry to the front of the active character list.
  $CFAD,$06 Set up the backward link to maintain the list structure.
  $CFB3,$02 Restore the saved character record from the stack.
  $CFB5,$01 Return.

c $CFB6 Find Character Script Entry
@ $CFB6 label=FindCharacterScriptEntry
D $CFB6 Searches through the character script entry list to find an entry matching the current character ID. Returns the entry pointer if found, otherwise returns #N$00.
R $CFB6 O:IX Character script entry pointer (if found)
R $CFB6 O:A #N$00 if not found, bit 0 set if found
  $CFB6,$03 Read the current character.

c $CFB9 Walk The Character Script Entries
@ $CFB9 label=FindCharacterScriptEntry_StartSearch
D $CFB9 Starts at the first character-script entry and follows each link until this character is found.
  $CFB9,$04 Use the character script entry list at #R$9FEB.
@ $CFBD label=FindCharacterScriptEntry_Loop
  $CFBD,$04 Keep the character being processed, then call #R$A0F7 to fetch object
. pointer from stack.
  $CFC1,$03 Go to #R$CFCC if the object pointer is zero.
  $CFC4,$05 Go to #R$CFBD if this entry is not the character being processed.
  $CFC9,$02,b$01 Mark that the entry was found.
  $CFCB,$01 Return with the entry pointer.

@ $CFCC label=FindCharacterScriptEntry_NotFound
N $CFCC No matching entry was found in the list.
  $CFCC,$01 Restore the caller's registers from the stack.
  $CFCD,$01 Set #N$00 (entry not found).
  $CFCE,$01 Return.

c $CFCF Get Script Pointer
@ $CFCF label=GetScriptPointer
D $CFCF Copies the next script address into the current step and into the timetable link.
N $CFCF Find character script entry.
  $CFCF,$03 Call #R$CFB6 to find this character's script entry.
  $CFD2,$06 Read the script address from the entry.
  $CFD8,$04 Record the current character command position.
  $CFDC,$08 Store #REGde at #REGiy+#N$01-#N$02 and keep the record.
  $CFE4,$0A Call #R$A104 to step pointer and compare.
  $CFEE,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $CFF4,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $CFF7,$0A Store it in the schedule entry and read the object attribute, then call
. #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.
  $D001,$06 Call #R$D008 to take the next script from the timetable.
  $D007,$01 Return.

c $D008 Get Script Pointer From Timetable
@ $D008 label=GetScriptPointerFromTimetable
D $D008 Takes the script address from the timetable, or from the matching entry when one exists.
  $D008,$09 Call #R$CFB6 to find character script entry.
  $D011,$05 Load the byte at #REGiy+#N$03 and jump to #R$D01F if #R$CFB6 found no script entry.
  $D016,$06 Read the action flags.
  $D01C,$03 Read the room at #REGix+#N$06.
@ $D01F label=GetScriptPointerFromTimetable_Next
  $D01F,$03 Record the current room.
  $D022,$03 Record the location value.
  $D025,$01 Return.

c $D026 Update Script Pointer
@ $D026 label=UpdateScriptPointer
D $D026 Finds the script entry for the current character and stores it in the timetable.
  $D026,$06 Call #R$CFB6 to find character script entry.
  $D02C,$05 Read the current room, and go to #R$D03B if #R$CFB6 found no matching entry.
  $D031,$06 Store the script pointer in the timetable entry (+#N$03/+#N$04).
  $D037,$03 Store the current room at offset #N$06.
  $D03A,$01 Return.

N $D03B Store it in the schedule field, then store that byte in the schedule field
@ $D03B label=UpdateScriptPointer_Next
  $D03B,$06 Store the schedule entry.
  $D041,$03 Store the schedule entry.
  $D044,$01 Return.

c $D045 Is Sherlock Wearing The China Man Disguise?
@ $D045 label=IsSherlockTheChinaMan
D $D045 Looks up the object recorded for Sherlock and stores the room that object reports.
N $D045 Sherlocks character ID can vary depending on whether he is wearing a
. disguise or not.
  $D045,$03 Fetch Sherlocks current character ID from *#R$9860.
  $D048,$06 Record Sherlock's disguise.
  $D04E,$01 Return.

c $D04F Adjust Object Table For Sum
@ $D04F label=AdjustObjectTableForSum
D $D04F Points the object walk at the object index at #R$8B6F, one record before the first
. entry.
  $D04F,$04 Use the objects at #R$8B6F.
  $D053,$02 Step back one field of the character record.
  $D055,$02 Step back one field of the character record.
  $D057,$02 Step back one field of the character record.
  $D059,$01 Return.

g $D05A Table: Tokens
@ $D05A label=Table_Tokens
D $D05A Tokens read by #R$D0DD.
W $D05A,$02 "#R($5DBF+(#PEEK(#PC+$01)*$0100+#PEEK(#PC)))(#TOKENPTR)".
L $D05A,$02,$05

g $D064 Table: Direction Tokens
@ $D064 label=Table_DirectionTokens
D $D064 Direction Tokens read by #R$D5FB.
W $D064,$02 "#R($5DBF+(#PEEK(#PC+$01)*$0100+#PEEK(#PC)))(#TOKENPTR)".
L $D064,$02,$0A

c $D078 Check Object Visibility
@ $D078 label=CheckObjectVisibility
D $D078 Sets the carry when the current character is visible and a matching object is found. Returns with the carry clear when the character is not visible.
R $D078 O:F Carry flag set if object is visible
  $D078,$05 Save the character record, the schedule entry and the count.
  $D07D,$04 Read the current character record.
  $D081,$03 Read the attribute type.
  $D084,$02,b$01 Keep only bit 7.
  $D086,$02 Skip the object when bit 7 is clear, so it is not visible.
  $D088,$06 Go to #R$D09E unless #R$C86E returned #N$FF.
N $D08E Fetch current character location.
  $D08E,$03 Call #R$D33C to read where the current character is.
  $D091,$06 Jump to #R$D0C6 if a marker on the record's flag byte is set.
  $D097,$07 Go to #R$D0C6 if it is not #N$01.
N $D09E It's late night/ evening (8 PM - 5 AM).
@ $D09E label=CheckObjectVisibility_Next
  $D09E,$04 Use the objects at #R$8B6F.
@ $D0A2 label=CheckObjectVisibility_Loop
  $D0A2,$05 Go to #R$D0C9 if the next object id is #N$FF.
  $D0A7,$07 Jump to #R$D0A2 if the character id is not #N$01.
  $D0AE,$03 Read the schedule entry.
  $D0B1,$06,b$04,$02 Jump back to #R$D0A2 unless bits 2 and 4 of the schedule entry are set and bits 3 and 6 are clear.
  $D0B7,$03 Read the schedule entry.
  $D0BA,$02,b$01 Keep only bit 7.
  $D0BC,$02 Go back and test the next object when bit 7 is clear.
  $D0BE,$08 Jump back to #R$D0A2 while the object is not in this location.
@ $D0C6 label=CheckObjectVisibility_TestObjectVisibility
  $D0C6,$03 Clear the carry and jump ahead to #R$D0CA.

N $D0C9 The condition holds, so the caller continues.
@ $D0C9 label=CheckObjectVisibility_ConditionHoldsCaller
  $D0C9,$01 Set the carry.
@ $D0CA label=CheckObjectVisibility_BringSavedCount
  $D0CA,$05 Restore the saved count, the saved schedule entry and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D0CF,$01 Return.

c $D0D0 Write Token To Buffer
@ $D0D0 label=WriteTokenToBuffer
D $D0D0 Writes the current text token into the message pointer at #R$68DE, with the top bit set
. so the printer treats it as a token.
R $D0D0 HL Pointer to output buffer
R $D0D0 DE Token value (word)
  $D0D0,$08 Use the text pointer (#R$68DE).
  $D0D8,$01 Advance through the text.
  $D0D9,$01 Store that byte at the pointer.
  $D0DA,$01 Restore #REGde from the stack.
  $D0DB,$01 Return.

g $D0DC Location Token Processing Flag
@ $D0DC label=LocationTokenProcessingFlag
D $D0DC Flag that enables location token processing in ProcessLocationToken.
. When set to #N$01, location token data is processed and when #N$00, processing is skipped.
. Read by #R$D0DD and #R$DBF7.
B $D0DC,$01

c $D0DD Process Location Token
@ $D0DD label=ProcessLocationToken
E $D0DD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9630 }
. LIST#
R $D0DD A Room ID
D $D0DD Looks the room up and, unless an exit line is already being printed, chooses the exit text from
. the room's direction bits.
  $D0DD,$04 Keep the room, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $D0E1,$04 Read the location-token flag at #R$D0DC.
  $D0E5,$05 Take the exit text and go to #R$D100 if location token processing is on.
  $D0EA,$03 Read the record's flag byte.
  $D0ED,$02,b$01 Take bits 1-3 of the flag byte as an even offset into the tokens at #R$D05A.
  $D0EF,$08 Use the tokens at #R$D05A.
  $D0F7,$06 Call #R$D0D0 to write token to buffer.
  $D0FD,$03 Use the message (#R$68DD).
@ $D100 label=ProcessLocationToken_Next
  $D100,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $D101,$01 Return.

c $D102 Process Location Token Display
@ $D102 label=ProcessLocationTokenDisplay
D $D102 Prints the room's exit line and restores the records it used.
N $D102 Process location token.
  $D102,$03 Call #R$D0DD to choose the room's exit text.
  $D105,$05 Save the character record, the schedule entry and the count.
  $D10A,$03 Call #R$D113 to describe the current room.
  $D10D,$05 Restore the saved count, the saved schedule entry and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D112,$01 Return.

c $D113 Describe The Current Room
@ $D113 label=DescribeCurrentRoom
D $D113 Looks the current room up, prints its name and lists the objects that are here.
  $D113,$04 Use the room as the count, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $D117,$04 Call #R$A277 with the room in #REGa.
  $D11B,$0A Keep the registers, read the current character and call #R$C863 to read that character's room.
  $D125,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D167 if the room is the end marker.
  $D12A,$08 Save the flags, read the text pointer at #R$68DE, save it and point #REGhl at #N$8000.
N $D132 Write token to buffer.
  $D132,$03 Call #R$D0D0 to write the token into the buffer.
  $D135,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $D13B,$04 Record the text pointer.
  $D13F,$05 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
N $D144 Print object attribute description.
  $D144,$03 Call #R$D5D3 to print the object's attribute text.
  $D147,$04 Call #R$D3D5 to run object entry alternate.
  $D14B,$04 Call #R$D4CC to count objects in location.
  $D14F,$03 Jump ahead to #R$D163 if no objects are in this room.
  $D152,$07 Store the object count and call #R$A887 to print token #N$09BF.
  $D159,$04 Pass the character attribute in #REGb.
  $D15D,$01 Restore the saved room from the stack.
  $D15E,$03 Call #R$D51A.
  $D161,$02 The name was printed, so restore the registers from #R$D167.

N $D163 Read the character field, then read the count.
@ $D163 label=DescribeCurrentRoom_ReadCharacter
  $D163,$03 Call #R$A414 to print a full stop.
  $D166,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
@ $D167 label=DescribeCurrentRoom_CharacterRecord
  $D167,$04 Restore #REGde, #REGbc and #REGix from the stack.
  $D16B,$03 Read the character record.
  $D16E,$03 Read the character record.
  $D171,$07 Call #R$D182 to print the message and take the count, then call #R$D60B to list the exits.

c $D178 Read The Count
D $D178 Turns the count into a direction name and prints it.
  $D178,$07 Take the count and call #R$D68C to print location exits with formatting, then call
. #R$D4A7 to list the objects here.
  $D17F,$03 Jump back to #R$A2A2 to print newline.

c $D182 Print The Set Message
@ $D182 label=PrintSetMessage
D $D182 Prints the message when an address is set.
  $D182,$05 If a message address is set, go to #R$A59F to print it.

c $D187 Print The Location Text
@ $D187 label=PrintLocationText
D $D187 Prints the location message for this character and records the object display.
  $D187,$05 Read the current character.
  $D18C,$06 Read the current character and call #R$C863 to read that character's room.
  $D192,$04 Jump to #R$D19F if the location from #R$C863 is #N$FF.
  $D196,$06 Record the current character.
  $D19C,$03 Record the object display flag.
@ $D19F label=PrintLocationText_SaveResult
  $D19F,$04 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $D1A3,$04 Record the current character.
  $D1A7,$06 Store the flag at #R$A2AA and call #R$D1C6.
  $D1AD,$05 Take a full stop and print it through #R$A9B7.

c $D1B2 Process Object Display With Check
@ $D1B2 label=ProcessObjectDisplayWithCheck
D $D1B2 Prints the exit line when the direction is open, and prints the closed-way line otherwise.
  $D1B2,$04 Call #R$D0DD to expand the location token.
  $D1B6,$04 Swap the two pointers, then call #R$D187 to print the location text.
  $D1BA,$05 Jump back to #R$D178 while #R$D078 finds the object hidden.
  $D1BF,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $D1C5,$01 Return.

c $D1C6 Record The Result In The Object Processing
E $D1C6 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9686 }
. LIST#
D $D1C6 Steps to the next schedule entry and clears the object-processing flag.
  $D1C6,$05 Take an offset of #N($0001,$04,$04) and keep #REGiy.
  $D1CB,$04 Copy the character record from #REGix into #REGiy.
  $D1CF,$09 Step to the next schedule entry, start the object from zero and store it in the object-processing flag, then call #R$D405 to test object attribute with flag.
  $D1D8,$02 Restore the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D1DA,$01 Return.

c $D1DB Jump To Handler
@ $D1DB label=JumpToHandler
D $D1DB Jumps to the handler address just looked up. Callers leave that address in the pointer.
  $D1DB,$01 Jump to the handler whose address the caller left in #REGhl.

c $D1DC Calculate Handler Offset
@ $D1DC label=CalculateHandlerOffset
E $D1DC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9B81 }
. LIST#
D $D1DC Adds #N$0F to the record's flag byte and steps #REGix on by that many bytes.
  $D1DC,$08 Read the record's flag byte and add #N$0F to the total.
  $D1E4,$06 Step to the next record.
@ $D1EA label=CalculateHandlerOffset_Loop
  $D1EA,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D1FF if this id matches the record's flag byte.
  $D1EF,$05 Jump to #R$D201 if this byte was the #N$FF end marker.
  $D1F4,$05 Go to #R$D204 if stepping this byte down leaves zero.
  $D1F9,$06 Skip this handler and try the next one.

N $D1FF The condition holds, so the caller continues.
@ $D1FF label=CalculateHandlerOffset_Next
  $D1FF,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $D200,$01 Return.

@ $D201 label=CalculateHandlerOffset_TestSignalStepFailed
  $D201,$03 Restore the end marker.
  $D204,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $D205,$01 Return.

c $D206 Fetch Next Object
@ $D206 label=FetchNextObject
E $D206 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9B93 }
. LIST#
D $D206 Steps to the next object in the list and reports whether the list has ended.
R $D206 IX Pointer to objects table
R $D206 O:IX Pointer to object table
R $D206 O:IY Pointer to object data
R $D206 O:F The zero flag is set if the end of the table is reached
  $D206,$01 Switch to the shadow registers.
  $D207,$05 Step to the next object record.
  $D20C,$09 Read the next object's data pointer.
  $D215,$05 Stop at the end of the object table (#N$FF).
  $D21A,$01 Switch back to the normal registers.
  $D21B,$01 Return.

c $D21C Fetch Next Object With ID
@ $D21C label=FetchNextObjectWithID
E $D21C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9BA9 }
. LIST#
D $D21C Keeps the id and steps to the next object.
R $D21C A Object ID to search for
R $D21C O:A Object ID that was searched for
R $D21C O:F Zero flag set when there is no further object
  $D21C,$05 Call #R$D206 to fetch next object.
  $D221,$01 Read the count.
  $D222,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $D223,$01 Return.

c $D224 Fetch Location Data Pointer
@ $D224 label=FetchLocationDataPointer
E $D224 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9BB1 }
. LIST#
D $D224 Turns a room id into the address of that room's data.
R $D224 A Room ID
R $D224 O:IX Pointer to location data
  $D224,$08 Use the locations at #R$840B.
  $D22C,$01 Double the room id, because each location entry is a word.
  $D22D,$01 Add the base table address to the offset to point to the requested room
. address.
  $D22E,$06 Read the location address for this room.
  $D234,$02 Restore the saved pointer and the saved source from the stack.
  $D236,$01 Return.

c $D237 Fetch Object Data Pointer
@ $D237 label=FetchObjectDataPointer
E $D237 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9BCA }
. LIST#
D $D237 Looks up an object in the objects table and returns a pointer to it's
. data structure.
R $D237 A Object ID
R $D237 O:IX Pointer to object data
