N $D23E This will be restored back into #REGhl by the end of the routine.
  $D23E,$07 Read the moved marker and read the location, saving pointer.
N $D23F Pretty clever way of using the stack here.
  $D245,$01 Swap the *#REGsp with the object pointer, restoring #REGhl to the original
. value.
  $D246,$02 Restore the saved character record from the stack.
  $D248,$01 Return.

c $D249 Process Object Location Recursively
@ $D249 label=ProcessObjectLocationRecursively
E $D249 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9C17 }
. LIST#
R $D249 A Object ID
R $D249 B Location ID
D $D249 Walks the objects inside the current one and records the room of each.
  $D249,$07 Call #R$D04F to adjust object table for sum.
@ $D250 label=ProcessObjectLocationRecursively_Loop
  $D250,$05 Go to #R$D267 if there is no further object.
  $D255,$05 Jump back to #R$D250 while this id does not match the schedule entry.
  $D25A,$0A Store it in the character attribute and read the record flag, then call #R$D249 to
. run object location recursively.
  $D264,$03 Fetch the next object from #R$D250.

N $D267 Return.
@ $D267 label=ProcessObjectLocationRecursively_Next
  $D267,$04 Restore the saved character record and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D26B,$01 Return.

c $D26C Look Up The Room Display Parameter
@ $D26C label=LookUpRoomDisplayParameter
E $D26C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9C78 }
. LIST#
D $D26C Looks up the object named by the room display parameter and restores the pointer
. it saved.
  $D26C,$08 Call #R$D275 to check character id range.
  $D274,$01 Return.

c $D275 Check Character ID Range
@ $D275 label=CheckCharacterIDRange
D $D275 Points #REGhl at the character being processed and falls through into #R$D278, which sets the carry for #N$FF, #N$FE or a matched id.
  $D275,$03 Take the character being processed.

c $D278 Test For The End Marker
E $D278 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9C78 }
. LIST#
D $D278 Treats either end marker as the end of the list.
  $D278,$04 Return, with the carry set, if the character is the end marker.
  $D27C,$04 Return, with the carry set, when the id is the other end marker (#N$FE).
  $D280,$07 Call #R$D288 to test for the end marker.
  $D287,$01 Return.

c $D288 Look Up Object And Test Moved Marker
@ $D288 label=LookUpObjectAndTestMovedMarker
E $D288 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9C8A }
. LIST#
D $D288 Looks the object up and keeps searching while the moved marker is not the end.
  $D288,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $D28B,$08 Jump to #R$D29A if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $D293,$05 Drop the saved value and jump back to #R$D288 while the moved marker does not match the character.
  $D298,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $D299,$01 Return.

N $D29A Clear the carry so a character id outside the range returns as no match.
@ $D29A label=LookUpObjectAndTestMovedMarker_Next
  $D29A,$01 Restore the saved flags from the stack.
  $D29B,$01 Report that the id was not found.
  $D29C,$01 Return.

c $D29D Get Character Attribute
@ $D29D label=GetCharacterAttribute
D $D29D Reads the character's last attribute and returns one more than its signed value.
  $D29D,$02 Leave #N$63 as the result.
  $D29F,$03 Read the character attribute.
  $D2A2,$01 Count up one.
  $D2A3,$01 Return.

c $D2A4 Random Number
@ $D2A4 label=GetRandomNumber
E $D2A4 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9C9F }
. LIST#
D $D2A4 Calls #R$D2AD to scale the roll, and makes the result negative when bit 7 is set.
  $D2A4,$06 Call #R$D2AD to scale the roll, and return if bit 7 is clear.
  $D2AA,$02 Make the result negative.
  $D2AC,$01 Return.

c $D2AD Scale The Roll
@ $D2AD label=ScaleTheRoll
E $D2AD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9CA8 }
. LIST#
D $D2AD Mixes the seed at #R$A037 with the counter at #R$A039 and scales #REGa down to the limit in #REGc.
  $D2AD,$08 Keep the record and the count, copy the roll into #REGc, and go to #R$D2B7 if bit 7 is clear.
  $D2B5,$02 Take #N$FF for the count.
@ $D2B7 label=ScaleTheRoll_Advance
  $D2B7,$01 Use the value in #REGa as the count.
@ $D2B8 label=ScaleTheRoll_Count
  $D2B8,$09 Jump to #R$D2C4 if the count has not reached zero.
  $D2C1,$03 Add one to the high byte of the counter at #R$A039.
@ $D2C4 label=ScaleTheRoll_ObjectAttributeFlag
  $D2C4,$07 Point #REGix at #R$A039 and read the random seed (#R$A037).
  $D2CB,$03 Add the seed byte.
  $D2CE,$06 Add the offset to the seed pointer, mix in the next seed byte and keep the pointer.
  $D2D4,$03 Use the random seed (#R$A037).
  $D2D7,$04 Jump back to #R$D2B8 if the mixed seed matches the seed at #R$A037.
  $D2DB,$03 Record the random seed.
@ $D2DE label=ScaleTheRoll_Loop
  $D2DE,$03 Jump ahead to #R$D2E8 if the first value is below the second.
  $D2E1,$02 Go to #R$D2E8 if the first value equals the second.
  $D2E3,$05 Shift the roll down and test it again from #R$D2DE.

N $D2E8 Return.
@ $D2E8 label=ScaleTheRoll_Continue
  $D2E8,$01 Take away the other byte.
  $D2E9,$03 Restore the saved count and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D2EC,$01 Return.

c $D2ED Start The Attribute Count
@ $D2ED label=StartTheAttributeCount
E $D2ED View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9CE8 }
. LIST#
D $D2ED Starts the attribute count at one and joins #R$D2F5.
  $D2ED,$05 Save #REGbc, set the count to #N$01 and jump ahead to #R$D2F5.

c $D2F2 Get Object Attribute Value
@ $D2F2 label=GetObjectAttributeValue
E $D2F2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9CED }
. LIST#
D $D2F2 Counts the matching object attributes and returns how many there were.
  $D2F2,$03 Save #REGbc and count #N$00.

c $D2F5 Count The Matching Attributes
@ $D2F5 label=CountMatchingAttributes
D $D2F5 Counts how many objects match this id and returns that count.
  $D2F5,$04 Keep the schedule entry.
  $D2F9,$02 Count #N$00.
  $D2FB,$03 Call #R$D305 to scan the objects for this id.
  $D2FE,$01 Read the low byte of the count.
  $D2FF,$05 Restore the saved schedule entry, the saved character record and the saved count from the stack.
  $D304,$01 Return.

c $D305 Scan Objects For This Id
@ $D305 label=ScanObjectsId
E $D305 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9D00 }
. LIST#
D $D305 Walks the object list at #R$8B6F until the id matches.
  $D305,$06 Use the objects at #R$8B6F.
@ $D30B label=ScanObjectsId_UseCharacter
  $D30B,$05 Go to #R$D334 if there is no further object.
  $D310,$05 Jump back to #R$D30B while this id does not match the schedule entry.
  $D315,$06 Keep the low byte and go to #R$D324 if the count is zero.
  $D31B,$06 Add the attribute at offset #N$02, and go to #R$D337 when the sum overflows.
  $D321,$03 Jump ahead to #R$D331 to fetch the next object.

N $D324 Add the schedule field onto the result, then store the result in the low byte of
. the count, then read the record flag.
@ $D324 label=ScanObjectsId_Next
  $D324,$06 Add the attribute at offset #N$03, and go to #R$D337 when the sum overflows.
  $D32A,$07 Store the sum as the count, read the record's flag byte and call #R$D305.
@ $D331 label=ScanObjectsId_FetchObject
  $D331,$03 Fetch the next object from #R$D30B.

N $D334 Return.
@ $D334 label=ScanObjectsId_Save
  $D334,$02 Restore the saved character record from the stack.
  $D336,$01 Return.

N $D337 Bring back the object id and carry on with the scan.
@ $D337 label=ScanObjectsId_BringObjectCarry
  $D337,$05 Jump back to #R$D334 to count #N$FF.

c $D33C Fetch Current Character Location
@ $D33C label=FetchCurrentCharacterLocation
E $D33C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9D37 }
. LIST#
D $D33C Reads the room of the character on screen and points at that room's data.
R $D33C O:IX Pointer to location data
  $D33C,$05 Read the current character record, keeping character.
  $D341,$07 Read the character attribute and call #R$D224 to fetch location data
. pointer.
  $D348,$01 Return.

c $D349 Search Two-Byte Length Table
@ $D349 label=SearchTwoByteTable
D $D349 Walks a list of pairs until the end marker.
R $D349 A Table ID to match
R $D349 IX Table to search
  $D349,$01 Switch to the shadow registers.
  $D34A,$02 Set the length of the data in #REGe (#N$02 bytes).
  $D34C,$02 Jump to #R$D351 to search table start.

c $D34E Search Three-Byte Length Table
@ $D34E label=SearchThreeByteTable
E $D34E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9DBD }
. LIST#
D $D34E Walks a list of three-byte groups until the end marker.
R $D34E A Table ID to match
R $D34E IX Table to search
R $D34E O:A Entry ID or #N$FF for no entry found
R $D34E O:IX Pointer to found entry
R $D34E O:F The zero flag is set when no entry was found
  $D34E,$01 Switch to the shadow registers.
  $D34F,$02 Set the length of the data in #REGe (#N$03 bytes).

c $D351 Search The Table
@ $D351 label=SearchTable
D $D351 Searches the table for the id in hand.
  $D351,$04 Use the command as the count.
  $D355,$02 Reset #REGd to #N$00 for the length offset calculation.
@ $D357 label=SearchTable_Loop
  $D357,$01 Fetch a byte from the table.
  $D358,$03 Go to #R$D363 if this entry is the wanted id.
  $D35B,$04 Jump to #R$D363 if this table entry is the end marker.
  $D35F,$01 Move to the next table entry.
  $D360,$03 Jump back to #R$D357 to check the next table entry.

N $D363 See whether this byte is the end marker before returning.
@ $D363 label=SearchTable_Return
N $D363 Either the table ID was matched, or the terminator was reached.
  $D363,$05 See whether this table entry is the end marker.
  $D368,$01 Switch back to the normal registers.
  $D369,$01 Return.

c $D36A Process Object Entry
@ $D36A label=ProcessObjectEntry
E $D36A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9DD9 }
. LIST#
D $D36A Steps through the objects and marks each one that passes the attribute test.
  $D36A,$04 Keep the schedule entry.
  $D36E,$03 Call #R$D206 to fetch the next object.
  $D371,$02 Go to #R$D393 if the next object id is #N$FF.
  $D373,$0A Point #REGiy seven bytes further on (#N($0007,$04,$04)) and call #R$A4E0 to compare the
. location data.
  $D37D,$04 Jump back to #R$D36E while the room fields do not match.
  $D381,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D393 if the object attribute flag is not zero.
  $D387,$08 Call #R$C821 to check object location match.
  $D38F,$02 Restore the object pointer from the stack.
  $D391,$02 Go back to #R$D36E for the next object if this one did not match.
@ $D393 label=ProcessObjectEntry_Next
  $D393,$03 Read the record flag.
  $D396,$04 Restore the saved schedule entry, the saved source and the saved count from the stack.
  $D39A,$01 Return.

c $D39B Process Object Entry Alternate
@ $D39B label=ProcessObjectEntryAlternate
E $D39B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9EA0 }
. LIST#
D $D39B Walks the objects in this room and describes each one that is here.
. Pointed at by #R$B4E7 and #R$B58E.
  $D39B,$06 Save the schedule entry, the source, the count and the character record.
  $D3A1,$01 Copy the character record from #REGix into #REGbc.
  $D3A2,$03 Take an offset of #N($0001,$04,$04).
@ $D3A5 label=ProcessObjectEntryAlternate_Loop
N $D3A5 Has the object count reached #N$6A?
  $D3A5,$08 Count one more, and go to #R$D3CD with #N$FF if the count has reached #N$6A.
  $D3AD,$04 Take the count, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $D3B1,$09 Step to the next schedule entry, then call #R$A4E0 to compare location data.
  $D3BA,$02 Jump back to #R$D3A5 while the room fields do not match.
  $D3BC,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D3CC if the object attribute flag is not zero.
  $D3C2,$06 Take the count and call #R$D471 to find an object by field two.
  $D3C8,$04 Jump back to #R$D3A5 if the entry is the end marker.
@ $D3CC label=ProcessObjectEntryAlternate_Next
  $D3CC,$01 Take the count.
@ $D3CD label=ProcessObjectEntryAlternate_BringSavedCharacter
  $D3CD,$03 Copy the object count from #REGbc into #REGix.
  $D3D0,$04 Restore the saved count, the saved source and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D3D4,$01 Return.

c $D3D5 Take The Found Object
@ $D3D5 label=TakeTheFoundObject
E $D3D5 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9EC7 }
. LIST#
D $D3D5 Points #REGiy at the object just found and continues at #R$D3D9. That entry steps
. seven bytes to the description flag and calls #R$D3E8.
  $D3D5,$04 Copy the object record from #REGix into #REGiy.
@ $D3D9 label=TakeTheFoundObject_DescriptionFlag
  $D3D9,$08 Keep the object and step seven bytes to its description flag.
  $D3E1,$03 Call #R$D3E8 to see whether this object has a special description.
  $D3E4,$03 Restore the saved source and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D3E7,$01 Return.

c $D3E8 Check Object Has Special Description
@ $D3E8 label=CheckObjectHasSpecialDescription
E $D3E8 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9ED6 }
. LIST#
D $D3E8 Reads the object's description word through #R$A7D0 and clears #R$A7EA unless bit 6 of #REGiy-#N$02 is set. Then prints the tokens at #REGiy+#N$02 and #REGiy+#N$04, or only the one at #REGiy+#N$04 when bit 5 of #REGiy-#N$01 is set.
  $D3E8,$08 Save the registers, then read the word at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01 into #REGde.
  $D3F0,$03 Call #R$A7D0 to test the token and set the flag.
  $D3F3,$06 Go to #R$D3FD if bit 6 of this byte is set.
  $D3F9,$04 Clear the token match flag at #R$A7EA.
@ $D3FD label=CheckObjectHasSpecialDescription_Next
  $D3FD,$06 Go to #R$D427 if bit 5 of this byte is set.
  $D403,$02 Go on to read the high byte of the object's description token.

c $D405 Check Object Attribute With Flag
@ $D405 label=CheckObjectAttributeWithFlag
E $D405 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9ED6 }
. LIST#
D $D405 Reads the object's attribute word and prints the attribute text selected by the object's flags.
  $D405,$08 Save the registers, then read the word at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01 into #REGde.
  $D40D,$03 Call #R$A7D0 to test the token and set the flag.
  $D410,$06 Jump to #R$D438 if the object processing source flag is set.

c $D416 Read The Schedule Field
D $D416 Reads the schedule pair and clears its top bit.
  $D416,$08 Read the schedule pair and clear its top bit.
  $D41E,$09 Call #R$A815 if bit 7 of the schedule entry is clear.

c $D427 Read The Schedule Field
@ $D427 label=ReadScheduleField
D $D427 Reads the next schedule pair and clears its top bit.
  $D427,$08 Read the schedule pair at #REGiy+#N$04 and clear its top bit.
  $D42F,$09 Call #R$A815 if bit 7 of the schedule entry is clear.
@ $D438 label=ReadScheduleField_Next
  $D438,$06 Read the word at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01 into #REGde.
  $D43E,$02 See whether the source word is empty.
  $D440,$02 Leave #N$50 as the result.
  $D442,$03 Call #R$A815 if the source word is not empty.
  $D445,$02 Restore the saved source and result from the stack.
  $D447,$01 Return.

c $D448 Read The Character's Room
@ $D448 label=ReadCharacterRoom
E $D448 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9F08 }
. LIST#
D $D448 Fetches this character's location and steps on to the next record.
  $D448,$08 Call #R$D33C to fetch current character location.
  $D450,$05 Step to the next record.
@ $D455 label=ReadCharacterRoom_Loop
  $D455,$05 Go to #R$D467 if the next object id is #N$FF.
  $D45A,$06 Jump back to #R$D455 if the high byte of this entry's data pointer is zero.
  $D460,$07 Read this entry's id and keep searching while it is not the character asked for.
@ $D467 label=ReadCharacterRoom_Restore
  $D467,$04 Restore the saved schedule entry, the saved count and the saved source from the stack.
  $D46B,$01 Return.

c $D46C Find Object By Field One
@ $D46C label=FindObjectByFieldOne
E $D46C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9F25 }
. LIST#
D $D46C Selects field 1 and searches for an object whose field matches #REGb.
  $D46C,$05 Take #N$01 and continue at #R$D474.

c $D471 Find Object By Field Two
@ $D471 label=FindObjectByFieldTwo
E $D471 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9F2D }
. LIST#
D $D471 Selects field 2 and searches for an object whose field matches #REGb.
  $D471,$03 Take #N$02.

c $D474 Find Object By Field
@ $D474 label=FindObjectByField
D $D474 Writes the field number into the search instruction, then walks the object records for a match.
  $D474,$05 Write the field number into the displacement of the instruction at #R$D48B.
  $D479,$07 Call #R$D33C to fetch current character location.
  $D480,$06 Step to the next record.
@ $D486 label=FindObjectByField_Loop
  $D486,$05 Go to #R$D491 if the next object id is #N$FF.
N $D48B #R$D474 writes #N$01 or #N$02 into the displacement of this instruction.
  $D48B,$06 Compare field #N$02 (modified by #R$D474) of this object with the value wanted, and keep searching if it differs.
@ $D491 label=FindObjectByField_Next
  $D491,$03 Restore the saved schedule entry and the saved count from the stack.
  $D494,$01 Return.

c $D495 Get Object First Location
@ $D495 label=GetObjectFirstLocation
E $D495 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9F82 }
. LIST#
D $D495 Returns at once when the id is #N$FF. Otherwise looks the object up. Returns #N$FF when the object appears more than once, and the first location at offset #N$0F when it appears once.
R $D495 A Object ID
R $D495 O:A #N$FF when the id is empty or the object appears more than once, else its first location
  $D495,$03 Return if the id is #N$FF.
  $D498,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $D49B,$05 See whether the object appears once.
  $D4A0,$02 Leave #N$FF when the object appears more than once.
  $D4A2,$01 Return if the object appears more than once.
N $D4A3 The object appears once, so return its first location.
  $D4A3,$03 Read the object's first location.
  $D4A6,$01 Return.

c $D4A7 Print Object List
@ $D4A7 label=PrintObjectList
E $D4A7 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9F94 }
. LIST#
D $D4A7 Counts the objects in this room and prints them when the room is not empty.
  $D4A7,$04 Save the schedule entry, the result and the count.
  $D4AB,$02 Set the location ID to #N$FF (current character location).
N $D4AD Count objects in location.
  $D4AD,$03 Call #R$D4CC to count the objects in this location.
N $D4B0 Skip printing if no objects were found.
  $D4B0,$02 Stop the list when there are no more objects in this room.
  $D4B2,$04 Keep the object count and take the message at #R$68E4.
N $D4B3 Print the introductory text for the object list.
  $D4B6,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $D4B9,$02 Restore the object count into #REGc from the stack.
  $D4BB,$02 Set the location ID to #N$FF (current character location) again.
  $D4BD,$0A Read the current character's record, then call #R$D51A to list the objects
. and describe them.
@ $D4C7 label=PrintObjectList_End
  $D4C7,$04 Restore the saved count, the saved result and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D4CB,$01 Return.

c $D4CC Count Objects In Location
@ $D4CC label=CountObjectsInLocation
E $D4CC View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$9D97 }
. LIST#
D $D4CC Counts how many objects are inside the current room.
R $D4CC A Location ID (or #N$FF for current character location)
R $D4CC O:A Count of visible objects found in the specified location
  $D4CC,$06 Save the character record, the schedule entry, the pointer and the count.
  $D4D2,$03 Call #R$D04F to set up the object table pointer.
  $D4D5,$02 Initialise the object counter in #REGb to zero.
  $D4D7,$01 Store the location ID in #REGc for later use.
@ $D4D8 label=CountObjectsInLocation_Loop
  $D4D8,$05 Go to #R$D4E6 if the next object id is #N$FF.
  $D4DD,$01 Take the count.
  $D4DE,$03 Call #R$D4EF to check if this object matches the location and visibility
. criteria.
N $D4E1 Skip this object if it does not match the location and visibility criteria.
  $D4E1,$02 Jump back to #R$D4D8 to continue checking objects if this one doesn't match.
N $D4E3 This object matches the criteria, so count it.
  $D4E3,$01 Increment the object counter in #REGb by one.
  $D4E4,$02 Jump back to #R$D4D8 to check the next object.

N $D4E6 Read the count.
@ $D4E6 label=CountObjectsInLocation_End
  $D4E6,$01 Transfer the object count from #REGb to #REGa.
  $D4E7,$01 Set the zero flag when the count is zero.
  $D4E8,$06 Restore the saved count, the saved pointer, the saved schedule entry and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D4EE,$01 Return.

c $D4EF Check Object Match Criteria
@ $D4EF label=CheckObjectMatchCriteria
D $D4EF Sets the carry when the object matches the id, the location and the remaining checks. Returns with the carry clear when any check fails.
R $D4EF A Object ID
R $D4EF B Location ID
R $D4EF O:F Carry flag set if object matches all criteria (location, visibility, type, etc.)
  $D4EF,$05 Go to #R$D518 if the object id does not match.
  $D4F4,$08 Go to #R$D518 if the object is the character being processed.
  $D4FC,$08 Go to #R$D518 if the object is not in this location.
  $D504,$07 Jump to #R$D518 if the character id is at least #N$02.
N $D50B Perform final validation check.
  $D50B,$06 Read the current character.
  $D511,$03 Call #R$D278 to perform an additional validation check.
  $D514,$02 Go to #R$D518 if #R$D278 sets the carry.
N $D516 All checks passed - object matches the criteria.
  $D516,$01 Mark the condition as met.
  $D517,$01 Return.

N $D518 Clear the carry so an object that failed the checks returns as no match.
@ $D518 label=CheckObjectMatchCriteria_NoMatch
N $D518 Object doesn't match any criteria.
  $D518,$01 Clear the carry before returning the failed object match.
  $D519,$01 Return.

c $D51A Print Object List With Formatting
@ $D51A label=PrintObjectListWithFormatting
D $D51A Saves the caller's registers and prints the formatted object list.
R $D51A B Location ID
  $D51A,$08 Call #R$D526 to print formatted object list.
  $D522,$03 Restore the saved source and the saved schedule entry from the stack.
  $D525,$01 Return.

c $D526 Print Formatted Object List
@ $D526 label=PrintFormattedObjectList
D $D526 Sets up the object table and prints each matching object.
R $D526 IX Context pointer (preserved but not directly used)
R $D526 A Object ID (starting object ID for the list, or #N$FF for all objects)
  $D526,$05 Call #R$D04F to set up the object table pointer, keeping character record.
@ $D52B label=PrintFormattedObjectList_Loop
  $D52B,$04 Keep the object id and call #R$D57D to find the next matching object.
  $D52F,$02 Go to #R$D55A if there is no further object.
N $D531 Mark this object as processed and prepare to print its name.
  $D531,$04 Set bit 7 of the object's byte at offset #N$06.
  $D535,$04 Clear the object processing source flag at #R$A028.
  $D539,$03 Call #R$D3D9 to read this object's description flag.
  $D53C,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D546 if the count is not zero.
N $D541 Print separator for the last object in the list.
  $D541,$03 Call #R$A414 to print a full stop.
  $D544,$02 Carry on and print the next object in the list.

N $D546 See whether the text byte is set, then leave the result this step chose, then
. print the character.
@ $D546 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_CheckSeparator
N $D546 Determine which separator to print based on how many objects remain.
  $D546,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D551 if one object remains.
N $D54A Print comma separator for multiple objects.
  $D54A,$02 Take a comma.
  $D54C,$03 Print the character in #REGa through #R$A9B7.
  $D54F,$02 Carry on and print the next object in the list.

N $D551 Print the dictionary word.
@ $D551 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_PrintAnd
N $D551 Print "and" separator before the last object.
  $D551,$03 Take token #N($005D,$04,$04).
  $D554,$03 Call #R$A887 to print token #N$5D.
@ $D557 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_LoopContinue
  $D557,$03 Keep looking for a matching object from #R$D52B.

N $D55A Points the object list at the right room, finds the next matching object, and counts the objects
. there.
@ $D55A label=PrintFormattedObjectList_CheckNested
N $D55A No more objects found in the main list - check for nested objects.
  $D55A,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $D55B,$03 Call #R$D04F to reset the object table pointer.
@ $D55E label=PrintFormattedObjectList_NestedLoop
  $D55E,$04 Keep the result and call #R$D57D to find the next matching object.
  $D562,$02 Finish at #R$D578 when no more objects are found.
  $D564,$04 Call #R$D4CC to count nested objects in the location, keeping find next
. matching object result.
  $D568,$02 Skip the nested list when nothing is inside this object.
N $D56A Print description and recursively print nested objects.
  $D56A,$01 Store the object count in #REGc for later use.
  $D56B,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $D56C,$03 Call #R$D590 to print object description.
  $D56F,$03 Call #R$D526 to print the formatted object list.
  $D572,$02 Carry on after the separator.

N $D574 Drop the saved flags and carry on after the separator.
@ $D574 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_SkipNested
  $D574,$02 Restore the saved values from the stack.
  $D576,$02 And jump to #R$D55E.
@ $D575 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_NestedDone

N $D578 Return.
@ $D578 label=PrintFormattedObjectList_End
N $D578 No nested objects found - restore registers and return.
  $D578,$03 Restore the saved result and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D57B,$01 Return.

c $D57C Find Next Matching Object
@ $D57C label=FindNextMatchingObject
D $D57C Hands the registers saved by the search back to the caller.
  $D57C,$01 Restore the caller's registers.

c $D57D Find The Next Matching Object
@ $D57D label=FindMatchingObject
D $D57D Finds the next object with this id, and gives up when the list ends or the object is not here.
  $D57D,$03 Call #R$D21C to fetch the next object with this id.
  $D580,$02 Go to #R$D584 if another object remains.
N $D582 Object not found, so return that failure to the caller.
  $D582,$01 Set the carry to report that no object was found.
  $D583,$01 Return.

N $D584 Read the record flag.
@ $D584 label=FindMatchingObject_CheckCriteria
  $D584,$04 Call #R$D4EF to check if the object matches the location and visibility
. criteria, keeping fetch next object with id result.
  $D588,$02 Go to #R$D57C if #R$D4EF does not match this object.
@ $D58A label=FindMatchingObject_Found
N $D58A Object matches - return with object ID.
  $D58A,$01 Restore the saved result from the stack.
  $D58B,$03 Read the record flag.
  $D58E,$01 Clear the carry to report that this object was found.
  $D58F,$01 Return.

c $D590 Print Object Description
@ $D590 label=PrintObjectDescription
D $D590 Looks the object up and prints either its special text or the ordinary description.
R $D590 A Object ID
R $D590 C Description type (#N$00 or #N$01)
  $D590,$07 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $D597,$06 Jump to #R$D5A5 if the special-description flag is clear.
@ $D59D label=PrintObjectDescription_Special
N $D59D Print the special description text for this object.
  $D59D,$03 Restore the saved flags from the stack, then save two copies of them back.
  $D5A0,$05 Print the text at #R$68B1 from #R$D5C1.

N $D5A5 Determine which standard description text to use based on the description type.
@ $D5A5 label=PrintObjectDescription_Standard
N $D5A5 Determine which standard description text to use based on the description type.
  $D5A5,$06 Take #N($0485,$04) and jump ahead to #R$D5AE if the count has run out.
  $D5AB,$03 Take the object token at #N($007A,$04,$04).
@ $D5AE label=PrintObjectDescription_PrintAttributes
  $D5AE,$04 Call #R$D5D3 to describe the object's attribute, saving pointer.
  $D5B2,$05 Enable *#R$A2AA (write #N$01 to it).
  $D5B7,$03 Call #R$D3D5 to read this object's description flag.
  $D5BA,$04 Disable *#R$A2AA (write #N$00 to it).
  $D5BE,$03 Take the cannot see message.
@ $D5C1 label=PrintObjectDescription_PrintText
  $D5C1,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $D5C4,$04 Restore the saved result, the saved count and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D5C8,$01 Return.

g $D5C9 Table: Object Attribute Description Text Tokens
@ $D5C9 label=Table_ObjectAttributeDescriptionTokens
D $D5C9 Object Attribute Description Text Tokens read by #R$D5D3.
B $D5C9,$01
L $D5C9,$01,$0A

c $D5D3 Print Object Attribute Description
@ $D5D3 label=PrintObjectAttributeDescription
D $D5D3 Reads the attribute type and prints the matching direction text.
R $D5D3 IX Object pointer
  $D5D3,$03 Take the object attribute description text tokens.
N $D5D6 Extract the attribute type from the object data.
  $D5D6,$03 Read the attribute type.
  $D5D9,$02,b$01 Keep the direction of the attribute type.
  $D5DB,$02 See whether the attribute type is #N$05.
  $D5DD,$07 Count down one, store the attribute type for the next step and move the
. pointer on by the length.
  $D5E4,$03 Call #R$A880 to print the token.
  $D5E7,$01 Restore the comparison from the stack.
  $D5E8,$01 Return unless the attribute type was #N$05.
N $D5E9 Print an additional text token for attribute type #N$06.
  $D5E9,$06 Jump back to #R$A887 to print token #N$08DB.

c $D5EF Get Location Exit Data
@ $D5EF label=GetLocationExitData
E $D5EF View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$A0AE }
. LIST#
D $D5EF Points at the exit list for the room, three bytes for each direction.
R $D5EF A Room ID
R $D5EF O:IX Pointer to exit data
R $D5EF O:BC Exit data length (3 bytes)
N $D5EF Fetch location data pointer.
  $D5EF,$03 Call #R$D224 to look this room up.
  $D5F2,$08 Step to the next record.
  $D5FA,$01 Return.

c $D5FB Get Direction Token
@ $D5FB label=GetDirectionToken
E $D5FB View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$A0BA }
. LIST#
D $D5FB This routine converts a direction value into the address of its
. corresponding token string.
R $D5FB A Direction value
R $D5FB O:DE Address of token string
  $D5FB,$03 Use the direction tokens at #R$D064.
  $D5FE,$02 Step #REGhl back two bytes.
  $D600,$03 Clear bit 7 of the direction.
  $D603,$02 Clear the high byte in #REGd.
  $D605,$02 Add #N$02 times the offset to the base table address.

c $D607 Read The Room Graphics Link
D $D607 Reads the room-graphics link stored at the pointer.
. Reached from the script records at #R$E182 and #R$E1AC.
  $D607,$01 Read the low byte of the room-graphics link.
  $D608,$01 Advance through this record.
  $D609,$01 Read the high byte of the room-graphics link.
  $D60A,$01 Return.

c $D60B Print Location Exits
@ $D60B label=PrintLocationExits
E $D60B View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$A0C8 }
. LIST#
D $D60B Reads this room's exits and prints them.
R $D60B A Room ID
  $D60B,$09 Call #R$D5EF to get location exit data.
  $D614,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D660 to step to the next schedule entry and take the end marker.

N $D61A Skip this exit when its door object is zero.
@ $D61A label=PrintLocationExits_Loop
  $D61A,$06 Jump to #R$D660 if the door object is zero.
  $D620,$07 Jump to #R$D660 if the direction is #N$0B or more.
  $D627,$06 Look up the door object.
  $D62D,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D660 if the object-attribute flag is clear.
  $D633,$06 Call #R$D5FB to get direction token.
  $D639,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D647 if the direction is below a tab.
  $D63D,$05 Take a step of #N($000A,$04,$04) and jump ahead to #R$D64D if the direction token is #N$09.
  $D642,$05 Use a step of #N($00E7,$04,$04) and join #R$D64D.

N $D647 Use the text: message at #R$68BC as the pointer, then use the text: exits at
. #R$68E9 as the pointer, then advance the schedule entry by the count.
@ $D647 label=PrintLocationExits_Next
  $D647,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
@ $D64D label=PrintLocationExits_PrintTextToken
N $D64D Print text token.
  $D64D,$03 Call #R$A887 to print the dictionary word.
  $D650,$06 Take the exits message, then call #R$A59F to print the exits message.
  $D656,$05 Keep the schedule entry, then call #R$D3D5 to run object entry alternate.
  $D65B,$03 Call #R$A414 to print a full stop.
  $D65E,$02 Restore the schedule entry from the stack.
@ $D660 label=PrintLocationExits_DoesMatchCharacter
  $D660,$0A Jump back to #R$D61A unless the next entry is the end marker (#N$FF).
  $D66A,$06 Restore the saved character record, the saved schedule entry, the saved source and the saved count from the stack.
  $D670,$01 Return.

c $D671 Find Next Valid Exit
@ $D671 label=FindNextValidExit
E $D671 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$A124 }
. LIST#
D $D671 Steps through the exit list until it finds one whose door object is zero.
R $D671 IX Pointer to exit data
R $D671 BC Exit data length
R $D671 O:F Zero flag clear when a valid exit is found
  $D671,$02 Step to the next exit entry.
  $D673,$02 Take the end marker.
  $D675,$03 See whether this exit is the end marker.
  $D678,$01 Return if this exit is the end marker.
  $D679,$06 Go to #R$D671 if the door object is not zero.
  $D67F,$06 Jump to #R$D671 if this exit has no direction.
  $D685,$04 Go to #R$D671 if the direction is #N$0B or more.
  $D689,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D68B,$01 Return.

c $D68C Print Location Exits With Formatting
@ $D68C label=PrintLocationExitsWithFormatting
E $D68C View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$A138 }
. LIST#
D $D68C Prints the exit lead-in and then each valid way out of the room.
R $D68C A Room ID
  $D68C,$09 Call #R$D5EF to get location exit data.
  $D695,$05 Go to #R$D6B1 if there is no further exit.
  $D69A,$06 Take the exits message, then call #R$A59F to print the exits message.
@ $D6A0 label=PrintLocationExitsWithFormatting_Loop
  $D6A0,$06 Call #R$D5FB to get direction token.
  $D6A6,$08 Jump back to #R$D6A0 while another exit is valid.
  $D6AE,$03 Call #R$A414 to print a full stop.
@ $D6B1 label=PrintLocationExitsWithFormatting_Next
  $D6B1,$06 Restore the saved count, the saved source, the saved schedule entry and the saved character record from the stack.
  $D6B7,$01 Return.

g $D6B8 Flags: Parser Workspace
D $D6B8 #R$D6B8 is cleared at the game entry point. #R$D6B8+#N$05 holds the parser pointer for the routines below.
. Read by #R$A040, #R$D6C1, #R$D835 and #R$D85B.
B $D6B8,$07

b $D6BF Display Record
D $D6BF Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.
N $D6BF Named from the script command at #R$9923.
B $D6BF,$02

c $D6C1 Parse Input
@ $D6C1 label=ParseInput
D $D6C1 Command parser. It reads the line buffer at #R$5B00, tokenises it, and handles backspace and quoting. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $D6C1 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D6BF.
  $D6C1,$06 Return zero through #R$DC15 if no command was entered.
  $D6C7,$04 Clear the parser state.
  $D6CB,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D6CD,$01 Return.
  $D6CE,$03 Take the line buffer.
@ $D6D1 label=ParseInput_Loop
  $D6D1,$08 Call #R$D9C5 to read the next character and jump to #R$D6E0 when it is #N$D0.
  $D6D9,$04 Jump back to #R$D6D1 while the character is not #N$C0.
  $D6DD,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D6DF,$01 Return.

N $D6E0 Hands the following step back to the caller and reports that the test failed.
@ $D6E0 label=ParseInput_Next
  $D6E0,$03 Hand back the address of the following step.
  $D6E3,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $D6E4,$01 Return.
  $D6E5,$08 Set the print mode to #N$01 and point #REGhl at the message at #R$6857+#N$02.
N $D6ED Print text and handle control characters.
  $D6ED,$03 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $D6F0,$08 Read the parser pointer, write #N$C0 there and read the word at #R$A029.
@ $D6F8 label=ParseInput_Byte
  $D6F8,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D6FF if this byte is not #N$2E.
  $D6FD,$02 Take a space.
  $D6FF,$03 Call #R$A9B7 to print the character.
  $D702,$05 Step on and jump back to #R$D6F8 until a space has been printed.
  $D707,$08 Print character #N$22 through #R$A9B7, then call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $D70F,$01 Clear #REGa so the print mode that follows starts at zero.
  $D710,$03 Record the result in the print mode.
  $D713,$01 Return.
B $D714,$01 Parser state byte, read by #R$D6C1 and #R$D99E.
N $D715 Named from the script command at #R$9928.
B $D715,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$D6CE.
N $D719 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D715.
  $D719,$01 Clear the parser state.
  $D71A,$03 Record the parser.
N $D71D Reset screen display.
  $D71D,$03 Call #R$C142 to reset the screen.
  $D720,$06 Jump to #R$D760 if the state byte at #R$D714 is set.
  $D726,$08 Call #R$A2AB to clear buffer.
  $D72E,$07 Take the line buffer.
@ $D735 label=ParseInput_CharacterParseInput
  $D735,$07 Store #N$C0 as this character's id, then call #R$D9C5 to skip spaces.
  $D73C,$09 Store #REGd, step on one byte and jump ahead to #R$D751 if the character is #N$E0.
  $D745,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D751 if the character is #N$F0.
  $D749,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D751 if the character is #N$90.
  $D74D,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D75B if the character is at least #N$80.
@ $D751 label=ParseInput_CharacterLowByte
  $D751,$06 Store #REGb and #REGc as the word at #REGiy+#N$00-#N$01.
  $D757,$04 Step on two places.
@ $D75B label=ParseInput_Pointer
  $D75B,$05 Jump back to #R$D735 while the result in #REGd is not #N$C0.
@ $D760 label=ParseInput_RecordParserPointer
  $D760,$06 Record the parser pointer.
  $D766,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D768,$01 Return.
N $D769 Named from the script command at #R$992D.
B $D769,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$D770.
N $D76D Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D769.
  $D76D,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D76F,$01 Return.
N $D770 Reached through the display record at #R$D769, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $D770,$03 Read the parser pointer.
  $D773,$01 Read the next input character.
  $D774,$02 Keep bits 4-7.
  $D776,$02 See whether the high nibble is #N$C0.
  $D778,$03 Jump to #R$DC15 when the high nibble is #N$C0.
  $D77B,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D77D,$01 Return.
N $D77E Named from the script command at #R$9932.
B $D77E,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.
N $D780 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D77E.
  $D780,$08 Clear the character script entry list and set the command mode at #R$A024 to #N$01.
  $D788,$09 Read the parser pointer, store it in the word at #R$A02B and call #R$AAB2.
  $D791,$06 Clear the initialised flag.
  $D797,$03 Read the word at #R$A02B.
  $D79A,$05 Store the parser pointer and go to #R$D7B2 if preparing the character succeeded.
  $D79F,$06 Read the first display link and store the pointer in the next display link.
  $D7A5,$03 Clear the pointer.
  $D7A8,$07 Store the zero pointer in the first display link and clear the parser workspace byte at #R$D6B8.
  $D7AF,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D7B1,$01 Return.

N $D7B2 Reads both display links, and takes the special path when the code is 2.
@ $D7B2 label=ParseInput_ReadsBothDisplay
  $D7B2,$04 Call #R$D87D to read the first display link.
  $D7B6,$04 Call #R$D88A to read the next display link.
  $D7BA,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D7D0 if the character is #N$02.
  $D7BE,$08 Set the print mode to #N$01 and point #REGhl at the message at #R$685F+#N$07.
N $D7C6 Print text and handle control characters.
  $D7C6,$03 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $D7C9,$07 Clear the print mode and return zero through #R$DC15.

N $D7D0 Read the script-cycle pointer and return when it is still set.
@ $D7D0 label=ParseInput_ScriptCyclePointer
  $D7D0,$06 Return if the script-cycle pointer at #R$A01F is not zero.
  $D7D6,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.
N $D7D9 Named from the script command at #R$9937.
B $D7D9,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$D7E1.
N $D7DD Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D7D9.
  $D7DD,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D7DF,$01 Return.
B $D7E0,$01 State byte for this input step. Read by #R$D7E1 and written by #R$D7E7 and
. #R$D870.
N $D7E1 Reached through the display record at #R$D7D9, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $D7E1,$06 Jump to #R$D7F1 if the state byte at #R$D7E0 is zero.
  $D7E7,$07 Clear the state byte at #R$D7E0 and call #R$D88A to read the next display link.
  $D7EE,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.

N $D7F1 Read the next display link and return when it is still set.
@ $D7F1 label=ParseInput_TestDisplayLinkSet
  $D7F1,$06 Return if the next display link at #R$9FEE+#N$04 is set.
  $D7F7,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.
N $D7FA Named from the script command at #R$993C.
B $D7FA,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.
N $D7FC Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$D7FA.
  $D7FC,$07 Clear the display.
  $D803,$04 Read the next display link.
  $D807,$09 Call #R$B191 to initialise display buffer.
N $D810 A command is being read.
  $D810,$08 Remember that a command is being read, and leave the line when the display buffer
. came back empty.
  $D818,$06 Store the display flag in the parser workspace byte at #R$D6B8.
  $D81E,$05 Write #N$01 to the token format flag.
  $D823,$06 Record the current room.
  $D829,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $D82C,$06 Record the location value and read the object table pointer.
  $D832,$03 Record the parser workspace.

c $D835 Release The Next Display Record
@ $D835 label=ReleaseNextDisplayRecord
D $D835 Reads the parser workspace, then reads the character
. record from the next display link.
  $D835,$06 Jump to #R$D858 if the parser workspace is set.
  $D83B,$04 Read the next display link.
@ $D83F label=ReleaseNextDisplayRecord_PointerSavedRecord
  $D83F,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $D845,$03 Store the pointer in the next display link.
  $D848,$07 Jump ahead to #R$D858 if the pointer is zero.
  $D84F,$09 Jump to #R$D83F if a marker on the moved marker is set.
@ $D858 label=ReleaseNextDisplayRecord_TestActionSucceeded
  $D858,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D85A,$01 Return.

c $D85B Record A Parser Failure
@ $D85B label=RecordParserFailure
D $D85B On failure, store the result in the parser workspace, then read the display flag.
  $D85B,$04 Clear the parser workspace.
  $D85F,$06 Jump to #R$D870 if the display is zero.
  $D865,$08 Store #N$FF in the saved character and call #R$D835 to release the next display record.
  $D86D,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15.

@ $D870 label=RecordParserFailure_StepSucceedsScript
  $D870,$06 Store #N$01 in the state byte at #R$D7E0 and save #REGhl.
  $D876,$03 Read the parser pointer.
  $D879,$03 Write #N$C0 at the parser pointer, restore #REGhl.
  $D87C,$01 Return.

c $D87D Read First Display Link
@ $D87D label=ReadFirstDisplayLink
D $D87D Reads the first display link at #R$9FF0, clears it, and follows the character record it
. held.
N $D87D Follow the pointer at #R$9FF0.
  $D87D,$07 Read the first display link into #REGix and take a zero pointer.
  $D884,$03 Store the pointer in the first display link.
  $D887,$03 Jump back to #R$A1AF to follow the record pointer.

c $D88A Read Next Display Link
@ $D88A label=ReadNextDisplayLink
D $D88A Reads the next display link at #R$9FEE+#N$04, clears it, and follows the character record it
. held.
N $D88A Follow the pointer at #R$9FEE+#N$04.
  $D88A,$07 Read the next display link into #REGix and take a zero pointer.
  $D891,$03 Store the pointer in the next display link.
  $D894,$03 Jump back to #R$A1AF to follow the record pointer.

g $D897 Quotation Mark Flag
D $D897 Set while the command line is inside quotation marks.
. Set by #R$D8BC, toggled by #R$D8F6 and #R$D908, and read by #R$D949.
B $D897,$01

c $D898 Read Command Line
@ $D898 label=ReadCommandLine
D $D898 Prints the input prompt and stores the typed command in the line buffer at #R$5B00.
  $D898,$06 Point the object attribute at the prompt token #N$0BB8.
  $D89E,$08 Set the print mode to #N$01 and call #R$D8AD.
  $D8A6,$05 Start the input from zero and store it in the print mode.
  $D8AB,$01 Restore #REGaf from the stack.
  $D8AC,$01 Return.

c $D8AD Read A Command Line
@ $D8AD label=ReadCommandLine_CommandLinePrints
E $D8AD View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$6DD6 }
. LIST#
D $D8AD Reads a command line. Prints a prompt and stores the text at #R$5B00.
  $D8AD,$05 Print character #N$3E through #R$A9B7.
  $D8B2,$05 Take a space and print it through #R$A9B7.
  $D8B7,$05 Take the line buffer.
@ $D8BC label=ReadCommandLine_Loop
  $D8BC,$07 Mark the command line as started.
@ $D8C3 label=ReadCommandLine_CompareLocationData
  $D8C3,$06 Go to #R$D9AC if #R$A536 returns zero.
  $D8C9,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D8D2 if bit 7 is clear.
  $D8CD,$05 Jump ahead to #R$D99E if the command is #N$40.
@ $D8D2 label=ReadCommandLine_TestMarkerClear
  $D8D2,$05 Call #R$D978 if bit 0 of #REGc is clear.
  $D8D7,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D8E2 if the input is not #N$18.
  $D8DD,$05 Delete the last input character and go back to #R$D8BC.

N $D8E2 A backspace is printed as a backspace unless the top bit of the count is set.
@ $D8E2 label=ReadCommandLine_TestBackspacePrintedBackspace
  $D8E2,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D900 if the command is not #N$08.
  $D8E6,$04 Jump back to #R$D8C3 while bit 7 is present.
  $D8EA,$05 Print character #N$08 through #R$A9B7.
  $D8EF,$07 Jump back to #R$D8C3 while this byte is not #N$22.
  $D8F6,$08 Toggle the quotation-mark flag at #R$D897.
  $D8FE,$02 Read the next key from #R$D8C3.

N $D900 A quotation mark toggles the typing marker. A key at or above #N$40 is stored as typed.
@ $D900 label=ReadCommandLine_TestQuotationMarkToggles
  $D900,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the key is at least #N$40.
  $D904,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D917 unless the key is a double quote (#N$22).
  $D908,$08 Toggle the quotation-mark flag at #R$D897.
  $D910,$03 Call #R$D968 to add a full stop when the quotation marker turns on.
  $D913,$04 Take a quotation mark and jump ahead to #R$D93C.

N $D917 A blank or a full stop ends this input, and a carriage return ends the line.
@ $D917 label=ReadCommandLine_TestBlankFullStop
  $D917,$04 Jump to #R$D93C if the player pressed "SPACE".
  $D91B,$04 Jump to #R$D949 if the player pressed "ENTER".
  $D91F,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the input is #N$2E.
  $D923,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the input is #N$2C.
  $D927,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the input is #N$2F.
  $D92B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the input is #N$60.
  $D92F,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D93C if the input is #N$2D.
  $D933,$04 Go to #R$D93C when the input is a colon.
  $D937,$05 Call #R$DAE8 and jump to #R$D8C3 if the character is not a digit.
@ $D93C label=ReadCommandLine_TestCountHasReached
  $D93C,$04 Jump to #R$D946 if the count has reached zero.
  $D940,$04 Store the character in the line buffer and print it through #R$A9B7.
  $D944,$02 Step to the next entry and count down one.
  $D946,$03 Return to #R$D8C3 for the next character of the command.

N $D949 Read the command marker, then leave a carriage return.
@ $D949 label=ReadCommandLine_CommandMarkerLeave
  $D949,$07 Call #R$D95C if the quotation mark flag at #R$D897 is zero.
  $D950,$03 Call #R$D968 to test for a sentence stop.
  $D953,$06 Take a new line and store it at the line buffer, then call #R$A9B7 to print
. character.
  $D959,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $D95B,$01 Return.

c $D95C Leave A Quote As The Result
@ $D95C label=LeaveQuoteResult
D $D95C Prints a quote (#N$22) after the sentence-stop test.
  $D95C,$03 Call #R$D968 to test for a sentence stop.
  $D95F,$06 Store #N$22 in the line and print it through #R$A9B7.
  $D965,$01 Advance through the text.
  $D966,$01 Count down the remaining steps.
  $D967,$01 Return.

c $D968 Test For A Full Stop
@ $D968 label=TestFullStop
D $D968 Prints a sentence stop when the previous character is not already one.
  $D968,$06 Jump ahead to #R$D976 if this byte is #N$2E.
  $D96E,$08 Step on, count one fewer, store #N$2E and print it through #R$A9B7.
@ $D976 label=TestFullStop_TestAdvanceThroughText
  $D976,$01 Advance through the text.
  $D977,$01 Return.

c $D978 Map The Cursor Keys
@ $D978 label=MapCursorKeys
E $D978 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$6E4F }
. LIST#
D $D978 Turns a cursor key into a compass letter: right (#N$09) to E, left (#N$08) to W, down (#N$0A) to S and up (#N$5B) to N.
  $D978,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D996 if the key is cursor right (#N$09).
  $D97C,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D99A if the key is cursor left (#N$08).
  $D980,$04 Jump ahead to #R$D992 if the key is cursor down (#N$0A).
  $D984,$03 Return unless the key is cursor up (#N$5B).
  $D987,$02 Take N for cursor up.
  $D989,$05 Store it at the line buffer and step to the next entry, then call #R$A9B7 to print
. character.
  $D98E,$01 Count down the remaining steps.
  $D98F,$02 Leave #N$0D as the result.
  $D991,$01 Return.

N $D992 Cursor down selects the letter S.
@ $D992 label=MapCursorKeys_South
  $D992,$04 Take the direction letter S and go to #R$D989 to store it in the line buffer and
. print it.

N $D996 Cursor right selects the letter E.
@ $D996 label=MapCursorKeys_East
  $D996,$04 Take the direction letter E and go to #R$D989 to store it in the line buffer and
. print it.

N $D99A Cursor left selects the letter W.
@ $D99A label=MapCursorKeys_West
  $D99A,$04 Take the direction letter W and go to #R$D989 to store it in the line buffer and
. print it.

c $D99E Ignore Input Until The Game Has Started
@ $D99E label=IgnoreInputUntilGameHasStarted
D $D99E Returns without deleting a character when the initialised flag is still clear.
  $D99E,$07 Jump to #R$D8C3 if the game has already been initialised.
  $D9A5,$07 Mark the parser state so input is ignored until the game has started.
@ $D9AC label=IgnoreInputUntilGameHasStarted_InputPrintCharacter
  $D9AC,$06 Print character #N$08 through #R$A9B7, keeping the flags.
  $D9B2,$03 Print the character through #R$A9B7.
  $D9B5,$01 Restore #REGa from the stack.
  $D9B6,$01 Return.

c $D9B7 Delete Input Character
@ $D9B7 label=DeleteInputCharacter
E $D9B7 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$6E8B }
. LIST#
D $D9B7 Deletes the character before the cursor on the command line and prints the correction.
N $D9B7 Delete the character before the cursor.
  $D9B7,$02 See whether bit 7 of the count is set.
  $D9B9,$01 Return if that marker is set.
  $D9BA,$05 Print character #N$08 through #R$A9B7.
  $D9BF,$04 Count one more, step back one byte and jump to #R$D9B7.

g $D9C3 Word: Saved Input Pointer
D $D9C3 Input pointer saved while the dictionary is measured.
. Written by #R$D9E4 and read by #R$DA21.
W $D9C3,$02

c $D9C5 Skip Spaces In The Input
@ $D9C5 label=SkipSpaces
D $D9C5 Skips blanks in the typed command and classifies the next character.
  $D9C5,$07 Jump back to #R$D9C6 if this byte is #N$20, saving source.
  $D9CC,$04 Step back to this character and store the pointer at #R$A029.
  $D9D0,$04 Jump to #R$DA06 if the player pressed "ENTER".
  $D9D4,$05 Go to #R$DA2F if the character is a full stop, a comma or a quote.
  $D9D9,$05 Go to #R$DA0A if the token has no dictionary entry.
  $D9DE,$06 Save #REGix and clear the match length at #R$DC14.
@ $D9E4 label=SkipSpaces_SaveInputPointer
  $D9E4,$04 Remember the input pointer at #R$D9C3.
@ $D9E8 label=SkipSpaces_MeasureDictionary
  $D9E8,$08 Read the dictionary address and measure the gap from it.
  $D9F0,$03 Jump ahead to #R$DA12 if the two pointers meet.
  $D9F3,$05 Copy the pointer into #REGde and call #R$A94D to handle the nested format.
  $D9F8,$07 Jump back to #R$D9E8 if the high nibble is #N$D0.
  $D9FF,$05 Go to #R$DA12 if the typed word does not match.
  $DA04,$02 The word matched, so measure the next one from #R$D9E4.

N $DA06 ENTER selects the result #N$C0.
@ $DA06 label=SkipSpaces_EnterResult
  $DA06,$04 ENTER uses the result #N$C0, stored from #R$DA0C.

N $DA0A Use #N$D0 when the word is not in the dictionary.
@ $DA0A label=SkipSpaces_UnknownWord
  $DA0A,$02 Take #N$D0 as the result for an unknown word.
  $DA0C,$03 Clear #REGbc (no time was given).
  $DA0F,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $DA10,$01 Store the result in the high byte of the source.
  $DA11,$01 Return.

N $DA12 The saved length decides whether this character is kept.
@ $DA12 label=SkipSpaces_CheckSavedLength
  $DA12,$08 Jump to #R$DA21 if the state byte at #R$DC14 is at least #N$04.
  $DA1A,$07 Go to #R$DA0A if the matched-word length does not equal the saved count.
@ $DA21 label=SkipSpaces_ReadInputPointer
  $DA21,$07 Read the input pointer and keep the high digit of that character.
  $DA28,$03 Take the dictionary index.
  $DA2B,$04 Swap in the saved pointer.
@ $DA2F label=SkipSpaces_ReturnWithHighByte
  $DA2F,$01 Restore the saved source from the stack.
  $DA30,$01 Store the result in the high byte of the source.
  $DA31,$01 Return.

g $DA32 Word: Dictionary Address
D $DA32 Address in the dictionary text, saved while a word is matched.
. Written by #R$DA50 and read by #R$D9E8.
W $DA32,$02

c $DA34 Find The Dictionary Entry For This Token
@ $DA34 label=FindTokenDictionaryEntry
D $DA34 Turns the low five bits of this character into the dictionary offset. The zero flag is set when the dictionary link is empty.
  $DA34,$08 Keep this character's dictionary length and turn it into an offset.
  $DA3C,$08 Use the dictionary index at #R$5D80.
  $DA44,$04 Read the next byte of the input byte.
@ $DA48 label=FindTokenDictionaryEntry_DictionaryLinkEmpty
  $DA48,$08 Go back to #R$DA48 while this dictionary link is empty.
  $DA50,$08 Add the dictionary base and store the address at #R$DA32.
  $DA58,$01 Read the high byte of the source.
  $DA59,$01 Merge the low byte of the source into the result.
  $DA5A,$01 Return when this dictionary link is empty.
  $DA5B,$07 Take the dictionary index and point #REGde at the word buffer (#R$5C0B).
  $DA62,$02 Count #N$01.
@ $DA64 label=FindTokenDictionaryEntry_RecordFlagByte
  $DA64,$07 Jump to #R$DA74 if this dictionary byte is below #N$40 (the word has ended).
  $DA6B,$09 Make the letter lower case, store it in the word buffer, and repeat from #R$DA64.

N $DA74 On failure, store the result in the working byte, then keep the match flags before
. returning.
@ $DA74 label=FindTokenDictionaryEntry_SignalStepFailed
  $DA74,$06 Clear the character at the pointer and remember the input length.
  $DA7A,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DA7C,$01 Return.

c $DA7D Match The Dictionary Word
@ $DA7D label=MatchDictionaryWord
D $DA7D Compares the typed word with this dictionary entry and keeps the match when it is longer than the one already saved.
  $DA7D,$06 Read the input length.
  $DA83,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DAA5 if the first value is at least the second.
  $DA89,$08 Point #REGde at the word buffer (#R$5C0B) and #REGhl at #N$5C42.
@ $DA91 label=MatchDictionaryWord_CompareLetters
  $DA91,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DA9D if this dictionary letter is zero.
  $DA95,$03 Jump ahead to #R$DA9D if the dictionary letter differs.
  $DA98,$05 Step to the next letter, count one more and jump back to #R$DA91.

N $DA9D Accepts the saved index while it is still within the match length.
@ $DA9D label=MatchDictionaryWord_AcceptLength
  $DA9D,$06 Go to #R$DAA9 if the match length equals the saved count.
  $DAA3,$02 Jump ahead to #R$DAA9 if the first value is below the second.
@ $DAA5 label=MatchDictionaryWord_NoMatch
  $DAA5,$02 Restore the text pointer from the stack.
  $DAA7,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $DAA8,$01 Return.

N $DAA9 Take the input length and remember it in the space marker.
@ $DAA9 label=MatchDictionaryWord_RememberLength
  $DAA9,$04 Take the dictionary index and store it in the workspace byte at #R$DC14.
@ $DAAD label=MatchDictionaryWord_NextEntry
  $DAAD,$08 Go to #R$DAC1 if bit 7 of the next byte is clear.
  $DAB5,$08 Step on two places, step to the next entry and take the dictionary index.
  $DABD,$04 Jump back to #R$DAAD to step to the next entry and step back one entry.

N $DAC1 Keep the match flags before returning.
@ $DAC1 label=MatchDictionaryWord_Matched
  $DAC1,$02 Restore the saved pointers from the stack.
  $DAC3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DAC5,$01 Return.

c $DAC6 Test For A Common Word
@ $DAC6 label=TestCommonWord
E $DAC6 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$6F30 }
. LIST#
D $DAC6 Classifies a vocabulary code, a numeral, a sentence stop or a comma for the command parser.
  $DAC6,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB67 if the dictionary index is #N$60.
  $DACB,$06 Jump to #R$DAEF if the character is a digit.
  $DAD1,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DAE2 if it is #N$2E.
  $DAD7,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DAE2 if it is #N$2C.
  $DADD,$03 Return unless the character is a quotation mark (#N$22).
  $DAE0,$02 Count #N$90.
@ $DAE2 label=TestCommonWord_TestAdvanceThroughRecord
  $DAE2,$01 Advance through this record.
  $DAE3,$01 Read the count.
  $DAE4,$03 Clear #REGbc (no time was given).
  $DAE7,$01 Return.

c $DAE8 Test For A Digit
@ $DAE8 label=TestDigit
D $DAE8 Sets the carry when #REGa is not a digit (#N$30 to #N$39).
  $DAE8,$03 Return if it is below #N$30.
  $DAEB,$02 See whether this byte is past the digits.
  $DAED,$01 Reverse the condition.
  $DAEE,$01 Return.

c $DAEF Parse A Time
@ $DAEF label=ParseATime
D $DAEF Reads hours, an optional ":" and minutes into #REGb/#REGc, then an "A" or "P"
. plus "M" suffix. "A" sets bit 6 of #REGb; "P" sets bit 6 of #REGc for PM.
  $DAEF,$03 Call #R$DBF7 to start from an empty length.
  $DAF2,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DB05 if this byte is #N$3A.
  $DAF8,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB05 when the input character is A.
  $DAFC,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB05 when the input character is P.
  $DB00,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB84 unless the character is a full stop, a comma or a space.
@ $DB05 label=ParseATime_CheckHours
  $DB05,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB4F if the count is at least #N$3C.
  $DB0A,$02 Set bit 7 of the count.
  $DB0C,$06 Start the number at zero, read the input character, and parse it.
  $DB12,$02 Go to #R$DB60 if the character is a full stop, a comma or a space.
@ $DB14 label=ParseATime_ReadMinutes
  $DB14,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB21 if the character is not #N$3A.
  $DB18,$04 Step to the next character and call #R$DBF7 to read a number.
  $DB1C,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB4F if the number is at least #N$3C.
@ $DB21 label=ParseATime_CheckSuffix
  $DB21,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DB60 if the character is a full stop, a comma or a space.
  $DB27,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB30 if the character is not #N$41.
  $DB2B,$02 Set bit 6 of the count.
  $DB2D,$03 Step to the next input character and continue at #R$DB37.

N $DB30 Accept "AM" or "PM": "A" has already set bit 6 of #REGb, and "P" sets bit 6 of
. #REGc for PM; both then require "M".
@ $DB30 label=ParseATime_AMPM
  $DB30,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB4F if the character is not #N$50.
  $DB34,$03 Set bit 6 of #REGc for PM and step to the next character.
@ $DB37 label=ParseATime_CheckM
  $DB37,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB4F if this byte is not #N$4D.
@ $DB3C label=ParseATime_TestStepEntry
  $DB3C,$01 Step to the next entry.
  $DB3D,$05 Jump back to #R$DB3C if this byte is #N$2E.
  $DB42,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB60 if the character is a comma or a space.
  $DB47,$04 Jump to #R$DB5F if the player pressed "ENTER".
  $DB4B,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DB5F if the character is #N$22.
@ $DB4F label=ParseATime_SignalNothingMatched
  $DB4F,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $DB50,$02 Leave #N$D0 as the result.
  $DB52,$03 Clear #REGbc (no time was given).
  $DB55,$01 Return.

c $DB56 Test For A Full Stop
@ $DB56 label=TestFullStop_TestReturnsCharacterSentence
D $DB56 Returns at once when the character is a sentence stop.
  $DB56,$03 Return if the input is #N$2E.

c $DB59 Test For A Comma
@ $DB59 label=TestComma
D $DB59 Returns at once for a comma, and also accepts a blank as the end of a word.
  $DB59,$03 Return if the input is #N$2C.
  $DB5C,$02 See whether the character just read is a space.
  $DB5E,$01 Return.

N $DB5F Step back one character and return #N$F0.
@ $DB5F label=TestComma_TestStepOneByte
  $DB5F,$01 Step back one entry.
  $DB60,$01 Set the zero flag.
  $DB61,$02 Leave #N$F0 as the result.
  $DB63,$01 Return.
B $DB64,$01 First number. Written by #R$DB6B and #R$DB84, and read by #R$DBC0.
B $DB65,$01 Second number. Written by #R$DB6B, #R$DB88, #R$DB94 and #R$DBA8.
. Read by #R$DBCE.
B $DB66,$01 Third number. Written by #R$DB72, #R$DB88, #R$DB9E and #R$DBB7.
. Read by #R$DBDB.
  $DB67,$04 Step to the next character and call #R$DBF7 to read a number.
  $DB6B,$07 Store this number as the first number and clear the second number.
  $DB72,$03 Clear the third number.
  $DB75,$08 Jump to #R$DBC0 if the next character is not a digit.
  $DB7D,$07 Read the next number into #REGb and continue at #R$DB88.

c $DB84 Start A Number
@ $DB84 label=StartParsedNumber
D $DB84 Starts a number with the first number cleared, then reads the separator that follows.
  $DB84,$04 Clear the first number.
@ $DB88 label=StartParsedNumber_ClearSecondThird
  $DB88,$07 Clear the second number and the third number.
  $DB8F,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DB9A if the character is not 'S'.
  $DB94,$04 Store the count as the second number.
@ $DB98 label=StartParsedNumber_Combine
  $DB98,$02 Jump ahead to #R$DBBF to combine the number.

N $DB9A The character 'D' stores the count as the third number.
@ $DB9A label=StartParsedNumber_TestLetterD
  $DB9A,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DBA4 if the character is not 'D'.
  $DB9E,$06 Store the count as the third number and jump to #R$DB98.

N $DBA4 A slash stores the count as the second number.
@ $DBA4 label=StartParsedNumber_TestSlash
  $DBA4,$04 Jump to #R$DB4F if the character is not '/'.
  $DBA8,$04 Store the count as the second number.
  $DBAC,$06 Jump ahead to #R$DBBF if the next character is '-'.
  $DBB2,$05 Jump to #R$DB4F if this character is not a digit.
  $DBB7,$08 Read the next number, store it as the third number and continue at #R$DBC0.

N $DBBF Steps past this character before the three numbers are combined.
@ $DBBF label=StartParsedNumber_StepPastCharacter
  $DBBF,$01 Step to the next character.
@ $DBC0 label=StartParsedNumber_CombineNumbers
  $DBC0,$08 Save the input pointer, load the first number into #REGhl and double it.
  $DBC8,$06 Multiply #REGhl by ten.
  $DBCE,$08 Add the second number and double the total.
  $DBD6,$05 Multiply #REGhl by six.
  $DBDB,$08 Add the third number and swap it with the saved input pointer.
  $DBE3,$07 Fail through #R$DB4F if the value is #N$4000 or more.
  $DBEA,$08 Move bit 7 of #REGc into #REGb and set bit 7 of #REGc.
  $DBF2,$05 Set bit 7 of #REGb and continue at #R$DB3D.

c $DBF7 Parse An Input Number
@ $DBF7 label=ParseInputNumber
D $DBF7 Reads decimal digits from the input and returns the number they make in #REGa.
  $DBF7,$03 Count #N$00.
@ $DBFA label=ParseInputNumber_DigitLoop
  $DBFA,$06 Jump to #R$DC10 if this character is not a digit.
  $DC00,$08 Reduce the total by #N$30.
  $DC08,$08 Fold this digit into the number and go back to #R$DBFA for the next one.

N $DC10 Return.
@ $DC10 label=ParseInputNumber_ReturnValue
  $DC10,$01 Read the low byte of the count.
  $DC11,$01 Restore #REGbc from the stack.
  $DC12,$01 Return.
B $DC13,$01 Length of the dictionary word. Written by #R$DA74 and read by #R$DA1A and
. #R$DA7D.
B $DC14,$01 Longest match accepted so far. Cleared by #R$D9DE, updated by #R$DAA9, and
. read by #R$DA12, #R$DA83 and #R$DA9D.

N $DC15 Shared exit. Returns zero for both results so the caller treats the test as failed.
@ $DC15 label=ParseInputNumber_ReturnZero
  $DC15,$03 Clear #REGa and #REGhl.
  $DC18,$01 Return.
B $DC19,$07 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$1B, value #N$20, parameters #N$40, #N$28,
. #N$00, #N$00, #N$00.
N $DC20 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DC19.
  $DC20,$04 Return if the character check did not match.
  $DC24,$06 Call #R$D46C to find an object by field one.
  $DC2A,$06 Jump to #R$DC4B if the location is zero.
  $DC30,$07 Keep the location in #REGb, save the character attribute and take #N$01.
  $DC37,$03 Record the location token processing flag.
N $DC3A Compare current character.
  $DC3A,$03 Call #R$DD0B to read the character the command is about.
  $DC3D,$07 Read the character attribute.
N $DC44 Compare current character.
  $DC44,$03 Call #R$DD0B to read the character the command is about.
  $DC47,$01 Start from zero.

N $DC48 Record the result in the location token processing flag at #R$D0DC, then mark
. success for the script.
  $DC48,$03 Store it in the location token processing flag.
@ $DC4B label=ParseInputNumber_ReportSuccess
  $DC4B,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DC4D,$01 Return.
N $DC4E Reached through the display record at #R$DC57, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DC4E,$06 Call #R$D46C to find an object by field one.
  $DC54,$03 Go to #R$DCDD to read the schedule entry from the current character.
B $DC57,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$4B, value #N$20, handler #R$DC4E.
N $DC5B Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DC57.
  $DC5B,$03 Read the character id from this record.
  $DC5E,$03 Store it as the saved character.
  $DC61,$01 Start from zero.
  $DC62,$03 Clear the display mode.
  $DC65,$01 Count down one.
  $DC66,$03 Store #N$FF as the room display parameter.
  $DC69,$03 Go to #R$DD07 to read the matched character's room.

c $DC6C Check Character ID
@ $DC6C label=CheckCharacterID
D $DC6C Sets the zero flag if the current character is #N$00, #N$01 or Sherlock (#N$64); otherwise returns non-zero.
  $DC6C,$06 Return if the character is zero.
  $DC72,$03 Return if it is #N$01.
  $DC75,$03 Return if the character is Sherlock (#N$64).
  $DC78,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DC7A,$01 Return.
B $DC7B,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$20, value #N$10.
N $DC7D Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DC7B.
  $DC7D,$03 Call #R$DC6C to see whether the character is zero, one, or Sherlock.
  $DC80,$01 Return unless the character is zero, one, or Sherlock.
N $DC81 Print newline.
  $DC81,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $DC84,$06 Read the character attribute, then call #R$D102 to run location token
. display.
  $DC8A,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DC8C,$01 Return.

c $DC8D Copy The Saved Character Id
@ $DC8D label=CopySavedCharacterIdentifier
D $DC8D Copies the saved character's id onto the current object.
. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
. Run through the table at #R$9F38 by #R$C972.
B $DC8D,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$6A, value #N$10, handler #R$DDB8.
N $DC91 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DC8D.
  $DC91,$08 Read the current object and read the current character record.
  $DC99,$06 Store the moved marker.
  $DC9F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DCA1,$01 Return.
N $DCA2 Reached through the display record at #R$DCC1, dispatched by #R$CB40.
N $DCA2 The room display parameter is #N$FF or #N$FE, or the object's room matches the character.
  $DCA2,$05 Jump ahead to #R$DCB6 if the room display parameter is #N$FF or #N$FE, or the
. object's room byte matches the character being processed.

c $DCA7 Apply The Room Display Parameter
@ $DCA7 label=ApplyRoomDisplay
D $DCA7 Tests the room named for the picture, and returns the failure the script expects when the test
. does not pass.
  $DCA7,$09 Call #R$D278 to test for the end marker.
  $DCB0,$03 Go to #R$DC15 to return zero when the carry is set.
  $DCB3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DCB5,$01 Return.

N $DCB6 Hands the following step back to the caller and reports that the test failed.
@ $DCB6 label=ApplyRoomDisplay_ReturnText
  $DCB6,$03 Hand back the address of the following step.
  $DCB9,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $DCBA,$01 Return.
  $DCBB,$06 Jump back to #R$A59F to print this message.
B $DCC1,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$69, value #N$10, handler #R$DCA2.
N $DCC5 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DCC1.
  $DCC5,$04 Read the current object.
  $DCC9,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $DCCC,$03 Store it on the object.
  $DCCF,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DCD1,$01 Return.
N $DCD2 Reached through the display record at #R$DD03, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DCD2,$06 Call #R$D448 to read the character's room.
@ $DCD8 label=ApplyRoomDisplay_Loop
  $DCD8,$05 Return zero through #R$DC15 when the character id is the end marker.

c $DCDD Read The Schedule Entry From The Current Character
@ $DCDD label=ReadScheduleEntryFromCurrentCharacter
D $DCDD Fails the script unless the character's script position and location allow the object description. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $DCDD,$03 Call #R$CBA7 to check the flag and start a new line.
  $DCE0,$04 Read the current character record.
  $DCE4,$08 Jump to #R$DC15 unless #REGiy+#N$01 is #N$FF.
  $DCEC,$07 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the location is zero.
  $DCF3,$06 Load the byte at #REGix+#N$01 and call #R$DD35.
  $DCF9,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if #R$DD35 returns non-zero.
  $DCFC,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DCFE,$01 Return.

g $DCFF Word: Looked-Up Room Record
D $DCFF Room record #R$DD24 stores after the lookup.
. Read by #R$CA9E and #R$E4F3.
W $DCFF,$02

g $DD01 Word: Room Saved For The Lookup
D $DD01 The low byte is the room id #R$DD1E stores before the lookup, or zero when that
. room was #N$FF. #R$CA9E reads it. The high byte stays zero.
W $DD01,$02

b $DD03 Display Record
D $DD03 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$60, value #N$20, handler #R$DCD2.
B $DD03,$04

c $DD07 Read Matched Character Room
@ $DD07 label=MatchedCharacterRoom
D $DD07 Reads the room stored on the character that matched the search.
. Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DD03.
  $DD07,$03 Read the room stored on the matched character.
  $DD0A,$01 Keep that room beside the character id.

c $DD0B Compare Current Character
@ $DD0B label=CompareCurrentCharacter
D $DD0B Reads the character the command is about.
  $DD0B,$03 Read the character being processed.

c $DD0E Match The Character And Store Its Room
@ $DD0E label=StoreMatchedCharacterRoom
D $DD0E When this is the character being processed, stores that character's room and looks the object up.
  $DD0E,$04 Take the character being processed.
  $DD12,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DD19 if this is not the character being processed.
  $DD16,$03 Record the current character attribute.
@ $DD19 label=StoreMatchedCharacterRoom_Next
  $DD19,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DD1E unless #REGb is #N$FF.
  $DD1D,$01 Start the character from zero.
  $DD1E,$06 Store the room at #R$DD01 and call #R$D224 to look it up.
  $DD24,$04 Store the current record at #R$DCFF.
  $DD28,$04 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $DD2C,$06 Store it in the character attribute, then call #R$D249 to run object location
. recursively.
  $DD32,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DD34,$01 Return.

c $DD35 Describe Object If Present
@ $DD35 label=DescribeObjectIfPresent
D $DD35 Returns zero when #REGa is zero, or when a bit of #N$28 is set in the object's attribute and its location is not below #REGiy+#N$02. Any other object returns non-zero.
  $DD35,$02 Return if the object is zero.
  $DD37,$05 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $DD3C,$03 Read the object attribute.
N $DD3F None of the bits #N$28 are set in the object attribute?
  $DD3F,$09 Jump to #R$DD4F if none of the bits #N$28 are set in the object attribute.
  $DD48,$05 Go to #R$DD4F if the object's location is below the byte at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $DD4D,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $DD4E,$01 Return.

@ $DD4F label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_Next
  $DD4F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DD51,$01 Return.
N $DD52 Named from the script commands at #R$E460 and #R$E7BE.
B $DD52,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$A4, value #N$00.
B $DD54,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $DD5B,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $DD5F,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $DD60,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $DD61,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$64, value #N$00, handler #R$DD6E.
N $DD65 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DD61.
  $DD65,$06 Read the object's first byte and store it as the location value.
  $DD6B,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DD6D,$01 Return.
N $DD6E Reached through the display record at #R$DD61, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DD6E,$06 Read the room display parameter and call #R$D471 to find an object by field two.
  $DD74,$05 Go to #R$DC15 if it is #N$FF.
  $DD79,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the record's flag byte is at least #N$0B.
  $DD81,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DD83,$01 Return.
N $DD84 Named from the script commands at #R$E476 and #R$E7CF.
B $DD84,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$6D, value #N$00, handler #R$DD91.
N $DD88 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DD84.
  $DD88,$06 Record the current room.
  $DD8E,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DD90,$01 Return.
  $DD91,$04 Read the current object.
  $DD95,$08 Go to #R$DC15 if the moved marker is #N$FF.
  $DD9D,$01 Return.
B $DD9E,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$0D, value #N$10.
N $DDA0 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DD9E.
  $DDA0,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $DDA3,$03 Call #R$CC0E to find the character.
N $DDA6 Was this character #N$FF?
  $DDA6,$06 Use zero and go to #R$DDAE if this character is #N$FF.
  $DDAC,$02 Take #N$01.
  $DDAE,$05 Call #R$B86A.
  $DDB3,$01 Return.
B $DDB4,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$50, handler #R$DDB8.
N $DDB8 Reached through the display record at #R$DC8D, #R$DDB4 and #R$DDD0, dispatched by #R$CB40.
N $DDB8 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DDB4.
  $DDB8,$05 Call #R$CBA7.
  $DDBD,$01 Return.
N $DDBE Named from the script commands at #R$EEBE, #R$EEE4, #R$EF04, #R$EF54, #R$EF75, #R$F0E9,
. #R$F22D, #R$F246, #R$F25B, #R$F268, #R$F278, #R$F28D and #R$F2AC.
B $DDBE,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$DDB8.
N $DDC2 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DDBE.
  $DDC2,$06 Store the room display parameter in the character script cycle.
  $DDC8,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DDCA,$01 Return.
W $DDCB,$02 The location text pointer, read by #R$DD35 and #R$DE60.
W $DDCD,$02 The object's description pointer, read by #R$DD35 and #R$DE60.
B $DDCF,$01 Data bytes #N$00, read by #R$DD35.
B $DDD0,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$45, value #N$00, handler #R$DDB8.
N $DDD4 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DDD0.
  $DDD4,$03 Read the display pointer.
  $DDD7,$04 Clear the marker.
@ $DDDB label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_MaskName
  $DDDB,$0A Keep the low six bits of the name, then call #R$DE48 to scale the high byte by sixty.
  $DDE5,$03 Remember the object's description.
  $DDE8,$06 Scale the word at #R$9FDF by sixty.
  $DDEE,$06 Remember the location text.
  $DDF4,$03 Store it in the workspace byte at #R$DDCF.
  $DDF7,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DE19 if bit 6 is present.
  $DDFB,$05 Write the letter p into the description marker.
  $DE00,$04 Jump ahead to #R$DE19 if bit 6 is present.
  $DE04,$08 Call #R$DE60, read the AM/PM letter at #R$9FE2 and go to #R$DE0E if the description pointer is below the location text.
  $DE0C,$02 Go to #R$DE16 if the description is past the location text.
@ $DE0E label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_Letter
N $DE0E Is the marker the letter a?
  $DE0E,$06 Use the letter p when the marker is the letter a.
  $DE14,$02 Take the letter a.
@ $DE16 label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_WorkspaceByte
  $DE16,$03 Store it in the workspace byte at #R$DDCF.
@ $DE19 label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_ObjectDescriptionLocation
  $DE19,$07 Read the object's description and the location text.
  $DE20,$06 Subtract the value at #R$DDCB from the one at #R$DDCD and take #N($02D0,$04), the half-day step added at #R$DE39.
  $DE26,$08 Read the AM/PM marker into #REGc and the description marker at #R$DDCF into #REGb.
  $DE2E,$05 Jump to #R$DE3C if the object's description pointer is below the location text
. pointer.
  $DE33,$02 Go to #R$DE3C if the object's description pointer equals the location text pointer.
  $DE35,$04 Go to #R$DE42 if the description marker matches AM/PM.
  $DE39,$03 The hours differ, so add one step and store the total from #R$DE42.

N $DE3C Add one step, and a second step when the description marker matches.
@ $DE3C label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_AdvancePointerSource
  $DE3C,$05 Add one step, and go to #R$DE42 if the description marker does not match AM/PM.
  $DE41,$01 Add one more step to the cycle count.
@ $DE42 label=DescribeObjectIfPresent_RecordCharacterScript
  $DE42,$03 Record the character script cycle.
  $DE45,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DE47,$01 Return.

c $DE48 Scale The High Byte By Sixty
@ $DE48 label=ScaleHighByteBySixty
D $DE48 Returns the low byte plus sixty times the high byte. When the high byte is #N$0C, only
. the low byte is kept.
  $DE48,$07 Clear the pointer.
  $DE4F,$03 Take sixty (#N$3C), the scale for the high byte.
  $DE52,$05 Go to #R$DE5A when the high byte is #N$0C, so only the low byte is kept.
@ $DE57 label=ScaleHighByteBySixty_Hour
  $DE57,$03 Add sixty to #REGhl once per hour in #REGb.
@ $DE5A label=ScaleHighByteBySixty_AddLowByte
  $DE5A,$02 Clear the high byte of the count.
  $DE5C,$01 Advance the pointer by the count.
  $DE5D,$02 Restore the saved count and the saved source from the stack.
  $DE5F,$01 Return.

c $DE60 Measure The Gap Between The Two Pointers
@ $DE60 label=MeasureGapBetweenTwoPointers_Next
D $DE60 Measures the gap between the object's description and the location text.
  $DE60,$05 Read the object's description.
  $DE65,$04 Read the location text.
  $DE69,$01 Clear the carry before subtracting the two text pointers.
  $DE6A,$02 Subtract the two text pointers to measure the gap.
  $DE6C,$02 Restore the saved source and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $DE6E,$01 Return.
B $DE6F,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$20, value #N$10.
N $DE71 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DE6F.
  $DE71,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $DE74,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $DE7A,$06 Call #R$D4CC to count objects in location.
  $DE80,$01 See whether it is zero.
  $DE81,$05 Point at the text at #R$68E9+#N$17, and go to #R$DE8C to list the objects when the count
. is not zero.
  $DE86,$05 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $DE8B,$01 Return.

N $DE8C Store the result in the low byte of the count, then read the character
. attribute, then store the result in the count.
@ $DE8C label=MeasureGapBetweenTwoPointers_ResultLowByte
  $DE8C,$05 Read the current character attribute.
  $DE91,$06 Read the character being processed, then call #R$D51A to list the objects
. and describe them.
  $DE97,$05 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $DE9C,$01 Return.

B $DE9D,$07 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$38, value #N$10, parameters #N$80, #N$21,
. #N$00, #N$00, #N$00.

c $DEA4 Set Bit Five Of The Current Object
@ $DEA4 label=SetCurrentObjectBitFive
D $DEA4 Sets bit 5 of the current object's flags and marks success.
. Handler placed after the display record at #R$DE9D. #R$DEB6 clears the bit this handler sets.
. Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DE9D.

  $DEA4,$04 Read the current object.
  $DEA8,$04 Set bit 5 of its flags.
  $DEAC,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DEAE,$01 Return.

B $DEAF,$07 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$38, value #N$10, parameters #N$00, #N$20,
. #N$00, #N$00, #N$00.

c $DEB6 Clear Bit Five Of The Current Object
@ $DEB6 label=ClearCurrentObjectBitFive
D $DEB6 Clears bit 5 of the current object's record and marks success.
. Handler placed after the display record at #R$DEAF. #R$DEA4 sets the bit this handler clears.
. Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DEAF.

  $DEB6,$08 Read the current object and clear an object flag.
  $DEBE,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DEC0,$01 Return.

c $DEC1 Store The Character Attribute
@ $DEC1 label=StoreCharacterAttribute
D $DEC1 Stores the saved character's attribute and the room graphics parameter
. on the current object. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
. Run through the table at #R$9F38 by #R$C972.
B $DEC1,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$7A, value #N$18, handler #R$DED9,
. parameters #N$09, #N$00, #N$00, #N$40, #N$28.
N $DECA Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DEC1.
  $DECA,$03 Read offset #N$0F of the record at #REGiy.
  $DECD,$03 Store it at offset #N$0F of the current object.
  $DED0,$03 Read the room graphics parameter.
  $DED3,$03 Store it as the moved marker.
  $DED6,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DED8,$01 Return.
N $DED9 Reached through the display record at #R$DEC1, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DED9,$08 Read the current object and read the secondary pointer.
  $DEE1,$03 Read the room graphics parameter.

c $DEE4 Combine The Result With The Character's Room
@ $DEE4 label=CombineCharacterRoom
D $DEE4 Fails the script unless the low three bits of #REGiy+#N$04 are set and #REGiy+#N$02 is above #REGix+#N$02 by more than the object count of the value in #REGa. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $DEE4,$01 Keep the value in #REGa as the count.
N $DEE5 None of the bits #N$07 are set in #REGiy+#N$04?
  $DEE5,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 if none of the bits #N$07 are set in the
. byte at #REGiy+#N$04.
  $DEED,$08 Go to #R$DF00 if the byte at #REGiy+#N$02 is below the byte at
. #REGix+#N$02.
  $DEF5,$05 Save the difference, take the value in #REGb and call #R$D2ED.
  $DEFA,$05 Go to #R$DF00 if that difference is below the object count from #R$D2ED.
  $DEFF,$01 Return if that difference is above the count from #R$D2ED.
@ $DF00 label=CombineCharacterRoom_ClearResult
  $DF00,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $DF01,$01 Return.
B $DF02,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$6A, value #N$1D, handler #R$DF24.
N $DF06 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DF02.
  $DF06,$04 Read the secondary pointer at #R$A015.
  $DF0A,$07 Read the current object.
  $DF11,$03 Store the moved marker.
  $DF14,$07 Store the character attribute.
  $DF1B,$06 Read the room display parameter, then call #R$D249 to run object location
. recursively.
  $DF21,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DF23,$01 Return.
N $DF24 Reached through the display record at #R$DF02, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DF24,$0A Pass the room display parameter in #REGb and the room graphics parameter in #REGa, and carry on at #R$C8C6.
N $DF2E Reached through the display record at #R$DF40 and #R$E135, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DF2E,$08 Go to #R$DF3D if the mother id is #N$FF.
  $DF36,$07 Go to #R$DC15 if the containing object is not this character.
@ $DF3D label=CombineCharacterRoom_TestActionSucceeded
  $DF3D,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DF3F,$01 Return.
B $DF40,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$78, value #N$10, handler #R$DF2E,
. parameters #N$80, #N$40, #N$00, #N$00, #N$00.
N $DF49 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DF40.
  $DF49,$02 Take #N$01 as the room flag.
  $DF4B,$03 Store the room flag.
  $DF4E,$03 Call #R$DC6C to check the character id.
  $DF51,$01 Return unless the character is Sherlock, #N$01, or zero.
  $DF52,$03 Call #R$DF5B to print the object's description.
  $DF55,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $DF58,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $DF5A,$01 Return.

c $DF5B Print Object Description Text
@ $DF5B label=PrintObjectDescriptionText
D $DF5B Prints the description text for an object specified by the room display parameter.
. If the object has custom description text, that is printed; otherwise a default description is used.
. Additional object information is then printed followed by a period.
  $DF5B,$06 Read the room display parameter and call #R$D237 to look up the object.
  $DF61,$04 Set bit 6 of the object's byte at offset #N$06.
  $DF65,$06 Load the object's description text pointer from offsets #N$0D/#N$0E.
  $DF6B,$05 Jump to #R$A59F if the description pointer is not zero.
  $DF70,$06 Take the default object message, then call #R$A59F to print the default object
. message.
  $DF76,$08 Call #R$D3D5, take a full stop and print it through #R$A9B7.
B $DF7E,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$88, value #N$00.
B $DF80,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $DF85,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $DF8A,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $DF8B,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$08, value #N$00.
N $DF8D Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DF8B.
  $DF8D,$06 Jump to #R$E000 if Basil Phipps's state is clear.
  $DF93,$05 Mark Basil Phipps's state as finished.
  $DF98,$04 Read the current object.
  $DF9C,$06 Store the low byte of the text pointer.
  $DFA2,$03 Store the high byte of the text pointer.
  $DFA5,$02 Jump ahead to #R$E000 to return non-zero.
B $DFA7,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$C8, value #N$00, handler #R$E01C.
B $DFAB,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $DFB0,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $DFB5,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $DFB6,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$07, value #N$F0.
N $DFB8 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DFB6.
  $DFB8,$08 Jump to #R$E000 if the room display parameter is not #N$59.
  $DFC0,$08 Jump to #R$E000 if the day of the week is not zero.
  $DFC8,$02 Jump to #R$DFCC, the special case object 6.

b $DFCA Display Record
D $DFCA Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message command at #R$EF99 passes this word to
. #R$C591.
N $DFCA Named from the script command at #R$EF99.
B $DFCA,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.

c $DFCC Special Case Object #N$06
@ $DFCC label=SpecialCaseObject_LooksBasilPhipps
D $DFCC Looks up #OBJECT($06) and fails the match unless his attribute agrees
. with Sherlock's disguise.
. Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DFCA, from the show-message command at #R$EF99. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $DFCC,$08 Call #R$D237 to look up #N$06.
  $DFD4,$09 Print the message via #R$9E74 if Sherlock's disguise does not match the
. character's attribute.
  $DFDD,$06 Take the message at #R$75D2 and print it via #R$9E74.
N $DFE3 Reached through the display record at #R$DFEA, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DFE3,$07 Read the character being processed, compare it with Sherlock (#N$64), and go to
. #R$DFFD, which returns zero through #R$DC15 when they differ.
B $DFEA,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$10, handler #R$DFE3.
N $DFEE Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DFEA.
  $DFEE,$02 Go to #R$E007.
B $DFF0,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$48, value #N$10, handler #R$E01C.
N $DFF4 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$DFF0.
  $DFF4,$04 Place character #N$64 from #R$E028.
N $DFF8 Reached through the display record at #R$E003, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $DFF8,$05 Read the character being processed and compare it with #N$01.
  $DFFD,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the character being processed is not #N$01.

  $E000,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E002,$01 Return.
B $E003,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$10, handler #R$DFF8.
@ $E007 label=SpecialCaseObject_LooksBasilPhipps_Next
N $E007 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E003.
  $E007,$04 Call #R$E03A to place character.
  $E00B,$04 Read the current object.
@ $E00F label=SpecialCaseObject_ClearMovedMarker
  $E00F,$07 Clear the moved marker.
  $E016,$03 Store the character attribute.
  $E019,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E01B,$01 Return.
N $E01C Reached through the display record at #R$DFA7, #R$DFF0, #R$E022 and #R$E0AD,
. dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E01C,$06 Read the character being processed, then clear the borrow so the caller can
. trust the result.
B $E022,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$48, value #N$10, handler #R$E01C.
N $E026 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E022.
  $E026,$02 Take #N$01.
  $E028,$03 Call #R$E03A to place the character.
  $E02B,$08 Read the current object and clear the moved marker.
  $E033,$06 Clear the location at #REGix+#N$0F.
  $E039,$01 Return.

c $E03A Place Character
@ $E03A label=PlaceCharacter
D $E03A Moves the character to the room the action names.
  $E03A,$07 Call #R$CC0E to find character.

N $E041 Store that byte in this character's id, then read the schedule entry from the
. current character's record, then read the low byte of the count.
  $E041,$07 Store the character id and read the current character record.
  $E048,$04 Call #R$D237 to look up that object.
  $E04C,$07 Remember this object record and read the marker at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $E053,$03 Store the moved marker.
  $E056,$07 Write #N$FF at #REGiy+#N$01 and read the location at #REGiy+#N$0F.
  $E05D,$03 Store the character attribute.
  $E060,$06 Clear the character attribute.
@ $E066 label=PlaceCharacter_Loop
  $E066,$06 Go to #R$E07C if the object count has reached #N$6E.
  $E06C,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $E06F,$08 Jump back to #R$E066 while the current character does not match the moved marker.
  $E077,$05 Store the room in the character's destination and go back to place them.

N $E07C Writes this value into each matching script entry, then makes it the character being processed.
@ $E07C label=PlaceCharacter_WritesValueEach
  $E07C,$05 Go to #R$E086 if #R$CFB6 found no script entry for this character.
  $E081,$05 Store the room in the character's location and go back to place them.

N $E086 Read the low byte of the count, then record the result in the character being
. processed, then return non-zero.
@ $E086 label=PlaceCharacter_Next
  $E086,$04 Record the current character.
  $E08A,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E08C,$01 Return.
B $E08D,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$8D, value #N$04.
B $E08F,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E096,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $E09A,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E09B,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $E09F,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E0A0,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$88, value #N$00.
B $E0A2,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E0A7,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E0AC,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E0AD,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$4D, value #N$07, handler #R$E01C.
N $E0B1 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E0AD.
  $E0B1,$07 Jump ahead to #R$E0DD if the current character attribute is not #N$58.
  $E0B8,$07 Jump to #R$E0DD if the day of the week is not #N$01.
  $E0BF,$08 Read the secondary pointer and set bit 7 of its byte at offset #N$06.
  $E0C7,$07 Mark the object-attribute flag and read the character attribute.
  $E0CE,$03 Store the character attribute.
  $E0D1,$06 Store the moved marker.
  $E0D7,$03 Point #REGhl at the message at #R$761D+#N$053D.
  $E0DA,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $E0DD,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E0DF,$01 Return.
B $E0E0,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$88, value #N$00.
B $E0E2,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E0E7,$03 #SCRIPT($03)
B $E0EA,$01 #SCRIPT($01)

b $E0EB Display Record
D $E0EB Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message command at #R$E0E7 passes this word to
. #R$C591.
N $E0EB Named from the script command at #R$E0E7.
B $E0EB,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.

c $E0ED Set The Held Mark
@ $E0ED label=SetHeldMark
D $E0ED Hands object #N$51 to the held-mark routine.
N $E0ED Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E0EB, from the show-message command at #R$E0E7.
  $E0ED,$05 Jump back to #R$9C56 to set object attribute bit 7.
B $E0F2,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$88, value #N$00.
B $E0F4,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E0F9,$03 #SCRIPT($03)
B $E0FC,$01 #SCRIPT($01)

b $E0FD Display Record
D $E0FD Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message command at #R$E0F9 passes this word to
. #R$C591.
N $E0FD Named from the script command at #R$E0F9.
B $E0FD,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.

c $E0FF Set The Other Held Mark
@ $E0FF label=SetHeldMark_OtherObject
D $E0FF Hands object #N$3F to the held-mark routine. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $E0FF Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E0FD, from the show-message command at #R$E0F9.
  $E0FF,$05 Jump back to #R$9C56 to set object attribute bit 7.
B $E104,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$08, value #N$60.
N $E106 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E104.
  $E106,$02 Take #N$01 as the room flag.
  $E108,$03 Store #REGa as the room flag.
  $E10B,$05 Return if the character is set.
N $E110 Print object description text.
  $E110,$03 Call #R$DF5B to print the object's description.
  $E113,$04 Read the current object.
N $E117 An object flag is set?
  $E117,$05 Return if an object flag is set.
  $E11C,$07 Set an object flag.
  $E123,$03 Store the low byte of a stored pointer.
  $E126,$09 Store it in the character record and take the message, then call #R$A59F to
. print the message.
  $E12F,$05 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $E134,$01 Return.
B $E135,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$78, value #N$50, handler #R$DF2E,
. parameters #N$80, #N$40, #N$00, #N$00, #N$00.
N $E13E Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E135.
  $E13E,$02 Take #N$01 as the room flag.
  $E140,$03 Store the room flag.
  $E143,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E146,$02 Return if this character id is not zero.
  $E148,$03 Call #R$E151 to print the display text.
  $E14B,$03 Call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $E14E,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E150,$01 Return.

c $E151 Lookup And Print Display Text
@ $E151 label=LookupAndPrintDisplayText
D $E151 Looks up a display text message based on the room display parameter and prints it to the screen.
N $E151 Print object description text.
  $E151,$03 Call #R$DF5B to print the object's description.
  $E154,$0A Call #R$D34E to search three-byte length table.
  $E15E,$03 Return if the entry is the end marker.
  $E161,$06 Load the text token pointer from the table entry.
  $E167,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $E16A,$01 Return.

g $E16B Table: Display Entry Lookup
@ $E16B label=Table_DisplayEntryLookup
D $E16B Lookup table for #R$E151. It maps room display parameter values to text token pointers.
. Each entry consists of a 1-byte parameter value followed by a 2-byte pointer to a text token buffer.
B $E16B,$01 Parameter value.
W $E16C,$02 Text token pointer.
L $E16B,$03,$05
B $E17A,$01 Terminator.

c $E17B Dispatch Room Graphics
@ $E17B label=DispatchRoomGraphics
D $E17B Chooses the picture and the room-display handler for the location on screen.
. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
. Reached through the object display table at #R$AA71, read by #R$C966.
B $E17B,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$8D, value #N$07.
B $E17D,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E182,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E187,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E188,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$0D, value #N$07.
N $E18A Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E188.
  $E18A,$03 Read the room graphics parameter.
  $E18D,$04 Go to #R$E199 if it is #N$10.
  $E191,$04 Go to #R$E199 if it is #ROOM($43).
  $E195,$04 Go to #R$E1D3 if it is not #ROOM($11).
@ $E199 label=DispatchRoomGraphics_Next
  $E199,$04 Point #REGix at the character state at #R$9993.
  $E19D,$04 Set bit 1 of the action flags.
  $E1A1,$02 Return non-zero through #R$E1D3.
B $E1A3,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$8D, value #N$07.
B $E1A5,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E1AC,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E1B1,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E1B2,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$07.
N $E1B4 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E1B2.
  $E1B4,$03 Read the room graphics parameter.
  $E1B7,$04 Go to #R$E1D3 if it is not #ROOM($43).
  $E1BB,$05 Go to #R$E1C6 if the object is in this location.
  $E1C0,$06 Call #R$E1FC and return zero through #R$DC15.

N $E1C6 Leave #N$5F as the result, then use the character Pairs as the character
. record, then mark bit 1 of the action flags.
@ $E1C6 label=DispatchRoomGraphics_LeaveResultCharacter
  $E1C6,$05 Call #R$E03A to place character.
  $E1CB,$08 Set the action-taken flag.
@ $E1D3 label=DispatchRoomGraphics_TestActionSucceeded
  $E1D3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E1D5,$01 Return.
B $E1D6,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$0D, value #N$17.
N $E1D8 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E1D6.
  $E1D8,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E1DB,$03 Call #R$E207 to see whether the id matches a record flag.
  $E1DE,$02 Go to #R$E1FC if no record flag matches.
  $E1E0,$08 Move to the next graphics entry, read the room graphics parameter and call #R$E201.
  $E1E8,$02 Go to #R$E1FC if the looked-up entry is #N$FF.
  $E1EA,$06 Read the message address.
@ $E1F0 label=DispatchRoomGraphics_Loop
  $E1F0,$08 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message, saving result and
. the pointer.
@ $E1F8 label=DispatchRoomGraphics_ActionSucceeded
  $E1F8,$01 Restore the saved character from the stack.
  $E1F9,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E1FB,$01 Return.

c $E1FC Print The Line About You
@ $E1FC label=PrintLineAboutYou
D $E1FC Prints the line that says "I ... you with that" and joins the shared print step.
  $E1FC,$05 Point #REGhl at the message at #R$761D and jump to #R$E1F0.

c $E201 Test For The End Marker
@ $E201 label=TestEndMarker_WalksThreeByte
D $E201 Walks the three-byte list and returns when the entry is the closing byte.
  $E201,$03 Call #R$D34E to search three-byte length table.
  $E204,$02 See whether this byte is the end marker.
  $E206,$01 Return.

c $E207 Find The Response For This Id
@ $E207 label=FindResponseForThisId
D $E207 Finds the response for the id in #REGa in the table at #R$E22A.
  $E207,$04 Point at the response table at #R$E22A.
@ $E20B label=FindResponseForThisId_Match
  $E20B,$05 Jump back to #R$E1F9 if this id matches the entry's id.
  $E210,$08 Jump to #R$E1F8 if this is the catch-all entry (#N$FE).
  $E218,$03 Jump to #R$E227 if the table has ended (#N$FF).
  $E21B,$07 Step to the first group and call #R$D34E to find the #N$FF that ends it.
  $E222,$05 Step past the terminator and test the next entry.
@ $E227 label=FindResponseForThisId_Ended
  $E227,$02 Drop the saved id and clear #REGa.
  $E229,$01 Return.

b $E22A Table: Responses By Id
@ $E22A label=Table_ResponsesById
D $E22A Response entries read by #R$E207. An entry starts with an id, followed by groups of a code byte and a message address, and ends with #N$FF. Id #N$FE is the catch-all. #N$FF ends the table.
B $E22A,$0B Id #N$08. Codes #N$10, #N$11 and #N$07, messages #R$761D+#N$0229, #R$761D+#N$0229 and #R$761D+#N$02DB.
B $E235,$0B Id #N$09. Codes #N$10, #N$11 and #N$06, messages #R$761D+#N$0332, #R$761D+#N$0332 and #R$761D+#N$03A7.
B $E240,$08 Id #N$0A. Codes #N$10 and #N$11, message #R$761D+#N$0418.
B $E248,$0B Id #N$06. Codes #N$10, #N$11 and #N$12, messages #R$761D+#N$049C, #R$761D+#N$049C and #R$761D+#N$010F.
B $E253,$0E Id #N$05. Codes #N$10, #N$11, #N$0D and #N$07, messages #R$761D+#N$04D9, #R$761D+#N$04D9, #R$761D+#N$87 and #R$761D+#N$02B7.
B $E261,$05 Id #N$04. Code #N$0F, message #R$722D+#N$0144.
B $E266,$08 Id #N$07. Codes #N$11 and #N$12, messages #R$761D+#N$01EF and #R$761D+#N$0105.
B $E26E,$05 Id #N$0D. Code #N$43, message #R$761D+#N$6A.
B $E273,$0E Id #N$5F. Codes #N$43, #N$10, #N$11 and #N$0D, messages #R$761D+#N$6A, #R$761D+#N$27, #R$761D+#N$27 and #R$761D+#N$10.
B $E281,$0B Catch-all id #N$FE. Codes #N$12, #N$10 and #N$11, message #R$683F+#N$06.
B $E28C,$01 Terminator #N$FF.

c $E28D Test This Character Against The Room
@ $E28D label=TestCharacterAgainstRoom
D $E28D Returns through #R$DC15 when the character being processed is the room display parameter. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $E28D,$0A Go to #R$DC15 if the character being processed is the room display parameter.
  $E297,$01 Return.
N $E298 Reached through the display record at #R$E2C7, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E298,$07 Jump to #R$E2A4 if the room display parameter is #N$1E.
  $E29F,$05 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the room display parameter is not #ROOM($21).
@ $E2A4 label=TestCharacterAgainstRoom_SearchPairs
  $E2A4,$08 Call #R$9C07 to search pair table.
  $E2AC,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if #R$9C07 finds no pair.
  $E2AF,$04 Read the current object.
  $E2B3,$07 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the record is not zero.
  $E2BA,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $E2BD,$03 Call #R$CFB9 to walk the character-script entries.
  $E2C0,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if #R$CFB9 finds a script entry.
  $E2C3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E2C5,$01 Return.
B $E2C6,$01 Script byte overwritten by #R$9AD9, read by #R$9AA3 and #R$9B50.
B $E2C7,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$10, handler #R$E298.
N $E2CB Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E2C7.
  $E2CB,$03 Read the character attribute.
  $E2CE,$01 Keep it as the count.
  $E2CF,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $E2D2,$01 Count one more.
  $E2D3,$03 Store that count in the script byte at #R$E2C6.
  $E2D6,$01 Count down one.
  $E2D7,$03 Call #R$DD0E to match the character and store its room.
  $E2DA,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E2DD,$01 Keep it as the count.
  $E2DE,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $E2E1,$01 Keep that parameter.
  $E2E2,$03 Take the workspace at #R$F2B6.
  $E2E5,$03 Read the script byte at #R$E2C6.
  $E2E8,$03 Call #R$E2EE to record the room display.
  $E2EB,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E2ED,$01 Return.

c $E2EE Record The Result In The Room Display
@ $E2EE label=RecordResultRoomDisplay
D $E2EE Records the room display, the graphics parameter and the current character, then stores the script position.
  $E2EE,$07 Record the room display parameter.
  $E2F5,$03 Record the room graphics parameter.
  $E2F8,$08 Record the current character.
N $E300 Find character.
  $E300,$03 Call #R$CC0E to find the character.
  $E303,$08 Read the word at #REGiy+#N$01-#N$02 into #REGhl and take #N$02.
  $E30B,$04 Call #R$CF80 to allocate memory and store character.
  $E30F,$07 Record the current character and store the low byte of #REGde at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $E316,$03 Store the high byte of #REGde at #REGiy+#N$02.
  $E319,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E31B,$01 Return.
N $E31C Reached through the display record at #R$E328, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E31C,$04 Read the current object.
  $E320,$07 Go to #R$DC15 if bit 5 of the current object's byte at offset #N$06 is clear.
  $E327,$01 Return.
B $E328,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$6A, value #N$10, handler #R$E31C.
N $E32C Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E328.
  $E32C,$03 Take the message at #R$6830+#N$06.
  $E32F,$03 Call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $E332,$07 Read the current object.
  $E339,$03 Read the high byte of the text pointer.
  $E33C,$06 Call #R$A419 to print the value in #REGhl, then call #R$A2A2 to print a new line.
  $E342,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E344,$01 Return.
N $E345 Reached through the display record at #R$E370, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E345,$04 Return if this character has no room graphic.
  $E349,$05 Write #N$25 to the saved character.
  $E34E,$01 Return.

c $E34F Read The Character's Text Pointers
@ $E34F label=ReadCharacterTextPointers
D $E34F Looks up the character being processed and compares its text pointer with the display pointer.
  $E34F,$08 Go to #R$E365 if no object carries this id.
  $E357,$06 Call #R$E39C to read the text pointer, then #R$E3A9 to read the display pointer.
  $E35D,$05 Jump ahead to #R$E365 if #REGhl is below #REGde.
  $E362,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E364,$01 Return.

N $E365 Hands the following step back to the caller and reports that the test failed.
@ $E365 label=ReadCharacterTextPointers_HandBack
  $E365,$03 Hand back the address of the following step.
  $E368,$01 Clear #REGa.
  $E369,$01 Return.
  $E36A,$06 Jump back to #R$A59F to print this message.
B $E370,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$66, value #N$1D, handler #R$E345.
N $E374 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E370.
  $E374,$03 Call #R$E34F to read the character being processed.
  $E377,$03 Call #R$E38F to bring the next bit through.
  $E37A,$08 Go to #R$E38C if no object carries this id.
  $E382,$06 Call #R$E39C to read the text pointer, then #R$E3A9 to read the display pointer.
  $E388,$04 Move the pointer on by the length, then call #R$E38F to store the shifted text pointer.
@ $E38C label=ReadCharacterTextPointers_Succeed
  $E38C,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E38E,$01 Return.

c $E38F Store The Shifted Text Pointer
@ $E38F label=StoreShiftedTextPointer
D $E38F Bring the next bit through the saved bit, then scale the pointer down to the next bit.
  $E38F,$06 Rotate #REGhl left through the carry and shift the low byte back down.
  $E395,$03 Store the low byte of the text pointer.
  $E398,$03 Store the high byte of the text pointer.
  $E39B,$01 Return.

c $E39C Read The Text Pointer's Low Byte
@ $E39C label=ReadTextPointerLowByte
D $E39C Reads the text pointer and rotates its low byte into the carry flag.
  $E39C,$08 Read the text pointer and rotate its low byte into the carry flag.
  $E3A4,$04 Shift #REGhl right by one bit, keeping the sign of #REGh.
  $E3A8,$01 Return.

c $E3A9 Read The Source At The Display Pointer
@ $E3A9 label=ReadSourceDisplayPointer
D $E3A9 Reads the display pointer and shifts it to the picture address. Always returns non-zero.
  $E3A9,$08 Read the display pointer.
  $E3B1,$06 Shift #REGde right one bit, keeping the sign of #REGd.
  $E3B7,$01 Return.
N $E3B8 Reached through the display record at #R$E3EA, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E3B8,$04 Return if the room display parameter does not apply.
  $E3BC,$04 Read the current character.
  $E3C0,$0A Call #R$CFB6 to find character script entry.
  $E3CA,$05 Record the current character.
  $E3CF,$03 Go to #R$E3E7 if #R$CFB6 found no script entry for this character.
  $E3D2,$06 Take the object id at offset #N$03 and call #R$D237 to look it up.
  $E3D8,$04 Read the current character record.
  $E3DC,$03 Read the attribute type.
N $E3DF None of the bits #N$70 are set in the tested state?
  $E3DF,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 if none of the bits #N$70 are set in the tested
. state.
@ $E3E7 label=ReadSourceDisplayPointer_TestActionSucceeded
  $E3E7,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E3E9,$01 Return.
B $E3EA,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$78, value #N$20, handler #R$E3B8,
. parameters #N$90, #N$40, #N$28, #N$00, #N$00.
@ $E3F3 label=ReadSourceDisplayPointer_CurrentCharacterRecord
N $E3F3 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E3EA.
  $E3F3,$02 Store the current room on the character from #R$E413.

c $E3F5 Check The Character For Room Graphics
@ $E3F5 label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics
D $E3F5 Checks the character id and returns when the room has nothing to draw.
. Reached through the display record at #R$E40A, dispatched by #R$CB40. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $E3F5,$07 Call #R$CBA7, then return if #R$DCA7 returns zero.
  $E3FC,$08 Read the current character record and read the current object.
  $E404,$06 Take the room display parameter and jump back to #R$DEE4.
B $E40A,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$78, value #N$20, handler #R$E3F5,
. parameters #N$90, #N$40, #N$28, #N$00, #N$00.
@ $E413 label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics_CharacterRecord
N $E413 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E40A.
  $E413,$07 Read the current character record.
  $E41A,$03 Store the room display parameter at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $E41D,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E41F,$01 Return.
N $E420 Reached through the display record at #R$E432, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E420,$03 Call #R$CBA7 to move to the next line when the flag is set.
  $E423,$09 Read the character being processed and take the room display parameter, then
. call #R$D278 to test character id range.
  $E42C,$03 Go to #R$DC15 if #R$D278 rejects this character.
  $E42F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E431,$01 Return.
B $E432,$09 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$78, value #N$20, handler #R$E420,
. parameters #N$10, #N$80, #N$28, #N$00, #N$00.
@ $E43B label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics_CurrentObject
N $E43B Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E432.
  $E43B,$04 Read the current object.
  $E43F,$07 Keep the object's marker and read the current character record.
  $E446,$03 Store the object's marker on the character at #REGiy+#N$01.
  $E449,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E44B,$01 Return.
N $E44C Reached through the display record at #R$E452, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E44C,$06 Check the flag at #R$CBA7 and compare the character from #R$E28D.
B $E452,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$EC, value #N$00, handler #R$E44C.
B $E456,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E45B,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E460,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E467,$03 #SCRIPT($03)
B $E46A,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E46B,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E472,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $E476,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E47D,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $E481,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E482,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $E487,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E488,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$27, value #N$00.
N $E48A Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E488.
  $E48A,$04 Read the current object.
  $E48E,$06 Record the current room.
  $E494,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E496,$01 Return.
B $E497,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$24, handler #R$E4B0.
N $E49B Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E497.
  $E49B,$04 Read the room graphics parameter and keep it in #REGb.
  $E49F,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $E4A2,$04 Read the current object.
  $E4A6,$07 Store #N$FF in the moved marker, then call #R$DD0E to compare the room
. display parameter with the current character.
  $E4AD,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E4AF,$01 Return.
N $E4B0 Reached through the display record at #R$E497, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E4B0,$04 Read the current object.
  $E4B4,$06 Jump to #R$E4C6 if the special-description flag is clear.
  $E4BA,$06 Read the character attribute, then call #R$D224 to look up this room.
  $E4C0,$06 Record the saved character.
@ $E4C6 label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics_Succeed
  $E4C6,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E4C8,$01 Return.
B $E4C9,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$04, value #N$00.
N $E4CB Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E4C9.
  $E4CB,$07 Point #REGhl at #R$9C56 and read the room display parameter.
  $E4D2,$04 Jump ahead to #R$E4DE if the room display parameter is #N$02.
  $E4D6,$04 Jump ahead to #R$E4DE if the room display parameter is #N$03.
  $E4DA,$04 Jump ahead to #R$E4E7 if the room display parameter is not #N$04.
@ $E4DE label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics_PrintMessage
  $E4DE,$06 Take the message, then call #R$A59F to print the message.
  $E4E4,$03 Take the routine at #R$9C60.
@ $E4E7 label=CheckCharacterForRoomGraphics_ReadCharacter
  $E4E7,$07 Keep the current character, call #R$E4FB.
  $E4EE,$01 Restore that character from the stack.
  $E4EF,$01 Restore the room display parameter from the stack.
  $E4F0,$03 Call #R$DD0E.
  $E4F3,$05 Read the record at #R$DCFF and clear its marker.
  $E4F8,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E4FA,$01 Return.

c $E4FB Dispatch Looked-Up Handler
@ $E4FB label=DispatchLookedUpHandler
D $E4FB Jumps to the handler the caller looked up.
  $E4FB,$01 Jump to the handler address in #REGhl.
N $E4FC Reached through the display record at #R$E518, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E4FC,$06 Call #R$E619 to scan objects.
  $E502,$03 Go to #R$DC15 if no object carries this id.
  $E505,$01 Return.
B $E506,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$AD, value #N$0D (bit 7: the
. script resumes at #R$E508).
B $E508,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E50F,$01 #SCRIPT($01)

c $E510 Store The Result In The High Byte
@ $E510 label=StoreResultHighByte
D $E510 The opening bytes are data.
. The instructions that follow store the display pointer.
. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
. Reached from the script record at #R$E508.
B $E510,$07 #SCRIPT($07)
B $E517,$01 #SCRIPT($01)
B $E518,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$6D, value #N$0D, handler #R$E4FC.
N $E51C Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E518.
  $E51C,$02 Take #N$FE for the location value.
  $E51E,$03 Store it at #R$9FD7.
  $E521,$06 Read the text pointer at offsets #N$09 and #N$0A.
  $E527,$02 Set bit 7 of the pointer's low byte.
  $E529,$03 Record the display pointer.
  $E52C,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E52E,$01 Return.
N $E52F Named from the script commands at #R$E456, #R$E732, #R$E745, #R$E751, #R$EEAA, #R$EF34,
. #R$F004, #R$F080, #R$F2BA, #R$F2D3, #R$F2FF and #R$F334.
B $E52F,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$07, value #N$07.
N $E531 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E52F.
  $E531,$02 Take #N$02 as the number of bytes to allocate.
  $E533,$03 Call #R$A11C to build the pointer the clock update stores.
  $E536,$07 Read the clock at #R$9FE5 into #REGhl and store #REGix in its place.
  $E53D,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $E540,$06 Store the high byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $E546,$03 Store the record's flag byte.
  $E549,$09 Jump to #R$E556 if the room graphics parameter is #N$FF.
N $E552 Get character attribute with sign.
  $E552,$03 Call #R$D2A4 to read the signed character attribute.
  $E555,$01 Use the get character attribute with sign result as the count.
@ $E556 label=StoreResultHighByte_MovedMarker
  $E556,$07 Add the count to the room display parameter and store it as the moved marker.
  $E55D,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E55F,$01 Return.

c $E560 Find The Character On The Saved Pointer
@ $E560 label=FindCharacterSavedPointer
D $E560 Walks the list at the saved character pointer until it finds the character being
. processed.
  $E560,$04 Use the saved pointer (#R$9FE7).
@ $E564 label=FindCharacterSavedPointer_FetchObjectPointer
  $E564,$04 Return if the object pointer is zero.
  $E568,$08 Jump back to #R$E564 while this id does not match the record's flag byte.
  $E570,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E572,$01 Return.
N $E573 Reached through the display record at #R$E57A and #R$E588, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E573,$06 Go to #R$DC15 if the character is not on the saved pointer.
  $E579,$01 Return.
N $E57A Named from the script commands at #R$E482, #R$E76C, #R$E7AC, #R$EEB6, #R$EF40, #R$F010,
. #R$F088, #R$F2C6, #R$F2E0, #R$F317 and #R$F352.
B $E57A,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$E573.
N $E57E Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E57A.
  $E57E,$03 Count down the link at offset #N$01.
  $E581,$01 Return if that link has not reached zero.
  $E582,$06 Call #R$E58C and return zero through #R$DC15.
N $E588 Named from the script commands at #R$E467, #R$E7B2, #R$E7B6, #R$EF96, #R$F2D0, #R$F2FC,
. #R$F32E, #R$F359, #R$F366 and #R$F3A2.
B $E588,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$E573.

c $E58C Copy The Clock Link
@ $E58C label=CopyClockLink
D $E58C Compares the current record with the clock workspace and copies the link kept
. beside the clock.
. Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E588. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
  $E58C,$0A Call #R$A104 to step pointer and compare.
  $E596,$06 Read the pointer saved ahead of this record.
  $E59C,$03 Store the low byte of the pointer in the previous record.
  $E59F,$06 Store it in the schedule entry, then call #R$A1C7 to run memory entry.
  $E5A5,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E5A7,$01 Return.
N $E5A8 Reached from the script commands at #R$E756, #R$E786, #R$E794, #R$E79C, #R$E7DC, #R$ECD0,
. #R$EF90, #R$F05C, #R$F085, #R$F13F, #R$F192, #R$F2BF, #R$F2DD, #R$F314 and #R$F34F, run by
. #R$CC82.
N $E5A8 Named from the script commands at #R$E756, #R$E786, #R$E794, #R$E79C, #R$E7DC, #R$ECD0,
. #R$EF90, #R$F05C, #R$F085, #R$F13F, #R$F192, #R$F2BF, #R$F2DD, #R$F314 and #R$F34F.
B $E5A8,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$10.
N $E5AA Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E5A8.
  $E5AA,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E5AC,$01 Return.
B $E5AD,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$4C, value #N$04, handler #R$E5B1.
N $E5B1 Reached through the display record at #R$E5AD, dispatched by #R$CB40.
N $E5B1 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E5AD.
  $E5B1,$04 Read the current object.
  $E5B5,$06 Compare the room graphics parameter with the character's attribute.
  $E5BB,$03 Go to #R$DC15 if the room graphics parameter does not match it.
  $E5BE,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E5C0,$01 Return.

b $E5C1 Display Record
D $E5C1 Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message command at #R$E781 passes this word to
. #R$C591.
N $E5C1 Named from the script command at #R$E781.
B $E5C1,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.

c $E5C3 Look Up The Current Object
@ $E5C3 label=LookUpCurrentObject
D $E5C3 Looks up the character id at #R$9860 and compares that object's attribute with the current character record. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $E5C3 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E5C1, from the show-message command at #R$E781.
  $E5C3,$06 Take the character id at #R$9860 and call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $E5C9,$07 Read byte #N$0F of this record and point #REGix at the character record at #R$A017.
  $E5D0,$08 Compare it with the character record, take the character being processed, and jump to #R$E5F0.
B $E5D8,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$07, handler #R$E5DF.
N $E5DC Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E5D8.
  $E5DC,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E5DE,$01 Return.
  $E5DF,$07 Point #REGix at the current object and read byte #N$0F.
  $E5E6,$04 Read the secondary pointer.
  $E5EA,$06 Compare #REGa with byte #N$0F of the record at #R$A015 and read the room graphics parameter.
@ $E5F0 label=LookUpCurrentObject_ZeroThrough
  $E5F0,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 when that comparison did not match.
  $E5F3,$07 Go to #R$DC15 if #REGa matches the room display parameter at #R$A034.
  $E5FA,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E5FC,$01 Return.
B $E5FD,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$47, value #N$00, handler #R$E604.
N $E601 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E5FD.
  $E601,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E603,$01 Return.
N $E604 Reached through the display record at #R$E5FD, dispatched by #R$CB40.
  $E604,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E607,$03 Call #R$C863 to get that character's location.
  $E60A,$02 Keep the location.
  $E60C,$03 Read the room display parameter.
  $E60F,$03 Call #R$C863 to get the object's location.
  $E612,$02 Compare the two locations.
  $E614,$05 Read the character again and jump back to #R$E5F0.

c $E619 Scan Objects
@ $E619 label=ScanObjects
D $E619 Walks the objects and stops on the entry the caller wants.
  $E619,$03 Keep the wanted id in #REGc and start the object counter at #N$FF (it is incremented
. first).
@ $E61C label=ScanObjects_Loop
  $E61C,$05 Return when the object id reaches #N$6E, past the last object.
  $E621,$03 Call #R$D237 to look that object up.
  $E624,$06 Go back to #R$E61C unless byte #N$01 of this object matches the id in #REGc.
  $E62A,$09 Go back to #R$E61C unless byte #N$07 of this object is #N$75.
  $E633,$08 Go to #R$E61C if the low nibble of object byte #N$08 is not #N$05.
  $E63B,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E63D,$01 Return.

N $E63E Named from the script command at #R$F2D8.
N $E63E Scans objects Patched.
@ $E63E label=ScanObjects_PatchedRecord
B $E63E,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$E642.
N $E642 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E63E.
  $E642,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E645,$03 Call #R$E619 to scan the objects for that id.
  $E648,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the object scan found nothing.
  $E64B,$08 Go to #R$DC15 if bit 7 of #REGh is clear.
  $E653,$01 Return.
N $E654 Named from the script commands at #R$F304, #R$F31C, #R$F339 and #R$F3B2.
B $E654,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$E658.
N $E658 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E654.
  $E658,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E65B,$03 Call #R$E619 to scan the objects for that id.
  $E65E,$03 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the object scan found nothing.
  $E661,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 if bit 7 of #REGh is set.
  $E669,$05 Go to #R$DC15 if the saved pointer is zero.
  $E66E,$01 Return.
N $E66F Named from the script commands at #R$F309, #R$F321, #R$F33E and #R$F3B7.
B $E66F,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$40, value #N$00, handler #R$E673.
N $E673 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E66F.
  $E673,$03 Read the character being processed.
  $E676,$03 Call #R$E619 to scan the objects for that id.
  $E679,$02 Go to #R$E683 if the object scan found nothing.
  $E67B,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 if the pointer is not zero.
@ $E683 label=ScanObjects_Next
  $E683,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E685,$01 Return.

c $E686 Reject Low Character Attribute
@ $E686 label=RejectLowCharacterAttribute
D $E686 Reads the character attribute and fails the action, through #R$DC15, when the attribute
. is below #N$32.
  $E686,$08 Return zero through #R$DC15 when the character attribute is below #N$32.
  $E68E,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E690,$01 Return.

c $E691 Process Character Attribute
@ $E691 label=ProcessCharacterAttribute
D $E691 Follows the character link for this attribute, and stops when the link runs out.
  $E691,$04 Return if the character attribute is below #N$32.
N $E695 Get character attribute.
  $E695,$03 Call #R$D29D to read the character's attribute.
  $E698,$01 Use the get character attribute result as the count.
@ $E699 label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_Loop
  $E699,$04 Point at the object display link.
@ $E69D label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_CountCharacterRepeat
  $E69D,$02 Count the next character and repeat from #R$E6A6.
  $E69F,$05 Jump back to #R$E699 while the object pointer is zero.
  $E6A4,$02 Try the next object from #R$E69D.

N $E6A6 Compare this object pointer with the list head.
@ $E6A6 label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_Next
  $E6A6,$07 Point #REGhl at the object display link and #REGde at #R$9FF0+#N$06, and clear the carry.
  $E6AD,$02 See whether this object pointer is #R$9FF0+#N$06.
  $E6AF,$02 Jump ahead to #R$E6B9 if the two pointers meet.
  $E6B1,$08 Take the police message, then call #R$A59F to print the police message.
@ $E6B9 label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_TestActionSucceeded
  $E6B9,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E6BB,$01 Return.

N $E6BC Named from the script commands at #R$F326 and #R$F3BC.
N $E6BC Individual script handlers. Each one ends by returning success or by bailing out
. through #R$DC15.
B $E6BC,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$0D, value #N$0C.
@ $E6BE label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_TestCount
N $E6BE Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E6BC.
  $E6BE,$08 Go to #R$E6E3 if no object carries this id.
  $E6C6,$06 Read the low byte of the text pointer and read the high byte of the text
. pointer.
  $E6CC,$03 Take the character busy message.
N $E6CF Print text and handle control characters.
  $E6CF,$03 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $E6D2,$09 Call #R$A419 to handle the picture for this message.
  $E6DB,$08 Print a quotation mark and start a new line.
@ $E6E3 label=ProcessCharacterAttribute_Succeeded
  $E6E3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E6E5,$01 Return.

b $E6E6 Display Record
D $E6E6 Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message commands at #R$F2F7, #R$F35F and
. #R$F371 pass this word to #R$C591.
N $E6E6 Named from the script commands at #R$F2F7, #R$F35F and #R$F371.
B $E6E6,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$4D, value #N$00, handler #R$E6F6.

c $E6EA Store The Action Flags
@ $E6EA label=StoreActionFlags
D $E6EA Copies the room display parameter onto the character and reports success.
N $E6EA Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E6E6, from the show-message commands at #R$F2F7,
. #R$F35F and #R$F371.
  $E6EA,$03 Call #R$CFB6 to find this character's script entry.
  $E6ED,$06 Copy the room display parameter into the character's action flags.
  $E6F3,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E6F5,$01 Return.

c $E6F6 Compare The Character With The Room
@ $E6F6 label=CompareCharacterWithRoom
D $E6F6 Fails the action unless the character being processed matches both room parameters. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $E6F6 Handler word in the display record at #R$E6E6, called via #R$CB34.
  $E6F6,$07 Compare the character being processed with the room display parameter.
  $E6FD,$03 Go to #R$DC15 when they match.
  $E700,$05 Count down one and point #REGix at the current object (#R$A013).
  $E705,$06 Go to #R$DC15 if it does not match byte #N$01 of that object.
  $E70B,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E70D,$01 Return.

b $E70E Display Record
D $E70E Display record read by #R$C9BF. The show-message command at #R$F35C passes this word to
. #R$C591.
N $E70E Named from the script command at #R$F35C.
B $E70E,$02 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$00, value #N$00.

c $E710 Print The Action Message
@ $E710 label=PrintActionMessage
D $E710 Prints the action message when the character's action flag is set. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $E710 Run by #R$CA64 after the display record at #R$E70E, from the show-message command at #R$F35C.
  $E710,$07 Save the television-data byte and call #R$CFB6 to find this character's script entry.
  $E717,$09 Point at the message at #R$6929+#N$032B, and go to #R$E725 to print it when the action flag is set.
  $E720,$05 Write #N$01 to the system Variables.
@ $E725 label=PrintActionMessage_PrintMessage
  $E725,$04 Call #R$A59F to print this message.
  $E729,$03 Store it in the system variables.
  $E72C,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $E72E,$01 Return.
