N $F380 Named from the script command at #R$F37A.
B $F380,$04 Display record read by #R$C9BF: flags #N$C8, value #N$00, handler #R$F389
. (bit 7: the script resumes at #R$F384).
B $F384,$04 #SCRIPT($04)
B $F388,$01 #SCRIPT($01)

c $F389 Compare The Room Parameters
@ $F389 label=CompareRoomParameters
D $F389 Compares the character being processed with the two room parameters. A non-zero return means the script step succeeded.
N $F389 Handler word in the display record at #R$F380, called via #R$CB34.
  $F389,$04 Point #REGix at the current object (#R$A013).
  $F38D,$04 Read the character being processed and count down one.
  $F391,$03 Compare it with byte #N$01 of that object.
  $F394,$03 Go to #R$DC15 if they differ.
  $F397,$03 Point #REGhl at the room display parameter at #R$A034.
  $F39A,$02 Bring the character id back up by one and compare it with the room display parameter.
  $F39C,$03 Go to #R$DC15 if it matches the room display parameter.
  $F39F,$02,b$01 Reset the zero flag.
  $F3A1,$01 Return.

b $F3A2 Script: Refresh The Room
D $F3A2 Shows a message, refreshes the room and jumps on.
. Named from the script commands at #R$F304, #R$F31C and #R$F339.
B $F3A2,$03 #SCRIPT($03)
B $F3A5,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $F3AA,$03 #SCRIPT($03)
B $F3AD,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $F3B2,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $F3B7,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $F3BC,$05 #SCRIPT($05)
B $F3C1,$01 #SCRIPT($01)

c $F3C2 Drawing
@ $F3C2 label=Drawing
E $F3C2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$7FA7 }
. LIST#
D $F3C2 Draws one location picture from line, fill and attribute commands. Pictures are not
. bitmaps. The pen starts at X=#N$7F and Y=#N$3F (Y counts up from the bottom, so this is
. screen row 112). #N$00 ends the picture. #N$08 followed by two bytes moves the pen.
. Bit 7 draws a line, bit 6 fills, and bit 5 paints attributes.
N $F3C2 Keep the caller's state and aim at the picture.
  $F3C2,$08 Keep the caller's registers and aim #REGiy at the picture passed in #REGhl.
N $F3CA Put the pen at its starting position.
  $F3CA,$0A Start the pen at X=#N$7F and Y=#N$3F (screen row 112), with the line counters at #N$01.
N $F3D4 Each command is one byte. #N$00 ends the picture.
@ $F3D4 label=Drawing_Loop
  $F3D4,$07 Finish the picture when the command byte is #N$00.
N $F3DB #N$08 moves the pen to the two coordinates that follow.
  $F3DB,$06 Step past this command and jump ahead to #R$F3EF unless it is #N$08.
  $F3E1,$07 Read the new X and mark it as a move.
  $F3E8,$07 Read the new Y and take the next command.
N $F3EF A set bit 7 is a line. Bits 0-2 choose the direction, and the next byte's top bits are the step and its low six bits the length.
@ $F3EF label=Drawing_Line
  $F3EF,$04 Jump to #R$F414 if this command does not draw a line.
  $F3F3,$04 The line direction is bits 0-2 of the command.
  $F3F7,$05 Keep the command's other line field.
  $F3FC,$07 The next byte's low six bits are the line length.
  $F403,$0C The top bits of that byte choose the step.
  $F40F,$03 Call #R$F56E to draw the line.
  $F412,$02 Jump back to #R$F3D4 for the next command.

N $F414 A set bit 6 is a fill. Bits 0-2 are the ink, and the next two bytes are the X and Y to fill from.
@ $F414 label=Drawing_Fill
  $F414,$04 Jump to #R$F42E if this command is not a fill.
  $F418,$0A Read the fill X and the ink colour.
  $F422,$05 Read the fill Y.
  $F427,$03 Call #R$F490 to fill the shape.
  $F42A,$01 Restore the pen from the stack.
  $F42B,$03 Take the next command.

N $F42E A set bit 5 paints attributes. Bits 0-2 become the paper colour, and the next bytes name the squares to paint.
@ $F42E label=Drawing_Attribute
  $F42E,$05 Take the next command unless this one paints attributes.
  $F433,$09 Use bits 0-2 as the paper colour.
  $F43C,$08 Read the attribute address.
  $F444,$05 Add #N$10 to the low byte of the attribute address.
@ $F449 label=Drawing_Run
  $F449,$09 Stop this attribute run when the command is #N$FF.
  $F452,$0B The low two bits are the direction and the rest is the run length.
@ $F45D label=Drawing_Paint
  $F45D,$0B Flip the ink when it matches the paper colour being painted, so it stays visible.
  $F468,$07 Write the paper colour into the attribute byte.
N $F46F Step up when the direction is up.
@ $F46F label=Drawing_Step
  $F46F,$03 Call #R$F514 when the direction is up.
N $F472 Step right when the direction is right.
  $F472,$01 Count down to the rightward direction.
  $F473,$03 Call #R$F540 when the direction is right.
N $F476 Step down when the direction is down.
  $F476,$01 Count down to the downward direction.
  $F477,$03 Call #R$F525 when the direction is down.
N $F47A Step left when the direction is left.
  $F47A,$01 Count down to the leftward direction.
  $F47B,$03 Call #R$F536 when the direction is left.
N $F47E Repeat for every attribute in the run.
  $F47E,$02 Repeat from #R$F45D for the length of the attribute run.
  $F480,$02 Read the next attribute run.

N $F482 Join the next step.
@ $F482 label=Drawing_AttributeDone
N $F482 The picture is finished.
  $F482,$03 Restore the pen from the stack.
  $F485,$03 Take the next picture command.

N $F488 Return.
@ $F488 label=Drawing_Finish
  $F488,$05 Restore the saved count, the saved source, the saved pointer and the saved picture command from the stack.
  $F48D,$01 Return.

g $F48E Flags: Fill Seeds
D $F48E One byte for each fill direction. #R$F490 records whether a seed is already saved for that row.
. Read by #R$F490.
B $F48E,$02

c $F490 Drawing Fill
@ $F490 label=DrawingFill
D $F490 Flood-fills from the pen and stores the ink at #R$F60B.
  $F490,$08 Store the ink, save the pen and the picture pointer, and push the #N$B0 end marker.
  $F498,$04 Save the end marker and read the pixel under the pen.
  $F49C,$02 Go to #R$F4A5 if the pixel under the pen is already set.
  $F49E,$05 Step the pen left and try that pixel again until the left edge.
  $F4A3,$02 No set pixel to the left, so start filling from #R$F4AB.

N $F4A5 Plot this pixel and step the pen right.
@ $F4A5 label=DrawingFill_Next
  $F4A5,$06 Plot this pixel, then step the pen right.
@ $F4AB label=DrawingFill_RecordDrawingPen
  $F4AB,$06 Clear the two bytes that remember a seed.
@ $F4B1 label=DrawingFill_Loop
  $F4B1,$07 Go to #R$F4CD if the pen is already at the top edge.
  $F4B8,$07 Jump ahead to #R$F4C8 if the pixel under the pen is already set.
  $F4BF,$06 Jump to #R$F4C8 if a seed is already saved for this row.
  $F4C5,$03 Save this spot as a seed and note it in #REGa.
@ $F4C8 label=DrawingFill_StepPen
  $F4C8,$04 Save that note, step the pen down.
  $F4CC,$01 Restore the note from the stack.
@ $F4CD label=DrawingFill_DrawingPenStep
  $F4CD,$06 Store the note and step the pen down.
  $F4D3,$04 Go to #R$F4EC, with #REGa=#N$00, if the pen could not step down.
  $F4D7,$07 Jump ahead to #R$F4E7 if the pixel under the pen is already set.
  $F4DE,$06 Jump to #R$F4E7 if a seed is already saved for this row.
  $F4E4,$03 Save this spot as a seed and note it in #REGa.
@ $F4E7 label=DrawingFill_StepPenUp
  $F4E7,$04 Save the note, step the pen up.
  $F4EB,$01 Restore the note from the stack.
@ $F4EC label=DrawingFill_DrawingPenDrawing
  $F4EC,$06 Store the note in the other pen byte and plot this pixel.
  $F4F2,$05 Go to #R$F4FF if the pen is already at the right edge.
  $F4F7,$05 Jump back to #R$F4B1 if the pixel under the pen is clear.
  $F4FC,$03 Plot this pixel.
@ $F4FF label=DrawingFill_ByteMatchesMarker
  $F4FF,$06 Take the next seed off the stack, and go back to #R$F499 until the #N$B0 end marker,
. pushed at the start of the fill, comes off.
  $F505,$05 Clear the drawing ink.
  $F50A,$02 Restore the picture pointer and the pen from the stack.
  $F50C,$01 Return.

c $F50D Keep Only The Bits That Are Set
@ $F50D label=KeepOnlyBitsThatAreSet
E $F50D View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$80EE }
. LIST#
D $F50D Reads the screen byte under the pen and keeps only the bits already set there.
  $F50D,$04 Call #R$F5FB to work out the pixel address.
  $F511,$01 Keep only the screen bits already set under the pen.
  $F512,$01 Restore the saved pointer from the stack.
  $F513,$01 Return.

c $F514 Attribute Up
@ $F514 label=AttributeUp
E $F514 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$80F5 }
. LIST#
D $F514 Moves the attribute address one row up. The attribute file begins at #N$5800, so a high byte of #N$57 means the step left the screen.
  $F514,$08 Keep the colour and the pen, and step the screen pointer back one attribute row.
  $F51C,$05 Jump ahead to #R$F522 if the high byte of the address is not #N$57.
  $F521,$01 Undo the step that passed the top row.
@ $F522 label=AttributeUp_Next
  $F522,$02 Restore the colour from the stack.
  $F524,$01 Return.

c $F525 Attribute Down
@ $F525 label=AttributeDown
E $F525 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8106 }
. LIST#
D $F525 Moves the attribute address one row down. A high byte of #N$5A means the step passed the end of the attribute file.
  $F525,$06 Keep the colour and step the attribute address down one row.
  $F52B,$05 Leave the new row in place when it is still on screen, and fall through when
. it is not.
  $F530,$01 Clear the carry before the row is stepped back.
  $F531,$02 Step back one row (#N$20) because the step left the attribute file.
  $F533,$02 Restore the pen and the colour from the stack.
  $F535,$01 Return.

c $F536 Attribute Left
@ $F536 label=AttributeLeft
E $F536 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8117 }
. LIST#
D $F536 Moves the attribute address one byte left, back toward the top of the attribute file at #N$5800.
  $F536,$02 Step back one attribute.
  $F538,$05 Jump ahead to #R$F53E unless the high byte is #N$57.
  $F53D,$01 Undo the step that passed the left edge.
@ $F53E label=AttributeLeft_Next
  $F53E,$01 Restore the colour from the stack.
  $F53F,$01 Return.

c $F540 Attribute Right
@ $F540 label=AttributeRight
E $F540 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8121 }
. LIST#
D $F540 Moves the attribute address one byte right. A high byte of #N$5A means it passed the end of the attribute file.
  $F540,$02 Step to the next attribute.
  $F542,$05 Skip the correction unless the pointer has gone past the end of the attribute file
. (high byte #N$5A).
  $F547,$01 Undo the step that passed the right edge.
@ $F548 label=AttributeRight_Next
  $F548,$01 Restore the colour from the stack.
  $F549,$01 Return.

c $F54A Step Pen Up
@ $F54A label=StepPenUp
E $F54A View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$812B }
. LIST#
D $F54A Moves the pen one pixel up the screen. #REGe counts up from the bottom row, because #R$F5FB
. hands it to the ROM PIXEL-ADD routine. When the pen would pass the top row (#REGe=#N$B0) it stays
. put and the zero flag is set.
  $F54A,$07 Move the pen up and return when it is still on screen.
  $F551,$03 The pen passed the top row, so step back and tell the caller it did not move.
  $F554,$01 Return.

c $F555 Step Pen Down
@ $F555 label=StepPenDown
E $F555 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$813A }
. LIST#
D $F555 Moves the pen one pixel down the screen. When #REGe would wrap through #N$FF (below the
. bottom row) the pen stays put and the zero flag is set.
  $F555,$07 Move the pen down and return when it is still on screen.
  $F55C,$01 The pen passed the bottom row, so step back.
  $F55D,$01 Return.

c $F55E Step Pen Right
@ $F55E label=StepPenRight
E $F55E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8141 }
. LIST#
D $F55E Moves the pen one pixel right, and steps back if X wraps.
  $F55E,$02 Move the pen right and return when it is still on the line.
  $F560,$04 The pen wrapped, so step back and tell the caller.
  $F564,$01 Return.

c $F565 Step Pen Left
@ $F565 label=StepPenLeft
E $F565 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8148 }
. LIST#
D $F565 Moves the pen one pixel left. The top bit stays set while X is on the picture, and is clear once the pen has passed the left edge.
  $F565,$04 Move the pen left and return when it is still on the picture.
  $F569,$04 The pen passed the left edge, so step back and tell the caller it did not move.
  $F56D,$01 Return.

c $F56E Drawing Line
@ $F56E label=DrawingLine
E $F56E View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$8151 }
. LIST#
D $F56E Draws a straight line from the pen. Bit 0 of the direction chooses a steep line.
  $F56E,$04 Jump ahead to #R$F5A2 if bit 0 is set (steep line).
  $F572,$05 Keep the picture pointer and the counters, then plot this pixel.
  $F577,$04 Jump ahead to #R$F582 if bit 2 is clear.
  $F57B,$05 Go to #R$F59F if the pen is already at the left edge.
  $F580,$02 The pen has moved left, so skip the rightward step and join #R$F587.

N $F582 Steps the pen right, and steps it up or down (bit 1) when this pixel column is finished.
@ $F582 label=DrawingLine_Next
  $F582,$05 Go to #R$F59F if the pen is already at the right edge.
@ $F587 label=DrawingLine_TestCountFinished
  $F587,$03 Jump ahead to #R$F59C if the count is not finished.
  $F58A,$04 Jump ahead to #R$F595 if bit 1 is clear.
  $F58E,$05 Go to #R$F59F if the pen is already at the bottom edge.
  $F593,$02 Carry on with the line once this step is ready.

N $F595 Steps the pen up and repeats for the pixels still left.
@ $F595 label=DrawingLine_StepPenUp
  $F595,$05 Go to #R$F59F if the pen is already at the top edge.
@ $F59A label=DrawingLine_CountHasRun
  $F59A,$01 Restore the counters from the stack.
  $F59B,$04 Keep a copy, and plot the next pixel from #R$F574 until the length in #REGl runs out.
@ $F59F label=DrawingLine_BringSavedCount
  $F59F,$02 Restore the saved count and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $F5A1,$01 Return.

N $F5A2 Plots this pixel and steps down when bit 1 is set.
@ $F5A2 label=DrawingLine_PlotPixel
  $F5A2,$05 Keep the picture pointer and the counters, then plot this pixel.
  $F5A7,$04 Jump ahead to #R$F5B2 if bit 1 is clear.
  $F5AB,$05 Go to #R$F5CF if the pen is already at the bottom edge.
  $F5B0,$02 Skip the upward step and carry on from #R$F5B7.

N $F5B2 Steps the pen up, and steps it left or right when this pixel row is finished.
@ $F5B2 label=DrawingLine_StepPenUpAgain
  $F5B2,$05 Go to #R$F5CF if the pen is already at the top edge.
@ $F5B7 label=DrawingLine_TestCountFinishedAgain
  $F5B7,$03 Jump ahead to #R$F5CC if the count is not finished.
  $F5BA,$04 Jump ahead to #R$F5C5 if bit 2 is clear.
  $F5BE,$05 Go to #R$F5CF if the pen is already at the left edge.
  $F5C3,$02 Carry on with the line once the other step is ready.

N $F5C5 Steps the pen right and repeats for the pixels still left.
@ $F5C5 label=DrawingLine_StepsPenRight
  $F5C5,$05 Go to #R$F5CF if the pen is already at the right edge.
@ $F5CA label=DrawingLine_CountHasRunAgain
  $F5CA,$01 Restore the counters from the stack.
  $F5CB,$04 And plot the next pixel from #R$F5A4 until the length in #REGl runs out.
@ $F5CF label=DrawingLine_BringSavedCountAgain
  $F5CF,$02 Restore the saved count and the saved pointer from the stack.
  $F5D1,$01 Return.

c $F5D2 Plot A Pixel
@ $F5D2 label=PlotPixel
E $F5D2 View the equivalent code in;
. #LIST
. { #HOBBIT$81B5 }
. LIST#
D $F5D2 Sets the pixel under the pen and paints its attribute with the ink at #R$F60B.
  $F5D2,$06 Ask the ROM for this pixel's address and keep the mask.
  $F5D8,$01 Take the high byte of the pixel address.
  $F5D9,$02 Keep the third of the screen it falls in.
  $F5DB,$03 Shift that third down to the low bits.
  $F5DE,$02 Add #N$58 to reach the attribute file.
  $F5E0,$05 Point at the attribute and clear its ink.
  $F5E5,$09 Rotate the ink at #R$F60B into the paper bits and skip the clash test when it already
. differs from the attribute.
  $F5EE,$02 Flip the ink when it matches the paper colour already there, so it stays visible.
  $F5F0,$05 Shift the ink back into place and merge it into the attribute.
  $F5F5,$02 Restore the pixel address and the mask from the stack.
  $F5F7,$03 Set the pixel in the display byte.
  $F5FA,$01 Return.

c $F5FB Pixel Address
@ $F5FB label=PixelAddress
D $F5FB Asks the ROM for the display address of this pixel and builds the bit mask for it. #REGe is
. counted up from the bottom of the screen, and the ROM turns that into the screen row.
  $F5FB,$06 Call #SKOOLKID$22AA(PIXEL_ADD).
  $F601,$04 Take the column the ROM returned, count one further, and start the mask at #N$01.
@ $F605 label=PixelAddress_Loop
  $F605,$05 Shift the pixel mask until it covers this column.
  $F60A,$01 Return.

g $F60B Flags: Drawing Ink
D $F60B Ink byte stored by #R$F490 and shifted into the attribute of the plotted pixel by #R$F5D2.
B $F60B,$01

b $F60C Table: Location Graphics
@ $F60C label=Table_LocationGraphics
D $F60C Pictures are drawing commands executed by #R$F3C2, not bitmaps. The pen starts at X=#N$7F and Y=#N$3F (Y counts up from the bottom, so this is screen row 112). #N$00 ends the picture. #N$08 and the next two bytes move the pen, with bit 7 of X set. Bit 7 draws a line: bits 0-2 are the direction and the next byte holds the step in its top bits and the length in its low six bits. Bit 6 fills in the ink from bits 0-2, using the next two bytes as X and Y. Bit 5 paints attributes: bits 0-2 are shifted up to the paper colour, the next two bytes form the attribute address with #N$10 added to the low byte, and each following byte is a run until #N$FF. Bits 0-1 of a run select up, right, down or left.
B $F60C,$01 #GFXLOC
W $F60D,$02 #GFXPIC
L $F60C,$03,$0E
B $F636,$01 Terminator.

u $F637 Unused
D $F637 Padding byte between the graphics table and the first picture.
B $F637,$01

b $F638 Graphics: Slater Street
@ $F638 label=Image_SlaterStreet
D $F638 #PICTURE($F638)(slater-street,Slater Street)
. Used by #R$F3C2 through the location graphics table at #R$F60C.
  $F638,$00B5

